[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Son et al., 2023-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8b666e9d4399c3942cf6386125e8c545d2331f16)
- venue: International Conference on Machine Learning
- citation count: 43
[method]
The paper studies a scale-conditioned transfer adaptation method for constructive DRL solvers, called Meta-SAGE with a scale-conditioned initial embedding. It starts from pretrained encoder-decoder policies such as POMO and Sym-NCO that were trained on N=100 instances. A learned module g_phi(h,N) transforms the original context embedding h into a scale-conditioned initial embedding h_S before test-time adaptation. The module is trained with a distillation objective that encourages h_S to approximate the embedding obtained after many SAGE adaptation steps, and with a zero-shot simulation objective that encourages h_S to already produce good solutions before adaptation. At inference, SAGE performs instance-wise parameter adaptation for a fixed number of steps, and the scale-conditioned embedding is used as the initial state. This is related to the proposed ICAM adaptation function f(N,d_ij) = -alpha log2 N d_ij because both inject problem-scale information into the construction process of a pretrained neural routing solver to improve cross-scale generalization, but the paper uses a learned embedding transformation and meta-adaptation rather than an explicit analytic pairwise-distance bias or an attention-free module.
[results]
On TSP, CVRP, PCTSP, and OP, Meta-SAGE generally improved objective gaps over active search, efficient active search, and simulation-guided beam search with EAS. For TSP N=500, POMO-based Meta-SAGE reached 17.131 with a 3.559% gap versus EAS 18.135 with a 9.362% gap and SGBS 18.191 with a 9.963% gap; Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE reached 17.095 with a 3.339% gap. For TSP N=1000, POMO-based Meta-SAGE reached 25.924 with a 12.038% gap versus EAS 30.744 with a 32.869% gap; Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE reached 25.798 with an 11.488% gap versus EAS 31.241 with a 35.017% gap. On CVRP N=200, POMO-based Meta-SAGE reached 22.001 with a -0.009% gap, slightly better than LKH3 22.003, while Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE reached 21.982 with a -0.094% gap; however, active search was better for Sym-NCO on CVRP N=200, reaching 21.955 with a -0.218% gap. On CVRP N=500, Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE reached 63.281 with a -0.028% gap, better than LKH3 63.299. On PCTSP, Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE outperformed OR-Tools at all scales, with gaps of -8.236% at N=200, -8.412% at N=500, and -31.31% at N=1000. On OP N=500, Meta-SAGE reached 98.762 with a -1.457% gap, better than Compass 97.323. The paper also reports that zero-shot costs before adaptation are lower than EAS, indicating that the scale-conditioned initial embedding improves transferability. The excerpt does not report confidence intervals or statistical significance tests.
[context]
Experiments used pretrained POMO and Sym-NCO models trained on N=100 instances and evaluated on larger instances: N=200 with 1,000 instances, N=500 with 128 instances, and N=1,000 with 128 instances. Baselines included active search, efficient active search, and simulation-guided beam search with EAS. Reference solvers were Concorde for TSP, LKH3 for CVRP, OR-Tools for PCTSP, and Compass for OP. Runtime was measured for solving whole instances on a single NVIDIA A100 40GB GPU and an Intel Scalable Gold CPU. EAS and SGBS were stopped when their runtime exceeded the runtime of Meta-SAGE with 200 adaptation iterations, while active search used 200 iterations per instance. The method is relevant to the proposed research plan because it tests the same core assumption that scale-aware conditioning can help neural routing solvers generalize from small training scales to larger test scales, but it does not evaluate the proposed explicit f(N,d_ij) bias, AAFM, or three-stage training scheme.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 2]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Gao et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d708a031c4699220dd3802bbd2f1933efd90204f)
- venue: International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence
- citation count: 99
[method]
The paper evaluates ELG-POMO, an ensemble of a global POMO policy and an auxiliary transferrable local policy for TSP and CVRP. The global policy is a Transformer encoder-decoder without positional embedding that encodes the full instance and decodes a tour by pointer attention over candidate nodes, with invalid actions masked and rewards equal to negative tour length. The local policy uses a different state and action space centered on the local neighborhood of the current node, and its construction process is similar to the global policy. Training is aligned with POMO on uniformly distributed instances with N=100, uses greedy inference, and POMO-based methods use x8 instance augmentation. Experiments compare ELG-POMO against heuristic solvers, POMO variants, cross-distribution, cross-scale, and diffusion-based methods on TSPLIB, CVRPLIB Set-X and Set-XXL, synthetic distributions, and uniform N=100 instances. This paper method is related to the proposed ICAM adaptation function because both target the same limitation of constructive neural routing solvers: poor cross-scale and cross-distribution generalization. ELG improves generalization by adding a local policy to a global POMO backbone, whereas the proposed ICAM method would inject an explicit scale- and distance-dependent bias f(N,d_ij) into attention and decoder compatibility; ELG is also listed as a baseline in the ICAM research plan.
[results]
On the N<=200 TSPLIB/CVRPLIB subsets, ELG-POMO outperforms all compared methods, with gap reductions of 0.53% on CVRP and 0.50% on TSP relative to the runner-up. On the 200<N<=1000 or 200<N<=1002 subsets, ELG-POMO reduces the CVRP gap from 6.95% for Omni-POMO to 6.46%, and reduces the TSP gap from 7.47% to 5.90%. On four real-world CVRPLIB Set-XXL instances with N between 3000 and 7000, ELG-POMO is best on three instances and substantially reduces gaps versus other POMO-based methods. On uniform N=100 instances, ELG-POMO obtains 0.22% gap on TSP100 and 1.22% on CVRP100, surpassing all compared methods on CVRP100 and remaining competitive on TSP100. On difficult synthetic TSP distributions, ELG* outperforms AMDKD on three of four N=100 distributions and all four N=200 distributions, with N=200 gaps of 1.84%, 1.62%, 1.43%, and 1.58% versus AMDKD gaps of 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54% for cluster, mixture, explosion, and rotation. Runtime is lower than BQ and diffusion-based methods, higher than pure POMO due to the local policy, and reported as 1.90s per instance compared with 16m for LKH3/HGS in one comparison, although heuristic solvers achieve better optimality gaps.
[context]
Training instances are uniformly distributed with N=100, matching the common POMO setting, while Omni-POMO uses a stronger cross-distribution and cross-scale training protocol. Evaluation uses average gap to best known solutions and average solving time. Test conditions include TSPLIB and CVRPLIB Set-X subsets with N<=200 and 200<N<=1000 or 1002, CVRPLIB Set-XXL real-world instances with N in [3000,7000], small-scale synthetic distributions with N=100 and N=200, and uniform N=100 in-distribution tests. Inference is greedy, POMO-based methods use x8 instance augmentation, and best known solutions are from Concorde or LKH3 for uniform instances and from benchmark BKS for TSPLIB/CVRPLIB. The provided sections do not specify hardware or detailed local-policy hyperparameters, but the method is reproducible from the stated POMO-aligned training and public code/data. This context matters for ICAM because the proposed adaptation function is intended to improve the same large-scale generalization regime where ELG already shows gains but still trails optimized heuristic solvers and some cross-scale methods on large TSP instances.
[end related work 2]

[start related work 3]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Luo et al., 2023-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3c2f14a28ba5e826c63c294f4cca01a8217a217e)
- venue: Neural Information Processing Systems
- citation count: 263
[method]
The paper studies LEHD, a constructive neural combinatorial optimization method for TSP and CVRP that uses a light encoder and a heavy decoder. The model is trained with supervised learning to construct partial solutions during training and complete tours or routes during inference, and it then applies Random Re-Construct, or RRC, iterations to improve the generated solution. This paper method is related to the proposed ICAM method and research plan because both target large-scale TSP/CVRP generalization and fast constructive inference. LEHD provides a decoder-centric constructive baseline, whereas the proposed ICAM method would additionally inject instance-specific scale and geometry information through the adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -α·log₂N·d_ij and an attention-free adaptation attention module.
[results]
On uniformly distributed TSP instances, LEHD has fast greedy inference and, with 100 RRC iterations, outperforms other learning-based methods with reasonable inference time. With more than 500 RRC iterations, the optimality gap is nearly optimal for TSP100 and TSP200, less than 0.2% for TSP500, and about 0.8% for TSP1000. On uniformly distributed CVRP instances, LEHD with 300 RRC iterations outperforms most learning-based methods on all instances except EAS on CVRP100, where EAS has better performance but much longer inference time. With 500 RRC iterations, LEHD outperforms LKH3 on CVRP100 through CVRP500, and with 1000 RRC iterations it reaches about a 1% gap to LKH3 on CVRP1000. On real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances, LEHD with greedy inference and 1000 RRC iterations outperforms POMO and BQ on all reported instances. In comparisons with search-based or improvement-based methods on 128 TSP instances, LEHD RRC100 achieves 0.0761% gap in 1.2 minutes on TSP200, 0.343% in 8 minutes on TSP500, and 1.218% in 43 minutes on TSP1000. This beats SO-mixed, which reports 0.636% in 21 minutes on TSP200, 2.401% in 32 minutes on TSP500, and 2.800% in 56 minutes on TSP1000, and also beats H-TSP with LKH-3, which reports 4.06% in 7.5 minutes on TSP1000, while Concorde achieves 0.000% but requires 3 minutes, 32 minutes, and 7.8 hours for TSP200, TSP500, and TSP1000 respectively. These outcomes are relevant to the ICAM research plan because they show that constructive learning plus post-construction improvement can approach strong solvers on large TSP/CVRP instances, but the paper does not evaluate the proposed scale-aware adaptation function or attention-free adaptation attention module.
[context]
The experiments use uniformly distributed TSP and CVRP instances with sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, as well as real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances with different sizes and distributions. Evaluation is based on optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference time, with greedy inference and RRC budgets of 100, 300, 500, or 1000 iterations. The comparison with SO-mixed and H-TSP uses 128 TSP instances. The paper also compares heavy decoder versus heavy encoder structures and supervised versus reinforcement training: POMO trained with supervised learning performs well on TSP100 but poorly on large-scale problems, supporting the paper’s claim that generalization comes from the heavy decoder structure; reinforcement learning with the POMO strategy does not converge in a reasonable time, while supervised learning is more suitable for training LEHD on TSP50 under the same computational budget. Hardware and full hyperparameter details are not specified in the provided excerpt. These conditions are directly relevant to the ICAM plan, which also emphasizes cross-scale generalization, TSP/CVRP evaluation, inference time, and architecture modifications for large-scale routing, but ICAM would add explicit instance-conditioned scale and pairwise-distance bias to the construction process rather than relying only on heavy-decoder construction and RRC.
[end related work 3]

[start related work 4]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Wang et al., 2024-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b6bceec0cce24f875623c275ed294461a96772dc)
- venue: arXiv.org
- citation count: 19
[method]
The paper introduces DAR, a distance-aware attention reshaping method for neural CVRP solvers. It computes standard dot-product attention scores from the current state query and encoder node features, then computes Euclidean distances from the current node to each candidate node. A logarithmic distance transform is applied, a hyperparameter K defines a local neighborhood, nodes inside the neighborhood receive positive distance scores and nodes outside receive negative scores, and the distance score is added to the attention score before normalization with a constant C=50 to produce the next-node probability. The method has no learned parameters and is designed to reduce attention dispersion when a model trained on small instances is applied to larger instances by prioritizing nearby nodes and suppressing distant high-attention nodes. This is closely related to the proposed ICAM adaptation function because both inject distance-based bias into the construction process, but DAR uses only current-to-candidate distance with fixed hyperparameters, whereas ICAM proposes a learnable, instance-scale-conditioned bias f(N,d_ij) = -alpha log2 N d_ij integrated into an attention-free encoder and decoder compatibility.
[results]
On CVRPLib, DAR achieved gaps of 5.66% on 100 Set-X instances with 100 to 1000 nodes, 5.20% on 35 Set-XML instances with 100 to 5000 nodes, and 10.82% on 6 Set-XXL instances with 3000 to 11000 nodes; on a 32-instance Set-X subset it reported 5.27%. It outperformed greedy, which had gaps of 21.03%, 19.64%, and 13.08%, POMO at 8.31%, 10.51%, and 30.85%, ELG at 5.92%, 5.95%, and 16.08%, and Omni-VRP at 6.47% and 10.60% with no Set-XXL value. On Set-XXL, DAR was better than TAM-AM at 30.07% and TAM-LKH3 at 22.50%, while GLOP variants were reported with negative gaps of -19.75% and -18.80%, making that part of the comparison ambiguous unless the BKS reference or sign convention is clarified. DAR did not beat LKH3 or HGS on Set-X and Set-XML, where LKH3 and HGS gaps were 1.00% and 0.11% or 0.00%, respectively, but it was much faster, taking 0.39 seconds on Set-X versus at least 16 minutes for LKH3 and HGS on the stated CPU.
[context]
The experiments focused on CVRP generalization from small to large scale. Training used randomly generated coordinates and demands, while testing used real CVRPLib instances: 100 Set-X instances, 35 Set-XML instances, and 6 Set-XXL instances spanning 100 to 11000 nodes. The distance module assumed coordinates in [0,1]x[0,1], used Euclidean distance, a logarithmic distance transform, a neighborhood-limit hyperparameter K, and a normalization constant C=50. Some baselines were run with the same training method and default settings when source code was available, while other baseline numbers were taken from original reports. The paper reports optimality gaps relative to best known solutions and inference time, and notes that DAR can use GPU parallelism, whereas classical solvers become slow on instances with more than 10000 nodes. Relative to the proposed research plan, this paper provides a CVRP-only, standard-attention, fixed-distance-bias comparison point and does not test TSP or ATSP, a learnable alpha, explicit scale conditioning by N, an attention-free module, or the three-stage training scheme.
[end related work 4]

