[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Chen et al., 2025-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c61adb54084184302659765dcc30d4a9e9ddcc10)
- venue: Neural Information Processing Systems
- citation count: 18
[method]
The paper's related method is Test-Time Projection Learning, abbreviated TTPL, which uses test-time designed projection strategies for large-scale TSP and CVRP instances and applies them to a constructive LEHD neural routing model. The reported pipeline includes greedy construction or greedy sampling, an optional Multi-View Decision Fusion component, and an optional RRC refinement stage with 1000 iterations. The method is evaluated on synthetic TSP and CVRP instances at 1K, 5K, 10K, 50K, and 100K nodes, as well as on TSPLIB and CVRPLIB benchmark groups. This method relates to the proposed ICAM adaptation function because both aim to improve cross-scale generalization of constructive neural routing solvers by injecting instance-specific, scale- and geometry-aware information into the solution construction process. The key difference is that the paper uses designed projections and multi-view decision fusion, whereas the proposed ICAM method uses a learnable scalar adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -α·log₂N·d_ij integrated into an attention-free encoder-decoder architecture.
[results]
On synthetic datasets, TTPL with purely greedy construction outperforms other greedy constructive methods on most scales except TSP1K, and TTPL with purely greedy sampling is best on all CVRP scales except CVRP1K. With RRC for 1000 iterations, TTPL achieves the best performance on all TSP scales, reducing optimality gap by 61.11%, 75.59%, 56.20%, 69.61%, and 68.29% for TSP1K, TSP5K, TSP10K, TSP50K, and TSP100K relative to the leading alternative method. For CVRP, TTPL with RRC reduces optimality gap by 53.98%, 85.99%, 86.20%, and 88.37% on CVRP5K, CVRP10K, CVRP50K, and CVRP100K relative to the strongest competing baseline. On TSPLIB, TTPL greedy obtains 6.04% gap for 1K < n ≤ 5K, 4.57% for n > 5K, and 5.80% overall, solving 33/33 instances; TTPL-MVDF obtains 3.81%, 4.05%, and 3.88%; TTPL-MVDF with RRC1000 obtains 1.04%, 2.12%, and 1.37%, solving 33/33 instances. On CVRPLIB, TTPL greedy obtains 11.89% for 1K < n ≤ 7K, 14.93% for n > 7K, and 13.19% overall, solving 14/14 instances; TTPL-MVDF obtains 10.41%, 11.71%, and 10.97%; TTPL-MVDF with RRC1000 obtains 5.20%, 10.56%, and 7.50%, solving 14/14 instances. These results are better than baselines including GLOP, ELG, BQ, LEHD, SIGD, INViT, and LEHD with RRC, and several baselines fail on larger groups due to GPU memory limits. The provided sections do not report statistical significance tests.
[context]
Experiments were conducted on a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPU with 24GB of memory. The evaluation setup uses optimality gap and solved-instance counts as primary metrics, and compares greedy construction, MVDF, and RRC1000 variants. Synthetic instance scales range from 1K to 100K nodes for TSP and CVRP, while benchmark groups are TSPLIB 1K < n ≤ 5K and n > 5K, and CVRPLIB 1K < n ≤ 7K and n > 7K. The context is relevant to the proposed research plan because it demonstrates that instance-conditioned projection or adaptation can substantially improve large-scale routing generalization, but it uses a different mechanism from ICAM: the paper relies on test-time projection design and multi-view fusion rather than a learnable scale-distance bias inside an attention-free transformer-style encoder-decoder.
[end related work 6]

