[method]
The paper uses a neural vehicle-routing solver called DGL, which dynamically aggregates local and global information to construct solutions for TSP and CVRP instances of varying scales and distributions. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because both approaches target the same core problem: improving cross-scale and cross-distribution generalization of constructive neural routing solvers. However, the paper’s method differs from the proposed ICAM adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -α·log₂N·d_ij: DGL relies on dynamic local/global feature aggregation rather than an explicit scale- and pairwise-distance-conditioned bias, and it uses a standard attention-based decoder rather than the proposed attention-free adaptation module. The paper also uses a replacement-based self-improvement learning (SIL) training method, which is related to the proposed research plan’s focus on training schemes that improve robustness and generalization. In this SIL method, training is initialized by randomly generating B VRP instances from a uniform distribution, producing initial pseudo-labels with a nearest-neighbor heuristic to warm up the model, and then using data augmentation together with random removal of portions of the generated dataset during training. The model is trained as a single model on 100-node uniform instances and then evaluated directly on larger and differently distributed instances, which mirrors the proposed ICAM evaluation of cross-scale generalization.
[results]
The provided excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than numerical optimality gaps. DGL is reported to achieve state-of-the-art performance compared with global-view baselines such as Omni-VRP, LEHD, and BQ on synthetic TSP instances. It is also reported to outperform IN-ViT in both solution quality and inference speed, and to improve solution quality over another SIL-based model, GD, when using replacement-based SIL. On real-world TSPLIB, CVRPLIB, and country-map instances, DGL is reported to consistently demonstrate superior generalization performance compared with baselines. The paper also notes that most models experience performance degradation when generalizing to varying scales and distributions, that UDC fails to solve TSPs under 100 nodes and CVRPs under 200 nodes due to unknown errors, and that some baselines are marked as out-of-memory on real-world sets. No specific numerical gap values, absolute objective values, or statistical significance tests are included in the provided excerpt.
[context]
Experiments were conducted on synthetic and real-world routing benchmarks. Synthetic data included the MSVDRP dataset with 16 TSP subsets across uniform, clustered, explosion, and implosion distributions at TSP-100, TSP-1000, TSP-5000, and TSP-10000, and 12 CVRP subsets at CVRP-50, CVRP-500, and CVRP-5000. Real-world data included TSPLIB95 with 77 instances ranging from 51 to 18,512 nodes, CVRPLIB Set-X with 100 instances ranging from 100 to 1,000 nodes, and eight country maps with 194 to 22,775 nodes. DGL had 4 decoder layers, hidden dimension 128, and 8 attention heads. Training used a learning rate of 1e-4 with decay rate 0.97, 100 epochs, 100 iterations per epoch, batch size 256, 64 samples per iteration, and a replacement rate of 12.5%. The hyperparameter k was set to 30 for TSP and 50 for CVRP. Baselines used publicly available pretrained parameters and default settings; LEHD and BQ were evaluated with greedy search, and DGL and IN-ViT used the same inference setup. Experiments ran on an Intel Xeon Platinum 8352V CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU with 24GB memory. Performance was measured as the average gap to LKH results for synthetic data and to best known solutions for real-world data.
