The paper describes a sequence-to-sequence pointer network, referred to as Ptr-Net, for planar point-set combinatorial tasks: convex hull, Delaunay triangulation, and the Travelling Salesman Problem. Inputs are n planar points P_j = (x_j, y_j) sampled uniformly from [0, 1] x [0, 1], and outputs are sequences C_P representing the solution associated with the point set. The model uses a single-layer LSTM with either 256 or 512 hidden units, content-based input attention, and a pointer mechanism to emit solution elements. This is related to the proposed ICAM/AAFM method only at the level of constructive neural solvers for TSP and routing-like sequence generation over point sets; it is not a direct implementation of the proposed AFT-full attention-free module, does not replace multi-head attention, and does not include the scale-conditioned adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -alpha * log2(N) * d_ij.
