[method]
The paper evaluates a pointer-network-based constructive neural combinatorial optimization model for Euclidean TSP. The model encodes point coordinates with an LSTM encoder and uses a pointer/attention glimpse to sequentially select cities, with a mechanism that prevents selecting the same city twice. It is trained using supervised learning on optimal tours and reinforcement learning pretraining, and then evaluated with greedy decoding, sampling, and active search at inference time. This relates to the proposed research method and overall research plan because both concern constructive neural solvers for TSP/routing that build solutions sequentially and can be compared as reinforcement-learning-based neural combinatorial optimization approaches. However, the paper does not use multi-head attention, attention-free transformers, or the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, so it is relevant as a prior NCO TSP method and baseline context rather than as a direct implementation of the proposed AAFM module.
[results]
The supplied text does not provide exact average tour lengths or optimality gaps. It reports that reinforcement learning pretraining significantly improves over supervised pointer networks, that all tested methods surpass Christofides, and that greedy reinforcement-learning pretraining is time-efficient and only a few percent worse than optimality. Inference-time search is reported as important for approaching optimality, with early stopping possible at a small performance tradeoff. RL pretraining-Sampling and RL pretraining-Active Search are described as the most competitive methods and recover optimal solutions in a significant number of test cases. Many RL pretraining methods outperform OR-Tools local search, including RL pretraining-Greedy@16, which is reported to run similarly fast. The method is reported as superior to simulated annealing but less competitive than tabu search and much less competitive than guided local search. Active search from an untrained model is reported to require 7 hours per TSP50 instance and 25 hours per TSP100 instance.
[context]
Experiments use Euclidean TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100 with 1,000 uniformly random test graphs in the unit square. Training uses mini-batches of 128 sequences, LSTM cells with 128 hidden units, 128-dimensional coordinate embeddings, the Adam optimizer, initial learning rates of 1e-3 for TSP20 and TSP50 and 1e-4 for TSP100, learning-rate decay by 0.96 every 5000 steps, uniform initialization in [-0.08, 0.08], L2 gradient clipping at 1.0, up to one attention glimpse, and baseline decay alpha = 0.99 for active search. Greedy methods run on a single Nvidia Tesla K80 GPU, while Concorde and LK-H run on an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3 CPU and OR-Tools runs on an Intel Haswell CPU. The paper evaluates only up to 100 nodes, so it does not directly test the 1,000+ node cross-scale generalization target of the proposed ICAM research plan.
