- venue: Neural Information Processing Systems
- citation count: 1311
[method]
The paper uses an attention-based neural route-construction model for the capacitated vehicle routing problem, trained with policy-gradient actor-critic methods, including asynchronous advantage actor-critic. The model parameterizes a stochastic policy that sequentially selects the next customer or depot. Feasibility is enforced through masking: nodes with zero demand are masked, all customers are masked when the vehicle load is zero, and customers whose demand exceeds the remaining load are masked. Inference is performed with greedy decoding and beam search using beam widths of 5 and 10. This method is related to the proposed research method because it is an attention-based constructive neural VRP solver of the type that the proposed AAFM would modify by replacing standard attention with an attention-free adaptation module and adding an instance-conditioned scale and distance bias. The provided excerpt does not explicitly specify multi-head attention, but it describes the attention-based policy network that the proposed method targets for architectural replacement.
[results]
Experiments were run on 1000 instances for each of VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100. For VRP10 and VRP20, where optimal solutions were obtained by mixed integer programming, the beam-search method with width 10 was best: roughly 95% of VRP10 instances were within 10% of optimality, and roughly 95% of VRP20 instances were within 13% of optimality. Outliers were within 20-25% of optimality. For VRP50 and VRP100, where optimal values were not computationally affordable, winning-rate comparisons showed that classical heuristics were outperformed by the other approaches in almost 100% of samples. RL-greedy was comparable with OR-Tools, while adding beam search increased the method's winning rate against OR-Tools to above 60%. Runtime analysis showed that the log of solution time divided by the number of nodes remained nearly constant for the RL decoders, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep runtimes grew faster than linearly with problem size. OR-Tools times were included but were not directly comparable because OR-Tools was implemented in C++ while the other baselines were implemented in Python.
[context]
The experimental setting used randomly generated capacitated VRP instances with customer and depot locations drawn uniformly from the unit square and customer demands drawn uniformly from the discrete set 1 through 9. The vehicle started at the depot, and the main experiments did not allow split deliveries. Problem sizes were 10, 20, 50, and 100 customers, with 1000 instances per size. The model used greedy decoding and beam search with beam widths 5 and 10. Baselines included Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized Clarke-Wright with randomization depth and iteration settings of 5,5 and 10,10, randomized Sweep with random initial angles, and OR-Tools. Optimal solutions for VRP10 and VRP20 were obtained by solving a mixed integer formulation. The paper does not evaluate large-scale generalization to 1000 or more nodes, does not use the proposed adaptation function f(N, d_ij), and does not implement the proposed AFT-full-based AAFM; therefore it provides a small-scale attention-based policy-gradient baseline context for the proposed ICAM research plan.
