[method]
The paper uses an attention-based reinforcement learning constructive solver for TSP and CVRP. The policy network has an encoder that applies multi-head self-attention over node embeddings, using coordinates and, for CVRP, demand information, and a decoder that uses attention over candidate nodes with a masked softmax layer to select the next node. For TSP, node-pair selection is used to reduce symmetry, and for CVRP the mask prevents selecting visited nodes or capacity-infeasible nodes. The policy is trained with REINFORCE using a baseline and a negative tour-length reward, and deployed with beam search and multiple sampled trajectories. This is directly related to the proposed research method because the paper’s standard multi-head attention encoder-decoder is the component that the proposed AAFM/ICAM plan would replace with an attention-free adaptation module, injecting f(N, d_ij) = -alpha log2(N) d_ij into pairwise attention or compatibility calculations to improve cross-scale generalization while reducing attention cost.
[results]
On random Euclidean instances, the model trained on 100 nodes achieved single-trajectory optimality gaps of 1.3% for TSP-100, 1.7% for TSP-200, and 2.4% for TSP-500. Beam search reduced these gaps to 1.0%, 1.3%, and 1.9%, respectively, and multiple trajectories reduced them to 0.9%, 1.2%, and 1.8%. For CVRP, single-trajectory gaps were 1.5%, 1.9%, and 2.5% for 100, 200, and 500 nodes; beam search gave 1.2%, 1.6%, and 2.1%, and multiple trajectories gave 1.1%, 1.5%, and 2.0%. These results outperformed OR-tools in most settings, where OR-tools gaps were around 1.5%, 2.0%, and 2.5%, but remained far from LKH or Concorde references, which were around 0.01%-0.03% or 0. The reported values are average optimality gaps, and no statistical significance tests are included. The increasing gap with scale, and the absence of 1,000+ node tests, directly motivate the proposed ICAM adaptation function and AAFM for large-scale routing.
[context]
Experiments used random Euclidean routing instances with coordinates in [0,1]^2, evaluated at 100, 200, and 500 nodes. CVRP instances included a depot, node demands, and vehicle capacity, commonly capacity 100 with demands in [1,100]. The model was trained on 100-node instances and tested at larger scales without retraining. The architecture used multi-head attention, commonly with 8 encoder/decoder layers, 16 heads, and hidden dimension 128, trained with REINFORCE, Adam, batch size 64, and multiple sampled trajectories. Deployment used beam search with beam size around 10 and about 100 sampled trajectories for diversification. Evaluation was based on optimality gap to LKH or Concorde reference solutions, with inference time also considered. The paper’s evidence is limited to 100-500 node generalization, whereas the proposed research plan targets 1,000-5,000 node TSP/CVRP and 1,000-node ATSP with an explicit scale and distance adaptation term.
