The paper evaluates a linearized attention transformer as an alternative to standard softmax multi-head attention. The method rewrites attention using a feature map so that the attention computation can be performed with lower memory and computational cost, applies causal masking for autoregressive sequence modeling, and implements a constant-memory gradient computation in CUDA. The experimental task is a synthetic sequence-duplication or copy task in which the model must copy a sequence of symbols. The model is a 4-layer transformer with 8 attention heads, trained with batch size 64 and the RAdam optimizer. Baselines are standard softmax attention and Reformer with lsh-4 hashing, with lsh-1 and lsh-8 also shown in the cost/memory figures. This relates to the proposed ICAM/AAFM method only indirectly: both approaches replace standard multi-head attention with a lower-complexity attention computation inside a transformer encoder, but the paper does not use Attention Free Transformer, AFT-full, an instance-conditioned adaptation function, or vehicle routing problem instances.
