On the synthetic copy task, the linear attention model converged smoothly and reached the same final cross-entropy loss as softmax attention, while achieving lower loss than Reformer lsh-4. The paper attributes the advantage over lsh-4 to the absence of hashing noise. The supplied text does not report exact final loss values, statistical significance, or precise per-method runtime and memory endpoints. It does state that the linear transformer requires significantly less GPU memory and computation than softmax attention and achieves competitive performance with Reformer. The convergence plot spans 0 to 10000 gradient steps with a cross-entropy loss axis from 1e-4 to 1e0, and the cost/memory figures span sequence lengths from 2^9 to 2^16, time from 10^0 to 10^3 milliseconds, and GPU memory from 10^1 to 10^3 MB, but no exact numerical values for each method are provided in the excerpt.
[context]
The experiments are conducted on synthetic data rather than real routing instances. The sequence length is at most 128, the symbol set contains 10 different symbols plus a dedicated separator, and the model is small: 4 layers and 8 attention heads. Training uses RAdam with a learning rate of 1e-3 reduced to 1e-4 after 3000 updates. The implementation is in PyTorch, with the constant-memory gradient computation implemented in approximately 200 lines of CUDA code. For the Reformer baseline, reversible layers are not used, and memory consumption is measured only for the self-attention layer. These conditions limit direct transferability to the proposed 12-layer ICAM encoder and large-scale TSP, CVRP, or ATSP generalization setting; the paper provides a generic efficiency and convergence precedent for non-softmax attention, not direct evidence that AFT-full or the proposed adaptation function f(N, d_ij) improves neural routing solver performance.
