The paper describes POMO, a policy-gradient training method for constructive neural combinatorial optimization. For each instance, a policy network samples N solution trajectories, the return of each trajectory is computed, and the expected-return gradient is estimated using an advantage term formed by subtracting a shared baseline from each trajectory return. The excerpt contrasts this shared baseline with a greedy-rollout baseline, in which each sample rollout is compared with its argmax counterpart from the same starting node. POMO is trained with mini-batch gradient ascent and is presented for routing and combinatorial problems such as TSP, CVRP, and 0-1 knapsack. It relates to the proposed AAFM/ICAM work because the research plan uses POMO as the original loss and as a baseline, while the proposed AAFM is an encoder attention replacement that would be trained within this same constructive reinforcement-learning framework.
