The paper evaluates a Transformer-based constructive routing solver called MDAM. It uses a 3-layer Transformer encoder with 128-dimensional features and 8 attention heads, where the top encoder layer acts as an Embedding Glimpse layer that re-embeds node representations at fixed intervals. The model then uses 5 parallel decoders, each with 128-dimensional vectors and 8 attention heads, to construct routes. Invalid nodes are masked at each step to handle constraints in TSP, CVRP, SDVRP, OP, PCTSP, and SPCTSP. Training uses a KL loss term with coefficient 0.01 to encourage diversity among decoders, and inference uses greedy decoding or a customized beam search with beam width 50 and a merging technique. This attention-based encoder-decoder design is directly related to the proposed AAFM/ICAM plan because the paper's standard multi-head attention modules are the components the proposed method would replace with an attention-free, instance-conditioned adaptation mechanism, and its TSP/CVRP evaluation protocol is relevant to the plan's routing benchmarks.
