[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Xin et al., 2021-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/db4c9a6804fcc784bedbb9c3831f1c5921bcd089)
- venue: Neural Information Processing Systems
- citation count: 211
[method]
The closest method in the paper to the proposed AAFM is the Sparse Graph Network encoder-decoder used in NeuroLKH. It converts each routing instance into a sparse directed graph, embeds nodes and edges, and decodes edge scores and, for TSP, node penalties. Edge scores are trained with a supervised loss that increases scores for edges in the optimal tour and decreases scores for non-optimal edges; these scores are then used to construct an edge candidate set for LKH. Node penalties are trained with an unsupervised loss that transforms edge distances and pushes the node degrees in the minimum 1-tree toward 2, analogous to subgradient optimization but learned across a distribution of instances. The combined loss is L = L_beta + eta_pi L_pi. The learned edge candidate set and transformed edge distances guide multiple LKH lambda-opt trials. This relates to the proposed AAFM and ICAM plan because both use a neural encoder to capture pairwise node relationships for routing and aim to improve large-scale solving efficiency, but the paper does not use multi-head attention, Attention Free Transformer, or the explicit scale-distance adaptation function f(N, d_ij); it is a neural local-search guidance method rather than a constructive attention-free decoder.
[results]
The excerpt reports qualitative and benchmark-level outcomes rather than complete numerical optimality gaps. For TSP, NeuroLKH is described as effective compared with baseline algorithms, but no specific objective values or gaps are included in the provided text. For CVRP, PDP, and CVRPTW, NeuroLKH significantly improves solution quality over original LKH under time limits equal to LKH with 100, 1000, and 10000 trials. At the 100-trial limit, both methods fail to find feasible solutions on less than 1% of 300-node PDP and CVRPTW test instances; infeasible visits are moved to the end to obtain feasible tours. On 43 CVRPLIB instances with 100-300 nodes, NeuroLKH improves average performance on 38, 38, and 31 instances at the 100, 1000, and 10000 trial time limits, respectively. On 11 Solomon R2 instances, it outperforms LKH in 32 of 33 settings. SGN inference for 1000 test instances takes 1s, 3s, 7s, 10s, 19s, and 40s for 40, 100, 200, 300, 500, and 1000 nodes, which is small relative to the LKH search time. No statistical significance tests are reported.
[context]
Experiments use 2D Euclidean instances with coordinates sampled from the unit square. TSP training uses 101-500 nodes, with 500000/|V| instances per size, about 780000 total, and optimal edges from Concorde for supervised edge-score training; testing uses 1000 instances per size. For CVRP, demands are uniform integers 1-9, capacity is 40 + 0.1|V|, training uses 101-500 nodes with 120000/|V| instances per size, about 180000 total, and 10 epochs. PDP and CVRPTW use 41-200 nodes with 240000/|V| instances per size, and labels are generated by LKH with 10000 trials because optimal solutions are unavailable. Node penalties are not used for CVRP, PDP, or CVRPTW; only edge candidate sets are learned. LKH search hyperparameters follow the LKH example scripts with the SPECIAL setting for CVRP, PDP, and CVRPTW, and the default LKH candidate-set size is k = 5. Evaluation uses 1000 test instances for the smallest and largest training sizes and a larger generalization size, with time limits set to LKH 100, 1000, and 10000 trials. Hardware and exact TSP LKH trial counts are not specified in the excerpt. This context is relevant to ICAM's goals of cross-scale generalization and efficient encoding, but the paper's SGN is not an AAFM implementation and does not test the proposed attention-free adaptation module.
