- venue: Neural Information Processing Systems
- citation count: 178
[method]
The paper evaluates a learning collaborative policy (LCP) scheme for DRL-based routing solvers. It builds on an attention-model constructive policy and adds two components: a seeder that generates M diversified candidate tours/routes while exploring the full combinatorial action space, and a reviser that decomposes each candidate into K segments and re-optimizes the routing sequence within each segment in parallel for I sequential iterations. The seeder is trained with entropy regularization and a linear entropy schedule, and the whole scheme is applied to TSP, PCTSP, and CVRP. This is related to the proposed AAFM because both target AM-style neural routing solvers for TSP/CVRP, but the paper uses a standard attention-based AM baseline plus collaborative policies rather than replacing multi-head attention with an attention-free transformer or adding the instance-conditioned adaptation function.
[results]
On small-scale instances, the method outperformed all DRL baselines and OR-Tools on TSP, PCTSP, and CVRP. In the N=100 component ablation, the best reported configuration reached cost gaps of 0.88 percent on TSP, 1.02 percent on PCTSP, and 2.37 percent on CVRP relative to state-of-the-art solvers, improving over the weakest reported row of 2.65 percent, 1.64 percent, and 3.43 percent. LCP and LCP* outperformed DRL-2opt by 0.33 percent at N=100. For PCTSP, AM plus LCP with M=640 and I=1 beat OR-Tools with 10 seconds of local search and was 4 times faster at N=50 and 2 times faster at N=100; AM plus LCP with M=1280 and I=5 was 1.0 percent worse than ILS but 11 times faster at N=100. In time-performance analysis, the method occupied a winner region below 10 seconds for PCTSP at N=100 and N=500 and for CVRP at N=500, and below 5 seconds for CVRP at N=100.
[context]
Experiments used TSP, PCTSP, and CVRP instances with N=20, 50, 100, and 500 nodes, plus real-world TSPLIB TSP instances in an appendix. Training hyperparameters were kept the same as the attention-model baseline except the seeder batch size was 1024; entropy scheduling used alpha=0.5, Nw=sum of i, and linear weight w_t=(N-t)/Nw. Runtime was controlled by sample width M and revision iterations I for the DRL method, and by allowed local-search time for OR-Tools. Baselines included Gurobi, OR-Tools, Concorde, ILS, LKH3, S2V-DQN, EAN, GAT-T, DRL-2opt, and AM. Hardware details were not specified in the provided text. The paper does not use AFT-full, the proposed adaptation function f(N, d_ij), or 1000+ node cross-scale training, so it provides an AM-based DRL routing baseline and speed-performance context for the proposed research plan rather than direct evidence for the AAFM architecture.
