[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Gao et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d708a031c4699220dd3802bbd2f1933efd90204f)
- venue: International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence
- citation count: 99
[method]
The related paper method is ELG-POMO, an attention-based autoregressive construction model for TSP and CVRP. It uses a POMO global policy with a Transformer encoder that encodes unordered node embeddings from the full instance without positional embedding, and a decoder that computes query-key compatibility between the current state, including the current node and loaded capacity, and candidate node keys. Invalid actions are masked, scores are clipped, softmax is applied, and the next node is selected until a complete tour or route is constructed. A local policy is added to focus on the local neighborhood of the current node and is ensembled with the global policy. Training uses REINFORCE with a baseline, the reward is the negative objective value, and inference uses greedy decoding with POMO-style instance augmentation. This method is directly related to the proposed research method because the proposed AAFM would replace the standard Transformer attention component in such an encoder with an Attention Free Transformer full variant and inject the instance-conditioned adaptation function f(N, d_ij) into encoder attention. The overall research plan also treats ELG as a constructive NCO baseline and plans an ablation comparing AAFM against standard multi-head attention.
[results]
On TSPLIB and CVRPLIB instances with N <= 200, ELG-POMO outperformed the compared learning methods, with gap reductions of 0.53% on CVRP and 0.50% on TSP relative to the runner-up. On instances with N in the range 200 to 1000 or 1002, ELG-POMO reduced the CVRP gap from 6.95% for Omni-POMO to 6.46%, and reduced the TSP gap from 7.47% for Omni-POMO to 5.90%, despite Omni-POMO using a stronger training setting with complex distributions and varying scales. However, ELG-POMO lagged behind BQ and LEHD on TSP in that larger-scale range. On real-world CVRPLIB Set-XXL instances with N between 3000 and 7000, ELG-POMO was best on three of four instances and improved over POMO-based methods, although exact gap values were not provided in the excerpt. Runtime was 1.90 seconds per instance, much lower than 16 minutes for LKH3 and HGS, but the optimality gap was still worse than those highly optimized solvers. On uniform N = 100 instances, ELG-POMO achieved a 0.22% gap on TSP and a 1.22% gap on CVRP, improving over POMO at 0.40% and 1.34% and Sym-POMO at 0.41% and 1.43%, but remaining behind Pointerformer at 0.16% and T2TCO at 0.18% on TSP. On difficult synthetic distributions, retrained ELG* outperformed AMDKD on three N = 100 distributions and all four N = 200 distributions, with N = 200 gaps of 1.84%, 1.62%, 1.43%, and 1.58% compared with AMDKD gaps of 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54%.
[context]
Training instances were randomly generated uniform TSP and CVRP instances with N = 100. ELG-POMO training was aligned with POMO, while POMO and Sym-POMO were early stopped using a validation set with N = 200. Test conditions included TSPLIB, CVRPLIB Set-X and Set-XXL, small-scale synthetic distributions such as cluster, cluster mixture, explosion, and rotation, and uniform small-scale instances. For the synthetic comparison, POMO and ELG were retrained on uniform, cluster, and cluster mixture distributions to align with AMDKD. Evaluation metrics were average gap to best known solutions and average runtime per instance. Inference used greedy mode, and POMO-based methods used x8 instance augmentation. Detailed hyperparameters and hardware specifications were not included in the provided excerpt, although the paper stated that code and data were available. The method is relevant to the research plan because ICAM targets the same large-scale generalization limitation and modifies the encoder attention mechanism used by attention-based constructive solvers such as ELG-POMO.
