[method]
The paper uses Neural-3-OPT, an encoder-decoder reinforcement-learning method for improving TSP tours. It starts from a randomly generated initial tour and repeatedly applies 3-opt moves. At each step, an encoder computes an embedding of the current solution and the best solution so far; a link-selector decoder chooses three links to remove, and a reconnection-selector decoder chooses the reconnecting type. The process is formulated as a Markov decision process with state consisting of the current tour and best tour, action consisting of three removed links and a reconnecting type, transition applying the 3-opt move, and reward equal to the improvement in the best tour length. The model is trained with actor-critic policy optimization. This relates to the proposed ICAM method because both use neural encoder-decoder architectures for TSP and both examine generalization from smaller training instances to larger test instances, but the paper method is an improvement/local-search model rather than a constructive routing model and does not include the proposed adaptation function, AAFM, or 12-layer encoder.
[results]
The provided excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than exact numerical values. On TSP200, Neural-3-OPT outperforms L-2-OPT when trained and evaluated on TSP200. For cross-scale transfer, the model trained on TSP50 significantly outperforms L-2-OPT on TSP200, while the model trained on TSP100 performs worse than L-2-OPT on TSP200. The TSP200 training curve also converges faster and reaches a better tour length than L-2-OPT. No optimality gaps, exact tour lengths, or statistical significance values are provided in the excerpt.
