- in_text_citation: [(Vidal et al., 2012-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/76706c2bbba476c0c0d51ded8e7868daaf722cb3)
- venue: Operational Research
- citation count: 746
[method]
The paper evaluates a hybrid genetic search with adaptive diversity control, called HGSADC, on the capacitated vehicle routing problem, which is one of the routing problems targeted by the research plan and corresponds to the classical HGS-type baseline named in the plan. HGSADC is a population-based metaheuristic that maintains feasible and infeasible subpopulations, uses biased fitness ranks that combine penalized cost and diversity contribution, and applies parent selection, crossover, education/local search, and adaptive population management. For the capacitated vehicle routing problem, the paper adapts the multi-period method by replacing the multi-period distance measure with a broken-pairs arc-similarity distance, replacing the multi-period crossover with order crossover on a cyclic giant tour, and limiting the local-search neighborhood to 20 nodes because some instances contain up to 483 customer visits. The method was implemented in C++ and calibrated with a meta-evolutionary parameter optimizer. This is relevant to the proposed Instance-Conditioned Adaptation Model because the proposed model is a constructive neural routing solver for capacitated vehicle routing and other routing problems, and the research plan explicitly compares against HGS-type classical solvers; the paper therefore provides a non-neural capacitated vehicle routing benchmark rather than an encoder-decoder, attention-free, or scale-adaptive constructive method.
[results]
On 34 well-known capacitated vehicle routing problem instances, HGSADC achieved an overall average gap to the best known solutions of 0.11%, which the paper reports as equal to the performance of the best highly specialized algorithm in the literature. It also found 12 new best known solutions for a subset of standard benchmark instances. The paper reports that computation times were similar to state-of-the-art methods, although detailed per-instance times are not fully specified in the provided text.
[context]
The capacitated vehicle routing problem experiments treated the problem as a special case of the multi-depot periodic vehicle routing problem with one depot and one period. The main algorithmic changes were the broken-pairs distance, order crossover, cyclic route concatenation, and a reduced local-search neighborhood of 20 nodes. Experiments were run on an AMD Opteron 250 processor at 2.4 GHz, and reported CPU times were converted to equivalent Pentium IV 3.0 GHz times. Parameter calibration used a meta-evolutionary optimizer, with most parameters averaged across problem classes, the education rate set to 1, and generation size problem-dependent. The adaptive diversity management scheme was retained for the capacitated vehicle routing problem, and evaluation was based on average gaps to best known solutions and comparisons to state-of-the-art methods on 34 standard capacitated vehicle routing problem instances.
[end related work 2]

