[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(He et al., 2015-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2c03df8b48bf3fa39054345bafabfeff15bfd11d)
- venue: Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition
- citation count: 247036
[method]
The paper studies how increasing network depth affects optimization and generalization by training plain and residual convolutional networks on CIFAR-10 and ImageNet. For CIFAR-10, the authors build 20-, 32-, 44-, 56-, 110-, and 1202-layer networks using 3x3 convolutions, batch normalization, global average pooling, and a 10-way softmax head. Residual versions add identity shortcut connections between paired 3x3 layers so that the residual and plain models have the same depth, width, and parameter count. Training uses SGD with a batch size of 128 on two GPUs, weight decay of 0.0001, momentum of 0.9, no dropout, and a learning rate of 0.1 with step decay at 32k and 48k iterations for 64k total iterations. A 45k/5k train/validation split is used to determine training termination. For the 110-layer model, a learning-rate warm-up of 0.01 is used until training error falls below 80%, after which training resumes at 0.1. The ImageNet setup uses SGD with batch size 256, learning rate 0.1 with plateau-based decay, training for up to 600,000 iterations, random resized 224x224 crops, horizontal flip, color augmentation, and batch normalization. Evaluation uses 10-crop testing for comparison and multi-scale fully convolutional inference for best results. This relates to the proposed ICAM method because ICAM uses a deeper 12-layer encoder and includes an ablation of model depth; the paper provides a controlled study of how depth scaling affects training stability and representation quality, although it is in image classification rather than vehicle routing.
[results]
On CIFAR-10, plain networks show higher training error as depth increases, while residual networks reduce this optimization difficulty and improve test accuracy with depth. The 110-layer residual network converges well and reaches 6.43% test error, reported as among state-of-the-art for that setting despite using fewer parameters than FitNet and Highway. A 1202-layer residual network reaches training error below 0.1% and 7.93% test error, indicating that extreme depth can be optimized but may overfit the small dataset without stronger regularization. Layer-response analysis shows residual layers have smaller standard deviations than plain layers, and deeper residual networks have even smaller response magnitudes. On ImageNet, residual networks outperform plain counterparts and support deeper bottleneck architectures, indicating that depth can improve representation when optimization is managed. These outcomes are relevant to ICAM's depth ablation: they suggest that a 12-layer encoder may be beneficial if training stability is maintained, but also caution that deeper encoders can overfit when data, regularization, or training scale are limited.
[context]
CIFAR-10 contains 50k training images and 10k test images in 10 classes. Inputs are 32x32 images with per-pixel mean subtraction. The CIFAR-10 architecture uses feature map sizes of 32x32, 16x16, and 8x8 with 16, 32, and 64 filters respectively, and identity shortcuts are used so residual models match plain models in depth, width, and parameters. The 1202-layer model has 19.4M parameters and is trained without maxout or dropout, so its weaker test performance relative to the 110-layer model is attributed to overfitting. Hardware for CIFAR-10 training is two GPUs. The ImageNet setup uses a larger batch size of 256, longer training, and multi-scale test inference. These conditions matter for the ICAM research plan because the proposed routing solver will train on generated instances of varying scale and will ablate encoder depth; the paper shows that depth experiments should control parameter count, use validation-based stopping, consider warm-up for deeper models, and account for overfitting when the model is much larger than the training data or when regularization is weak.
[end related work 4]

