[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Bello et al., 2016-11)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d7878c2044fb699e0ce0cad83e411824b1499dc8)
- venue: International Conference on Learning Representations
- citation count: 2035
[method]
The paper trains a pointer-network TSP solver with an LSTM encoder and up to one attention glimpse, using reinforcement-learning pretraining to construct tours autoregressively and then evaluates greedy decoding, sampling, and active search. The pointer module selects the next city and prevents revisiting, which is analogous to the decoder compatibility calculation in the proposed ICAM. This method is related to the proposed research plan because both use constructive encoder-decoder neural routing models for TSP, but the paper uses a small LSTM/pointer architecture and standard RL pretraining rather than ICAM's 12-layer attention-free encoder, instance-conditioned adaptation function, or large-scale CVRP/ATSP training.
[results]
On 1,000 test graphs for TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100, RL pretraining significantly improves over supervised pointer-network training, and all evaluated RL methods surpass Christofides, including greedy decoding without search. Greedy methods are time-efficient and are only a few percent worse than optimal solutions. Inference-time search improves solution quality but increases running time, and early stopping gives a small performance tradeoff. RL pretraining-Sampling and RL pretraining-Active Search are the most competitive learned methods and recover optimal tours in a significant number of cases. For small solution spaces, Sampling with a finetuned softmax temperature outperforms Active Search, while for larger solution spaces Active Search is superior when controlling for the number of sampled solutions or running time. Many RL pretraining methods outperform OR-Tools local search, including RL pretraining-Greedy@16 with similar speed, but the learned methods are less competitive than tabu search and guided local search. Active Search from an untrained model is competitive but requires 7 hours per TSP50 instance and 25 hours per TSP100 instance.
[context]
Experiments use Euclidean TSP instances with 20, 50, and 100 nodes, points drawn uniformly in the unit square, and 1,000 test graphs per scale. Training uses mini-batches of 128, LSTM cells with 128 hidden units, 128-dimensional coordinate embeddings, Adam with initial learning rate 1e-3 for TSP20/TSP50 and 1e-4 for TSP100, decayed by 0.96 every 5,000 steps, uniform initialization in [-0.08, 0.08], and gradient L2 clipping to 1.0. The model uses up to one attention glimpse; Active Search uses baseline decay alpha=0.99. Greedy methods run on a single Nvidia Tesla K80 GPU, while Concorde and LKH run on an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3 3.50 GHz CPU and OR-Tools on an Intel Haswell CPU. The paper does not test the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, attention-free module, 12-layer encoder, or large-scale 1,000+ node generalization, so it serves as a small-scale RL constructive TSP baseline rather than direct validation of ICAM.
[end related work 5]

