[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Nazari et al., 2018-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0366b6396610708a77540564050a90a761a28937)
- venue: Neural Information Processing Systems
- citation count: 1311
[method]
The paper evaluates an encoder-decoder neural constructive solver for capacitated vehicle routing. The encoder embeds customer and depot coordinates and demands, and the decoder uses an attention-based compatibility score to select the next customer or depot at each construction step. Training uses policy gradient methods with an actor-critic formulation, specifically asynchronous advantage actor-critic, while inference uses either greedy decoding or beam search with beam widths of 5 and 10. Feasibility is enforced through masking: nodes with zero demand cannot be selected, all customer nodes are masked when the vehicle load is exactly zero, and customers whose demand exceeds the remaining load are masked, so split deliveries are not allowed in the main experiments. The method is tested on randomly generated capacitated VRP instances with customer and depot locations in the unit square, integer demands from 1 to 9, and varied vehicle capacities. Evaluation uses optimality gaps for small instances, pairwise winning rates for larger instances, and solution-time comparisons against Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized variants of these heuristics, and OR-Tools.
[results]
For VRP10 and VRP20, beam search with width 10 was the best-performing decoder. Roughly 95% of VRP10 instances were at most 10% away from optimal, and VRP20 instances were at most 13% away from optimal, with outliers within 20-25% of optimality. For VRP50 and VRP100, the neural methods outperformed the classical heuristics in almost all instances. In the reported pairwise winning-rate results, RL-BS(10) beat RL-Greedy in 91.9% of instances and RL-BS(5) in 57.7%; RL-BS(5) beat RL-Greedy in 85.8%; and the OR-Tools comparisons were 41.5% for RL-Greedy, 54.6% for RL-BS(5), and 60.2% for RL-BS(10). Runtime analysis showed that the log of solution time divided by the number of nodes remained nearly constant for the RL decoders, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep grew faster than linearly. The neural framework was faster than the randomized heuristics on larger instances, although exact timing comparison with OR-Tools was limited because OR-Tools was implemented in C++ while the other baselines were implemented in Python.
[context]
The experiments used 1000 instances per problem size for VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100. Node and depot locations were sampled uniformly in the unit square, demands were discrete uniform integers from 1 to 9, and split deliveries were disallowed. Decoding used greedy selection or beam search with beam widths 5 and 10. Training used standard policy gradient/A3C actor-critic methods, with implementation details deferred to the appendix. Baselines included Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized variants, and OR-Tools; optimal solutions for small instances were obtained using a mixed-integer formulation. Hardware and full hyperparameter settings were not specified in the provided sections. This method is directly related to the proposed ICAM research plan because it is also an encoder-decoder constructive routing model with decoder compatibility scoring and beam search, but it relies on conventional attention-based scoring and policy-gradient training rather than the proposed attention-free AAFM, instance-conditioned adaptation function, deeper 12-layer encoder, and three-stage cross-scale training aimed at generalizing from 100 to 5000 node instances.
[end related work 6]

