[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Liao et al., 2019-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/05cfda7882696febbf8f90374e722dc9c4632121)
- venue: Journal of Mechanical Design
- citation count: 108
[method]
The paper implements a deep Q-network (DQN) router for VLSI global routing. The simulated environment parses chip grid size, edge capacities, reduced capacities from pre-routed nets or obstacles, and net pin lists; it decomposes multi-pin nets into two-pin subproblems, feeds states to the DQN, allows six directional moves subject to boundary and capacity constraints, updates edge capacities after each move, and returns reward feedback. The DQN agent scores possible next states with a Q-network, selects an action using epsilon-greedy, stores transitions in a replay buffer, and updates weights by backpropagation. Experiments generate 4x4x2 and 8x8x2 routing problems with random pin locations, net counts, and capacities, tune batch size, learning rate, epsilon, discount factor, and network structure, and compare DQN solutions against A* using wire length and overflow. This is related to the proposed ICAM plan as an RL-based constructive routing model and as a scale/hyperparameter study, but it is not an encoder-decoder, attention-free, or instance-conditioned adaptation architecture.
[results]
On 4x4x2 toy problems with 3 nets, maximum 2 pins per net, and 2000 episodes, batch size 128 produced reward oscillations and did not increase reward faster than smaller batches; learning rate 1e-4 was the best trade-off, while 1e-5 was too slow and unstable and 1e-3 caused strong oscillations. A decayed epsilon schedule from 0.05 to 0.01 after 2000 episodes caused a minor performance decrease relative to no decay and was not adopted. On 40 generated problems, gamma=0.9 was best: DQN produced lower wire length than A* on 97.5% of problems, and all 40 problems showed non-negative wire-length decrease relative to A*. DQN overflow was zero in all cases, while A* had nonzero overflow on a small proportion of problems. Gamma values 1, 0.95, and 0.8 produced better wire length than A* on 80%, 90%, and 95% of problems, respectively. Reward oscillations increased as gamma increased, and gamma=0.8 showed lower reward plateaus than gamma=1.
[context]
The main experiments used 8x8x2 two-layer grid problems with 40 randomly generated problems per case; three of the most congested edges identified from A* solutions had reduced capacity. General parameter tuning used 4x4x2 problems with 3 nets and 2000 episodes. Tested hyperparameters included batch sizes 32, 64, and 128; learning rates 1e-3, 1e-4, and 1e-5; epsilon values 0.02, 0.05, and 0.10; and gamma values 1, 0.95, 0.9, and 0.8. The reward structure penalized random-walk steps and rewarded reaching the target pin, with wire length and overflow as solution-quality metrics. Hardware, software, and TSP/CVRP/ATSP instance distributions are not specified, so the findings are specific to VLSI grid routing and should not be directly generalized to the proposed ICAM vehicle-routing architecture.
[end related work 8]

