[method]
The paper trains a policy-gradient reinforcement learning model to improve an initial TSP tour by selecting 2-opt moves. The model uses an encoder-decoder architecture: a graph convolutional encoder with dual encoding produces node and tour embeddings, a policy decoder selects the next 2-opt move, and a value decoder estimates state value. Training uses random Euclidean TSP instances with 20, 50, and 100 nodes in the unit square, random feasible initial tours, curriculum episode lengths, reward clipping, and a critic/entropy loss. It is evaluated on 512 validation/test instances and 10,000 instances per size, with optimality gaps against Concorde, tour costs, running times, and comparisons to classical 2-opt first-improvement/best-improvement heuristics with restarts, OR-Tools, construction heuristics, and neural construction/improvement methods. This is related to the proposed ICAM plan because both use encoder-decoder neural models for TSP and examine generalization to larger instances, but the paper's method is an improvement heuristic rather than a constructive solver and does not use the proposed adaptation function or attention-free module.
[results]
With 500 improvement steps, the learned policy achieved optimality gaps of 0.01% on TSP20, 0.36% on TSP50, and 1.84% on TSP100, with times of 5, 7, and 10 minutes for 10,000 instances. With 1,000 steps, gaps were 0.00%, 0.21%, and 1.26%, with times of 10, 13, and 21 minutes. With 2,000 steps, gaps were 0.00%, 0.12%, and 0.87%, with times of 15, 29, and 41 minutes. At 500 steps it outperformed the compared GAT-T improvement method that used 1,000 steps; at 1,000 steps it matched GAT-T on TSP50 with about one-fifth the samples and achieved a lower TSP100 gap than the compared methods. A policy trained on TSP50 and tested on TSP100 reached 1.86% gap with 1,000 samples and 1.37% with 2,000 samples. In the 2-opt comparison on TSP100, the policy produced lower median tour cost and less dispersion than first-improvement and best-improvement 2-opt with restarts, though exact boxplot values are not provided in the text. The narrative also reports best-found gaps within 0.1% for TSP50 and 0.7% for TSP100, while the table reports 0.12% and 0.87% at 2,000 steps.
[context]
Experiments used a single RTX 2080Ti GPU. Batch size was 512 for TSP20/TSP50 and 256 for TSP100, with 10 or 20 minibatches per epoch. Training ran 200 epochs for TSP20 and 300 epochs for TSP50/TSP100; average epoch times were 2m01s, 3m05s, and 7m16s. Hyperparameters included gamma 0.99, L2 penalty 1e-5, learning rate 0.001 with 0.98 decay, value loss weight 0.5, entropy/heuristic loss weights 0.0045 for TSP20/TSP50 and 0.0018 for TSP100 with 0.9 decay, embedding dimension 128, 3 encoder layers, one RNN block, and episode-length schedules. Mixed precision was used for TSP50/TSP100. The method starts from random initial tours and samples multiple improvement trajectories; it does not explicitly condition on instance scale with a learnable adaptation function and does not use the 12-layer attention-free encoder proposed in ICAM. Its largest tested scale is 100 nodes, so it provides small-scale TSP evidence and a relevant baseline for cross-scale generalization, but not direct validation of 1,000+ node routing generalization.
