[method]
The paper implements an attention-based REINFORCE encoder-decoder router for sequencing instTerm pairs in track-assignment detailed routing. Problem files containing instTerm locations and net information are parsed; a track assigner fixes instTerm locations, a pin decomposer converts nets into instTerm pairs, and each pair is represented as a graph node with coordinates and net index. Instances are padded to a common graph size using Pad Empty all-zero nodes. The encoder-decoder uses multi-head attention as a message-passing mechanism over relative spatial information and constructs a permutation of instTerm pairs; a rollout baseline and REINFORCE gradient estimator optimize a weighted sum of wirelength and openings, where openings are instTerm pairs left unconnected, and the exact weights are not specified in the provided text. A genetic algorithm baseline sequences the same pairs using chromosomes as permutations, with fitness set to the negative cost, partially matched crossover, swap mutation, 10 generations, population 10, elites 4, and one mutation. This is related to the proposed ICAM because both are encoder-decoder constructive routing solvers, but the paper relies on standard multi-head attention and REINFORCE, whereas ICAM proposes an attention-free AAFM and an instance-conditioned adaptation function to improve cross-scale generalization and efficiency. The paper's decoder sequence construction is also a direct contrast to ICAM's decoder compatibility calculation, where the adaptation function is integrated.
[results]
On Small500 and Large500, the genetic router achieved lower cost than the attention router in almost all problems, but the attention router's cost was close, with most Small500 differences within 100 cost units. On Large500, the attention router outperformed GA in more cases, though GA remained better overall. The attention router's training and test performance were similar, indicating it could solve unseen problems, but the paper does not report a formal statistical test comparing solution quality between the two routers. Training cost varied because of REINFORCE delayed reward, and the rollout baseline reduced but did not eliminate variation. Runtime was more than two orders of magnitude faster for attention: Small500 GA took more than 10 seconds per problem while attention took less than 0.1 seconds; Large500 GA took close to 100 seconds per problem while attention took slightly more than 0.1 seconds. Training on Small5000 improved attention quality substantially, approaching GA. These results relate to the research plan by showing that an attention-based constructive router can generalize to unseen instances and accelerate routing, but also that multi-head attention-based REINFORCE may leave quality gaps and training instability, motivating ICAM's AAFM and scale-conditioned adaptation.
[context]
Experiments used analog IC detailed routing problem sets on sub-16 nm commercial technologies. Small set contained Comparator and OpAmp placements with 10 to 100 instTerms per problem; Large set contained ADC placements with 100 to 1000 instTerms per problem. Experiments included Small100, Small500, Small5000, Large100, and Large500, with 60 percent training, 20 percent validation, and 20 percent test. Attention training used Python and PyTorch, batch size 5, 100 epochs, rollout baseline updated by a one-sided t-test, and Pad Empty padding; the implementation text also reports B=20 training batches and T=5 batch size. Training took about 6 minutes per epoch for Small500 and 25 minutes per epoch for Large500 on an Intel Core i7-6850 CPU workstation. GA used limited generations to control runtime. This context relates to ICAM's cross-scale evaluation because the paper tests small-to-large instTerm counts analogous to node-scale generalization, but the domain is IC detailed routing rather than vehicle routing, and the paper does not use the proposed adaptation function or attention-free AAFM.
