[method]
The paper studies LEHD, a constructive neural combinatorial optimization model with a light encoder and heavy decoder for TSP and CVRP. It learns to construct partial solutions during training using supervised learning, then generates complete solutions by greedy decoding at inference, and optionally applies Random Re-Construct (RRC), an inference-time improvement procedure that repeatedly reconstructs parts of the solution for 100, 300, 500, or 1000 iterations. The method is evaluated on uniformly distributed TSP and CVRP instances of sizes 100 to 1000, real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances, and 128 large TSP instances of sizes 200, 500, and 1000. Baselines include POMO, BQ, EAS, SGBS, LKH3, Concorde, SO-mixed, and H-TSP. This is related to the proposed ICAM method because both are encoder-decoder constructive routing solvers aimed at large-scale TSP/CVRP generalization, but LEHD uses a light encoder/heavy decoder and RRC rather than ICAM's proposed 12-layer attention-free encoder, AAFM, and instance-conditioned adaptation function.
[results]
On uniform TSP, LEHD greedy inference is fast, and with 100 RRC iterations it outperforms other learning-based methods; with more than 500 RRC iterations it reaches near-optimal gaps of nearly 0% for TSP100 and TSP200, less than 0.2% for TSP500, and around 0.8% for TSP1000. On uniform CVRP, with 300 RRC iterations LEHD outperforms most learning-based methods on all instances except EAS on CVRP100, which requires much longer inference time; LEHD outperforms LKH3 on CVRP100-CVRP500 with 500 RRC iterations and is about 1% behind LKH3 on CVRP1000 with 1000 RRC iterations. On real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances, LEHD with greedy inference and 1000 RRC iterations outperforms POMO and BQ on all reported instances. In the large TSP comparison on 128 instances, LEHD RRC100 achieves 0.0761% gap in 1.2 minutes on TSP200, 0.343% in 8 minutes on TSP500, and 1.218% in 43 minutes on TSP1000, compared with SO-mixed gaps of 0.636%, 2.401%, and 2.800% with times of 21, 32, and 56 minutes, and H-TSP with LKH-3 gap of 4.06% in 7.5 minutes on TSP1000; Concorde is 0.000% but takes 3 minutes, 32 minutes, and 7.8 hours respectively. LEHD RRC100 has a lower gap than H-TSP on TSP1000 but a longer runtime. POMO trained with supervised learning on TSP100 performs well at TSP100 but poorly on large-scale problems, and reinforcement learning with the POMO strategy does not converge in a reasonable time, while supervised training of LEHD on TSP50 under the same budget is more effective. No statistical significance tests are reported in the provided excerpt.
[context]
Experimental conditions include greedy inference and RRC budgets of 100, 300, 500, or 1000 iterations; large TSP comparisons use 128 instances per size and report optimality gap relative to Concorde or LKH3 plus wall-clock inference time. The excerpt does not specify hardware, training epochs, batch size, learning rate, or full implementation details, so reproducibility is limited to the reported inference settings and problem sizes. The paper's central context is that heavy decoder construction, rather than supervised learning alone, contributes to large-scale generalization, and that RRC improves solution quality at the cost of additional inference time. This context is directly relevant to the ICAM research plan, which targets cross-scale generalization on TSP/CVRP/ATSP, compares against POMO and classical solvers, and uses optimality gaps and inference times as metrics, but ICAM additionally proposes a deeper attention-free encoder and instance-conditioned adaptation that are not present in LEHD.
