[method]
The paper studies a hybrid genetic search with adaptive diversity control for time-window vehicle routing variants, including PVRPTW, MDVRPTW, and SDVRPTW. The algorithm maintains feasible and infeasible subpopulations, applies parent selection, crossover, local-search education, repair, survivor selection, diversification, and penalty adaptation. For large instances it periodically performs structural and geometrical decomposition, solves subproblems recursively, and reinserts reconstituted solutions. It also introduces constant-time move evaluation for duration and time-window infeasibility and calibrates temporal pruning parameters. This is related to the proposed progressive training scheme only indirectly: both target large-scale routing performance, and the paper provides a classical HGS baseline plus component-ablation evidence for scale-related mechanisms, whereas the proposed method uses staged neural training to learn scale-independent features.
[results]
The full method outperformed the compared state-of-the-art methods for PVRPTW, MDVRPTW, and SDVRPTW, including a parallel iterative method using 64 processors, and was comparable to a strong hybrid genetic algorithm for VRPTW while using fewer problem-specific components. It retrieved or improved 105 of 109 best-known solutions for the PVRPTW, MDVRPTW, and SDVRPTW groups and strictly improved 75 of 109. For VRPTW it retrieved or improved 292 of 356 best-known solutions and strictly improved 158 of 356, including five new best-known solutions with one fewer vehicle on large-scale instances. Average standard deviation was 0.17 percent for MDVRPTW and 0.27 percent for PVRPTW on the corresponding benchmark group. In component sensitivity tests using five runs, removing the diversity-and-cost objective or removing time-window-infeasible solutions degraded performance, while decomposition had a significant effect on large PVRPTW instances with subproblems up to 560 customers but a smaller effect on MDVRPTW and SDVRPTW where per-resource subproblems contained 90 to 240 visits. Parameter calibration on 10 two-hundred-customer instances with 20 runs per setting found the best average gap near gamma_TW=1.0 and gamma_WT=0.2; a 50-run comparison gave an average gap of 0.53 percent for that setting versus 0.68 percent for the distance-only setting, with a Wilcoxon test p-value of 0.000.
[context]
Experiments were implemented in C++ and compiled with g++ -O3 on an Intel Xeon 2.93 GHz processor. Benchmark instances involved 48 to 1008 customers, up to 9 depots, 10 periods, and 6 vehicle types; new larger instances involved 360 to 960 customers and up to 4608 total services. Main runs used It_NI=5000, It_dec=2000, nbElit=8, n_close=3, P_rep=0.5, |Gamma|=40, mu=25, lambda=40, and xi_REF=0.2. Calibration used 36 combinations of gamma_TW and gamma_WT from {0, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5}, reduced |Gamma|=20, It_NI=2000, and decomposition turned off. Fleet minimization was handled by starting with a fleet size of 100 and iteratively reducing it when feasible solutions were found. Evaluation metrics included average and best objective values, gap to best-known solutions, runtime, and standard deviation.
