[method]
The paper uses a constructive reinforcement-learning method, S2V-DQN, for graph optimization problems including TSP. It represents the greedy policy with a structure2vec graph embedding network that updates node embeddings by aggregating neighbor information over T iterations, typically T=4, and computes Q(h(S), v; Θ) from the node embedding and a pooled graph embedding. Training uses fitted Q-iteration combined with n-step Q-learning: complete episodes construct solutions by adding nodes until a termination criterion, n-step returns are stored in an experience-replay buffer, and stochastic gradient descent is performed on random batches of state-action-return-next-state tuples. This method is related to the proposed ICAM progressive-training method because both learn constructive policies for routing/TSP-like problems and target generalization to larger instances, but the paper does not use the proposed three-stage warm-up, varying-scale, or top-k elite POMO training; it instead learns a Q-function with graph embeddings and evaluates transfer to larger graphs.
[results]
The excerpt reports that S2V-DQN significantly outperforms all competing methods on real-world benchmark instances for MVC, MAXCUT, and TSP, and that the learned heuristics preserve effectiveness when applied to graphs much larger than the training instances. It also reports that the learned policies can be interpreted as new greedy rules, such as balancing node degree with remaining-graph connectivity for MVC and avoiding cancellation of existing cut edges for MAXCUT. No numerical optimality gaps, statistical significance values, or runtime measurements are provided in the supplied text. In relation to the proposed method, these outcomes support the broader research-plan goal of improving cross-scale generalization of constructive neural solvers, but they do not demonstrate the specific benefit of the proposed progressive multi-scale or top-k elite training scheme.
[context]
Experimental conditions include synthetic MVC and MAXCUT instances generated as Erdős-Rényi or Barabási-Albert graphs with node counts sampled from a range such as 50-100, and 2-D TSP instances generated as uniformly random or clustered points using a DIMACS TSP Challenge generator. The method is evaluated on larger instances and on publicly available real-world benchmark datasets for MVC, MAXCUT, TSP, and set covering. Key implementation details include a p-dimensional node embedding, T=4 embedding iterations, seven parameter groups in the Q-function, n-step returns, and experience-replay batch updates. Hardware, exact training budgets, and hyperparameter values are not specified in the excerpt. For the proposed ICAM plan, this context is relevant because it shows a related RL constructive baseline trained on smaller synthetic instances and tested at larger scale, but it lacks the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, attention-free module, and staged training on 100 to 500 nodes with top-k elite loss.
