[method]
The paper uses a policy-gradient actor-critic training method for a neural constructive solver on capacitated VRP. A stochastic policy parameterized by theta is trained to choose the next customer or depot; an actor outputs next-node probabilities and a critic estimates return, with asynchronous advantage actor-critic reported in the appendix. Training and evaluation use randomly generated CVRP instances in the unit square with discrete uniform demands 1-9 and no split deliveries. Feasibility is enforced by masking: zero-demand nodes are masked, all customers are masked when remaining load is zero, and customers whose demand exceeds remaining load are masked. Decoding is evaluated with greedy selection and beam search of widths 5 and 10. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because both are training schemes for RL-based constructive routing solvers, but the paper uses a standard single-stage policy-gradient setup rather than the proposed warm-up, varying-scale, and POMO/top-k elite training; it therefore serves as a small/medium-scale RL baseline and motivates the need for cross-scale training in the research plan.
[results]
On 1000 instances per problem size, RL with beam search width 10 was the best reported decoder. For VRP10 and VRP20, where optimal solutions were obtained by mixed-integer formulation, about 95% of VRP10 instances and about 13% of VRP20 instances were within 10% of optimality, and outliers were within 20-25% of optimality. For VRP50 and VRP100, where optimal values were not computed, winning-rate comparisons showed classical heuristics were outperformed by the other methods in almost all samples. RL-BS(10) beat RL-Greedy on 91.9% of instances, RL-BS(5) on 57.7%, CW-Greedy on 99.8%, CW-Rnd(5,5) on 99.4%, CW-Rnd(10,10) on 99.2%, sweep variants on 99.3%, and OR-Tools on 60.2%. RL-Greedy was weaker against OR-Tools at 41.5%, and RL-BS(5) at 54.6%. Runtime analysis showed the log of solution time per node was nearly constant across RL decoders, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep runtimes grew faster than linearly; the RL framework was faster than randomized heuristics on larger problems, though exact OR-Tools timing comparisons were limited by C++ versus Python implementations.
[context]
Experimental conditions included 1000 sampled instances for each problem size, with VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100 and different vehicle capacities, although exact capacity values were not provided in the excerpt. Node and depot locations were uniformly generated in [0,1]x[0,1], demands were discrete uniform integers from 1 to 9, and split deliveries were disallowed in the main experiments. Beam search widths were 5 and 10; randomized Clarke-Wright used randomization depth/iterations (5,5) and (10,10), and randomized Sweep used multiple initial angles. Optimal solutions for VRP10 and VRP20 were obtained by solving a mixed-integer formulation. Hardware, training epochs, learning rates, and other hyperparameters were not specified in the provided excerpt; OR-Tools was implemented in C++ while other baselines were in Python, limiting direct runtime comparison.
