[method]
The paper uses a controlled comparison of supervised learning and reinforcement learning within a neural TSP pipeline to study how training paradigm and inference search affect generalization. Identical model configurations are trained on small TSP instances, specifically TSP20-50, and then evaluated on smaller and larger instance sizes, including TSP200, which is described as intractable to learn from directly. Supervised learning is implemented with teacher forcing against an optimal solver, while reinforcement learning uses a rollout baseline that encourages better greedy behavior. Inference is evaluated under three decoding regimes: greedy decoding, beam search, and sampling. The paper also compares against a non-learned furthest insertion heuristic baseline, which constructively builds a partial tour by inserting the node whose distance to the nearest already-inserted tour node is maximized. This method is related to the proposed progressive training approach and the ICAM research plan because both focus on learning from small or medium-scale instances to improve performance on larger instances, and both emphasize the interaction between training objectives, model confidence, and solution search during large-scale evaluation.
[results]
The provided excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than numerical values. With greedy decoding, reinforcement-learning-trained models perform better than supervised-learning-trained models on the training size and on larger graphs. With beam search or sampling, supervised-learning-trained models perform better than reinforcement-learning-trained models. The reinforcement-learning rollout baseline produces very confident next-node predictions, even outside the training size range, whereas supervised learning with teacher forcing produces less confident predictions and weaker greedy decoding, but a probability distribution that is more amenable to beam search and sampling. No quantitative optimality gaps, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance values are provided in the supplied text.
[context]
The experiments are ablations of a base model configuration and are designed to compare state-of-the-art ideas fairly in terms of model capacity and training data. The training data are small TSP instances, TSP20-50, and the evaluation includes larger instances such as TSP200. The key experimental condition is that the same model architecture is used for both supervised and reinforcement learning, so the observed differences are attributed to the learning paradigm and decoding method rather than model capacity. The furthest insertion heuristic serves as a simple non-learned baseline for assessing whether the neural models generalize reasonably. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM method because the proposed three-stage training scheme, including warm-up on 100-node instances, varying-scale training from 100 to 500 nodes, and top-k elite training with a POMO-based loss, also aims to improve cross-scale generalization and solution quality before large-scale evaluation. The paper’s findings suggest that such a training scheme should be evaluated not only with greedy decoding but also with beam search or sampling, since the relative strength of reinforcement-learning-style and supervised-style training can depend on the inference search strategy.
