[method]
The paper studies cross-scale training of an attention-based TSP solver using different task-sampling schedules. It trains the model on TSP instances from size 4 to 100 and compares single-task training, uniform stochastic sampling over sizes, classic monotonically increasing curriculum, and an adaptive staircase curriculum. In the adaptive staircase, task difficulty advances only when the validation optimality gap reaches a 0.05 threshold; if the threshold is not met after a full epoch, the task difficulty is decremented. The number of base trials per epoch is set to (10 + current_graph_size - min_graph_size)/(max_graph_size - min_graph_size), and training is stopped one epoch earlier than other strategies, causing about a 1.6% reduction in exposed data. A separate generalization experiment trained 147 single-task models from TSP 4 to 150, each initialized from a classic-curriculum pretrained model and trained for 20 epochs, then tested them on sizes 10 to 300. This is related to the proposed progressive ICAM training scheme because both use small-scale warm-up and multi-scale sampling to improve generalization, although the paper does not use top-k elite training or POMO-style losses.
[results]
The paper reports that performance is strongly correlated with training size and forms a skewed Gaussian centered at the trained task size, with transfer widening but weakening as problem size increases. Models that move to larger tasks without revisiting smaller tasks initially improve but later decay on farther tasks, indicating catastrophic forgetting. Uniform sampling is described as closest to a constant-factor approximator but has a larger optimality gap across sizes than most other methods. Classic curriculum gives more consistent performance than uniform but still forgets smaller-task knowledge. Adaptive staircase is reported as the best trade-off between forgetting and average performance across problem sizes. The excerpt does not provide exact optimality-gap values or statistical significance tests; the main quantitative detail is the approximately 1.6% data reduction for adaptive staircase.
[context]
Experiments used TSP instances, REINFORCE with a greedy rollout baseline, learning rate 1e-2, 100 epochs, 2,500 batches of 512 samples per epoch, and parameter initialization Uniform(-1/sqrt(d), 1/sqrt(d)). Training took about 50 hours on 4 P100 GPUs depending on dominant problem size. Evaluation used optimality gap percentage between average model cost and HK lower bounds computed over 10,000 TSPs per size, with exhaustive search over 100,000 TSPs for sizes 4-9; lower bounds for larger test sizes were extrapolated. The adaptive staircase used a 0.05 optimality-gap threshold and the base-trial formula above. These conditions are TSP-specific and do not include vehicle routing, POMO losses, top-k trajectory selection, or the 100-500 node uniform sampling range used in the proposed method.
[end related work 8]

