[method]
The paper applies efficient active search (EAS) to the POMO constructive solver for TSP and CVRP, and to the L2D method for JSSP. EAS is a test-time parameter-update strategy in which only selected model components are modified during a 200-iteration search: EAS-Emb updates embeddings, EAS-Lay updates added-layer parameters, and EAS-Tab updates tabular/intensification-related parameters, while the original active search updates a broader set of parameters. The implementation extends existing POMO and L2D code, uses the Adam optimizer, and tunes the hyperparameters lambda, sigma, alpha, and the learning rate by Bayesian optimization on validation instances drawn from the same distribution as the test instances. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because it uses POMO for TSP/CVRP and addresses solution-quality improvement and generalization, but it is not the same as the proposed progressive 100-to-500-node training scheme or the proposed top-k elite loss; it is a test-time active-search adaptation rather than a multi-stage training method.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not report numerical optimality gaps, absolute objective values, or statistical significance tests. Qualitatively, pure sampling did not improve solution quality over the 200 search iterations for TSP, CVRP, or JSSP. For TSP, EAS-Emb and EAS-Lay had nearly identical performance, and EAS-Tab slightly outperformed both; the original active search was significantly more unstable, which the authors attribute to greater sensitivity to the learning rate. For CVRP, all EAS variants found better average solutions than the original active search after only a few iterations, suggesting that fixing most parameters simplifies the learning problem and speeds convergence. For JSSP, EAS-Emb was significantly better than the other methods because the L2D model uses only two node features and a complex node-embedding generation procedure, making direct embedding modification more effective than fine-tuning the entire embedding process.
[context]
Each experiment used a single Nvidia Tesla V100 GPU and a single Intel Xeon 4114 CPU core at 2.2 GHz. Hyperparameters were tuned on separate validation instances sampled from the same distribution as the test instances, and the tuned hyperparameters were not adjusted for larger instances used to evaluate generalization performance. The search trajectory analysis monitored solution quality over 200 iterations, averaging over all JSSP test instances and over the first 1,000 TSP and CVRP test instances. The excerpt does not specify the exact node scales, instance distributions, or numerical baseline comparisons, so its direct relevance to the proposed 100-to-500-node progressive training and top-k elite training is limited to the shared POMO/TSP/CVRP setting and the broader motivation of improving large-scale generalization.
