The paper’s relevant method is a task-distribution generalization study combined with Reptile-based meta-learning for neural combinatorial optimization solvers. It defines instance distributions, or tasks, parameterized by graph size N, number of modes M, vehicle capacity C, and coordinate scale L. For TSP and CVRP, it first trains the reinforcement-learning Attention Model (AM) and the supervised Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) on a single task and then tests them on other tasks in the same class to measure generalization deterioration. It then trains meta-learning variants, referred to as meta-AM and multi-AM, over prior task distributions: a variable-size distribution with N in {10, 20, 30, 50}, a variable-mode distribution with M in {1, 2, 5}, a mixed variable-size/mode distribution with (N, M) in {20, 30, 50} × {1, 2, 4}, and a CVRP variable-capacity distribution with C in {10, 30, 40}. The meta-trained models are evaluated on unseen interpolation and extrapolation tasks, and on real-world TSPlib and CVRPlib instances by fine-tuning. Evaluation uses average optimality gap relative to Concorde for TSP and LKH for CVRP, together with sample efficiency and training-time measurements. This relates to the proposed progressive training method because both approaches aim to improve cross-scale generalization by exposing the model to multiple scales or task distributions. The paper’s variable-size task distribution and meta-training/fine-tuning are conceptually similar to the proposed Stage 2 varying-scale training and the broader goal of learning scale-independent features, but the paper does not use a staged warm-up, uniform scale sampling over [100, 500], or a top-k elite POMO-based loss.
