- in_text_citation: [(Choo et al., 2022-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7734c5d2bc3838edcfa40a311d0b9bb72bc8cde9)
- venue: Neural Information Processing Systems
- citation count: 126
[method]
The paper applies Simulation-guided Beam Search (SGBS) and SGBS+EAS to Transformer-like policy networks pre-trained with POMO-style reinforcement learning for TSP, CVRP, and FFSP. SGBS performs breadth-first search over partial solutions using expansion, simulation, and pruning, while EAS adds randomly initialized insertion layers to the pretrained policy and iteratively runs SGBS, samples additional solutions, and performs policy-gradient ascent on the insertion layers until convergence or timeout. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because both build on POMO-trained routing policies and aim to improve solution quality and cross-scale performance, but the paper’s method is primarily an inference-time search plus lightweight policy-gradient refinement rather than a staged curriculum from 100 to 500 nodes or a top-k elite trajectory loss.
[results]
On TSP, SGBS+EAS reduced optimality gaps by 45%, 33%, and 35% relative to EAS on three instance sets. On CVRP, SGBS+EAS reduced gaps by 52%, 56%, and 59% relative to EAS and outperformed LKH3 by a large margin; on n=100 CVRP it achieved a 0.11% gap to HGS, indicating competitive solution quality but not competitive runtime. On FFSP, SGBS+EAS significantly outperformed traditional CPLEX and metaheuristic baselines, although the provided excerpt does not report exact gap values.
[context]
Experiments were run on A100 GPUs with 80 GB memory, with reported runtimes adjusted to a single-GPU premise. TSP and CVRP used n=100 with 10,000 instances, plus generalization tests at n=150 and n=200 with 1,000 instances each. Baselines included Concorde, HGS, LKH3, DACT, NLNS, and DPDP. POMO x8 instance augmentation was used. SGBS hyperparameters were set to beta=gamma=10 for TSP and beta=gamma=4 for CVRP. FFSP used 1,000 instances each for n=20, 50, and 100 jobs with three stages and four machines per stage. The paper does not report training on scales up to 500 or top-k elite losses, so its evidence for large-scale generalization is limited to modest scale shifts from 100 to 150 and 200 nodes.
