[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Drakulic et al., 2023-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/72da01c00ef44b039cefe7e9701da2b3d3412f71)
- venue: Neural Information Processing Systems
- citation count: 132
[method]
The paper uses a single-stage constructive neural routing model trained by imitation of expert trajectories with a cross-entropy loss. The model is trained on synthetic 100-node instances for Euclidean TSP, CVRP, OP, and ATSP, using expert solutions generated by Concorde for TSP, LKH for ATSP and CVRP, and EA4OP for OP. For TSP, training trajectories are formed by sampling sub-paths of length n between 4 and N from optimal tours, so that any sub-path is treated as an optimal path-TSP sub-problem; a similar sub-path or sub-sequence sampling strategy is used for CVRP, OP, and KP. The model has 9 layers, 12 attention heads, embedding size 192, and feed-forward dimension 512. Training uses batches of 1024 for 500 epochs with Adam, initial learning rate 7.5e-4, and decay 0.98 every 50 epochs. At test time, the model is evaluated with greedy decoding and beam search, and non-Perceiver variants limit the subgraph to the 250 nearest neighbors of the origin node. Evaluation is performed on synthetic instances of sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, with CVRP capacities of 50, 80, 100, and 250, as well as TSPLib instances up to 4461 nodes and Euclidean CVRPLib instances. The reported metrics are optimality gap and inference time, with TSP gaps computed against Concorde and CVRP gaps computed against LKH reference solutions. This method is related to the proposed progressive training approach because it is a baseline constructive NCO training and evaluation protocol for cross-scale generalization: the paper trains only at 100 nodes and tests transfer to larger scales, whereas the proposed ICAM method adds staged warm-up, varying-scale training from 100 to 500 nodes, and top-k elite trajectory training with a joint POMO/top-k loss to explicitly learn scale-independent features and refine solution quality.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical optimality gaps, inference times, or statistical significance values. It reports qualitative outcomes: the model shows superior generalization to larger graphs even with greedy decoding, which produces a single solution while many baselines generate several hundred solutions and select the best. With greedy decoding, the model is slightly slower than Sym-NCO, competitive with POMO, and significantly faster than other compared models. Beam search improves optimality gaps but increases inference time. Replacing quadratic attention with a PerceiverIO architecture substantially reduces inference time for larger graphs at the cost of some performance drop, while still achieving strong performance relative to other neural combinatorial optimization baselines. On TSPLib and CVRPLib, the model significantly outperforms end-to-end baseline policies even with greedy decoding. The only baselines described as competitive are Att-GCN+MCTS and DIFUSCO+2opt, but both are hybrid methods with expensive search, are 2 to 15 times slower, and are designed for TSP only. These results are relevant to the proposed method because they show that small-scale expert-imitation training can generalize to larger instances, but they do not test the proposed progressive varying-scale or top-k elite training scheme, leaving open whether staged scale variation and elite trajectory refinement can further improve large-scale solution quality.
[context]
The experimental context includes training on 100-node synthetic instances because this is described as the largest size for which near-optimal solutions are reasonably fast to obtain and is commonly used in the literature. The expert dataset contains 1 million solutions. For asymmetric TSP, Euclidean distance matrices are used with random symmetry breaking. The same hyperparameters are used across problems: 9 layers, 12 attention heads, embedding size 192, feed-forward dimension 512, batch size 1024, 500 epochs, Adam with initial learning rate 7.5e-4 and decay 0.98 every 50 epochs. Deep learning models are run on a single Nvidia Tesla V100-S GPU with 24GB memory, while non-neural solvers are run on an Intel Xeon E5-2670 CPU with 256GB memory in one thread. At test time, non-Perceiver models restrict attention to the 250 nearest neighbors of the origin node to reduce inference time without significant impact on solution quality. This context is directly relevant to the proposed research plan because the paper’s 100-node training scale matches the proposed Stage 1 warm-up scale, its evaluation on 200, 500, and 1000-node instances aligns with the plan’s focus on large-scale generalization, and its use of expert trajectories and cross-entropy imitation contrasts with the proposed Stage 3 top-k elite training, which instead focuses on the best k trajectories and combines a POMO loss with a new top-k loss.
