[start related work 14]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Son et al., 2023-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8b666e9d4399c3942cf6386125e8c545d2331f16)
- venue: International Conference on Machine Learning
- citation count: 43
[method]
The paper studies a scale-conditioned meta-adaptation method for constructive DRL routing solvers, applied to POMO and Sym-NCO. The base models are encoder-decoder policies pretrained on 100-node instances. A shared SML module g_phi(h,N) converts the original context embedding h into a scale-conditioned initial embedding h_S using the instance size N. SML is trained with two objectives: a distillation objective that aligns h_S with the expected instance-wise adapted embedding after many adaptation steps, and a zero-shot simulation objective that treats h_S as the zero-adaptation state and rewards it for producing good solutions before any test-time update. At inference, the model performs instance-wise adaptation for a fixed number of iterations and is compared with active search, efficient active search, and simulation-guided beam search. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because both aim to improve cross-scale generalization of POMO-style constructive DRL models from 100-node training to larger routing instances; the paper's contribution is a scale-conditioned meta-learning initialization and test-time adaptation, not a three-stage progressive pretraining schedule or a top-k elite loss. It is also conceptually adjacent to the ICAM plan's instance-conditioned adaptation function because both use problem scale to condition the model, but the paper's module operates on node embeddings and is trained by distillation and zero-shot simulation rather than by a learnable pairwise-distance bias.
[results]
Across TSP, CVRP, PCTSP, and OP, Meta-SAGE generally improved the pretrained POMO and Sym-NCO models over active search, efficient active search, and simulation-guided beam search. For TSP, POMO-based Meta-SAGE achieved objective gaps of 0.391% at N=200, 3.559% at N=500, and 12.038% at N=1000, compared with 0.455%, 9.362%, and 32.869% for efficient active search; Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE achieved 0.387%, 3.339%, and 11.488%. For CVRP, POMO-based Meta-SAGE reached 22.001 with a -0.009% gap at N=200 and 63.322 with a 0.035% gap at N=500, while Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE reached 21.982 with a -0.094% gap at N=200 and 63.281 with a -0.028% gap at N=500, both better than the LKH3 reference at those scales; at N=1000 the gaps were 0.683% and 0.685%. For PCTSP, Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE outperformed the OR-Tools reference at all scales: 7.299 with an -8.236% gap at N=200, 11.175 with an -8.412% gap at N=500, and 16.219 with a -31.31% gap at N=1000. For OP, Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE achieved 49.05 with a reported 0.337% gap at N=200, 98.762 with a -1.457% gap at N=500, and 154.307 with a 4.562% gap at N=1000; the N=500 result was better than the Compass reference. The only noted exception among adaptation baselines was Sym-NCO on CVRP at N=200, where active search had a lower objective, 21.955 with a -0.218% gap, but required 28.2H versus 2.7H for Meta-SAGE. The provided results report objective gaps and runtimes but do not report confidence intervals or statistical significance tests.
[context]
Experimental conditions: POMO and Sym-NCO were pretrained on N=100 instances and evaluated without retraining on larger scales. Test sets contained 1,000 instances at N=200 and 128 instances at N=500 and N=1000. Hardware was a single NVIDIA A100 40GB GPU and an Intel Scalable Gold CPU. Meta-SAGE and efficient active search or simulation-guided beam search used 200 adaptation iterations; efficient active search and simulation-guided beam search were stopped if their runtime exceeded Meta-SAGE's 200-iteration runtime. Active search was run per instance for 200 iterations because it could not be batched. Reported time is total time to solve all instances. Baseline gaps were computed against Concorde for TSP, LKH3 for CVRP, OR-Tools for PCTSP, and Compass for OP. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because the proposed method also starts from 100-node training and targets large-scale TSP/CVRP evaluation, but it uses progressive multi-scale training and top-k elite POMO loss rather than the paper's scale-conditioned meta-adapter and fixed 200-iteration test-time adaptation.
