[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Gao et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d708a031c4699220dd3802bbd2f1933efd90204f)
- venue: International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence
- citation count: 99
[method]
The paper evaluates ELG-POMO, a constructive neural solver for TSP and CVRP that ensembles a global POMO policy with a transferrable local policy. The global policy is a Transformer encoder-decoder without positional embeddings, trained by reinforcement learning with POMO-style instance augmentation and a shared or greedy baseline. The local policy constructs solutions using local neighborhood information around the current node and is combined with the global policy to select the next node. The model is trained only on uniformly distributed instances with 100 nodes and tested in greedy inference mode on TSPLIB, CVRPLIB Set-X and Set-XXL, and synthetic distributions. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because both are built around POMO-style autoregressive construction and aim to improve cross-scale generalization, but the paper's method does not use staged warm-up, varying-scale training from 100 to 500 nodes, or a top-k elite loss; instead it improves generalization through a local policy while keeping training scale fixed.
[results]
On TSPLIB and CVRPLIB subsets with N <= 200, ELG-POMO outperforms all compared methods, reducing the average gap to best known solutions by 0.53% for CVRP and 0.50% for TSP relative to the runner-up. On the harder N in (200, 1000] subset, it reduces the CVRP gap from 6.95% for Omni-POMO to 6.46%, and reduces the TSP gap from 7.47% for Omni-POMO to 5.90%, although it remains behind BQ and LEHD on TSP at that scale. On real-world CVRPLIB Set-XXL instances with N between 3000 and 7000, it performs best on three of four instances and substantially lowers the gap compared with other POMO-based methods. On uniform in-distribution N=100 instances, it achieves a 0.22% gap on TSP100 and a 1.22% gap on CVRP100, surpassing POMO and Sym-POMO and remaining competitive with Pointerformer and T2TCO on TSP. On synthetic difficult distributions, ELG-POMO retrained on uniform, cluster, and cluster mixture outperforms AMDKD on three of four distributions at N=100 and all four at N=200; at N=200 its gaps are 1.84% for cluster, 1.62% for mixture, 1.43% for explosion, and 1.58% for rotation, compared with 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54% for AMDKD. Runtime is lower than BQ and diffusion-based methods and higher than pure POMO, with a reported 1.90 seconds per instance on TSPLIB/CVRPLIB, but its optimality gap remains worse than highly optimized solvers such as HGS.
[context]
Training uses uniformly distributed TSP/CVRP instances with 100 nodes, matching the common POMO training setting. Evaluation uses greedy decoding, POMO x8 instance augmentation for POMO-based methods, average gap to best known solutions, and average solving time. Test conditions include cross-distribution and cross-scale benchmarks: TSPLIB and CVRPLIB Set-X split into N <= 200 and 200 < N <= 1000 or 1002, CVRPLIB Set-XXL with N between 3000 and 7000, and small-scale synthetic distributions including cluster, cluster mixture, explosion, and rotation. The paper does not report statistical significance, hardware details, or the exact hyperparameters in the provided excerpt, but it states that ELG-POMO's training is aligned with POMO so that the comparison isolates the effect of the local policy. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because the proposed three-stage training would modify the same POMO-based training pipeline, adding warm-up, varying-scale sampling, and top-k elite refinement, whereas the paper's fixed-scale POMO training provides the baseline behavior and shows where scale-independent learning remains challenging.
[end related work 15]

[start related work 16]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Luo et al., 2023-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3c2f14a28ba5e826c63c294f4cca01a8217a217e)
- venue: Neural Information Processing Systems
- citation count: 263
[method]
The paper evaluates a constructive neural combinatorial optimization model called LEHD, with a light encoder and heavy decoder, trained mainly by supervised learning on TSP and CVRP instances. The model learns to construct partial solutions during training and complete tours or routes during inference, and it uses Random Re-Construct, or RRC, iterations to improve greedy solutions. The experimental study also compares POMO, POMO with random sampling, POMO with RRC, reinforcement-learning training, and search-based or improvement-based solvers. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because the proposed method also targets large-scale generalization of constructive routing solvers and builds on POMO-style trajectory selection and loss design. The paper provides evidence that training objective and decoder-heavy architecture affect cross-scale performance, but it does not implement the proposed warm-up, varying-scale, or top-k elite training stages. It also reports that RRC can break the performance upper limit of the labeled solution, which is conceptually aligned with the proposed top-k elite trajectory refinement.
[results]
On uniformly distributed TSP, LEHD has fast greedy inference and, with 100 RRC iterations, outperforms other learning-based methods; with more than 500 RRC iterations, the gap is nearly optimal for TSP100 and TSP200, less than 0.2% for TSP500, and about 0.8% for TSP1000. On CVRP, LEHD with 300 RRC iterations outperforms most learning-based methods on all instances except EAS on CVRP100, and with 500 RRC iterations it outperforms LKH3 on CVRP100-CVRP500, while reaching about a 1% gap to LKH3 on CVRP1000 with 1000 RRC iterations. On real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances, LEHD with greedy inference and 1000 RRC iterations outperforms POMO and BQ. POMO trained by supervised learning reaches good performance on TSP100 but performs poorly on larger instances, and reinforcement-learning training with the POMO strategy does not converge in a reasonable time, while supervised learning is better on TSP50 under the same budget. In the 128-instance large TSP comparison, Concorde achieves 0.000% gaps in 3m, 32m, and 7.8h for TSP200, TSP500, and TSP1000; SO-mixed achieves 0.636% in 21m, 2.401% in 32m, and 2.800% in 56m; LEHD greedy achieves 0.859% in 3s, 1.560% in 0.3m, and 3.168% in 1.6m; LEHD RRC100 achieves 0.0761% in 1.2m, 0.343% in 8m, and 1.218% in 43m. For TSP1000, H-TSP with LKH-3 achieves 4.06% in 7.5m, while LEHD greedy achieves 3.168% in 1.6m and LEHD RRC100 achieves 1.218% in 43m. No statistical significance tests are reported.
[context]
Experiments use uniformly distributed TSP and CVRP instances and real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances at sizes including 100, 200, 500, and 1000 nodes; large TSP comparisons use 128 instances. Evaluation metrics are optimality gap and inference time, with greedy inference and RRC budgets of 100, 300, 500, and 1000 iterations. Training comparisons are made on TSP50 and TSP100 under the same computational budget, and POMO is trained by supervised learning on TSP100. The excerpt does not provide hardware specifications, exact optimizer settings, or the full self-improved training loss, so reproducibility is limited to the reported instance sizes, RRC budgets, and comparison setup. This context is relevant to the ICAM research plan because it establishes a baseline for large-scale constructive NCO generalization and shows that POMO-style methods need architectural or training improvements to scale, which motivates the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation and progressive top-k training.
