[method]
The paper uses guided non-autoregressive knowledge distillation to convert the autoregressive POMO teacher into a non-autoregressive student, GNARKD-POMO, and compares the student against the POMO teacher. POMO is evaluated in greedy mode without augmentation and greedy mode with x8 augmentation, while the student produces a solution in a single non-autoregressive pass. Inputs are synthetic TSP and CVRP instances: node and depot coordinates are sampled uniformly from the unit square, CVRP node demands are drawn uniformly from the discrete set 1 through 9, and capacity is set to 1. Test sets contain 10,000 instances for n=50 and n=100. Experiments run on an Intel i5-11400F CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 3060Ti GPU with 8GB memory, using the largest feasible batch size. Metrics include learning gap relative to the teacher, acceleration ratio for one instance and for 10,000 instances, and maximum solvable node size under the memory limit. This is related to the proposed method because POMO is the teacher/baseline whose POMO loss is reused in Stage 3, and the paper directly probes POMO solution quality, inference cost, and large-scale memory limits, which are central to the ICAM plan's large-scale generalization and POMO comparisons. The paper does not implement the proposed warm-up, varying-scale, or top-k elite training.
[results]
For POMO versus GNARKD-POMO, the student is faster but slightly worse in solution quality. On TSP n=50, greedy no augmentation gives a 0.26% learning gap with 3.62x single-instance and 2.93x total acceleration; greedy x8 augmentation gives a 0.01% gap with 3.31x and 3.47x acceleration. On TSP n=100, no augmentation gives a 0.69% gap with 3.77x and 4.50x acceleration; x8 augmentation gives a 0.11% gap with 3.35x and 5.23x acceleration. On CVRP n=50, no augmentation gives a 3.02% gap with 2.04x and 3.52x acceleration; x8 augmentation gives a 1.73% gap with 2.06x and 3.92x acceleration. On CVRP n=100, no augmentation gives a 7.10% gap with 2.27x and 4.97x acceleration; x8 augmentation gives a 4.51% gap with 3.35x and 4.96x acceleration. In the scale/memory test, POMO with multiple greedy rollouts solves up to 1,000 TSP nodes under 8GB, while GNARKD-POMO solves up to 9,000 nodes; with x8 augmentation, POMO is limited to 500 nodes while GNARKD-POMO reaches 3,100 nodes. No statistical significance tests are reported.
[context]
The synthetic evaluation is limited to small instances, n=50 and n=100, with 10,000 test instances per setting, so it does not directly test the proposed 100-to-500 progressive training or 1,000+ generalization. The 8GB GPU memory constraint is central to the maximum-node-size comparison and to batch-size choices. POMO multi-rollout inference is the main source of its higher computational cost, whereas GNARKD-POMO uses a single non-autoregressive output matrix and reduces memory and time. The real-world TSPLIB check uses 20 TSP instances with 51 to 159 nodes and Concorde as the exact baseline; the paper reports that POMO has strong solution quality but higher resource demand, while GNARKD-POMO has shorter inference time. These conditions are relevant to the ICAM plan because they quantify POMO baseline behavior and the practical memory bottleneck that large-scale ICAM evaluation must address, but they do not provide evidence for the proposed top-k elite loss or scale-varying training stages.
[end related work 17]

