[method]
The paper does not implement the proposed progressive multi-scale training or top-k elite loss. Its related method is DAR, an inference-time attention-reshaping technique for neural CVRPLib solvers that addresses the same large-scale generalization problem targeted by the ICAM research plan. DAR computes standard dot-product attention scores from the decoder query and encoder node features, computes Euclidean distances from the current node to all unvisited candidate nodes, converts distances into distance scores using a logarithmic function under the assumption that coordinates lie in [0,1]x[0,1], assigns negative scores to nodes outside a K-neighborhood, adds the distance score to the attention score, normalizes with a scaling constant C=50, and uses the resulting weights to sample the next node. This is conceptually related to the proposed ICAM adaptation function f(N,d_ij)=-alpha log2N d_ij because both use distance-based bias to counter attention dispersion, but DAR is a fixed inference-time heuristic rather than a learned instance-conditioned training scheme.
[results]
On CVRPLib Set-X with 100 instances and 100 to 1000 nodes, DAR achieved a 5.66% gap to BKS in the main comparison, improving over POMO at 8.31%, ELG at 5.92%, Omni-VRP at 6.47%, and greedy at 21.03%, but below LKH3 at 1.00% and HGS at 0.11%. On 32 Set-X instances with 500 to 1000 nodes, DAR achieved 5.27%, better than AM at 24.65%, TAM-AM at 10.97%, TAM-LKH3 at 9.87%, GLOP at 19.75%, and GLOP-LKH3 at 18.80%. On 35 Set-XML instances with 100 to 5000 nodes, DAR achieved 5.20%, better than POMO at 10.51%, Omni-VRP at 10.60%, and ELG at 5.95%. On 6 Set-XXL instances with 3000 to 11000 nodes, DAR achieved 10.82%, better than POMO at 30.85%, ELG at 16.08%, and greedy at 13.08%, and competitive with decomposition baselines TAM-AM at 30.07%, TAM-LKH3 at 22.50%, GLOP at 19.75%, and GLOP-LKH3 at 18.80%. Inference time for DAR was 0.39 seconds on Set-X, whereas LKH3 and HGS required at least 16 minutes. No statistical significance tests were reported.
[context]
Training used randomly generated node coordinates and demands in the style of the AM and POMO training protocol; testing used CVRPLib Set-X, expanded Set-XML, and Set-XXL instances. DAR assumes node coordinates in [0,1]x[0,1], uses Euclidean distance, a logarithmic distance transformation, a hyperparameter K to limit the neighborhood, and C=50 for normalization. It adds no trainable parameters. Baselines greedy, POMO, and ELG were run with source code and default settings under the same training environment where possible; other baseline numbers were taken from original papers. Hardware context: LKH3 and HGS comparison used an Intel Gold 6148 Skylake 2.4 GHz processor, while DAR was evaluated on GPU-parallel inference. This context is relevant to the proposed method because the ICAM plan also targets CVRPLib, TSP, and ATSP large-scale generalization and compares against POMO and ELG, but the paper's DAR provides a distance-bias baseline rather than evidence for progressive warm-up, varying-scale training, or top-k elite loss.
