[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Liu et al., 2024-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b0ee2cee04851e6bc421df03dc0bf2afcf179ba3)
- venue: International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence
- citation count: 12
[method]
The paper studies a prompt-learning method for cross-distribution vehicle routing. A fixed pretrained attention encoder-decoder, originally trained on uniform CVRP instances of size 100, is used as both feature extractor and solution constructor. For each instance, the feature extractor produces a feature vector that selects one key from a pool of 16 key-prompt pairs; the associated prompt, consisting of 5 prompted tokens per encoder layer across 6 multi-head attention layers with embedding size 128, is inserted into the frozen model. The prompted model constructs a tour, and reinforcement learning updates only the selected prompt using the resulting solution reward. Training exposes the prompts sequentially to 341 distributions with problem sizes 50 to 200, including uniform and clustered Gaussian geometries. The paper also evaluates top-k prompt inference, where the best k matched prompts are used, and compares against heuristic solvers, single-distribution POMO, diverse-distribution POMO, and meta-learning methods.
[results]
On zero-shot generalization to 12 new distributions, the prompt method achieved average gap 3.10 percent without augmentation, compared with 3.97 percent for meta-learning POMO and 4.36 percent for the omni meta-learning method. With data augmentation, the prompt method achieved average gap 1.72 percent, compared with 2.50 percent and 2.76 percent respectively. On training distributions, the paper reports that prompt learning outperforms the meta-learning methods and single-distribution POMO on problem sizes 50, 100, and 200, and that using the top 8 matched prompts ranks first across all three sizes. The paper also reports lower training cost than second-order meta-learning: about 1 day on a 25 GB GPU after adjusting for larger batch size, versus several days and much larger memory for meta-learning baselines.
[context]
The experimental setting uses CVRP instances of sizes 50, 100, and 200, batch size 64, 1,000 instances per epoch, up to 10,000 epochs, learning rate decay from 1e-3 to 1e-5, and about 2.5 days on a single 11 GB GPU for the reported prompt training. Heuristic baselines were run with time limits of 5 seconds and 10 seconds for sizes 50 and 100, and 20 seconds and 60 seconds for size 200. This paper is related to the proposed progressive training method because it tests adaptation of a 100-node pretrained routing model to other scales and distributions using lightweight trainable components and top-k selection. However, it is not a direct validation of the proposed method: its largest scale is 200 nodes, its top-k mechanism selects prompts rather than elite trajectories, and it does not use a staged 100-to-500 scale curriculum or a joint POMO plus top-k trajectory loss.
[end related work 19]

