[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Xiao et al., 2024-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/21327634916cb1e5692857c372ed82cfccac0ec0)
- venue: arXiv.org
- citation count: 1
[method]
The paper studies a plug-and-play size-generalization scaling factor, called ESF, for neural VRP solvers. ESF rescales attention or compatibility scores by a logarithmic size ratio: at test time it uses the ratio between the training size and the test size, and at training time it uses a corresponding logarithmic ratio based on a reference size and the training size. It is applied to existing constructive solvers, including models pretrained on 50 or 100 nodes and a multi-size solver trained on 50-200 nodes. This is related to the proposed progressive varying-scale training and the ICAM plan because both use problem size to condition solution construction and target cross-scale generalization; however, the paper's method is a scalar size adjustment rather than a staged warm-up, uniform-scale curriculum, or top-k elite trajectory loss.
[results]
On 10,000 randomly generated TSP and CVRP instances, test-time ESF improved TSP cross-size optimality gaps by an average of 14.09% for scaling up and 7.32% for scaling down. For CVRP, ESF improved downscaling by 5.97% but worsened upscaling by 3.54%, which the authors attribute to increased depot selection and variable solution sequence length. On large-scale TSP instances of size 1000, ESF improved two strong pretrained models by an average relative improvement of 10.76%. When ESF was applied during training of a multi-size solver, TSP in-size performance improved by 18.38% and cross-size performance by 13.8%; CVRP in-size performance improved by 4.51% and cross-size performance by 4.86%.
[context]
Experiments were run on an Intel Xeon Gold 6254 CPU and NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. Test sets contained 10,000 randomly generated instances per size or distribution. Test-time ESF baselines were trained on 50 or 100 nodes and evaluated at 50, 100, and 200 nodes; large-scale evaluation used test size 1000. Training-time ESF used a solver trained on 50-200 nodes and evaluated in-size at 100, 150, and 200 nodes and cross-size at 300, 500, and 1000 nodes. Some comparisons used greedy rollout with 8 augmentations, and a few-shot setting could not evaluate instances larger than 500 due to GPU memory constraints. The ESF is generic and model-agnostic, but its fixed scaling factor can hurt CVRP upscaling, suggesting that dynamic or problem-specific adjustment may be needed.
[end related work 20]

