[method]
The paper uses a reinforcement-learning constructive solver for the capacitated vehicle routing problem, with an encoder-decoder pointer-style network trained by policy gradient methods. The training setup includes an actor network that predicts probabilities over the next node to visit and a critic network that estimates value, with asynchronous advantage actor-critic training described in the appendix. Routes are decoded either greedily or with beam search of width 5 or 10. Feasibility masking is used to enforce CVRP constraints: zero-demand nodes are not visited, all customer nodes are masked when the vehicle load is exactly zero, and customers whose demand exceeds the remaining load are masked, so split deliveries are not allowed in the main experiments. This method is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because both are neural constructive routing solvers evaluated on CVRP, but the paper's method does not include ICAM's instance-conditioned adaptation function, attention-free adaptation module, multi-stage cross-scale training, or evaluation on 100 to 5,000-node instances and ATSP.
[results]
On 1,000 instances per problem size, beam search with width 10 was the best-performing decoder. For VRP10 and VRP20, where optimal solutions were available, roughly 95% of instances were within 10% of optimality for VRP10 and within 13% of optimality for VRP20, and even outliers were within 20-25% of optimality. In larger VRP50 and VRP100 comparisons, the RL methods outperformed Clarke-Wright and Sweep heuristics in nearly all instances. RL-greedy was comparable to OR-Tools, while RL-BS(10) achieved a winning rate above 60% against OR-Tools; the reported table shows 60.2% for RL-BS(10) and 41.5% for RL-greedy against OR-Tools. Runtime analysis showed that the log of solution time divided by the number of nodes remained nearly constant for the RL decoders, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep runtimes grew faster than linearly, and the RL framework was faster than the randomized heuristics on larger instances. These results are relevant to the proposed plan because they show that constructive RL solvers can be competitive on small CVRP instances, but they do not establish the large-scale cross-scale generalization that ICAM targets.
[context]
The experiments used one-vehicle capacitated VRP instances with customer and depot locations uniformly sampled in the unit square and integer demands uniformly sampled from 1 to 9. Problem sizes were VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100, with 1,000 instances per size and different vehicle capacities. Optimal solutions for VRP10 and VRP20 were obtained by solving a mixed-integer formulation, while VRP50 and VRP100 were evaluated using winning-rate comparisons because optimal values were not computationally affordable. Baselines included Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized versions of both, and Google OR-Tools; OR-Tools was implemented in C++ while the other baselines were implemented in Python, limiting exact runtime comparisons. Hardware specifications, statistical significance tests, confidence intervals, TSP/ATSP results, and training scales beyond the tested instance sizes are not reported. In relation to the proposed research plan, this context is smaller-scale and single-problem, whereas ICAM will evaluate 100 to 5,000-node TSP/CVRP and up to 1,000-node ATSP, use instance-conditioned adaptation and an attention-free module, and compare against stronger classical and neural baselines using optimality gaps and inference times.
