The paper evaluates size-adaptive ReLU recurrent neural networks for predicting the edge clique cover number of graphs. A single-layer RNN uses n recurrent units for graphs up to n vertices, processes the flattened adjacency matrix as a binary sequence, and produces a real-valued output constrained to [0,1] through parameter restrictions. A multi-layer version stacks d recurrent layers. The experimental comparison includes a feed-forward ReLU network, an LSTM, a majority/random baseline, and a benchmark heuristic. This relates to the proposed ICAM research plan as a neural combinatorial graph problem evaluation, but it differs from the proposed method because it predicts a graph property on small graphs rather than constructing TSP, CVRP, or ATSP solutions at 100 to 5,000 nodes, and it uses recurrent layers rather than an attention-free encoder-decoder.
[results]
The RNN and LSTM generally outperform the feed-forward network across dense, medium-dense, sparse, and mixed graph scenarios. With sufficient training samples, the RNN reaches performance comparable to or better than the benchmark heuristic, and reasonable performance is reported with as few as 4,000 training samples. Under Gaussian noise, the RNN and LSTM remain above the heuristic even at relative error levels of 10% or more, and a two-layer RNN slightly outperforms the single-layer RNN. No statistical significance tests are reported. The paper also derives polynomial sample-complexity bounds, scaling roughly as n^6/epsilon^2 for the single-layer case and d^2 n^6/epsilon^2 for the multi-layer case, and reports that for epsilon = delta = 0.1 the required sample fraction of all graphs up to size n is 5.82% at n = 10 and 0.000963% at n = 11.
[context]
The inputs are random Erdos-Renyi graphs with 10 to 18 vertices and edge probabilities of 0.1, 0.5, and 0.9, plus mixed scenarios. The maximum graph size is limited to 18 vertices because exact edge clique cover number labels are computationally intractable for larger graphs. Training sample sizes are varied on a logarithmic scale, roughly from 2^9 to 2^15 or 2^18 in mixed settings. Hardware, optimizer, and full training hyperparameters are not specified in the provided text. The experimental scale is much smaller than the proposed ICAM evaluation, which targets routing instances from 100 to 5,000 nodes and explicitly tests cross-scale generalization, so the paper provides contextual support for neural graph solvers rather than direct validation of ICAM.
