[method]
The paper evaluates HGSADC, a population-based hybrid genetic search with adaptive diversity control for multi-depot, multi-period vehicle routing and CVRP. It uses two subpopulations, a biased fitness that combines penalized-cost rank and diversity-contribution rank, a normalized Hamming distance over service patterns and depot assignments, parent selection and crossover, education or local search, and periodic population management. For CVRP, the method is adapted by replacing the periodic distance with a broken-pairs distance, using OX crossover on cyclic giant tours, and limiting the local-search neighborhood to 20 nodes, while retaining the adaptive diversity control. The experimental setup compares HGSADC against previously reported state-of-the-art methods and best-known solutions, using average gaps and run times over 10 independent runs. This is related to the proposed ICAM plan because ICAM will benchmark against classical HGS-family solvers and report optimality gaps and inference times on CVRP; the paper provides a classical HGS-family reference for CVRP and multi-depot periodic VRP, though it does not test neural constructive or two-stage NCO methods.
[results]
On 42 PVRP and 33 MDVRP benchmark instances, HGSADC achieved an average gap of +0.20% to previous best-known solutions for PVRP and -0.01% for MDVRP, with per-instance standard deviations of 0.15% and 0.05%, respectively. It produced new best average results on 41 of 42 PVRP instances and all 33 MDVRP instances, and new best-known solutions on 20 PVRP and 9 MDVRP instances. For short MDVRP runs, the average time to the final solution was 2.15 minutes, and with 5 times 10^4 iterations and 1 hour, previous best-known solutions were retrieved on all runs for 21 of 33 MDVRP instances. On 10 constructed MDPVRP instances, the average gap was +0.42%, and the best-of-10 gap was +0.13%. On 34 CVRP instances, the overall gap to best-known solutions was 0.11%, matching the best specialized algorithm reported, and 12 new best-known solutions were found. Sensitivity analysis showed education, population management, crossover, infeasible solutions, and repair all contributed, with education most important; adaptive diversity control outperformed no diversity, objective-space dispersal, and solution-space dispersal, maintaining higher population entropy and continuing to improve near the time limit.
[context]
Experiments were implemented in C++ on an AMD Opteron 250 at 2.4 GHz, with reported times converted to equivalent Pentium IV 3.0 GHz run times. Standard stopping conditions were 10^4 iterations and 10 minutes, 2 times 10^4 iterations and 30 minutes, and 5 times 10^4 iterations and 1 hour; MDPVRP used 30 minutes with 10^4 iterations. Parameters were calibrated with CMA-ES per problem class; most parameters were averaged across classes, generation size was 40 for PVRP, 70 for MDVRP, and 100 for MDPVRP, and education rate was fixed to 1. Instance sizes ranged from 50 to 417 customers for PVRP and MDVRP, with some large problems over 450 visits causing population-size reduction; CVRP instances included up to 483 customer visits. The paper's conditions are relevant to the proposed ICAM evaluation as a classical HGS-family baseline for CVRP gap and runtime, but the paper does not cover large-scale 1,000+ node instances, TSP or ATSP, neural baselines, or ICAM's instance-conditioned adaptation.
[end related work 2]

