[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Bello et al., 2016-11)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d7878c2044fb699e0ce0cad83e411824b1499dc8)
- venue: International Conference on Learning Representations
- citation count: 2035
[method]
The paper evaluates a pointer-network-based neural combinatorial optimization method for Euclidean TSP, using supervised learning, reinforcement-learning pretraining, and inference-time search variants including greedy decoding, sampling, and active search. It compares these neural methods against classical solvers and heuristics: Christofides, OR-Tools vehicle routing local search, Concorde, and LK-H, on TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100. The RL pretraining plus sampling or active search pipeline is an early two-stage neural combinatorial optimization approach analogous to the proposed comparison against MCTS-based methods, TAM, and GLOP. This paper is directly related to the proposed research method because it uses the same general evaluation logic of benchmarking a neural routing solver against exact and heuristic solvers while reporting solution quality and inference time, although it is limited to small-scale TSP and does not include the proposed CVRP and ATSP settings or modern baselines such as POMO, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, MCTS-based methods, TAM, or GLOP.
[results]
Reported outcomes show that reinforcement-learning pretraining significantly improves over supervised learning, and all evaluated neural methods surpass Christofides. Greedy neural methods are described as time-efficient and only a few percent worse than optimality. Inference-time search improves solution quality but increases running time; RL pretraining-Sampling and RL pretraining-Active Search recover optimal solutions in a significant number of test cases. Many RL pretraining methods outperform OR-Tools local search, including RL pretraining-Greedy@16, which runs similarly fast. For small solution spaces, RL pretraining-Sampling with a finetuned softmax temperature outperforms Active Search, while for larger solution spaces Active Search is superior when controlling for the number of sampled solutions or running time. Active Search from an untrained model is competitive but requires 7 hours per TSP50 instance and 25 hours per TSP100 instance. Compared with metaheuristics, the neural method is superior to simulated annealing but slightly less competitive than tabu search and much less competitive than guided local search. These results support the proposed plan's emphasis on reporting optimality gaps and inference times when comparing ICAM to classical solvers and neural combinatorial optimization baselines.
[context]
Experimental conditions include 1,000 test graphs per TSP size, with points drawn uniformly at random in the unit square. Models use mini-batches of 128 sequences, LSTM cells with 128 hidden units, 128-dimensional coordinate embeddings, the Adam optimizer, an initial learning rate of 1e-3 for TSP20 and TSP50 and 1e-4 for TSP100, learning-rate decay by a factor of 0.96 every 5,000 steps, uniform parameter initialization in [-0.08, 0.08], L2 gradient clipping to 1.0, and up to one attention glimpse. Active Search uses baseline decay alpha = 0.99. Search mini-batches consist of replications or permutations of the test sequence. Hardware includes a single Nvidia Tesla K80 GPU for the greedy neural methods, an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3 3.50GHz CPU for Concorde and LK-H, and an Intel Haswell CPU for OR-Tools. Metrics are average tour length, ratio to optimality, running time, and performance as a function of the number of solutions considered. This context is relevant to the ICAM research plan because it shows a small-scale TSP benchmarking setup that the proposed method extends to larger TSP, CVRP, and ATSP instances, broader baseline families, and explicit optimality-gap and inference-time evaluation.
[end related work 4]

