[method]
The paper uses a constructive reinforcement-learning method called S2V-DQN for TSP and other graph problems. It represents the current partial solution with a structure2vec graph embedding, computes node embeddings over T=4 iterations, and defines Q(h(S), v; Theta) from the node embedding and a pooled graph embedding. The policy greedily selects the next node to append to a partial tour until all nodes are visited, with the helper maintaining the tour and the cost set to negative tour length. Training uses n-step Q-learning combined with fitted Q-iteration and experience replay, updating parameters on random batches of stored transitions. For TSP, instances are fully connected 2D graphs with Euclidean edge weights, generated from DIMACS-style uniform or clustered point distributions, and real-world benchmark instances are also used. This method is related to the proposed ICAM plan because it is a constructive neural combinatorial optimization baseline for TSP, comparable in category to POMO, MatNet, ELG, and Pointerformer, but it does not use the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, AAFM, or vehicle-routing constraints, so its relevance is limited to TSP baseline comparison and large-scale generalization context.
[results]
The supplied paper text reports that S2V-DQN significantly outperforms all competing methods for TSP, MVC, and MAXCUT, and that the learned heuristics remain effective on instances larger than those used during training. However, the excerpt does not provide numeric tour lengths, optimality gaps, inference times, statistical significance, or exact baseline names, so quantitative comparison with the proposed ICAM metrics cannot be extracted from the provided material.
[context]
