[method]
The paper evaluates efficient active search (EAS) as a search-time improvement procedure for constructive neural combinatorial optimization models. For TSP and CVRP, EAS is integrated into the POMO constructive method; for JSSP, it is integrated into L2D. The EAS variants update restricted subsets of model parameters rather than the full model: EAS-Emb directly modifies node embeddings, EAS-Lay updates an added layer, and EAS-Tab is associated with a tabu/intensification-style search component. The procedure samples solutions over 200 search iterations, uses the Adam optimizer, and tunes the hyperparameters lambda, sigma, alpha, and the learning rate by Bayesian optimization on validation instances drawn from the same distribution as test instances. This is related to the proposed research plan because POMO is one of the constructive NCO baselines that ICAM will compare against, and the paper provides evidence about how constructive routing models can be improved through active search and parameter updates, although it does not implement instance-conditioned adaptation, attention-free modules, or large-scale generalization tests.
[results]
The provided excerpt reports qualitative search-trajectory outcomes rather than numeric optimality gaps. For all three problems, pure sampling produced solution quality that did not improve over the 200 iterations, while all active search variants improved solution quality over time. On TSP, EAS-Emb and EAS-Lay had nearly identical performance, with EAS-Tab slightly better; the original active search was much more unstable, attributed to a learning rate that was too high despite validation tuning. On CVRP, all EAS variants found better solutions on average than the original active search after only a few iterations, suggesting that fixing most parameters simplifies learning and speeds convergence. On JSSP, EAS-Emb was significantly better than the other methods because the L2D model has only two node features and a complex embedding generation procedure, so directly modifying embeddings was more effective than fine-tuning the full embedding process. No exact objective values, gap percentages, or inference times are given in the excerpt.
[context]
Experiments were run on a GPU cluster using one Nvidia Tesla V100 GPU and one Intel Xeon 4114 CPU core at 2.2 GHz per experiment. The models were based on existing POMO and L2D implementations, with minor modifications to add EAS; trained models from the original authors were used when available. Hyperparameters were tuned on separate validation instances from the same distribution as test instances and were not retuned for larger generalization instances. The trajectory analysis monitored 200 search iterations and averaged quality over all JSSP test instances and the first 1,000 TSP and CVRP test instances. The setting is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because it uses POMO-based TSP/CVRP constructive baselines and solution-quality monitoring, but it does not specify instance scales, optimality-gap baselines against classical solvers, or inference-time comparisons required by the proposed method.
