The paper uses a hybrid neural-guided local search method for the TSP. A graph neural network is trained by supervised learning on the line graph of the TSP instance, using only edge weight, defined as the Euclidean distance between adjacent nodes, as the input feature. The model predicts an approximation of the global regret associated with including each edge in the tour. These predicted edge regrets are then used by a regret-guided local search metaheuristic, together with the original TSP graph, to construct and improve tours. This method is relevant to the proposed ICAM research plan because it is a learning-based TSP solver evaluated using optimality gaps and inference times against classical solvers and other neural routing methods, but it differs from ICAM in that it is a regret-guided local search approach rather than a constructive attention-free neural solver, and it is evaluated only on small TSP instances.
