- citation count: 126
[method]
The paper evaluates Simulation-guided Beam Search (SGBS) and SGBS+EAS as inference-time search and fine-tuning methods for constructive neural solvers. SGBS performs breadth-first beam search over a decision tree, using expansion, simulation, and pruning to improve solutions generated by a pre-trained policy. SGBS+EAS adds insertion layers to the policy, uses SGBS-generated solutions plus sampled solutions for policy-gradient updates, and returns the best incumbent solution. Experiments use Transformer-like POMO-trained attention models for TSP and CVRP, with x8 instance augmentation, and a MatNet-based solver for FFSP. The evaluation setup closely matches the proposed ICAM baseline plan: it compares against classical solvers Concorde for TSP, HGS for CVRP, and LKH3; against constructive neural baselines including POMO-trained models and MatNet; and against additional learned heuristics DACT, NLNS, and DPDP. It also resembles two-stage neural search or fine-tuning approaches such as MCTS-based, TAM, or GLOP methods because it first searches with a learned policy and then refines an auxiliary module. Metrics are optimality gaps, solution quality, and runtime, with GPU runtime adjusted to a single-GPU premise and CPU solver wall-clock times reported separately.
[results]
SGBS+EAS significantly outperforms the compared learned heuristics. On three TSP instance sets, it reduces optimality gaps by 45%, 33%, and 35% relative to EAS alone. On CVRP instance sets, it reduces gaps by 52%, 56%, and 59% relative to EAS alone and outperforms LKH3 by a large margin. On CVRP with 100 nodes, SGBS+EAS achieves a 0.11% gap to HGS, indicating quality competitive with a strong handcrafted solver, although the paper states it is not competitive in runtime. For FFSP, SGBS+EAS is reported to significantly outperform CPLEX mixed-integer programming and metaheuristic baselines, but the provided excerpt does not give exact numerical gaps.
[context]
Experiments run on Nvidia A100 GPUs with 80 GB memory; reported GPU runtimes are adjusted under a single-GPU premise. TSP and CVRP use 10,000 instances with 100 nodes, plus 1,000-instance test sets with 150 and 200 nodes for domain-shift evaluation; a real-world-like CVRP dataset is discussed in an appendix. FFSP uses 1,000 instances each for 20, 50, and 100 jobs, with three stages and four machines per stage. SGBS hyperparameters are (beta, gamma) = (10, 10) for TSP and (4, 4) for CVRP, selected by trial runs and described as not highly sensitive within reasonable ranges. The same POMO-trained model is used for greedy construction, sampling, and EAS; EAS insertion layers are randomly initialized and trained by policy gradient until convergence or timeout. Classical solvers are CPU-bound, so their wall-clock times are not directly comparable to GPU-computed neural method times.
