The paper evaluates a step-wise constructive neural routing solver trained by imitation learning from expert trajectories. It creates a one-million-solution expert dataset for Euclidean TSP, CVRP, OP, ATSP, and KP using Concorde, LKH, and EA4OP, then samples subpaths of length 4 to N from optimal tours to form minibatches, which augments training data. The model has 9 layers, 12 attention heads, embedding size 192, and feed-forward dimension 512, and is trained with cross-entropy loss, Adam with initial learning rate 7.5e-4 and decay 0.98 every 50 epochs, batch size 1024, and 500 epochs. It is trained on synthetic size-100 instances and tested on synthetic sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, plus TSPLib instances up to 4461 nodes and Euclidean CVRPLib instances. It is compared with classical solvers such as OR-Tools, LKH, and HGS, hybrid methods such as DIFUSCO+2opt, Att-GCN+MCTS, and SGBS, and constructive neural baselines such as AM, TransformerTSP, MDAM, POMO, DIMES, and Sym-NCO. The evaluation uses optimality gap and inference time. This is directly related to the proposed ICAM method because the research plan also targets TSP, CVRP, and ATSP, emphasizes large-scale generalization, and proposes comparison against classical solvers, constructive NCO methods, and MCTS-based two-stage methods using optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference times.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not include the numeric values from the results table, but it reports that the model shows superior generalization to larger graphs even with greedy decoding, which generates a single solution while baselines generate several hundred solutions and select the best. Beam search improves optimality gaps but increases runtime. Replacing quadratic attention with PerceiverIO gives a considerable reduction in inference time for larger graphs with some performance drop, while still outperforming other NCO baselines. On TSPLib and CVRPLib, the model significantly outperforms end-to-end baseline policies with greedy decoding. The only competitive methods are Att-GCN+MCTS and DIFUSCO+2opt, but both are hybrid, TSP-only, and 2 to 15 times slower. With greedy decoding, the model is slightly slower than Sym-NCO, competitive with POMO, and significantly faster than other models. No statistical significance tests are reported in the excerpt.
[context]
Training uses synthetic size-100 instances; ATSP uses Euclidean distance matrices with randomly broken symmetries. Test instances use sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, with CVRP capacities of 50, 80, 100, and 250 respectively. The expert dataset contains one million solutions, and subpath sampling starts at length 4. For non-Perceiver models, test-time subgraphs are limited to the 250 nearest neighbors of the origin node to reduce inference time without significant quality impact. Deep learning models run on a single Nvidia Tesla V100-S GPU with 24GB memory, while classical solvers run on an Intel Xeon E5-2670 CPU with 256GB memory in one thread. TSP optimality gaps use Concorde optima, while CVRP gaps use LKH solutions as reference for comparability even though HGS is better. This context is relevant to ICAM because the proposed plan also uses LKH3, HGS, and Concorde-style references, reports inference time, and focuses on large-scale routing, but ICAM extends evaluation to 5,000 nodes and adds baselines such as MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, TAM, and GLOP that are not present in the paper.
