[method]
The paper evaluates ELG-POMO, a constructive neural vehicle routing method that ensembles a global POMO policy with an auxiliary local policy. This is directly relevant to the proposed ICAM research plan because ELG is one of the constructive NCO baselines named in the proposed comparison, and the paper also reports results for POMO, Pointerformer, TAM, and classical solvers that ICAM plans to compare against. ELG-POMO uses a Transformer encoder-decoder without positional embedding as the global policy: the encoder represents the full instance, the decoder scores candidate next nodes through query-key compatibility, invalid actions are masked, and the solution is constructed autoregressively. The local policy operates on the local neighborhood of the current node and is combined with the global policy to form the final action distribution. The paper also compares classical solvers LKH3 and HGS, uses Concorde as the TSP best-known-solution reference, and includes TAM as a divide-and-conquer/two-stage baseline in an appendix comparison on CVRPLIB Set-X with N >= 500 and Set-XXL, although the provided excerpt does not give detailed TAM numbers. Evaluation uses greedy inference, POMO-style x8 instance augmentation, average gap to best known solutions, and average runtime.
[results]
ELG-POMO outperformed all compared methods on the N <= 200 TSPLIB and CVRPLIB cross-distribution subset, with gap reductions of 0.53 percentage points on CVRP and 0.50 percentage points on TSP relative to the runner-up. On the N in (200, 1000] or (200, 1002] subset, ELG-POMO reduced the CVRP gap from 6.95% for Omni-POMO to 6.46%, and reduced the TSP gap from 7.47% to 5.90%; it outperformed BQ and LEHD on CVRP but lagged behind them on TSP. On four CVRPLIB Set-XXL real-world instances with N = 3000 to 7000, ELG-POMO was best on three instances and reduced optimality gaps compared with other POMO-based methods. In the synthetic-distribution TSP test, ELG* was retrained on uniform, cluster, and cluster mixture instances and tested on cluster, mixture, explosion, and rotation instances; it beat AMDKD on three of four N = 100 distributions and all four N = 200 distributions, with N = 200 gaps of 1.84%, 1.62%, 1.43%, and 1.58% versus AMDKD gaps of 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54%. On uniform N = 100 in-distribution tests, ELG-POMO achieved a 0.22% gap on TSP100 and a 1.22% gap on CVRP100, improving over POMO's 0.40% and 1.34%, but remaining behind Pointerformer at 0.16% and T2TCO at 0.18% on TSP100, and behind HGS at 0.52% and LKH3 at 0.00% on CVRP100. Runtime was reported as 1.90 seconds per instance versus 16 minutes for the classical-solver comparison, while the optimality gap remained worse than HGS.
