The paper studies LEHD, a constructive neural combinatorial optimization model with a light encoder and heavy decoder, trained primarily by supervised learning and an efficient self-improved training procedure that does not require labeled optimal solutions. It learns to construct partial solutions during training and complete solutions during inference, then applies Random Re-Construct, abbreviated RRC, as a post-construction improvement step by repeatedly reconstructing parts of the solution. Experiments use uniformly distributed TSP and CVRP instances of sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, as well as real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances. The evaluation compares greedy inference and RRC with 100, 300, 500, or 1000 iterations against constructive baselines such as POMO and BQ, learning-based methods such as EAS and SGBS, and classical or search-based solvers including LKH3, Concorde, SO-mixed, and H-TSP. Metrics are optimality gap relative to reference or optimal solutions and wall-clock inference time. This is directly related to the proposed ICAM plan because ICAM also targets large-scale TSP, CVRP, and ATSP generalization with an encoder-decoder constructive solver and plans to compare against POMO, LKH3, Concorde, and other constructive or two-stage NCO methods using gap and inference time; LEHD plus RRC is a relevant strong constructive baseline and a contrast for whether a heavy decoder plus re-constructive improvement can replace or complement ICAM's instance-conditioned adaptation and attention-free architecture.
[results]
On uniform TSP, LEHD with 100 RRC iterations outperforms other learning-based methods at reasonable inference time; with more than 500 RRC iterations it reaches nearly optimal gaps for TSP100 and TSP200, less than 0.2% for TSP500, and about 0.8% for TSP1000. On uniform CVRP, LEHD with 300 RRC iterations outperforms most learning-based methods on all instances except EAS on CVRP100, which is slower; EAS degrades on CVRP500 and no reasonable-budget result is reported for CVRP1000. With 500 RRC iterations, LEHD outperforms LKH3 on CVRP100 through CVRP500, and with 1000 RRC iterations it is about 1% worse than LKH3 on CVRP1000. On real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib, greedy inference plus 1000 RRC iterations outperforms POMO and BQ on all reported instances. In the search-based comparison on 128 TSP instances, LEHD greedy gaps are 0.859% for TSP200 in 3 seconds, 1.560% for TSP500 in 0.3 minutes, and 3.168% for TSP1000 in 1.6 minutes; LEHD RRC100 gaps are 0.0761% for TSP200 in 1.2 minutes, 0.343% for TSP500 in 8 minutes, and 1.218% for TSP1000 in 43 minutes. SO-mixed gaps are 0.636% in 21 minutes, 2.401% in 32 minutes, and 2.800% in 56 minutes; Concorde is 0.000% with 3 minutes, 32 minutes, and 7.8 hours. On TSP1000, H-TSP with LKH-3 has a 4.06% gap in 7.5 minutes, while LEHD RRC100 has a 1.218% gap in 43 minutes. Ablations indicate that the heavy decoder, rather than supervised learning alone, drives large-scale generalization, and supervised training is more practical than reinforcement learning for LEHD under the same computational budget.
[context]
The experimental context includes TSP and CVRP instances with 100 to 1000 nodes, uniform distributions and real-world TSPLib or CVRPLib distributions, with 128 instances used in the large-scale TSP comparisons against SO-mixed and H-TSP. Inference budgets are explicit: greedy inference, 100 RRC iterations, 300 RRC iterations, 500 RRC iterations, and 1000 RRC iterations. The paper emphasizes fast greedy inference and controlled RRC budgets, comparing against methods that require powerful external solvers or long search times. The model is trained with supervised learning and self-improved training without labeled solutions; a reinforcement-learning POMO variant is reported to be too costly to converge in reasonable time, so a TSP50 same-budget comparison is used. These conditions are relevant to ICAM because ICAM's plan also uses scale-varying training, multiple trajectory or augmentation scenarios, and large-scale evaluation up to 5000 nodes for TSP and CVRP, so the paper's 1000-node results and RRC budget sensitivity provide a baseline for judging whether ICAM's instance-conditioned adaptation can improve gap-time tradeoffs beyond heavy-decoder construction and re-constructive improvement.
[end related work 22]

