[method]
The paper describes GLOP, a two-stage neural routing solver that is directly relevant to the proposed research method because GLOP is listed as one of the two-stage NCO baselines against which ICAM will be compared. GLOP learns local policies for solving (sub-)TSP instances and global policies for partitioning larger routing problems into sub-TSPs. For TSP, it first constructs an initial tour using Random Insertion, then decomposes the tour into independent subtours and trains a local reviser to reconstruct those subtours in order to reduce total tour length. The local policy is trained by minimizing expected solution length with a REINFORCE-based estimator, using the average length of two greedy rollouts as the baseline. For general routing problems, a global policy predicts a partition heatmap, samples node partitions in parallel, passes the sampled partitions to the local GLOP solver, and is trained with REINFORCE using the average reward of sampled partitions for the same instance as the baseline. The experimental setup covers large-scale TSP instances of 500, 1,000, 10,000, and 100,000 nodes, cross-distribution TSP instances, 49 symmetric TSPLIB EUC2D instances with fewer than 1,000 nodes normalized to the unit square, ATSP instances, CVRP instances at 1,000, 2,000, 5,000, and 7,000 nodes plus CVRPLIB instances, and PCTSP instances at 500, 1,000, and 5,000 nodes. This relates to the ICAM research plan because ICAM aims to improve cross-scale generalization for TSP, CVRP, and ATSP and explicitly plans to compare against GLOP using optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference time.
