The paper evaluates a multi-task construction-based neural vehicle routing solver with mixture-of-experts layers, called MVMoE, against classical and neural baselines. This is directly related to the proposed ICAM baseline-comparison method because it uses POMO-style constructive neural solvers and classical routing solvers as comparison points, and it reports optimality-gap-style performance on multiple VRP variants. MVMoE uses an encoder-decoder autoregressive construction policy: static node features such as coordinates, demand, and time-window start/end times are encoded, and the decoder uses the last selected node embedding plus dynamic features such as remaining vehicle capacity, current time, partial route length, and an open-route indicator to select the next valid node. Missing attributes for a selected VRP variant are padded with default values, typically zeros. The model is trained across 16 VRP variants formed from five constraints, with a VRP variant randomly selected in each training step. The evaluated variants include a 4-expert MVMoE configuration and a lower-computation MVMoE/4E-L configuration. Baselines include HGS for CVRP and VRPTW with default maximum 20000 iterations without improvement, LKH3 for CVRP, OVRP, VRPL, and VRPTW with 10000 trials and 1 run, OR-Tools for all 16 variants using parallel cheapest insertion as the first-solution strategy and guided local search, and neural baselines POMO trained per task and POMO-MTL trained multi-task. Evaluation reports percentage performance gaps or objective degradation on trained and unseen VRP variants, including zero-shot and few-shot generalization tests.
[results]
On the six trained VRPs, single-task POMO was better than multi-task solvers on each individual problem, but its average performance was much worse: POMO trained only on CVRP had 16.815% average performance, while POMO-MTL had 2.102% and MVMoE/4E had 1.925%. MVMoE consistently outperformed POMO-MTL. MVMoE/4E was slightly better than MVMoE/4E-L but required more computation, while MVMoE/4E-L showed stronger out-of-distribution generalization. On ten unseen VRP variants, zero-shot MVMoE significantly outperformed POMO-MTL across all variants. In the few-shot setting on n=50, after fine-tuning on 10,000 target-VRP instances per epoch, described as 0.01% of the total training instances, MVMoE generalized more favorably than POMO-MTL on VRPBLTW and OVRPBLTW. The excerpt does not provide statistical tests, confidence intervals, or p-values, and it does not explicitly report neural inference times, so the reported advantage should be interpreted as a performance-gap comparison rather than a statistically validated or latency-validated superiority.
[context]
Experiments were run on NVIDIA A100-80GB GPUs and AMD EPYC 7513 CPUs at 2.6 GHz. Instance sizes were n=50 and n=100 for benchmark comparisons. Classical solver time limits were 20 seconds for n=50 and 40 seconds for n=100, with longer 200-second and 400-second runs also provided for OR-Tools. Classical solvers solved 32 instances in parallel on 32 CPU cores. The study covered 16 VRP variants with five constraints, of which 6 were trained and 10 were unseen. Few-shot fine-tuning used 10,000 instances per epoch, implying a total training set of roughly 100 million instances if 10,000 is 0.01%. The exact instance-generation distributions are not specified in the provided excerpt. The paper's comparison is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because it uses POMO and classical routing solvers as baselines and reports solution-quality gaps on VRP variants, but it does not include Concorde, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, MCTS-based methods, TAM, or GLOP, and it does not test the large-scale 1,000+ node cross-scale generalization setting central to ICAM. Additionally, LKH3 and HGS are heuristic classical solvers, and the excerpt does not state that their outputs are certified optimal, so the percentage gaps should be interpreted relative to reported solver performance rather than proven optima.
