[method]
The paper evaluates a unified divide-and-conquer neural solver, UDC, for large-scale combinatorial optimization, including TSP and CVRP. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because the plan also targets large-scale TSP/CVRP/ATSP, uses LKH3-based gaps and inference time, and compares against POMO, ELG, TAM, GLOP, and MCTS-based methods; UDC is a two-stage NCO reference and uses ICAM as one of the constructive policies for VRP subproblems. UDC constructs a sparse graph for each instance, uses an anisotropic graph neural network heatmap dividing policy to generate an initial solution, decomposes the instance into subproblems based on that solution, and solves the subproblems with constructive neural policies such as POMO or ICAM for vehicle routing. It trains the dividing and conquering policies jointly with REINFORCE and a Divide-Conquer-Reunion process that adds a reunion subproblem between adjacent subproblems to reduce biased rewards. The main extracted experiments use 500, 1000, and 2000 node TSP and CVRP instances, with 128 instances for TSP500 and CVRP500 and 100 instances for CVRP1000 and CVRP2000.
