- in_text_citation: [(Bello et al., 2016-11)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d7878c2044fb699e0ce0cad83e411824b1499dc8)
- venue: International Conference on Learning Representations
- citation count: 2035
[method]
The paper evaluates a constructive neural combinatorial optimization method for Euclidean TSP using a pointer-network-style model with an LSTM encoder and up to one attention glimpse. The model is trained with supervised learning and reinforcement-learning pretraining, then decoded at inference time using three settings: greedy decoding as a single-trajectory baseline, sampling as a multiple-trajectory setting, and active search as an adaptive multiple-trajectory setting. Experiments use randomly generated Euclidean TSP instances with 20, 50, and 100 cities, with 1,000 test graphs per scale. Evaluation reports average tour length, ratios or gaps to optimality, and running time, and compares the neural methods against Christofides, a general vehicle-routing solver with local search and metaheuristics, and optimal or empirically optimal solutions from Concorde and LK-H. This is directly related to the proposed method because it uses the same broad evaluation logic: objective value, optimality gap relative to a strong solver, inference time, and comparison between single-trajectory and multiple-trajectory decoding. It also provides context for the research plan by showing how constructive neural routing methods can be benchmarked under different search budgets, although the paper is limited to small-scale TSP and does not test CVRP, ATSP, large-scale generalization, or instance augmentation in the sense proposed for ICAM.
[results]
On 1,000 random unit-square TSP instances for each of TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100, reinforcement-learning pretraining substantially improves over supervised pointer-network training. All evaluated neural methods outperform Christofides, including greedy decoding without inference-time search. The greedy methods are reported as time-efficient and only a few percent worse than optimality, but exact average tour lengths and gap percentages are not included in the provided text. Inference-time search improves solution quality at the cost of longer running time. RL pretraining-Sampling and RL pretraining-Active Search are the most competitive neural methods and recover optimal tours in a significant number of test cases. For smaller solution spaces, sampling with a tuned softmax temperature outperforms active search, while for larger solution spaces active search is superior when controlling either the number of sampled solutions or the running time. Active search started from an untrained model also produces competitive tours but requires 7 hours per TSP50 instance and 25 hours per TSP100 instance. Compared with OR-Tools metaheuristics, the paper reports that the neural methods are superior to simulated annealing, slightly less competitive than tabu search, and much less competitive than guided local search. No statistical significance tests are reported in the provided excerpt.
[context]
Experimental conditions are specific to small-scale Euclidean TSP: 20, 50, and 100 cities, points drawn uniformly from the unit square, and 1,000 test instances per problem size. Training uses mini-batches of 128 sequences, LSTM cells with 128 hidden units, 128-dimensional coordinate embeddings, the Adam optimizer, an initial learning rate of 1e-3 for TSP20 and TSP50 and 1e-4 for TSP100, learning-rate decay by 0.96 every 5,000 steps, uniform parameter initialization in [-0.08, 0.08], and L2 gradient clipping to 1.0. The model uses up to one attention glimpse, and active search uses a baseline decay of 0.99. Search mini-batches consist of replications or permutations of the test sequence. Running times are measured on a single Nvidia Tesla K80 GPU for the neural greedy methods, while Concorde and LK-H run on an Intel Xeon CPU E5-1650 v3 at 3.50 GHz and the vehicle-routing solver runs on an Intel Haswell CPU. This context matters for the proposed ICAM plan because the paper demonstrates the relevance of objective value, optimality gap, and inference-time search tradeoffs, but it does not provide evidence for ICAM’s target settings of 1,000 to 5,000 node instances, CVRP or ATSP, LKH3-based optimality gaps, or instance-augmentation decoding scenarios.
