[results]
For VRP10 and VRP20, beam width 10 was the best-performing decoding setting. About 95% of instances were within 10% of optimal for VRP10 and within 13% of optimal for VRP20, and even outliers were within 20-25% of optimality. For VRP50 and VRP100, classical heuristics were outperformed by the other approaches in almost 100% of samples. RL-greedy was comparable to OR-Tools, while beam search increased the winning rate above 60%. In the extracted winning-rate table, the final column consistent with OR-Tools shows RL-BS(10) at 60.2%, RL-BS(5) at 54.6%, and RL-Greedy at 41.5%. Solution-time analysis showed that the log of solution time per node remained nearly constant across RL decoders, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep grew faster than linearly; the RL framework was faster than randomized heuristics on larger instances, although exact OR-Tools timing comparisons were confounded by C++ versus Python implementations.
[context]
Experiments used one-vehicle capacitated VRP instances with customers and depot uniformly sampled in the unit square, demands uniformly drawn from integers 1 to 9, no split deliveries, and 1,000 instances per problem size for VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100. Feasibility masking prevented visiting zero-demand nodes, forced return to the depot when the vehicle load was zero, and masked customers whose demand exceeded the remaining load. Baselines included Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized variants, and Google OR-Tools; optimal solutions for VRP10 and VRP20 were obtained by solving a mixed-integer formulation. Hardware, exact vehicle capacities, and full training hyperparameters are not fully specified in the provided excerpt.
