[method]
The paper describes POMO's shared-baseline policy-gradient method for constructive combinatorial optimization. For each instance, the policy samples N solution trajectories, each associated with a different starting action, and computes the return of each trajectory. Instead of using a per-trajectory greedy-rollout return as the baseline, POMO uses a shared baseline formed from the returns of the N sampled trajectories. The policy is then updated with a REINFORCE-style gradient ascent on the advantage, i.e., the trajectory return minus the shared baseline, in a mini-batch training loop. This method relates to the proposed research method because the proposed evaluation explicitly compares single-trajectory, multiple-trajectory, and instance-augmentation settings, and to the research plan because the plan builds on POMO-style training and top-k elite trajectory selection. The supplied excerpt does not detail instance augmentation or LKH3-based optimality-gap evaluation, so the connection is primarily to multiple-trajectory scoring and baseline construction.
[results]
The excerpt provides only qualitative results. It reports that the shared POMO baseline yields lower-variance policy gradients than a greedy-rollout baseline, that the POMO advantage term has zero mean, and that the greedy-rollout baseline tends to produce negative advantages because sampled rollouts often fail to outperform argmax greedy rollouts. It also claims that the shared baseline discourages premature convergence to suboptimal policies by making each trajectory compete with N-1 other heterogeneous trajectories, and that it is more efficient than baselines requiring a separate critic network or a cloned-policy forward pass. No numerical objective values, optimality gaps, inference times, statistical significance, or quantitative baseline comparisons are given in the provided text.
[context]
The experimental context is a REINFORCE-based constructive solver in which N trajectories are sampled per instance and a shared baseline is computed from those trajectories. The baseline may depend on the initial action, but POMO uses a common baseline across the sampled trajectories. The comparison setting is a greedy-rollout baseline in which each sampled rollout is evaluated against the argmax rollout from the same starting node. The excerpt does not specify problem domains, instance sizes, number of trajectories N, hardware, learning rates, training steps, datasets, or inference-time measurement protocol, so those reproducibility details are absent.
