[method]
The paper evaluates a constructive neural solver, LEHD, built from a light encoder and heavy decoder, trained with supervised learning on partial solutions, and improved by a random re-construct (RRC) post-processing step. For each instance, the model first produces a greedy solution, which corresponds to a single decoding trajectory, and then applies a fixed number of RRC iterations, which corresponds to multiple inference passes rather than explicitly independent trajectory sampling. The experiments use uniformly distributed TSP and CVRP instances with 100, 200, 500, and 1000 nodes, as well as real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances. The evaluation reports solution quality as percentage optimality gap and wall-clock inference time, using budgets such as greedy, 100, 300, 500, and 1000 RRC iterations. This is related to the proposed ICAM evaluation because the proposed method also plans to report objective values, optimality gaps relative to LKH3, total inference times, and results under single trajectory, multiple trajectory, and instance augmentation settings; the paper's greedy-versus-RRC protocol and gap/time reporting provide a directly comparable template, although the paper does not explicitly use instance augmentation and uses Concorde for TSP optimal gaps in addition to LKH3 comparisons.
[results]
On uniform TSP, LEHD with 100 RRC iterations outperforms other learning-based methods at reasonable inference time. With more than 500 RRC iterations, the gap is nearly optimal for TSP100 and TSP200, below 0.2% for TSP500, and about 0.8% for TSP1000. On uniform CVRP, 300 RRC iterations outperform most learning-based methods on all instances except EAS on CVRP100; 500 RRC iterations outperform LKH3 on CVRP100 through CVRP500, and 1000 RRC iterations reach about a 1% gap to LKH3 on CVRP1000. On real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances, greedy inference and 1000 RRC iterations significantly outperform POMO and BQ. In large-scale TSP comparisons, LEHD greedy has gaps of 0.859%, 1.560%, and 3.168% on TSP200, TSP500, and TSP1000 with times of 3 seconds, 0.3 minutes, and 1.6 minutes; LEHD RRC100 has gaps of 0.0761%, 0.343%, and 1.218% with times of 1.2 minutes, 8 minutes, and 43 minutes. These results beat SO-mixed, which has gaps of 0.636%, 2.401%, and 2.800%, and beat H-TSP with LKH-3 on TSP1000, which has a 4.06% gap, while Concorde remains at 0.000% but with much longer runtimes.
[context]
The supplied sections specify instance scales of 100 to 1000 nodes for uniform TSP/CVRP tests and real-world TSPLib/CVRPLib tests, and they specify RRC budgets of 100, 300, 500, and 1000 iterations. Inference times are reported per method, but hardware specifications and statistical significance tests are not provided in the supplied text. The paper attributes the generalization advantage to the heavy decoder architecture rather than supervised learning alone, based on comparisons with POMO. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM research plan because ICAM targets large-scale TSP, CVRP, and ATSP generalization and should report comparable gap/time trade-offs across single trajectory, multiple trajectory, and augmentation settings, using LKH3-based gaps and explicit inference budgets analogous to the paper's RRC settings.
