The paper evaluates a hybrid MDVRP heuristic, referred to as GANCP+, on out-of-range and out-of-distribution instances. GANCP+ represents each candidate solution as a customer-to-depot allocation chromosome, uses a supervised neural cost estimator to score the implied subproblem CVRPs, repairs infeasible depot assignments by moving customers to depots with sufficient remaining capacity, and evolves candidates using binary tournament selection, uniform crossover, FLIP/SWAP mutations, and targeted elite copying. The best final candidates are then solved with a CVRP solver. For the evaluation most relevant to the proposed method, the authors test 50 randomly generated out-of-range MDVRP instances whose subproblem sizes fall outside the trained range, and eight out-of-distribution instance classes generated from different customer-positioning and demand distributions. This relates to the proposed research method because it also assesses solution quality relative to a strong solver and reports computational advantage, and it probes generalization beyond the training scale and distribution; however, the paper uses VROOM rather than LKH3 and does not report single-trajectory, multi-trajectory, or instance-augmentation decoding settings.
[results]
On the 50 out-of-range instances, GANCP+ outperformed VROOM for problem sizes N in the interval [101, 250), but did not outperform VROOM for relatively smaller or larger out-of-range instances; the positive performance gap in those cases was described as relatively small. On the eight out-of-distribution classes, evaluated over 10 runs, GANCP+ produced satisfactory results despite positive gaps in some cases. The D4 class, which most closely resembled the training distribution, had average performance over 10 runs that outperformed VROOM. The largest deviation from optimality occurred in D5, where customers were purely clustered and demands were unitary. After fine-tuning the neural estimator with 32,000 CVRP instances per distribution class plus additional Phase III step-4 data, performance improved in multiple distribution classes, but classes with high training MAPE, specifically D1 and D6, showed decreased average performance relative to the no-fine-tuning results. The paper also reports a considerable computational advantage for out-of-distribution data, although exact inference-time values are not provided in the excerpt.
[context]
The out-of-range set consisted of 50 random MDVRP instances with subproblem size N_D outside [50, 500]. The out-of-distribution set comprised eight instance classes generated from three customer-positioning rules, random, clustered, and random-clustered, crossed with two demand rules, unitary and quadrant-dependent, with depots placed randomly on the grid. The main neural model was trained on uniform random grid instances with demands from a uniform distribution. Fine-tuning used 32,000 CVRP instances per class plus Step 4 Phase III instances, about 50% of the main train/validation data, and initialized from Phase III weights. The comparison solver was VROOM, and out-of-distribution results were summarized as averages over 10 runs. Hardware, exact optimality-gap values, and total inference times are not specified in the provided text.
