The paper evaluates a neural constructive routing solver called INViT on TSP and CVRP instances, using an evaluation protocol closely related to the proposed method. It reports average optimality gaps computed from solution objective values against reference solutions, and it also reports total inference time per dataset. Reference solutions are obtained with Gurobi for TSP-100, LKH3 for TSP-1000, TSP-5000, and TSP-10000, HGS for CVRP, and known optimal values for TSPLIB and CVRPLIB. The model is trained with a REINFORCE-style objective using data augmentation, including rotation, reflection, normalization, and varied starting points. A baseline model is maintained with greedy rollout, while the training model uses random sampling; the baseline is updated when the training model performs better. At test time, data augmentation is used and the best augmented solution is selected. The paper compares INViT against several constructive neural routing baselines, including POMO, Omni-TSP/VRP, PointerFormer, ELG-v1, LEHD, and BQ-NCO. This setup relates directly to the proposed research method because it uses objective-value-based gaps, LKH3-based optimality gaps for large TSP instances, inference-time reporting, and augmentation-based test-time evaluation, all of which align with the research plan’s goal of assessing large-scale generalization and practical inference cost for neural routing solvers.
[results]
INViT-3V achieved the best reported average gaps on the large-scale generated datasets: 7.97% on TSP-5000, 7.52% on TSP-10000, and 5.63% on CVRP-5000. Most comparison methods had average gaps above 10% on these large-scale datasets, except BQ-NCO on CVRP-5000. For cross-distribution generalization, the relative gap increase from uniform to other distributions was 31% for TSP-5000, 33% for TSP-10000, and 8% for CVRP-5000; these were slightly worse than the corresponding Omni-TSP/VRP values of 9% and 8% for TSP-5000 and TSP-10000, and -40% for CVRP-5000. INViT-2V showed similar generalization performance with lower inference time, while INViT-3V provided a slight performance improvement. On small- and medium-scale datasets, such as TSP-100, TSP-1000, and CVRP-500, INViT did not consistently outperform the strongest baselines; the gap to the best baseline was described as subtle on TSP-1000 and larger on CVRP-500. On public TSPLIB and CVRPLIB instances, the method remained competitive on small- and medium-scale instances and maintained strong generalization, although some out-of-memory issues were noted. The provided excerpt does not give numeric inference-time values, but it states that inference time was reported and that INViT-2V had lower inference time than INViT-3V.
