[method]
The paper evaluates a constructive neural combinatorial optimization method for Euclidean TSP using a pointer network with an LSTM encoder and up to one attention glimpse. The model is trained either by supervised learning on optimal tours or by reinforcement learning pretraining, and then evaluated with different inference-time search strategies: greedy decoding, sampling, and active search. This is related to the proposed ICAM research method because the paper provides a prior NCO baseline and a component-wise evaluation pattern: changing the training signal from supervised to RL and changing the inference search strategy changes both solution quality and runtime, which is analogous to the proposed ablations of ICAM adaptation components, AAFM versus MHA, training stages, model depth, and hyperparameters such as alpha.
[results]
RL pretraining substantially improved over supervised learning, and the reported RL pretraining variants surpassed Christofides. Greedy RL pretraining was time-efficient and was only a few percent worse than optimality. Inference-time search improved solution quality but increased runtime, and early stopping of sampling or active search gave a small objective tradeoff. RL pretraining-Sampling and RL pretraining-Active Search were the most competitive neural methods and recovered optimal solutions in a significant number of test cases. For smaller solution spaces, sampling with a tuned softmax temperature outperformed active search and ran faster; for larger solution spaces, active search was superior when controlling for either the number of sampled solutions or runtime. Several RL pretraining methods outperformed OR-Tools local search, including RL pretraining-Greedy@16, while active search from an untrained model required about 7 hours per TSP50 instance and 25 hours per TSP100 instance. The method was less competitive than guided local search and only slightly less competitive than tabu search. The provided sections do not report p-values, confidence intervals, or other statistical significance tests.
[context]
Experiments used 1,000 random test graphs for TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100, with points drawn uniformly from the unit square. Training used mini-batches of 128 sequences, LSTM hidden size 128, 128-dimensional coordinate embeddings, Adam optimization, initial learning rate 1e-3 for TSP20 and TSP50 and 1e-4 for TSP100, learning-rate decay by 0.96 every 5000 steps, uniform initialization in [-0.08, 0.08], gradient L2 clipping at 1.0, and active-search baseline decay alpha set to 0.99. Search mini-batches used replications or permutations of test sequences. The neural methods ran on a single Nvidia Tesla K80 GPU, while Concorde and LK-H ran on an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3 3.50GHz CPU and OR-Tools ran on an Intel Haswell CPU. Metrics included average tour length, running time, ratio to optimality, and number of solutions considered. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because it shows the scale, hyperparameter choices, and runtime-quality tradeoffs for constructive NCO on TSP, but it does not include instance-conditioned adaptation, attention-free modules, multi-stage training, CVRP, ATSP, or large-scale 1,000+ node generalization.
