[method]
The paper evaluates an attention-based neural constructive solver for the capacitated vehicle routing problem. The model is trained with policy gradient actor-critic methods, with asynchronous advantage actor-critic details deferred to the appendix. At inference time, the decoder constructs routes using either greedy decoding or beam search with beam widths 5 and 10. Feasibility is enforced through masking: customers with zero demand are masked, all customers are masked when the vehicle’s remaining load is exactly zero, and customers whose demand exceeds the remaining load are masked, so the main experiments do not allow split deliveries. The experimental setup generates CVRP instances with customers and depot uniformly in the unit square, integer demands uniformly sampled from 1 to 9, one vehicle with varying capacity, and 1,000 instances per problem size for VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100. The method is compared with Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized variants of these heuristics, and Google OR-Tools. This paper method is related to the proposed ICAM research plan as a prior reinforcement-learning-based constructive VRP solver and as an evaluation template for decoder/inference-time search and attention-based policy training. However, it does not implement the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, Adaptation Attention Free Module, three-stage training scheme, or learnable alpha parameter, so its relevance to the proposed ablation method is indirect: it provides baseline context for how decoder choice and policy-gradient training affect CVRP solution quality and runtime.
[results]
For VRP10 and VRP20, optimal solutions were obtained by solving a mixed integer formulation. Beam width 10 was the best-performing RL decoder: roughly 95% of VRP10 instances were within 10% of optimality, and roughly 95% of VRP20 instances were within 13% of optimality. Outliers were within 20-25% of optimality. For VRP50 and VRP100, where optimal values were not computationally affordable, pairwise winning rates were reported. Classical heuristics were outperformed by the other approaches in almost 100% of samples. RL-greedy was described as comparable to OR-Tools, while beam search increased the winning rate above 60%. The reported winning-rate table shows RL-BS(10) values of 91.9, 57.7, 99.8, 99.4, 99.2, 99.3, 99.3, 99.3, and 60.2 across the compared baselines, with the final value consistent with the text’s statement that beam search exceeds 60% against OR-Tools. Runtime analysis showed that the log of solution time divided by the number of customer nodes remained nearly constant for RL greedy and beam search, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep runtimes increased faster than linearly. The RL framework was faster than the randomized heuristics on larger problems, although exact OR-Tools time comparison was limited by the C++ implementation of OR-Tools versus Python implementations of the other baselines.
[context]
Key experimental conditions include a single-vehicle capacitated VRP, no split deliveries in the main experiments, random uniform node locations in the unit square, discrete uniform demands from 1 to 9, 1,000 instances per problem size, problem sizes of 10, 20, 50, and 100 customers, and varying vehicle capacities that are not fully specified in the provided excerpt. Decoding used greedy selection or beam search with beam widths 5 and 10. Training used standard policy gradient actor-critic methods, with A3C details in an appendix not included in the provided text. Hardware, exact hyperparameters, and full training details are not specified in the provided excerpt. This context matters for the proposed ICAM ablations because the paper demonstrates that decoder choice and policy-gradient training affect CVRP solution quality and runtime, but it does not provide ablations for ICAM-specific components such as the adaptation function, AAFM versus standard multi-head attention, progressive training stages, model depth, or alpha sensitivity.
