The paper evaluates a constructive neural routing solver, LEHD, through ablation-style component comparisons and large-scale benchmark comparisons. The ablation comparisons isolate the contribution of architecture, training objective, and post-construction improvement: (i) a heavy-decoder LEHD architecture is compared with a heavy-encoder POMO architecture trained by supervised learning on TSP100; (ii) supervised learning is compared with reinforcement learning using the POMO strategy for training LEHD on TSP50 under the same computational budget; and (iii) the Random Re-Construct inference procedure is compared with greedy inference and random-sampling baselines, including POMO-Sample100 and POMO-RRC100. The paper also compares LEHD greedy and LEHD with 100 RRC iterations against Concorde, SO-mixed, and H-TSP with LKH3 on TSP200, TSP500, and TSP1000 instances. Evaluation uses optimality gap and wall-clock inference time. This is related to the proposed ICAM ablation plan because it isolates the contribution of individual design choices, but it does not test the proposed adaptation function, AAFM, three-stage training scheme, model depth, or learnable parameter alpha.
[results]
The ablation outcomes are reported mainly qualitatively. POMO with a heavy encoder trained by supervised learning reaches good training performance on TSP100 but still performs poorly on large-scale problems, supporting the conclusion that LEHD's generalization advantage comes from the heavy decoder structure rather than supervised learning alone. Reinforcement learning with the POMO strategy does not converge in a reasonable time; under the same computational budget on TSP50, supervised learning is reported to be more suitable for training LEHD. RRC is reported to improve over greedy inference and random sampling, with LEHD plus 100 RRC iterations outperforming other learning-based methods and larger RRC budgets producing stronger results. With more than 500 RRC iterations, LEHD achieves nearly optimal gaps on TSP100 and TSP200, less than 0.2% on TSP500, and around 0.8% on TSP1000. For CVRP, 300 RRC iterations outperform most learning-based methods, 500 RRC iterations outperform LKH3 on CVRP100 through CVRP500, and 1000 RRC iterations achieve about a 1% gap to LKH3 on CVRP1000. In the large-scale TSP comparisons, LEHD RRC100 achieves 0.0761% gap in 1.2 minutes on TSP200, 0.343% gap in 8 minutes on TSP500, and 1.218% gap in 43 minutes on TSP1000, compared with SO-mixed gaps of 0.636%, 2.401%, and 2.800% and H-TSP with LKH3 at 4.06% on TSP1000. No statistical significance tests are reported.
