- in_text_citation: [(Ye et al., 2023-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5a0387b105e3bf63c8837aa49b08935714dbf831)
- venue: AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence
- citation count: 143
[method]
The paper’s relevant method is GLOP’s two-stage curriculum training and component-level evaluation framework for a divide-and-conquer routing solver. GLOP first trains a local TSP policy by generating initial tours with Random Insertion, decomposing tours into independent subtours, and learning a reviser to reconstruct those subtours. The local policy is trained by minimizing expected subtour length using a REINFORCE estimator, with the average length of two greedy rollouts used as the baseline. GLOP then trains a global partition policy that outputs a partition heatmap, samples node partitions, solves the resulting sub-TSPs with the trained local policy, and optimizes expected final solution length using REINFORCE with per-instance averaged rewards as the baseline. The paper also includes extended evaluations such as an ablation study of local-policy components, cross-distribution performance analysis, and stability analysis. These elements are directly related to the proposed ICAM research method because ICAM also plans systematic ablations to isolate the effects of architectural components, attention-free versus standard attention, training stages, model depth, and the learnable adaptation parameter alpha. GLOP therefore provides a comparable large-scale routing evaluation protocol and a baseline context for testing whether ICAM’s instance-conditioned adaptation and three-stage training improve cross-scale generalization on TSP, CVRP, and ATSP.
[results]
The supplied excerpt reports qualitative rather than quantitative outcomes. It states that GLOP outperforms H-TSP despite not learning an upper-level TSP policy, and that GLOP achieves competitive solutions compared with select-and-optimize while being hundreds of times more efficient. No numerical ablation values, per-component performance deltas, significance tests, or exact objective-value gaps are provided in the excerpt, so the quantitative contribution of each GLOP component cannot be extracted from the given text.
[context]
Experiments were conducted on a 12-core Intel Xeon Platinum 8255C CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. The evaluation used uniformly sampled large-scale TSP instances of 500, 1,000, 10,000, and 100,000 nodes, cross-distribution test instances, 49 symmetric TSPLIB EUC2D instances with fewer than 1,000 nodes normalized to the unit square, ATSP instances, CVRP instances of 1,000, 2,000, 5,000, and 7,000 nodes with 100 instances per scale, CVRPLIB instances, and PCTSP instances of 500, 1,000, and 5,000 nodes with prize sampling parameters K_n set to 9, 12, and 20 respectively. Baselines were selected to have adjustable runtime so that comparisons could be matched to GLOP, and evaluation metrics included objective value and runtime. This context is relevant to the ICAM plan because ICAM targets similar problem families and scales, especially 100 to 5,000 nodes for TSP and CVRP and up to 1,000 nodes for ATSP, and proposes ablations to determine whether its adaptation function, attention-free module, deeper encoder, and three-stage training improve large-scale generalization relative to existing constructive and two-stage routing solvers.
[end related work 11]

