[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Wang et al., 2024-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b6bceec0cce24f875623c275ed294461a96772dc)
- venue: arXiv.org
- citation count: 19
[method]
The paper uses a distance-aware attention reshaping method called DAR for a neural vehicle routing solver. It first computes standard dot-product attention scores from encoder features for candidate nodes, then computes Euclidean distances from the currently selected node to all candidate nodes. These distances are converted into distance scores using a logarithmic transformation under the assumption that nodes lie in a unit square, with a hyperparameter K used to favor nearby nodes and penalize distant nodes. The distance scores are added to the original attention scores, normalized with a scaling constant C set to 50, and the resulting weights are used to select the next node. This method is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because both inject distance-based geometric information into attention or compatibility calculations to improve cross-scale generalization. It is also relevant to the proposed ablation studies because it contains distance-based adaptation components and hyperparameters, such as K and C, that are conceptually analogous to the adaptation function and hyperparameter sensitivity questions in the ICAM plan, although DAR does not use a learnable alpha, scale factor N, or an attention-free module.
[results]
On CVRPLib, DAR achieved optimality gaps of 5.66 percent on 100 Set-X instances with 100 to 1000 nodes, 5.20 percent on 35 Set-XML instances with 100 to 5000 nodes, and 10.82 percent on 6 Set-XXL instances with 3000 to 11000 nodes. On a 32-instance Set-X subset with 500 to 1000 nodes, the gap was 5.27 percent. DAR outperformed greedy, POMO, Omni-VRP, ELG, and several decomposition baselines on the reported sets. Compared with greedy, DAR reduced gaps from 21.03 percent to 5.66 percent on Set-X, from 19.64 percent to 5.20 percent on Set-XML, and from 13.08 percent to 10.82 percent on Set-XXL. Compared with ELG, DAR improved from 5.92 percent to 5.66 percent on Set-X, from 5.95 percent to 5.20 percent on Set-XML, and from 16.08 percent to 10.82 percent on Set-XXL. On Set-XXL, DAR also outperformed TAM-AM, TAM-LKH3, GLOP, and GLOP-LKH3, whose gaps were 30.07 percent, 22.50 percent, 19.75 percent, and 18.80 percent respectively. DAR did not beat LKH3 or HGS on smaller sets, where LKH3 achieved 1.00 percent and HGS achieved 0.11 percent or 0.00 percent, but DAR was much faster, requiring 0.39 seconds versus at least 16 minutes for LKH3 and HGS on Set-X. No statistical significance tests were reported.
[context]
Training used randomly generated node coordinates and demands for small-scale instances, while testing used CVRPLib Set-X, expanded Set-XML, and Set-XXL instances. DAR assumes node coordinates are in a unit square, uses Euclidean distance, applies a logarithmic distance score, uses K to limit the effective neighborhood, and sets C to 50. The method adds no extra learnable parameters. Some baseline results were taken from original papers, while greedy, POMO, and ELG were run with the same training method and default settings. Hardware context included LKH3 and HGS running on an Intel Gold 6148 Skylake 2.4 GHz processor, while DAR used GPU parallel processing. The provided text does not report the specific ablations proposed in the research plan, such as AAFM versus standard multi-head attention, individual training stages, model depth effects, or sensitivity to a learnable alpha parameter.
[end related work 12]

