[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Xiao et al., 2024-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/21327634916cb1e5692857c372ed82cfccac0ec0)
- venue: arXiv.org
- citation count: 1
[method]
The paper conducts component-level effectiveness analyses, which are methodologically similar to the proposed ablation studies, for two generalization components: a size-scaling factor (ESF) and a distribution-specific decoder (DS decoder). ESF is a fixed log-size ratio, log(n_tr/n_te) at test time and log(n_b/n_tr) during training, applied to the attention or selection weights of existing neural VRP solvers such as AM, POMO, MDAM, LEHD, BQ, and OMNI-VRP. It does not change the base model architecture and is evaluated by comparing original models against the same models with ESF added. The DS decoder trains one decoder per distribution pattern with a shared encoder and evaluates cross-distribution generalization by comparing single-distribution POMO, AMDKD, OMNI-VRP, and the DS decoder approach. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because ESF is a simple scale-conditioned modification analogous to the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -alpha log2 N d_ij, and the paper isolates the contribution of scale-aware and distribution-aware components. However, the paper does not test an attention-free module, three-stage training, model depth, or a learnable alpha, so its evidence is only partially aligned with the proposed ablation scope.
[results]
For test-time ESF on TSP, the baseline models degrade when scaling up or down, while ESF gives an average relative improvement of 14.09% for upscaling and 7.32% for downscaling. For CVRP, ESF improves downscaling by 5.97% but reduces upscaling performance by 3.54%, indicating that the fixed scaling factor can be harmful for CVRP upscaling. On large-scale TSP using LEHD and BQ, ESF gives an average relative improvement of 10.76%. For training-time ESF with OMNI-VRP, TSP performance improves by 18.38% on in-size generalization and 13.8% on cross-size generalization, while CVRP improves by 4.51% on in-size and 4.86% on cross-size generalization. For the DS decoder, the average optimality gap improves by 35.44% over POMO and 22.31% over AMDKD. The proportion of cases where AMDKD performs worse than a single-distribution POMO model is 92%, whereas the DS decoder has deterioration in 16.7% of cases. The best DS decoder shows no deterioration relative to single-distribution POMO when solving instances from the corresponding distribution. The additional inference time is about 0.01 minute, and the paper estimates that training three teacher models for AMDKD on TSP-100 would take about 15 days. Robustness tests show minimal performance differences when the DS decoder is trained on Uniform-rectangle, Diagonal, or Gaussian distributions instead of Uniform, Cluster, or Uniform-Cluster mixed distributions.
[context]
Experiments use 10,000 randomly generated VRP instances per test condition. Size-generalization tests use TSP and CVRP instances of sizes 50, 100, and 200, with large-scale TSP tests at size 1000. Real-world benchmark tests use TSPLIB instances with 52 to 200 nodes and CVRPLIB instances with 32 to 101 nodes. Hardware is an Intel Xeon Gold 6254 CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. Distribution-generalization experiments use Uniform, Cluster, and Uniform-Cluster mixed distributions for training, and Implosion, Explosion, Expansion, plus the three training distributions, for testing. The DS decoder experiments follow AMDKD settings, use Adam with learning rate 1e-4, match training iterations, and use greedy rollout with 8 augmentations during testing. TSPLIB experiments are repeated 10 times with different random seeds and reported as mean with a 95% confidence interval. The paper does not provide ablations for AAFM versus standard MHA, three-stage training, encoder depth, or sensitivity to a learnable alpha, so its findings should be interpreted as evidence for fixed log-size scaling and distribution-specific decoding rather than direct validation of the full ICAM design.
[end related work 13]

Processing method: We will design a simple yet efficient adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -α·log₂N·d_ij that incorporates both scale information (N) and pairwise node distances (d_ij) with a single learnable parameter α. This function will provide instance-specific bias throughout the neural solution construction process while maintaining computational efficiency; this is the central mechanism by which ICAM injects scale- and geometry-aware information into the routing solver.
Processing method: We will replace traditional Multi-Head Attention (MHA) operations with a novel attention mechanism based on Attention Free Transformer (AFT-full). AAFM will explicitly incorporate the adaptation function into attention calculations, enabling direct capture of pairwise relationships between nodes while reducing computational complexity compared to standard attention mechanisms; this module operationalizes the adaptation function inside the encoder's attention computation.
Processing method: We will implement a progressive training approach: (1) Stage 1 - warm-up training on small-scale instances (100 nodes) for stability, (2) Stage 2 - varying-scale training on instances with scales randomly sampled from uniform distribution [100,500] to learn cross-scale features, and (3) Stage 3 - top-k elite training focusing on the best k trajectories with a joint loss function combining original POMO loss and a new top-k loss; this training scheme is intended to teach scale-independent features and refine solution quality before large-scale evaluation.
Processing method: We will develop ICAM using an encoder-decoder structure where the adaptation function is integrated into both the encoding phase (through AAFM) and the compatibility calculation in the decoder. The model will use a deeper 12-layer encoder to generate better node embeddings while maintaining efficiency through the attention-free design; this architecture combines the proposed adaptation function and AAFM into a complete constructive routing model.
Processing method: We will compare against classical solvers (Concorde, LKH3, HGS), constructive NCO methods (POMO, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer), and two-stage NCO approaches (MCTS-based methods, TAM, GLOP). Performance will be measured using optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference times; this comparison positions ICAM against both exact/heuristic solvers and neural combinatorial optimization baselines.
Processing method: We will evaluate ICAM on three routing problems: Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP), Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem (CVRP), and Asymmetric Traveling Salesman Problem (ATSP). Test instances will be generated following established protocols, with scales ranging from 100 to 5,000 nodes for TSP/CVRP and up to 1,000 nodes for ATSP; this defines the primary problem coverage and scale range used to test cross-scale generalization.
Processing method: We will assess performance using objective values, optimality gaps compared to optimal solutions from LKH3, and total inference times. We will report results for single trajectory, multiple trajectories, and instance augmentation scenarios to provide comprehensive performance analysis; these metrics quantify solution quality, optimality, and practical inference cost across different decoding settings.
Processing method: We will conduct systematic ablation studies to validate: (1) effectiveness of individual components in the adaptation function, (2) impact of AAFM versus standard MHA, (3) contribution of each training stage, (4) effects of different model depths, and (5) sensitivity to hyperparameter settings including the learnable parameter α; these ablations isolate the contribution of each proposed design choice to overall performance.
