- in_text_citation: [(Chen, and Tian, 2018-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f2b840c3c14b9f05106589c1be0a2dd4a494c0ba)
- venue: Neural Information Processing Systems
- citation count: 459
[method]
The paper studies a reinforcement-learning neural rewriter for optimization problems, including vehicle routing. The method starts from an initial feasible solution, computes a state-dependent set of local regions, selects a region using a region-picking policy, selects an applicable local rewriting rule using a rule-picking policy, and applies the rule to update the solution iteratively until convergence. The policies are trained with a reward-based Q-actor-critic objective. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because it is a learned vehicle-routing improvement method whose generalization behavior across instances is evaluated, although the supplied excerpt does not describe TSPLIB, CVRPLIB, rotation, or explosion instance distributions.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not provide quantitative outcomes, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance for the vehicle-routing generalization evaluation. It only indicates that the paper contains sections on scalability, robustness, and generalization, but no numerical results are available in the provided text.
[context]
Experiments were run on a server with 2 Quadro GP100 GPUs and 80 CPU cores. Neural network evaluation used 1 GPU, while search algorithms used 4 CPU cores and a 10-second per-instance timeout. The neural networks were implemented in PyTorch. The rewriter requires an initial feasible solution and operates through iterative local rewrites, so its evaluation depends on instance generation, initial solution quality, and the definition of local regions and rewriting rules.
[end related work 1]

