[method]
The paper evaluates a graph ConvNet-based constructive solver for 2D Euclidean TSP on uniformly generated instances in the unit square. Separate models are trained and evaluated for fixed problem sizes of 20, 50, and 100 nodes. Each model uses 30 graph convolutional layers, a 3-layer MLP with hidden dimension 300, a 20-nearest-neighbor adjacency graph, and a fixed beam width of 1,280. Training uses 1,000,000 instance-solution pairs per size, with validation and test sets of 10,000 pairs each. Optimal tours are obtained using Concorde. During evaluation, the predicted adjacency matrix is converted into a valid tour using search or sampling strategies, and performance is measured by average predicted tour length and optimality gap relative to the optimal solution. This relates to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing method because it represents an in-distribution TSP evaluation protocol on generated uniform instances; the proposed method would extend such evaluation by adding rotation and explosion distributions and benchmark datasets such as TSPLIB and CVRPLIB to test robustness beyond the primary generated distribution.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not report quantitative performance values, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance for the evaluation procedure. It only defines the evaluation setup and metrics, including average predicted tour length and optimality gap, but no numerical outcomes are available in the supplied text.
[context]
Key experimental conditions include 2D Euclidean TSP instances with node locations sampled uniformly at random in the unit square, problem sizes of 20, 50, and 100 nodes, 1,000,000 training pairs per size, and 10,000 validation and test pairs per size. Concorde is used to obtain optimal tours. Hyperparameters are identical across sizes: 30 graph convolutional layers, 3 MLP layers, hidden dimension 300, beam width 1,280, and 20 nearest neighbors for the adjacency matrix. Training uses Adam with an initial learning rate of 0.001, mini-batches of 20 instances, and 10,000 randomly selected training instances per epoch. Evaluation uses arbitrarily large batch sizes that fit GPU memory. The excerpt does not include rotation or explosion distributions, TSPLIB, CVRPLIB, or large-scale instances beyond 100 nodes.
