- in_text_citation: [(Joshi et al., 2020-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7d45afc1f12cba92a66da4e83a96380ac0a15997)
- venue: Constraints
- citation count: 202
[method]
The paper uses a controlled ablation study of a unified TSP pipeline to test zero-shot generalization. Identical model configurations are trained on small generated TSP instances, specifically TSP20-50, using either supervised learning with teacher forcing from an optimal solver or reinforcement learning with a rollout baseline. The trained models are then evaluated on smaller and larger TSP instances, including TSP200, under three inference/search settings: greedy decoding, beam search, and sampling. A non-learned furthest insertion heuristic is used as a baseline, constructing a partial tour by repeatedly inserting the node whose nearest current tour node is farthest. The method is relevant to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing because it provides a generalization-evaluation protocol for constructive neural routing solvers beyond the training scale, although it tests size generalization rather than rotation, explosion, TSPLIB, or CVRPLIB distributions.
[results]
The paper reports qualitative outcomes only in the supplied excerpt. Under greedy decoding, reinforcement-learning-trained models outperform supervised-learning-trained models on the training size and on larger graphs. Under beam search or sampling, supervised-learning-trained models outperform their reinforcement-learning counterparts. The reinforcement-learning rollout baseline produces very confident next-node predictions even outside the training size range, while supervised teacher forcing produces less confident predictions but probability distributions that are more amenable to beam search and sampling. No numerical optimality gaps, solution-quality values, inference times, or statistical significance tests are provided in the excerpt.
[context]
Experimental conditions include a base model configuration with one pipeline component ablated per experiment, fair comparison intended in model capacity and training data, training on small TSP20-50 instances, evaluation across a wider size range including TSP200, and comparison against a simple furthest insertion heuristic. The inference phase is varied across greedy decoding, beam search, and sampling. Hardware, exact model size, training steps, beam width, number of samples, and statistical details are not specified in the provided text. This context is useful for ICAM because it highlights the need to align training and inference/search when evaluating cross-scale or cross-distribution generalization, but it does not directly validate ICAM's rotation/explosion or benchmark-dataset robustness claims.
[end related work 3]

