[method]
The paper uses a controlled CVRPLIB benchmarking protocol to compare the Swap*-based HGS-CVRP variant against HGS-2012 and six other CVRP heuristics: HILS, LKH-3, KGLS, SISR, FILO, and OR-Tools GLS. It runs each algorithm on 100 classical CVRPLIB instances with 100 to 1000 customers, records the best objective value at 1, 2, 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 50, 75, and 100 percent of the time limit, performs ten independent runs, and computes the percentage gap to the best known solution. This method is related to the proposed research method and plan only in that it evaluates HGS, a planned baseline, on CVRPLIB benchmark instances; it does not evaluate ICAM, rotation or explosion distributions, TSPLIB, or neural cross-scale generalization.
[results]
In the displayed portion of Table 2 covering 18 fully listed instances, HGS-CVRP had an average gap of 0.005 percent and reached the best known solution on 16 instances, with gaps of 0.07 percent on X-n106-k14 and 0.02 percent on X-n153-k22. HGS-2012 averaged 0.011 percent gap, FILO 0.069 percent, SISR 0.048 percent, HILS 0.207 percent, KGLS 0.193 percent, LKH-3 0.309 percent, and OR-Tools 1.934 percent. HGS-CVRP was therefore among the best methods in the excerpt, often tied with HGS-2012, FILO, or HILS at the best known solution, and clearly ahead of OR-Tools and LKH-3 on the listed instances.
[context]
Experiments used a single thread of an Intel Gold 6148 Skylake 2.4 GHz processor with 40 GB RAM on CentOS 7.8.2003. C/C++ implementations were compiled with g++ 9.1.0 and Java implementations used OpenJDK 13.0.1. The time limit was T_max = n x 240/100 seconds, giving 4 minutes for 100 customers and 40 minutes for 1000 customers. Ten runs were used; deterministic KGLS and OR-Tools were varied by random customer permutations. Gaps were computed against best known solution values from CVRPLIB. The paper reports averages and gaps but does not report statistical significance tests in the excerpt.
