[method]
The paper evaluates a constructive neural routing model by training it on 1 million expert solutions for synthetic size-100 instances and then testing it on synthetic instances of 100, 200, 500, and 1,000 nodes drawn from the same distribution, as well as on standard benchmark datasets: TSPLib instances up to 4,461 nodes and Euclidean CVRPLib instances. For CVRP, the synthetic test sets use capacities of 50, 80, 100, and 250 for the four graph sizes. The model is a 9-layer attention-based architecture with 12 attention heads, embedding size 192, and feed-forward dimension 512. It is trained by imitation learning from expert trajectories using a cross-entropy loss, with batch size 1024, 500 epochs, Adam optimizer, initial learning rate 7.5e-4, and learning-rate decay of 0.98 every 50 epochs. Training trajectories are generated by sampling subpaths of length between 4 and N from expert tours, which provides automatic data augmentation. At test time, except for Perceiver-based variants, the model limits the subgraph to the 250 nearest neighbors of the origin node to reduce inference time. The evaluation compares the model against classical solvers, hybrid methods, and deep constructive baselines, and reports optimality gap and inference time. This relates to the proposed ICAM research method because it uses multi-scale synthetic tests and standard benchmark datasets to assess generalization and robustness, but it does not explicitly test distribution shifts such as rotation or explosion, which are central to the proposed cross-distribution evaluation.
[results]
The provided section reports relative and qualitative performance outcomes rather than exact numerical optimality gaps. The model is reported to show superior generalization to larger graphs even with greedy decoding, which generates a single solution, whereas many baselines generate several hundred solutions and select the best. In terms of runtime, the model is slightly slower than Sym-NCO, competitive with POMO, and significantly faster than other deep baselines. Beam search improves optimality gaps but increases runtime. Replacing quadratic attention with a PerceiverIO architecture reduces inference time for larger graphs at the cost of some performance drop, while still remaining competitive with other neural combinatorial optimization baselines. On TSPLib and CVRPLib, the model is reported to significantly outperform end-to-end baseline policies even with greedy decoding. The only methods described as competitive are Att-GCN+MCTS and DIFUSCO+2opt, but both are hybrid methods, are designed for TSP only, and are reported to be 2 to 15 times slower. These results support the proposed research plan’s goal of demonstrating cross-scale and benchmark robustness, but they do not provide direct evidence for robustness under rotation or explosion distribution shifts.
[context]
The experimental conditions include training on synthetic size-100 instances with 1 million expert solutions, testing on synthetic sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1,000 from the same distribution, and additional testing on TSPLib and CVRPLib. Expert solutions are obtained using Concorde for TSP, LKH for ATSP and CVRP, and EA4OP for OP. For TSP, optimality gap is computed against Concorde solutions; for CVRP, LKH solutions are used as the reference for comparability with prior work. Deep learning models are run on a single Nvidia Tesla V100-S GPU with 24GB memory, while classical solvers are run on an Intel Xeon E5-2670 CPU with 256GB memory in one thread. These conditions are relevant to the proposed ICAM evaluation because they define the benchmark datasets, scale range, reference-solution protocol, and hardware setup against which large-scale generalization claims can be compared. However, the paper’s synthetic test distribution is the same as the training distribution, so the proposed method’s additional rotation and explosion tests would extend the robustness evaluation beyond what is reported in the paper.
