[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Gao et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d708a031c4699220dd3802bbd2f1933efd90204f)
- venue: International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence
- citation count: 99
[method]
The paper evaluates an ensemble neural construction method, ELG-POMO, for TSP and CVRP. It combines a global POMO policy that encodes the full instance with a Transformer encoder-decoder and an auxiliary transferable local policy that constructs solutions using local neighborhood states and actions. The global policy uses masked attention scores to select valid next nodes, and the local policy is trained in alignment with POMO so that the comparison between ELG-POMO and POMO isolates the effect of the local policy. For the cross-distribution evaluation relevant to the proposed ICAM plan, ELG-POMO is trained only on uniform N=100 instances and then tested in greedy mode on TSPLIB and CVRPLIB subsets, CVRPLIB Set-XXL real-world instances, and synthetic small-scale instances from rotation, explosion, cluster, and cluster-mixture distributions. The evaluation metrics are average optimality gap to best known solutions and average runtime per instance.
[results]
On TSPLIB and CVRPLIB instances with N<=200, ELG-POMO outperforms all compared learning methods, reducing the gap by 0.53% on CVRP and 0.50% on TSP relative to the runner-up. On the cross-distribution and cross-scale subset with 200<N<=1000 or 200<N<=1002, it reduces the CVRP gap from 6.95% for Omni-POMO to 6.46%, and reduces the TSP gap from 7.47% to 5.90%, although it still lags behind BQ and LEHD on that TSP subset. On four CVRPLIB Set-XXL real-world instances with N=3000-7000, ELG-POMO is best on three instances and substantially improves gaps over POMO-based methods. On synthetic TSP distributions, the retrained ELG* model beats AMDKD on three of four N=100 distributions and all four N=200 distributions, with N=200 gaps of 1.84% for cluster, 1.62% for mixture, 1.43% for explosion, and 1.58% for rotation, compared with AMDKD gaps of 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54%, respectively. On uniform N=100 in-distribution tests, ELG-POMO achieves a 0.22% gap on TSP100 and a 1.22% gap on CVRP100, which is competitive with strong learning methods and better than POMO, Sym-POMO, LEHD, and BQ, though still worse than HGS on CVRP100. Runtime is lower than BQ and diffusion-based methods, higher than pure POMO because of the added local policy, and much lower than LKH3 in the reported 1.90s versus 16m comparison, although optimality gaps remain worse than non-learning solvers.
[context]
Training uses uniform node distributions at N=100, matching common POMO settings, and POMO-based baselines are early-stopped using larger-scale validation to reduce overfitting. Inference is greedy, and POMO-based methods use x8 instance augmentation. Test conditions include TSPLIB and CVRPLIB subsets split by scale, CVRPLIB Set-XXL instances with N=3000-7000, and synthetic TSP and CVRP instances at N=100 and N=200 from rotation, explosion, cluster, and cluster-mixture distributions. The synthetic comparison with AMDKD uses retrained POMO* and ELG* models aligned to AMDKD training distributions, namely uniform, cluster, and cluster mixture. This setup directly parallels the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing on rotation and explosion instances plus TSPLIB and CVRPLIB benchmarks, providing a related baseline for assessing whether cross-scale gains persist under out-of-distribution and real-world routing instances.
[end related work 8]

