[results]
On the real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib evaluation, the paper states that LEHD has good greedy inference performance and, with 1000 Random Re-Construct iterations, significantly outperforms POMO and BQ on all instances, demonstrating robust generalization. The provided excerpt does not include the exact Table 2 gap values. In the provided large-scale TSP comparison tables using 128 instances, LEHD greedy achieves gaps of 0.859% on TSP200, 1.560% on TSP500, and 3.168% on TSP1000, with runtimes of 3 seconds, 0.3 minutes, and 1.6 minutes. With 100 Random Re-Construct iterations, LEHD improves to 0.0761% on TSP200, 0.343% on TSP500, and 1.218% on TSP1000, with runtimes of 1.2 minutes, 8 minutes, and 43 minutes. These results are better than SO-mixed, which reports 0.636%, 2.401%, and 2.800% gaps, and better than H-TSP with LKH-3, which reports 4.06% on TSP1000, while Concorde remains optimal at 0.000% but with much longer runtimes of 3 minutes, 32 minutes, and 7.8 hours. On uniformly generated instances, the paper reports that with more than 500 Random Re-Construct iterations LEHD is nearly optimal on TSP100 and TSP200, within 0.2% on TSP500, and around 0.8% on TSP1000. For CVRP, LEHD outperforms most learning-based methods and outperforms LKH3 on CVRP200 and CVRP500 with 500 Random Re-Construct iterations, reaching about a 1% gap to LKH3 on CVRP1000 with 1000 iterations.
[context]
The relevant experimental conditions include real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances with different sizes and distributions, as well as uniformly distributed generated TSP and CVRP instances. The benchmark evaluation uses greedy inference and 1000 Random Re-Construct iterations for LEHD, while other budgets of 100, 300, 500, and 1000 iterations are used in the broader experiments. The TSP comparison tables use 128 instances. The provided excerpt does not specify hardware, exact instance lists, or statistical significance tests. This paper relates to the proposed method because it provides a comparable cross-distribution and benchmark-dataset validation protocol for a constructive neural combinatorial optimization model, whereas the ICAM research plan proposes to use rotation and explosion distributions together with TSPLIB and CVRPLIB to test whether instance-conditioned adaptation yields cross-scale gains beyond the primary generated instance distributions.
