The paper contains a cross-distribution and benchmark evaluation of GLOP that is directly related to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing. GLOP uses a local TSP solver that creates an initial tour with Random Insertion, divides the tour into independent subtours, and applies a learned reviser to reconstruct those subtours. For general routing problems, GLOP adds a global policy that outputs a partition heatmap to split instances into sub-TSPs. The local reviser is trained with REINFORCE using the average length of two greedy rollouts as a baseline, and the global policy is trained with REINFORCE using the average reward of sampled partitions for the same instance as a baseline. The cross-distribution evaluation tests GLOP on non-training distribution instances, 49 symmetric TSPLIB EUC2D instances with fewer than 1000 nodes after min-max normalization to the unit square, and several large-scale CVRPLIB instances. The evaluation compares objective value and runtime against state-of-the-art baselines with adjustable runtime.
