[method]
The paper's related method is a cross-scale and cross-distribution evaluation protocol for a neural constructive routing solver called INViT. It trains a REINFORCE-based encoder-decoder with data augmentation, including rotation, reflection, normalization, and varied starting nodes, and maintains a separate baseline model. During training, the baseline tour is produced by greedy rollout and the training tour by random sampling; the loss uses the performance difference between the two. At test time, augmented instances are evaluated and the best augmented solution is kept, and the baseline parameters are replaced when the trained model outperforms the baseline. For the part related to the proposed method, the paper evaluates generated MSVDRP TSP and CVRP instances across multiple scales and distribution families, and public benchmarks: 77 symmetric TSPLIB instances with 51 to 18512 nodes and 100 CVRPLIB Set-X instances with 100 to 1000 nodes. It compares against representative constructive baselines and reports average gap to near-optimal solutions from Gurobi, LKH3, HGS, or known optima, along with inference time. This is directly related to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing because both use distribution-shift and public benchmark evaluation to check whether large-scale gains persist beyond the primary generated instance distributions, although the paper uses rotation/reflection/normalization augmentation and non-uniform/clustered distributions rather than explicitly naming explosion distributions.
[results]
INViT-3V achieved the best reported large-scale gaps: 7.97% on TSP-5000, 7.52% on TSP-10000, and 5.63% on CVRP-5000. Most comparison methods had average gaps above 10% on these large-scale datasets, except BQ-NCO on CVRP-5000. When moving from uniform to other distributions, INViT-3V had relative gap increases of 31% on TSP-5000 and 33% on TSP-10000, and 8% on CVRP-5000; Omni-TSP/VRP had smaller relative increases of 9%, 8%, and -40%, but INViT was trained only on uniform distributions and still outperformed Omni-TSP/VRP on all large-scale datasets. INViT-2V showed similar generalization with lower inference time, and INViT-3V gave slight improvements. On small and medium scales, such as TSP-100, TSP-1000, and CVRP-500, INViT did not always outperform baselines; the differences were subtle, with a relative gap of 10% to the best baseline on TSP-1000 and 43% on CVRP-500. On public TSPLIB and CVRPLIB Set-X instances, the paper reports that strong generalization still held and performance was comparable to baselines on small and medium instances, which was better than its relative performance on the generated MSVDRP datasets. No statistical significance tests are reported.
