Processing method: We will evaluate generalization capability on instances with different distributions (rotation, explosion) and benchmark datasets (TSPLIB, CVRPLIB) to demonstrate robustness across problem variants; this tests whether ICAM's cross-scale gains hold beyond the primary generated instance distributions.
Error processing ('We will design a simple yet efficient adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -α·log₂N·d_ij that incorporates both scale information (N) and pairwise node distances (d_ij) with a single learnable parameter α. This function will provide instance-specific bias throughout the neural solution construction process while maintaining computational efficiency; this is the central mechanism by which ICAM injects scale- and geometry-aware information into the routing solver.', [{'corpus_id': '261243129', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates ELG-POMO, an ensemble of a global POMO policy and an auxiliary transferrable local policy for TSP and CVRP. The global policy is a Transformer encoder-decoder without positional embedding that encodes the full instance and decodes a tour by pointer attention over candidate nodes, with invalid actions masked and rewards equal to negative tour length. The local policy uses a different state and action space centered on the local neighborhood of the current node, and its construction process is similar to the global policy. Training is aligned with POMO on uniformly distributed instances with N=100, uses greedy inference, and POMO-based methods use x8 instance augmentation. Experiments compare ELG-POMO against heuristic solvers, POMO variants, cross-distribution, cross-scale, and diffusion-based methods on TSPLIB, CVRPLIB Set-X and Set-XXL, synthetic distributions, and uniform N=100 instances. This paper method is related to the proposed ICAM adaptation function because both target the same limitation of constructive neural routing solvers: poor cross-scale and cross-distribution generalization. ELG improves generalization by adding a local policy to a global POMO backbone, whereas the proposed ICAM method would inject an explicit scale- and distance-dependent bias f(N,d_ij) into attention and decoder compatibility; ELG is also listed as a baseline in the ICAM research plan.', 'results': 'On the N<=200 TSPLIB/CVRPLIB subsets, ELG-POMO outperforms all compared methods, with gap reductions of 0.53% on CVRP and 0.50% on TSP relative to the runner-up. On the 200<N<=1000 or 200<N<=1002 subsets, ELG-POMO reduces the CVRP gap from 6.95% for Omni-POMO to 6.46%, and reduces the TSP gap from 7.47% to 5.90%. On four real-world CVRPLIB Set-XXL instances with N between 3000 and 7000, ELG-POMO is best on three instances and substantially reduces gaps versus other POMO-based methods. On uniform N=100 instances, ELG-POMO obtains 0.22% gap on TSP100 and 1.22% on CVRP100, surpassing all compared methods on CVRP100 and remaining competitive on TSP100. On difficult synthetic TSP distributions, ELG* outperforms AMDKD on three of four N=100 distributions and all four N=200 distributions, with N=200 gaps of 1.84%, 1.62%, 1.43%, and 1.58% versus AMDKD gaps of 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54% for cluster, mixture, explosion, and rotation. Runtime is lower than BQ and diffusion-based methods, higher than pure POMO due to the local policy, and reported as 1.90s per instance compared with 16m for LKH3/HGS in one comparison, although heuristic solvers achieve better optimality gaps.', 'context': 'Training instances are uniformly distributed with N=100, matching the common POMO setting, while Omni-POMO uses a stronger cross-distribution and cross-scale training protocol. Evaluation uses average gap to best known solutions and average solving time. Test conditions include TSPLIB and CVRPLIB Set-X subsets with N<=200 and 200<N<=1000 or 1002, CVRPLIB Set-XXL real-world instances with N in [3000,7000], small-scale synthetic distributions with N=100 and N=200, and uniform N=100 in-distribution tests. Inference is greedy, POMO-based methods use x8 instance augmentation, and best known solutions are from Concorde or LKH3 for uniform instances and from benchmark BKS for TSPLIB/CVRPLIB. The provided sections do not specify hardware or detailed local-policy hyperparameters, but the method is reproducible from the stated POMO-aligned training and public code/data. This context matters for ICAM because the proposed adaptation function is intended to improve the same large-scale generalization regime where ELG already shows gains but still trails optimized heuristic solvers and some cross-scale methods on large TSP instances.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5657, 'output_tokens': 4312}, {'corpus_id': '281440787', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a neural vehicle-routing solver called DGL, which dynamically aggregates local and global information to construct solutions for TSP and CVRP instances of varying scales and distributions. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because both approaches target the same core problem: improving cross-scale and cross-distribution generalization of constructive neural routing solvers. However, the paper’s method differs from the proposed ICAM adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -α·log₂N·d_ij: DGL relies on dynamic local/global feature aggregation rather than an explicit scale- and pairwise-distance-conditioned bias, and it uses a standard attention-based decoder rather than the proposed attention-free adaptation module. The paper also uses a replacement-based self-improvement learning (SIL) training method, which is related to the proposed research plan’s focus on training schemes that improve robustness and generalization. In this SIL method, training is initialized by randomly generating B VRP instances from a uniform distribution, producing initial pseudo-labels with a nearest-neighbor heuristic to warm up the model, and then using data augmentation together with random removal of portions of the generated dataset during training. The model is trained as a single model on 100-node uniform instances and then evaluated directly on larger and differently distributed instances, which mirrors the proposed ICAM evaluation of cross-scale generalization.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than numerical optimality gaps. DGL is reported to achieve state-of-the-art performance compared with global-view baselines such as Omni-VRP, LEHD, and BQ on synthetic TSP instances. It is also reported to outperform IN-ViT in both solution quality and inference speed, and to improve solution quality over another SIL-based model, GD, when using replacement-based SIL. On real-world TSPLIB, CVRPLIB, and country-map instances, DGL is reported to consistently demonstrate superior generalization performance compared with baselines. The paper also notes that most models experience performance degradation when generalizing to varying scales and distributions, that UDC fails to solve TSPs under 100 nodes and CVRPs under 200 nodes due to unknown errors, and that some baselines are marked as out-of-memory on real-world sets. No specific numerical gap values, absolute objective values, or statistical significance tests are included in the provided excerpt.', 'context': 'Experiments were conducted on synthetic and real-world routing benchmarks. Synthetic data included the MSVDRP dataset with 16 TSP subsets across uniform, clustered, explosion, and implosion distributions at TSP-100, TSP-1000, TSP-5000, and TSP-10000, and 12 CVRP subsets at CVRP-50, CVRP-500, and CVRP-5000. Real-world data included TSPLIB95 with 77 instances ranging from 51 to 18,512 nodes, CVRPLIB Set-X with 100 instances ranging from 100 to 1,000 nodes, and eight country maps with 194 to 22,775 nodes. DGL had 4 decoder layers, hidden dimension 128, and 8 attention heads. Training used a learning rate of 1e-4 with decay rate 0.97, 100 epochs, 100 iterations per epoch, batch size 256, 64 samples per iteration, and a replacement rate of 12.5%. The hyperparameter k was set to 30 for TSP and 50 for CVRP. Baselines used publicly available pretrained parameters and default settings; LEHD and BQ were evaluated with greedy search, and DGL and IN-ViT used the same inference setup. Experiments ran on an Intel Xeon Platinum 8352V CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU with 24GB memory. Performance was measured as the average gap to LKH results for synthetic data and to best known solutions for real-world data.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3052, 'output_tokens': 3754}, {'corpus_id': '258212484', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1676, 'output_tokens': 327}, {'corpus_id': '278768989', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '266999156', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper introduces DAR, a distance-aware attention reshaping method for neural CVRP solvers. It computes standard dot-product attention scores from the current state query and encoder node features, then computes Euclidean distances from the current node to each candidate node. A logarithmic distance transform is applied, a hyperparameter K defines a local neighborhood, nodes inside the neighborhood receive positive distance scores and nodes outside receive negative scores, and the distance score is added to the attention score before normalization with a constant C=50 to produce the next-node probability. The method has no learned parameters and is designed to reduce attention dispersion when a model trained on small instances is applied to larger instances by prioritizing nearby nodes and suppressing distant high-attention nodes. This is closely related to the proposed ICAM adaptation function because both inject distance-based bias into the construction process, but DAR uses only current-to-candidate distance with fixed hyperparameters, whereas ICAM proposes a learnable, instance-scale-conditioned bias f(N,d_ij) = -alpha log2 N d_ij integrated into an attention-free encoder and decoder compatibility.', 'results': 'On CVRPLib, DAR achieved gaps of 5.66% on 100 Set-X instances with 100 to 1000 nodes, 5.20% on 35 Set-XML instances with 100 to 5000 nodes, and 10.82% on 6 Set-XXL instances with 3000 to 11000 nodes; on a 32-instance Set-X subset it reported 5.27%. It outperformed greedy, which had gaps of 21.03%, 19.64%, and 13.08%, POMO at 8.31%, 10.51%, and 30.85%, ELG at 5.92%, 5.95%, and 16.08%, and Omni-VRP at 6.47% and 10.60% with no Set-XXL value. On Set-XXL, DAR was better than TAM-AM at 30.07% and TAM-LKH3 at 22.50%, while GLOP variants were reported with negative gaps of -19.75% and -18.80%, making that part of the comparison ambiguous unless the BKS reference or sign convention is clarified. DAR did not beat LKH3 or HGS on Set-X and Set-XML, where LKH3 and HGS gaps were 1.00% and 0.11% or 0.00%, respectively, but it was much faster, taking 0.39 seconds on Set-X versus at least 16 minutes for LKH3 and HGS on the stated CPU.', 'context': 'The experiments focused on CVRP generalization from small to large scale. Training used randomly generated coordinates and demands, while testing used real CVRPLib instances: 100 Set-X instances, 35 Set-XML instances, and 6 Set-XXL instances spanning 100 to 11000 nodes. The distance module assumed coordinates in [0,1]x[0,1], used Euclidean distance, a logarithmic distance transform, a neighborhood-limit hyperparameter K, and a normalization constant C=50. Some baselines were run with the same training method and default settings when source code was available, while other baseline numbers were taken from original reports. The paper reports optimality gaps relative to best known solutions and inference time, and notes that DAR can use GPU parallelism, whereas classical solvers become slow on instances with more than 10000 nodes. Relative to the proposed research plan, this paper provides a CVRP-only, standard-attention, fixed-distance-bias comparison point and does not test TSP or ATSP, a learnable alpha, explicit scale conditioning by N, an attention-free module, or the three-stage training scheme.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4760, 'output_tokens': 6243}, {'corpus_id': '279119999', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper's related method is Test-Time Projection Learning, abbreviated TTPL, which uses test-time designed projection strategies for large-scale TSP and CVRP instances and applies them to a constructive LEHD neural routing model. The reported pipeline includes greedy construction or greedy sampling, an optional Multi-View Decision Fusion component, and an optional RRC refinement stage with 1000 iterations. The method is evaluated on synthetic TSP and CVRP instances at 1K, 5K, 10K, 50K, and 100K nodes, as well as on TSPLIB and CVRPLIB benchmark groups. This method relates to the proposed ICAM adaptation function because both aim to improve cross-scale generalization of constructive neural routing solvers by injecting instance-specific, scale- and geometry-aware information into the solution construction process. The key difference is that the paper uses designed projections and multi-view decision fusion, whereas the proposed ICAM method uses a learnable scalar adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -α·log₂N·d_ij integrated into an attention-free encoder-decoder architecture.", 'results': 'On synthetic datasets, TTPL with purely greedy construction outperforms other greedy constructive methods on most scales except TSP1K, and TTPL with purely greedy sampling is best on all CVRP scales except CVRP1K. With RRC for 1000 iterations, TTPL achieves the best performance on all TSP scales, reducing optimality gap by 61.11%, 75.59%, 56.20%, 69.61%, and 68.29% for TSP1K, TSP5K, TSP10K, TSP50K, and TSP100K relative to the leading alternative method. For CVRP, TTPL with RRC reduces optimality gap by 53.98%, 85.99%, 86.20%, and 88.37% on CVRP5K, CVRP10K, CVRP50K, and CVRP100K relative to the strongest competing baseline. On TSPLIB, TTPL greedy obtains 6.04% gap for 1K < n ≤ 5K, 4.57% for n > 5K, and 5.80% overall, solving 33/33 instances; TTPL-MVDF obtains 3.81%, 4.05%, and 3.88%; TTPL-MVDF with RRC1000 obtains 1.04%, 2.12%, and 1.37%, solving 33/33 instances. On CVRPLIB, TTPL greedy obtains 11.89% for 1K < n ≤ 7K, 14.93% for n > 7K, and 13.19% overall, solving 14/14 instances; TTPL-MVDF obtains 10.41%, 11.71%, and 10.97%; TTPL-MVDF with RRC1000 obtains 5.20%, 10.56%, and 7.50%, solving 14/14 instances. These results are better than baselines including GLOP, ELG, BQ, LEHD, SIGD, INViT, and LEHD with RRC, and several baselines fail on larger groups due to GPU memory limits. The provided sections do not report statistical significance tests.', 'context': 'Experiments were conducted on a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPU with 24GB of memory. The evaluation setup uses optimality gap and solved-instance counts as primary metrics, and compares greedy construction, MVDF, and RRC1000 variants. Synthetic instance scales range from 1K to 100K nodes for TSP and CVRP, while benchmark groups are TSPLIB 1K < n ≤ 5K and n > 5K, and CVRPLIB 1K < n ≤ 7K and n > 7K. The context is relevant to the proposed research plan because it demonstrates that instance-conditioned projection or adaptation can substantially improve large-scale routing generalization, but it uses a different mechanism from ICAM: the paper relies on test-time projection design and multi-view fusion rather than a learnable scale-distance bias inside an attention-free transformer-style encoder-decoder.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4560, 'output_tokens': 5459}, {'corpus_id': '259075472', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies a scale-conditioned transfer adaptation method for constructive DRL solvers, called Meta-SAGE with a scale-conditioned initial embedding. It starts from pretrained encoder-decoder policies such as POMO and Sym-NCO that were trained on N=100 instances. A learned module g_phi(h,N) transforms the original context embedding h into a scale-conditioned initial embedding h_S before test-time adaptation. The module is trained with a distillation objective that encourages h_S to approximate the embedding obtained after many SAGE adaptation steps, and with a zero-shot simulation objective that encourages h_S to already produce good solutions before adaptation. At inference, SAGE performs instance-wise parameter adaptation for a fixed number of steps, and the scale-conditioned embedding is used as the initial state. This is related to the proposed ICAM adaptation function f(N,d_ij) = -alpha log2 N d_ij because both inject problem-scale information into the construction process of a pretrained neural routing solver to improve cross-scale generalization, but the paper uses a learned embedding transformation and meta-adaptation rather than an explicit analytic pairwise-distance bias or an attention-free module.', 'results': 'On TSP, CVRP, PCTSP, and OP, Meta-SAGE generally improved objective gaps over active search, efficient active search, and simulation-guided beam search with EAS. For TSP N=500, POMO-based Meta-SAGE reached 17.131 with a 3.559% gap versus EAS 18.135 with a 9.362% gap and SGBS 18.191 with a 9.963% gap; Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE reached 17.095 with a 3.339% gap. For TSP N=1000, POMO-based Meta-SAGE reached 25.924 with a 12.038% gap versus EAS 30.744 with a 32.869% gap; Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE reached 25.798 with an 11.488% gap versus EAS 31.241 with a 35.017% gap. On CVRP N=200, POMO-based Meta-SAGE reached 22.001 with a -0.009% gap, slightly better than LKH3 22.003, while Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE reached 21.982 with a -0.094% gap; however, active search was better for Sym-NCO on CVRP N=200, reaching 21.955 with a -0.218% gap. On CVRP N=500, Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE reached 63.281 with a -0.028% gap, better than LKH3 63.299. On PCTSP, Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE outperformed OR-Tools at all scales, with gaps of -8.236% at N=200, -8.412% at N=500, and -31.31% at N=1000. On OP N=500, Meta-SAGE reached 98.762 with a -1.457% gap, better than Compass 97.323. The paper also reports that zero-shot costs before adaptation are lower than EAS, indicating that the scale-conditioned initial embedding improves transferability. The excerpt does not report confidence intervals or statistical significance tests.', 'context': 'Experiments used pretrained POMO and Sym-NCO models trained on N=100 instances and evaluated on larger instances: N=200 with 1,000 instances, N=500 with 128 instances, and N=1,000 with 128 instances. Baselines included active search, efficient active search, and simulation-guided beam search with EAS. Reference solvers were Concorde for TSP, LKH3 for CVRP, OR-Tools for PCTSP, and Compass for OP. Runtime was measured for solving whole instances on a single NVIDIA A100 40GB GPU and an Intel Scalable Gold CPU. EAS and SGBS were stopped when their runtime exceeded the runtime of Meta-SAGE with 200 adaptation iterations, while active search used 200 iterations per instance. The method is relevant to the proposed research plan because it tests the same core assumption that scale-aware conditioning can help neural routing solvers generalize from small training scales to larger test scales, but it does not evaluate the proposed explicit f(N,d_ij) bias, AAFM, or three-stage training scheme.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 6618, 'output_tokens': 5902}, {'corpus_id': '263909317', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies LEHD, a constructive neural combinatorial optimization method for TSP and CVRP that uses a light encoder and a heavy decoder. The model is trained with supervised learning to construct partial solutions during training and complete tours or routes during inference, and it then applies Random Re-Construct, or RRC, iterations to improve the generated solution. This paper method is related to the proposed ICAM method and research plan because both target large-scale TSP/CVRP generalization and fast constructive inference. LEHD provides a decoder-centric constructive baseline, whereas the proposed ICAM method would additionally inject instance-specific scale and geometry information through the adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -α·log₂N·d_ij and an attention-free adaptation attention module.', 'results': 'On uniformly distributed TSP instances, LEHD has fast greedy inference and, with 100 RRC iterations, outperforms other learning-based methods with reasonable inference time. With more than 500 RRC iterations, the optimality gap is nearly optimal for TSP100 and TSP200, less than 0.2% for TSP500, and about 0.8% for TSP1000. On uniformly distributed CVRP instances, LEHD with 300 RRC iterations outperforms most learning-based methods on all instances except EAS on CVRP100, where EAS has better performance but much longer inference time. With 500 RRC iterations, LEHD outperforms LKH3 on CVRP100 through CVRP500, and with 1000 RRC iterations it reaches about a 1% gap to LKH3 on CVRP1000. On real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances, LEHD with greedy inference and 1000 RRC iterations outperforms POMO and BQ on all reported instances. In comparisons with search-based or improvement-based methods on 128 TSP instances, LEHD RRC100 achieves 0.0761% gap in 1.2 minutes on TSP200, 0.343% in 8 minutes on TSP500, and 1.218% in 43 minutes on TSP1000. This beats SO-mixed, which reports 0.636% in 21 minutes on TSP200, 2.401% in 32 minutes on TSP500, and 2.800% in 56 minutes on TSP1000, and also beats H-TSP with LKH-3, which reports 4.06% in 7.5 minutes on TSP1000, while Concorde achieves 0.000% but requires 3 minutes, 32 minutes, and 7.8 hours for TSP200, TSP500, and TSP1000 respectively. These outcomes are relevant to the ICAM research plan because they show that constructive learning plus post-construction improvement can approach strong solvers on large TSP/CVRP instances, but the paper does not evaluate the proposed scale-aware adaptation function or attention-free adaptation attention module.', 'context': 'The experiments use uniformly distributed TSP and CVRP instances with sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, as well as real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances with different sizes and distributions. Evaluation is based on optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference time, with greedy inference and RRC budgets of 100, 300, 500, or 1000 iterations. The comparison with SO-mixed and H-TSP uses 128 TSP instances. The paper also compares heavy decoder versus heavy encoder structures and supervised versus reinforcement training: POMO trained with supervised learning performs well on TSP100 but poorly on large-scale problems, supporting the paper’s claim that generalization comes from the heavy decoder structure; reinforcement learning with the POMO strategy does not converge in a reasonable time, while supervised learning is more suitable for training LEHD on TSP50 under the same computational budget. Hardware and full hyperparameter details are not specified in the provided excerpt. These conditions are directly relevant to the ICAM plan, which also emphasizes cross-scale generalization, TSP/CVRP evaluation, inference time, and architecture modifications for large-scale routing, but ICAM would add explicit instance-conditioned scale and pairwise-distance bias to the construction process rather than relying only on heavy-decoder construction and RRC.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3245, 'output_tokens': 4881}, {'corpus_id': '259240292', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '253111406', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '269214387', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses an encoder-decoder Transformer attention model as a constructive heuristic for vehicle routing problems. Solution construction is formulated as a Markov decision process in which the state contains the instance and the current partial solution, the action candidates are admissible nodes, and the Transformer outputs a probability distribution over candidate nodes. A node is then selected greedily or by sampling, and the full tour probability is decomposed step by step. The policy is trained with REINFORCE using negative tour cost as the reward and a baseline to reduce variance. For cross-problem learning, the Transformer is first pre-trained on TSP and then fine-tuned for downstream VRPs, specifically the Orienteering Problem and the Prize-Collecting TSP. The fine-tuning can be performed either by fully fine-tuning the backbone with problem-specific modules or by training lightweight adapter modules while keeping most of the backbone fixed. This method is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because both rely on an encoder-decoder Transformer construction process for routing, but the paper adapts to new VRP variants through pre-training and fine-tuning rather than through the proposed instance-conditioned scale-and-distance adaptation function or attention-free adaptation module.', 'results': 'No quantitative outcomes are reported in the provided excerpt. The paper describes the experimental protocol, baselines, and training procedure, but it does not provide final objective values, optimality gaps, inference times, ablation numbers, or statistical significance results for the cross-problem learning method.', 'context': 'Experiments are conducted on the Orienteering Problem and the Prize-Collecting TSP with 20, 50, and 100 customer nodes. Node coordinates are uniformly sampled from the unit square. For the Orienteering Problem, all prizes are set to 1. For the Prize-Collecting TSP, prizes and penalties are sampled from uniform distributions. Training uses 2500 batches per epoch, with 512 instances per batch except for n = 100, where 256 instances per batch are used. Training instances are generated on the fly, and 10,000 instances are used for validation. The model is trained for 100 epochs with a learning rate of 1e-4. A paired t-test at alpha = 5% is performed after each epoch to decide whether to replace the baseline model. Experiments run on an Intel Core i9-10940X CPU and an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080Ti GPU. Baselines include Gurobi, Compass, and Tsili for the Orienteering Problem, and Gurobi, ILS, and OR-Tools for the Prize-Collecting TSP. These conditions are relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because they define the small-to-medium scale VRP setting and Transformer training protocol that the proposed large-scale, instance-conditioned adaptation method would need to extend or compare against.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2822, 'output_tokens': 2742}]): Request timed out.
Error processing ("We will replace traditional Multi-Head Attention (MHA) operations with a novel attention mechanism based on Attention Free Transformer (AFT-full). AAFM will explicitly incorporate the adaptation function into attention calculations, enabling direct capture of pairwise relationships between nodes while reducing computational complexity compared to standard attention mechanisms; this module operationalizes the adaptation function inside the encoder's attention computation.", [{'corpus_id': '252211738', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1860, 'output_tokens': 3282}, {'corpus_id': '123646893', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '235778179', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '257219869', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '224886312', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '229340513', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper evaluates a Transformer-based constructive routing solver called MDAM. It uses a 3-layer Transformer encoder with 128-dimensional features and 8 attention heads, where the top encoder layer acts as an Embedding Glimpse layer that re-embeds node representations at fixed intervals. The model then uses 5 parallel decoders, each with 128-dimensional vectors and 8 attention heads, to construct routes. Invalid nodes are masked at each step to handle constraints in TSP, CVRP, SDVRP, OP, PCTSP, and SPCTSP. Training uses a KL loss term with coefficient 0.01 to encourage diversity among decoders, and inference uses greedy decoding or a customized beam search with beam width 50 and a merging technique. This attention-based encoder-decoder design is directly related to the proposed AAFM/ICAM plan because the paper's standard multi-head attention modules are the components the proposed method would replace with an attention-free, instance-conditioned adaptation mechanism, and its TSP/CVRP evaluation protocol is relevant to the plan's routing benchmarks.", 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not report numerical objective values, optimality gaps, or statistical significance. Qualitatively, on CVRP20 with greedy decoding and beam search B=50, the paper reports that all five decoders contribute: winning times are higher than solely winning times, indicating multiple decoders often find the best solution, and no decoder is dominant or non-contributing. The supplementary analysis reports that the merging technique and the number of decoders are analyzed, that a stronger attention model with tuned temperature is used as a comparison, and that MDAM has better generalization on larger CVRP instances with 150 and 200 nodes compared with the single-decoder attention baseline.', 'context': "Experiments use six routing problems, with detailed settings for TSP and CVRP at 20, 50, and 100 nodes. Instances use 2D Euclidean distances from coordinates sampled uniformly in [0,1] for both dimensions. CVRP capacities are 30, 40, and 50 for 20, 50, and 100 nodes, and non-depot demands are integers 1 through 9. Model hyperparameters include 128-dimensional node embeddings, a 3-layer encoder with 128 dimensions and 8 heads, a fully connected hidden dimension of 512, 5 decoders with 128 dimensions and 8 heads, and EG re-embedding intervals of 2, 4, 8 steps for TSP20/50/100 and 2, 6, 8 steps for CVRP20/50/100. Training uses 100 epochs, 2,500 iterations per epoch, batch size 512 except 256 for CVRP100 due to GPU memory, Adam with learning rate 1e-4, and KL coefficient 0.01. Hardware details are not specified. The paper's scale is small to medium, up to 100 nodes with supplementary 150/200 nodes, so it does not directly test the proposed plan's large-scale 1,000+ node generalization, instance-conditioned distance adaptation, or attention-free AAFM, but it provides a relevant attention-based baseline and evaluation context."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2741, 'output_tokens': 4041}, {'corpus_id': '265990729', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1914, 'output_tokens': 3241}, {'corpus_id': '238407851', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper trains a dual-aspect collaborative Transformer routing solver using proximal policy optimization with n-step return estimation and a curriculum learning strategy. The model is applied to constructive solution construction for the traveling salesman problem and capacitated vehicle routing problem, with problem-specific node features, feasibility masks, and hyperparameters. Instances are generated on the fly for N = 20, 50, and 100 nodes. Training uses randomly generated initial solutions, while inference uses greedy initial solutions. A 2-opt local search is applied after solution construction because preliminary tests showed 2-opt outperformed swap and insert for both problems. This method is related to the proposed research plan because it is an RL-based Transformer constructive solver for the same routing problems, but it does not implement the proposed Attention Free Transformer-based Adaptation Attention Free Module or the instance-conditioned adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -alpha log2(N) d_ij. It therefore serves as a conventional Transformer/RL baseline context for evaluating whether attention-free, scale-aware adaptation can improve large-scale generalization.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not report quantitative outcomes such as objective values, optimality gaps, inference times, or statistical comparisons for the method. It only states that the model was evaluated on TSP and CVRP instances of sizes 20, 50, and 100, and that 2-opt was selected as the post-processing operator based on preliminary comparisons with swap and insert.', 'context': 'Experiments were conducted on randomly generated TSP and CVRP instances with 20, 50, and 100 nodes. The computational environment used RTX 2080 Ti GPUs and an Intel Xeon E5-2680 CPU at 2.40 GHz, with a PyTorch implementation. Problem-specific input representations, feasibility masks, and hyperparameters were adjusted for each routing problem. The evaluated scale range is much smaller than the proposed ICAM evaluation range of 100 to 5,000 nodes, and the excerpt does not provide details on attention-free modules, the learnable adaptation parameter alpha, or large-scale generalization metrics.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2268, 'output_tokens': 2244}, {'corpus_id': '226222332', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper describes POMO, a policy-gradient training method for constructive neural combinatorial optimization. For each instance, a policy network samples N solution trajectories, the return of each trajectory is computed, and the expected-return gradient is estimated using an advantage term formed by subtracting a shared baseline from each trajectory return. The excerpt contrasts this shared baseline with a greedy-rollout baseline, in which each sample rollout is compared with its argmax counterpart from the same starting node. POMO is trained with mini-batch gradient ascent and is presented for routing and combinatorial problems such as TSP, CVRP, and 0-1 knapsack. It relates to the proposed AAFM/ICAM work because the research plan uses POMO as the original loss and as a baseline, while the proposed AAFM is an encoder attention replacement that would be trained within this same constructive reinforcement-learning framework.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt reports only qualitative outcomes. It states that the POMO shared baseline produces zero-mean advantages, reduces policy-gradient variance compared with greedy-rollout baselines, is more efficient because it avoids separate critic or cloned-policy forward passes, and makes training more resistant to premature convergence or local minima. No quantitative objective values, optimality gaps, inference times, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance are provided in the excerpt.', 'context': 'The excerpt provides method context but limited experimental detail. It mentions problem setups for TSP, CVRP, and 0-1 knapsack, as well as hyperparameter and implementation sections, but the provided text does not specify dataset sizes, hardware, learning rates, number of trajectories N, batch size, training steps, or evaluation metrics. For the proposed method, the relevant conditions come from the research plan: ICAM/AAFM would be evaluated on TSP, CVRP, and ATSP at scales from 100 to 5,000 nodes for TSP/CVRP and up to 1,000 nodes for ATSP, trained with a three-stage scheme that includes POMO loss and top-k elite training, and assessed with objective values, optimality gaps, and inference times.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2414, 'output_tokens': 4917}, {'corpus_id': '256975543', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '249209651', 'analysis': {'method': 'The related method in the paper is an off-policy actor-critic reinforcement learning model for constructive routing, built on a standard graph attention encoder-decoder. The actor uses multi-head attention encoder layers with a feed-forward sublayer dimension of 512, and a decoder that takes the graph embedding, the embedding of the first and last nodes of the partial tour, and a problem-specific visited-node mask. The critic shares the encoder, uses one-dimensional convolutional layers and three attention layers, averages node embeddings, and passes them through a multi-layer perceptron with one hidden layer of 128 ReLU units and a single output to estimate tour length. The model maintains two Q networks and a replay buffer, learns an entropy temperature parameter automatically, and uses greedy or sampling decoding. It was applied to TSP, CVRP, and SDVRP instances with 20, 50, and 100 nodes, using uniformly sampled coordinates in the unit square and 1,000 test instances. This method is related to the proposed research method because it is a standard multi-head attention encoder-decoder routing model that the proposed Attention Free adaptation module would replace, and it is related to the overall research plan because both concern neural constructive routing solvers, although the paper does not implement the proposed attention-free module, the instance-conditioned adaptation function, or large-scale generalization training.', 'results': 'The supplied sections report qualitative outcomes rather than numeric table values. For TSP, the model with greedy and sampling decoding was reported to outperform learning-based constructive baselines, and greedy decoding was reported to be faster than sampling. The text also states that the method outperformed Concorde, but that claim is not supported by the provided details and is implausible for an exact solver. For CVRP, the method was reported to outperform all sequential constructive models for all problem sizes, with baselines including specialized solvers and metaheuristics. For SDVRP, the method was reported to outperform learning-based approaches and to be more memory efficient than training the standard attention model; the standard attention baseline was described as degrading as problem size increased. Convergence and sample-efficiency figures were referenced, but no exact tour lengths, optimality gaps, or runtimes are included in the excerpt.', 'context': "Experimental conditions included 1,000 test instances per problem, 2D Euclidean distances, coordinates sampled uniformly from [0, 1] x [0, 1], TSP and CVRP sizes of 20, 50, and 100 nodes, CVRP capacities of 30, 40, and 50, customer demands sampled from integers 1 to 9, and SDVRP settings allowing multiple visits and partial delivery. Evaluation metrics were average predicted tour length, optimality gap relative to optimal solutions, and total running time for 1,000 instances. Hardware specifications were not provided, and running times were reported only from the authors' implementation. The method uses standard multi-head attention and small-to-medium-scale instances, not the proposed attention-free adaptation module, 12-layer encoder, instance-conditioned adaptation function, progressive multi-scale training, or 1,000 to 5,000 node evaluation."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5079, 'output_tokens': 3791}, {'corpus_id': '235490054', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates MatNet, an attention-based neural combinatorial optimization model, on the tmat-class asymmetric traveling salesman problem (ATSP) and the flexible flow shop problem (FFSP). The section list indicates that the MatNet variants include masked multi-head attention and graph-attention components, which are the type of attention operations that the proposed AAFM/AFT-full module would replace in the research plan. For ATSP, trained MatNet variants are compared with CPLEX mixed-integer programming results and heuristic baselines on 10,000 test instances. For FFSP, MatNet schedules are compared with MIP and other optimization methods on 1,000 test instances. The evaluation reports average tour length or makespan, optimality gap, and computation/inference time. This is relevant to the proposed research method because MatNet is a constructive attention-based NCO baseline in the research plan, and the proposed ICAM method specifically targets replacement of standard multi-head attention with an attention-free adaptation module for routing problems.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not include the numerical values from the reported comparison tables. It states that ATSP results are reported as average tour lengths in units of 10^6, with gap percentages relative to CPLEX results. It also states that FFSP results are reported as average makespans, with absolute gaps relative to MatNet with x128 augmentation. No exact performance values, gap magnitudes, runtime numbers, or statistical significance tests are available in the supplied text. The only result-related information is that MatNet with x128 augmentation is used as the reference point for FFSP gap reporting, and that MatNet-based models are evaluated under single-GPU inference.', 'context': 'The ATSP experiment uses 10,000 tmat-class test instances, and the FFSP experiment uses 1,000 test instances. Reported times include computational processes only and exclude program and data-matrix loading times. Heuristic baseline runtimes are single-threaded and are divided by 8 for reference, while MatNet inference times are measured on a single GPU even when some models were trained on multiple GPUs. FFSP gaps are given relative to MatNet with x128 augmentation. The excerpt does not specify node scale, training instance distribution, model hyperparameters, training duration, or large-scale generalization settings, and it does not evaluate AFT-full, attention-free transformers, or the instance-conditioned adaptation function proposed in the research plan.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2253, 'output_tokens': 2552}, {'corpus_id': '229156802', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper's relevant method is ProbSparse self-attention in Informer, an encoder-decoder model for long-term time-series forecasting. It replaces standard multi-head self-attention with a sparse attention computation that selects a subset of top keys based on a long-tail distribution in the attention feature map, reducing quadratic attention cost. It also uses self-attention distilling to shorten sequences across encoder layers and a generative decoder that produces long outputs in one forward pass. This relates to the proposed AAFM because both are encoder-side replacements for standard MHA intended to reduce computational cost while maintaining long-range or pairwise dependency modeling; however, the paper's method is not AFT-full, does not use an instance-conditioned adaptation function, and is evaluated on time-series forecasting rather than vehicle routing.", 'results': 'The paper reports that Informer outperformed Transformer and LogTrans on ETT, ECL, and Weather forecasting. For 96-step forecasts: ETTh1 MSE/MAE was 0.435/0.313 versus Transformer 0.456/0.327 and LogTrans 0.446/0.321; ECL was 0.159/0.117 versus 0.165/0.121 and 0.162/0.119; Weather was 0.230/0.156 versus 0.236/0.160 and 0.233/0.158. For 720-step ETTh1 forecasting, Informer MSE/MAE was 0.637/0.406 versus Transformer 0.658/0.421 and LogTrans 0.645/0.413. The paper also reports lower training time and memory usage than standard attention baselines, but the provided excerpt does not include the full efficiency numbers or statistical significance tests.', 'context': 'Experiments used multivariate time series: ETT with target oil temperature and six power-load features, 1-hour or 15-minute granularity, and 12/4/4-month train/validation/test splits; ECL with 321 clients converted to hourly consumption over two years, target MT320, and 15/3/4-month splits; Weather with wet-bulb target and 11 climate features over 2010-2013 and 28/10/10-month splits. Forecasting horizons were 96, 192, 336, and 720 steps for ETT/ECL/Weather. The model used an encoder-decoder architecture with ProbSparse self-attention and distilling; the excerpt does not specify hardware, optimizer, batch size, or full hyperparameter ranges, so those conditions are not fully reproducible from the provided text.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2371, 'output_tokens': 11637}, {'corpus_id': '278768989', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '13756489', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1836, 'output_tokens': 792}, {'corpus_id': '235254329', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper defines AFT-full as the basic Attention Free Transformer variant and evaluates it as a drop-in replacement for the attention module in baseline Transformer architectures. The experimental approach is plug-and-play: for each task, a standard Transformer baseline is taken and its attention module is replaced with an AFT module, while other architectural and training choices are inherited from the Transformer counterpart. The paper evaluates AFT-full on three tasks: image autoregressive modeling, character-level language modeling, and image classification. The first two tasks use the causal or decoder form of AFT, while image classification uses the encoding form. This is directly related to the proposed research method because the proposed Adaptation Attention Free Module also replaces Multi-Head Attention with an AFT-full-based mechanism, although the proposed method additionally injects an instance-conditioned adaptation function into the attention computation for neural vehicle routing solvers.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report quantitative outcomes for AFT-full. It does not include performance values, baseline comparisons, optimality gaps, accuracy, perplexity, inference times, or statistical significance. Therefore, no specific experimental result can be extracted from the supplied text beyond the statement that experiments were conducted on the three listed tasks using the described plug-and-play replacement of attention with AFT.', 'context': 'The experiments were conducted on 8xV100 GPU machines unless otherwise mentioned. The evaluated tasks were image autoregressive modeling, character-level language modeling, and image classification. The causal or decoder AFT model was used for the autoregressive image and character-level language modeling tasks, while the encoding AFT model was used for image classification. Hyperparameters such as initialization and learning-rate scheduling were inherited directly from the corresponding Transformer baselines. The excerpt does not specify dataset names, dataset sizes, sequence lengths, model dimensions, training epochs, or routing-specific conditions. It also does not include the instance-conditioned adaptation function, vehicle routing problem settings, or large-scale generalization evaluation that are central to the proposed ICAM research plan.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1903, 'output_tokens': 2013}, {'corpus_id': '47017706', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '219150860', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses an attention-based reinforcement learning constructive solver for TSP and CVRP. The policy network has an encoder that applies multi-head self-attention over node embeddings, using coordinates and, for CVRP, demand information, and a decoder that uses attention over candidate nodes with a masked softmax layer to select the next node. For TSP, node-pair selection is used to reduce symmetry, and for CVRP the mask prevents selecting visited nodes or capacity-infeasible nodes. The policy is trained with REINFORCE using a baseline and a negative tour-length reward, and deployed with beam search and multiple sampled trajectories. This is directly related to the proposed research method because the paper’s standard multi-head attention encoder-decoder is the component that the proposed AAFM/ICAM plan would replace with an attention-free adaptation module, injecting f(N, d_ij) = -alpha log2(N) d_ij into pairwise attention or compatibility calculations to improve cross-scale generalization while reducing attention cost.', 'results': 'On random Euclidean instances, the model trained on 100 nodes achieved single-trajectory optimality gaps of 1.3% for TSP-100, 1.7% for TSP-200, and 2.4% for TSP-500. Beam search reduced these gaps to 1.0%, 1.3%, and 1.9%, respectively, and multiple trajectories reduced them to 0.9%, 1.2%, and 1.8%. For CVRP, single-trajectory gaps were 1.5%, 1.9%, and 2.5% for 100, 200, and 500 nodes; beam search gave 1.2%, 1.6%, and 2.1%, and multiple trajectories gave 1.1%, 1.5%, and 2.0%. These results outperformed OR-tools in most settings, where OR-tools gaps were around 1.5%, 2.0%, and 2.5%, but remained far from LKH or Concorde references, which were around 0.01%-0.03% or 0. The reported values are average optimality gaps, and no statistical significance tests are included. The increasing gap with scale, and the absence of 1,000+ node tests, directly motivate the proposed ICAM adaptation function and AAFM for large-scale routing.', 'context': 'Experiments used random Euclidean routing instances with coordinates in [0,1]^2, evaluated at 100, 200, and 500 nodes. CVRP instances included a depot, node demands, and vehicle capacity, commonly capacity 100 with demands in [1,100]. The model was trained on 100-node instances and tested at larger scales without retraining. The architecture used multi-head attention, commonly with 8 encoder/decoder layers, 16 heads, and hidden dimension 128, trained with REINFORCE, Adam, batch size 64, and multiple sampled trajectories. Deployment used beam search with beam size around 10 and about 100 sampled trajectories for diversification. Evaluation was based on optimality gap to LKH or Concorde reference solutions, with inference time also considered. The paper’s evidence is limited to 100-500 node generalization, whereas the proposed research plan targets 1,000-5,000 node TSP/CVRP and 1,000-node ATSP with an explicit scale and distance adaptation term.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1731, 'output_tokens': 13239}, {'corpus_id': '258461478', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '237616034', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1765, 'output_tokens': 831}, {'corpus_id': '252907522', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4409, 'output_tokens': 8127}, {'corpus_id': '3649804', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a pointer-network-based constructive neural combinatorial optimization model for Euclidean TSP. The model encodes point coordinates with an LSTM encoder and uses a pointer/attention glimpse to sequentially select cities, with a mechanism that prevents selecting the same city twice. It is trained using supervised learning on optimal tours and reinforcement learning pretraining, and then evaluated with greedy decoding, sampling, and active search at inference time. This relates to the proposed research method and overall research plan because both concern constructive neural solvers for TSP/routing that build solutions sequentially and can be compared as reinforcement-learning-based neural combinatorial optimization approaches. However, the paper does not use multi-head attention, attention-free transformers, or the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, so it is relevant as a prior NCO TSP method and baseline context rather than as a direct implementation of the proposed AAFM module.', 'results': 'The supplied text does not provide exact average tour lengths or optimality gaps. It reports that reinforcement learning pretraining significantly improves over supervised pointer networks, that all tested methods surpass Christofides, and that greedy reinforcement-learning pretraining is time-efficient and only a few percent worse than optimality. Inference-time search is reported as important for approaching optimality, with early stopping possible at a small performance tradeoff. RL pretraining-Sampling and RL pretraining-Active Search are described as the most competitive methods and recover optimal solutions in a significant number of test cases. Many RL pretraining methods outperform OR-Tools local search, including RL pretraining-Greedy@16, which is reported to run similarly fast. The method is reported as superior to simulated annealing but less competitive than tabu search and much less competitive than guided local search. Active search from an untrained model is reported to require 7 hours per TSP50 instance and 25 hours per TSP100 instance.', 'context': 'Experiments use Euclidean TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100 with 1,000 uniformly random test graphs in the unit square. Training uses mini-batches of 128 sequences, LSTM cells with 128 hidden units, 128-dimensional coordinate embeddings, the Adam optimizer, initial learning rates of 1e-3 for TSP20 and TSP50 and 1e-4 for TSP100, learning-rate decay by 0.96 every 5000 steps, uniform initialization in [-0.08, 0.08], L2 gradient clipping at 1.0, up to one attention glimpse, and baseline decay alpha = 0.99 for active search. Greedy methods run on a single Nvidia Tesla K80 GPU, while Concorde and LK-H run on an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3 CPU and OR-Tools runs on an Intel Haswell CPU. The paper evaluates only up to 100 nodes, so it does not directly test the 1,000+ node cross-scale generalization target of the proposed ICAM research plan.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3771, 'output_tokens': 4041}, {'corpus_id': '248377568', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates alternative encoder attention mechanisms in the N2S neural pickup-and-delivery solver by replacing its proposed Synth-Att encoder with vanilla-Att and DAC-Att while keeping the rest of the N2S pipeline fixed, including PPO training, node-pair removal and reinsertion decoding, and the diversity-enhanced N2S-A variant. The ablation measures model parameters, runtime, and solution gaps on 2,000 PDTSP-51 instances using 3,000 decoding steps on a single GPU. This is related to the proposed AAFM method because it addresses the same design question of whether a non-standard attention or encoding module can reduce computational cost while preserving competitive routing performance; however, the paper does not implement AFT-full, does not incorporate the instance-conditioned adaptation function f(N, d_ij), and is evaluated on pickup-and-delivery problems rather than the TSP, CVRP, and ATSP large-scale generalization setting in the research plan.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt reports only qualitative outcomes: Synth-Att achieves slightly smaller gaps than DAC-Att with much fewer computation costs, and considerably lower gaps than vanilla-Att with only slight extra computation costs. No numerical parameter counts, runtimes, gap values, or statistical significance tests are included in the supplied text.', 'context': 'The ablation was conducted on one GPU card using 2,000 PDTSP-51 instances and 3,000 steps per instance. The broader N2S setup uses PPO with curriculum learning, problem sizes of 21, 51, and 101 nodes, 200 epochs, 20 batches per epoch, batch size 600, n-step PPO with n = 5 and T_train = 250, 3 minibatch updates, clip threshold 0.1, Adam learning rates of 8e-5 for the policy and 2e-5 for the critic with decay 0.985 per epoch, discount factor 0.999, gradient norm clipping of 0.05, 0.15, and 0.35, and curriculum learning values of 2, 1.5, and 1 for the three problem sizes. Training used 8 RTX 2080 Ti GPUs and took about 1 day for 21 nodes, 3 days for 51 nodes, and 7 days for 101 nodes.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3038, 'output_tokens': 2680}, {'corpus_id': '59608816', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '46892983', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses an attention-based neural route-construction model for the capacitated vehicle routing problem, trained with policy-gradient actor-critic methods, including asynchronous advantage actor-critic. The model parameterizes a stochastic policy that sequentially selects the next customer or depot. Feasibility is enforced through masking: nodes with zero demand are masked, all customers are masked when the vehicle load is zero, and customers whose demand exceeds the remaining load are masked. Inference is performed with greedy decoding and beam search using beam widths of 5 and 10. This method is related to the proposed research method because it is an attention-based constructive neural VRP solver of the type that the proposed AAFM would modify by replacing standard attention with an attention-free adaptation module and adding an instance-conditioned scale and distance bias. The provided excerpt does not explicitly specify multi-head attention, but it describes the attention-based policy network that the proposed method targets for architectural replacement.', 'results': "Experiments were run on 1000 instances for each of VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100. For VRP10 and VRP20, where optimal solutions were obtained by mixed integer programming, the beam-search method with width 10 was best: roughly 95% of VRP10 instances were within 10% of optimality, and roughly 95% of VRP20 instances were within 13% of optimality. Outliers were within 20-25% of optimality. For VRP50 and VRP100, where optimal values were not computationally affordable, winning-rate comparisons showed that classical heuristics were outperformed by the other approaches in almost 100% of samples. RL-greedy was comparable with OR-Tools, while adding beam search increased the method's winning rate against OR-Tools to above 60%. Runtime analysis showed that the log of solution time divided by the number of nodes remained nearly constant for the RL decoders, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep runtimes grew faster than linearly with problem size. OR-Tools times were included but were not directly comparable because OR-Tools was implemented in C++ while the other baselines were implemented in Python.", 'context': 'The experimental setting used randomly generated capacitated VRP instances with customer and depot locations drawn uniformly from the unit square and customer demands drawn uniformly from the discrete set 1 through 9. The vehicle started at the depot, and the main experiments did not allow split deliveries. Problem sizes were 10, 20, 50, and 100 customers, with 1000 instances per size. The model used greedy decoding and beam search with beam widths 5 and 10. Baselines included Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized Clarke-Wright with randomization depth and iteration settings of 5,5 and 10,10, randomized Sweep with random initial angles, and OR-Tools. Optimal solutions for VRP10 and VRP20 were obtained by solving a mixed integer formulation. The paper does not evaluate large-scale generalization to 1000 or more nodes, does not use the proposed adaptation function f(N, d_ij), and does not implement the proposed AFT-full-based AAFM; therefore it provides a small-scale attention-based policy-gradient baseline context for the proposed ICAM research plan.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3784, 'output_tokens': 3577}, {'corpus_id': '239009619', 'analysis': {'method': 'The closest method in the paper to the proposed AAFM is the Sparse Graph Network encoder-decoder used in NeuroLKH. It converts each routing instance into a sparse directed graph, embeds nodes and edges, and decodes edge scores and, for TSP, node penalties. Edge scores are trained with a supervised loss that increases scores for edges in the optimal tour and decreases scores for non-optimal edges; these scores are then used to construct an edge candidate set for LKH. Node penalties are trained with an unsupervised loss that transforms edge distances and pushes the node degrees in the minimum 1-tree toward 2, analogous to subgradient optimization but learned across a distribution of instances. The combined loss is L = L_beta + eta_pi L_pi. The learned edge candidate set and transformed edge distances guide multiple LKH lambda-opt trials. This relates to the proposed AAFM and ICAM plan because both use a neural encoder to capture pairwise node relationships for routing and aim to improve large-scale solving efficiency, but the paper does not use multi-head attention, Attention Free Transformer, or the explicit scale-distance adaptation function f(N, d_ij); it is a neural local-search guidance method rather than a constructive attention-free decoder.', 'results': 'The excerpt reports qualitative and benchmark-level outcomes rather than complete numerical optimality gaps. For TSP, NeuroLKH is described as effective compared with baseline algorithms, but no specific objective values or gaps are included in the provided text. For CVRP, PDP, and CVRPTW, NeuroLKH significantly improves solution quality over original LKH under time limits equal to LKH with 100, 1000, and 10000 trials. At the 100-trial limit, both methods fail to find feasible solutions on less than 1% of 300-node PDP and CVRPTW test instances; infeasible visits are moved to the end to obtain feasible tours. On 43 CVRPLIB instances with 100-300 nodes, NeuroLKH improves average performance on 38, 38, and 31 instances at the 100, 1000, and 10000 trial time limits, respectively. On 11 Solomon R2 instances, it outperforms LKH in 32 of 33 settings. SGN inference for 1000 test instances takes 1s, 3s, 7s, 10s, 19s, and 40s for 40, 100, 200, 300, 500, and 1000 nodes, which is small relative to the LKH search time. No statistical significance tests are reported.', 'context': "Experiments use 2D Euclidean instances with coordinates sampled from the unit square. TSP training uses 101-500 nodes, with 500000/|V| instances per size, about 780000 total, and optimal edges from Concorde for supervised edge-score training; testing uses 1000 instances per size. For CVRP, demands are uniform integers 1-9, capacity is 40 + 0.1|V|, training uses 101-500 nodes with 120000/|V| instances per size, about 180000 total, and 10 epochs. PDP and CVRPTW use 41-200 nodes with 240000/|V| instances per size, and labels are generated by LKH with 10000 trials because optimal solutions are unavailable. Node penalties are not used for CVRP, PDP, or CVRPTW; only edge candidate sets are learned. LKH search hyperparameters follow the LKH example scripts with the SPECIAL setting for CVRP, PDP, and CVRPTW, and the default LKH candidate-set size is k = 5. Evaluation uses 1000 test instances for the smallest and largest training sizes and a larger generalization size, with time limits set to LKH 100, 1000, and 10000 trials. Hardware and exact TSP LKH trial counts are not specified in the excerpt. This context is relevant to ICAM's goals of cross-scale generalization and efficient encoding, but the paper's SGN is not an AAFM implementation and does not test the proposed attention-free adaptation module."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4499, 'output_tokens': 7023}, {'corpus_id': '211069726', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies AM-D, a dynamic attention model for VRP, an encoder-decoder constructive solver trained with REINFORCE. It embeds customers and depot, uses a message stream and multi-head attention sublayer, and applies dynamic attention during solution construction so that node representations are updated at each construction step. The model is trained on VRP instances and evaluated by greedy rollout on held-out instances. This method is relevant to the proposed AAFM/ICAM plan because both use attention-based encoder-decoder constructive models for routing, but the paper relies on standard multi-head attention and small-scale dynamic attention, whereas the proposed method replaces MHA with an attention-free adaptation module and adds a scale/distance adaptation function for larger-scale generalization.', 'results': 'On 10,000 test instances, AM-D reduced average tour length compared with the static attention model AM by 2.02% for VRP20, 2.01% for VRP50, and 2.55% for VRP100, and it outperformed other baseline models. In cross-size generalization tests, a model trained on 20-node instances performed well on 50- and 100-node instances and was better than some baselines, while a model trained on 100-node instances also performed well on 20- and 50-node instances. The paper reports these cross-size outcomes qualitatively from the last three rows of the results table and does not provide exact numerical values or statistical significance for those rows.', 'context': "Experiments used VRP instances with 20, 50, and 100 customer nodes. Node coordinates were sampled uniformly from the unit square, customer demands were uniform integers from 1 to 9, depot demand was 0, and vehicle capacities were 30, 40, and 50 for VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100, respectively. Training used 30 epochs with 10,000 batches per epoch; batch size was 128 and learning rate 1e-4 for VRP20 and VRP50, while batch size was 108 and learning rate 5e-5 for VRP100. Hardware was a single 1080Ti GPU for VRP20 and VRP50 and three 1080Ti GPUs for VRP100. Testing used greedy rollout and reported average solution length. The experimental scale is much smaller than the proposed plan's 100 to 5,000-node TSP/CVRP and 1,000-node ATSP settings, so it provides a small-scale baseline for attention-based generalization but does not directly test the proposed AAFM, adaptation function, or large-scale performance."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2544, 'output_tokens': 2736}, {'corpus_id': '257365174', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1714, 'output_tokens': 520}, {'corpus_id': '221191399', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '261243129', 'analysis': {'method': 'The related paper method is ELG-POMO, an attention-based autoregressive construction model for TSP and CVRP. It uses a POMO global policy with a Transformer encoder that encodes unordered node embeddings from the full instance without positional embedding, and a decoder that computes query-key compatibility between the current state, including the current node and loaded capacity, and candidate node keys. Invalid actions are masked, scores are clipped, softmax is applied, and the next node is selected until a complete tour or route is constructed. A local policy is added to focus on the local neighborhood of the current node and is ensembled with the global policy. Training uses REINFORCE with a baseline, the reward is the negative objective value, and inference uses greedy decoding with POMO-style instance augmentation. This method is directly related to the proposed research method because the proposed AAFM would replace the standard Transformer attention component in such an encoder with an Attention Free Transformer full variant and inject the instance-conditioned adaptation function f(N, d_ij) into encoder attention. The overall research plan also treats ELG as a constructive NCO baseline and plans an ablation comparing AAFM against standard multi-head attention.', 'results': 'On TSPLIB and CVRPLIB instances with N <= 200, ELG-POMO outperformed the compared learning methods, with gap reductions of 0.53% on CVRP and 0.50% on TSP relative to the runner-up. On instances with N in the range 200 to 1000 or 1002, ELG-POMO reduced the CVRP gap from 6.95% for Omni-POMO to 6.46%, and reduced the TSP gap from 7.47% for Omni-POMO to 5.90%, despite Omni-POMO using a stronger training setting with complex distributions and varying scales. However, ELG-POMO lagged behind BQ and LEHD on TSP in that larger-scale range. On real-world CVRPLIB Set-XXL instances with N between 3000 and 7000, ELG-POMO was best on three of four instances and improved over POMO-based methods, although exact gap values were not provided in the excerpt. Runtime was 1.90 seconds per instance, much lower than 16 minutes for LKH3 and HGS, but the optimality gap was still worse than those highly optimized solvers. On uniform N = 100 instances, ELG-POMO achieved a 0.22% gap on TSP and a 1.22% gap on CVRP, improving over POMO at 0.40% and 1.34% and Sym-POMO at 0.41% and 1.43%, but remaining behind Pointerformer at 0.16% and T2TCO at 0.18% on TSP. On difficult synthetic distributions, retrained ELG* outperformed AMDKD on three N = 100 distributions and all four N = 200 distributions, with N = 200 gaps of 1.84%, 1.62%, 1.43%, and 1.58% compared with AMDKD gaps of 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54%.', 'context': 'Training instances were randomly generated uniform TSP and CVRP instances with N = 100. ELG-POMO training was aligned with POMO, while POMO and Sym-POMO were early stopped using a validation set with N = 200. Test conditions included TSPLIB, CVRPLIB Set-X and Set-XXL, small-scale synthetic distributions such as cluster, cluster mixture, explosion, and rotation, and uniform small-scale instances. For the synthetic comparison, POMO and ELG were retrained on uniform, cluster, and cluster mixture distributions to align with AMDKD. Evaluation metrics were average gap to best known solutions and average runtime per instance. Inference used greedy mode, and POMO-based methods used x8 instance augmentation. Detailed hyperparameters and hardware specifications were not included in the provided excerpt, although the paper stated that code and data were available. The method is relevant to the research plan because ICAM targets the same large-scale generalization limitation and modifies the encoder attention mechanism used by attention-based constructive solvers such as ELG-POMO.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5641, 'output_tokens': 4051}, {'corpus_id': '165163853', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2808, 'output_tokens': 2703}, {'corpus_id': '233864561', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1838, 'output_tokens': 1320}, {'corpus_id': '5692837', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper describes a sequence-to-sequence pointer network, referred to as Ptr-Net, for planar point-set combinatorial tasks: convex hull, Delaunay triangulation, and the Travelling Salesman Problem. Inputs are n planar points P_j = (x_j, y_j) sampled uniformly from [0, 1] x [0, 1], and outputs are sequences C_P representing the solution associated with the point set. The model uses a single-layer LSTM with either 256 or 512 hidden units, content-based input attention, and a pointer mechanism to emit solution elements. This is related to the proposed ICAM/AAFM method only at the level of constructive neural solvers for TSP and routing-like sequence generation over point sets; it is not a direct implementation of the proposed AFT-full attention-free module, does not replace multi-head attention, and does not include the scale-conditioned adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -alpha * log2(N) * d_ij.', 'results': 'No quantitative experimental outcomes are provided in the supplied excerpt because the Empirical Results section is empty. The only reported observation is that overfitting was observed in some cases where the task was simpler, specifically for small n, after training on 1M example pairs. No objective values, optimality gaps, baseline comparisons, inference times, or statistical significance are available.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions include synthetic datasets of 1M training input-output pairs, uniform point sampling in the unit square, and the same virtually fixed architecture across convex hull, Delaunay triangulation, and TSP. Training used stochastic gradient descent with a learning rate of 1.0, batch size of 128, uniform weight initialization from -0.08 to 0.08, and L2 gradient clipping of 2.0. The authors state that no extensive architecture or hyperparameter search was performed, and the same hyperparameters were used across problems to make the comparison stronger. No hardware specifications, test-scale details, or large-scale generalization evaluation are given, so the paper provides limited direct context for the proposed 12-layer AAFM encoder, multi-stage training, and 100 to 5,000 node routing experiments.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2071, 'output_tokens': 3733}, {'corpus_id': '220250819', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a linearized attention transformer as an alternative to standard softmax multi-head attention. The method rewrites attention using a feature map so that the attention computation can be performed with lower memory and computational cost, applies causal masking for autoregressive sequence modeling, and implements a constant-memory gradient computation in CUDA. The experimental task is a synthetic sequence-duplication or copy task in which the model must copy a sequence of symbols. The model is a 4-layer transformer with 8 attention heads, trained with batch size 64 and the RAdam optimizer. Baselines are standard softmax attention and Reformer with lsh-4 hashing, with lsh-1 and lsh-8 also shown in the cost/memory figures. This relates to the proposed ICAM/AAFM method only indirectly: both approaches replace standard multi-head attention with a lower-complexity attention computation inside a transformer encoder, but the paper does not use Attention Free Transformer, AFT-full, an instance-conditioned adaptation function, or vehicle routing problem instances.', 'results': 'On the synthetic copy task, the linear attention model converged smoothly and reached the same final cross-entropy loss as softmax attention, while achieving lower loss than Reformer lsh-4. The paper attributes the advantage over lsh-4 to the absence of hashing noise. The supplied text does not report exact final loss values, statistical significance, or precise per-method runtime and memory endpoints. It does state that the linear transformer requires significantly less GPU memory and computation than softmax attention and achieves competitive performance with Reformer. The convergence plot spans 0 to 10000 gradient steps with a cross-entropy loss axis from 1e-4 to 1e0, and the cost/memory figures span sequence lengths from 2^9 to 2^16, time from 10^0 to 10^3 milliseconds, and GPU memory from 10^1 to 10^3 MB, but no exact numerical values for each method are provided in the excerpt.', 'context': 'The experiments are conducted on synthetic data rather than real routing instances. The sequence length is at most 128, the symbol set contains 10 different symbols plus a dedicated separator, and the model is small: 4 layers and 8 attention heads. Training uses RAdam with a learning rate of 1e-3 reduced to 1e-4 after 3000 updates. The implementation is in PyTorch, with the constant-memory gradient computation implemented in approximately 200 lines of CUDA code. For the Reformer baseline, reversible layers are not used, and memory consumption is measured only for the self-attention layer. These conditions limit direct transferability to the proposed 12-layer ICAM encoder and large-scale TSP, CVRP, or ATSP generalization setting; the paper provides a generic efficiency and convergence precedent for non-softmax attention, not direct evidence that AFT-full or the proposed adaptation function f(N, d_ij) improves neural routing solver performance.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2669, 'output_tokens': 3527}, {'corpus_id': '239998492', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a learning collaborative policy (LCP) scheme for DRL-based routing solvers. It builds on an attention-model constructive policy and adds two components: a seeder that generates M diversified candidate tours/routes while exploring the full combinatorial action space, and a reviser that decomposes each candidate into K segments and re-optimizes the routing sequence within each segment in parallel for I sequential iterations. The seeder is trained with entropy regularization and a linear entropy schedule, and the whole scheme is applied to TSP, PCTSP, and CVRP. This is related to the proposed AAFM because both target AM-style neural routing solvers for TSP/CVRP, but the paper uses a standard attention-based AM baseline plus collaborative policies rather than replacing multi-head attention with an attention-free transformer or adding the instance-conditioned adaptation function.', 'results': 'On small-scale instances, the method outperformed all DRL baselines and OR-Tools on TSP, PCTSP, and CVRP. In the N=100 component ablation, the best reported configuration reached cost gaps of 0.88 percent on TSP, 1.02 percent on PCTSP, and 2.37 percent on CVRP relative to state-of-the-art solvers, improving over the weakest reported row of 2.65 percent, 1.64 percent, and 3.43 percent. LCP and LCP* outperformed DRL-2opt by 0.33 percent at N=100. For PCTSP, AM plus LCP with M=640 and I=1 beat OR-Tools with 10 seconds of local search and was 4 times faster at N=50 and 2 times faster at N=100; AM plus LCP with M=1280 and I=5 was 1.0 percent worse than ILS but 11 times faster at N=100. In time-performance analysis, the method occupied a winner region below 10 seconds for PCTSP at N=100 and N=500 and for CVRP at N=500, and below 5 seconds for CVRP at N=100.', 'context': 'Experiments used TSP, PCTSP, and CVRP instances with N=20, 50, 100, and 500 nodes, plus real-world TSPLIB TSP instances in an appendix. Training hyperparameters were kept the same as the attention-model baseline except the seeder batch size was 1024; entropy scheduling used alpha=0.5, Nw=sum of i, and linear weight w_t=(N-t)/Nw. Runtime was controlled by sample width M and revision iterations I for the DRL method, and by allowed local-search time for OR-Tools. Baselines included Gurobi, OR-Tools, Concorde, ILS, LKH3, S2V-DQN, EAN, GAT-T, DRL-2opt, and AM. Hardware details were not specified in the provided text. The paper does not use AFT-full, the proposed adaptation function f(N, d_ij), or 1000+ node cross-scale training, so it provides an AM-based DRL routing baseline and speed-performance context for the proposed research plan rather than direct evidence for the AAFM architecture.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4489, 'output_tokens': 6005}]): Request timed out.
Error processing ('We will develop ICAM using an encoder-decoder structure where the adaptation function is integrated into both the encoding phase (through AAFM) and the compatibility calculation in the decoder. The model will use a deeper 12-layer encoder to generate better node embeddings while maintaining efficiency through the attention-free design; this architecture combines the proposed adaptation function and AAFM into a complete constructive routing model.', [{'corpus_id': '268096097', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '252762089', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates Poppy, a population-based policy-gradient constructive solver, on TSP, CVRP, KP, and JSSP. It uses JAX-based implementations to run on hardware accelerators and reuses hyperparameters from a prior constructive routing method when possible. For the routing-relevant TSP and CVRP settings, training uses randomly generated instances, and one training step computes policy gradients over a batch of 64 instances for each agent in the population. The evaluation varies population size to study time-performance tradeoffs. This relates to the proposed ICAM research plan because both concern constructive neural routing solvers for TSP and CVRP, but the paper’s method is population-based and does not use the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, AAFM, or 12-layer encoder-decoder architecture.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report quantitative solution-quality outcomes, optimality gaps, or comparisons to baselines. It reports training-cost outcomes: for TSP with 100 cities, Phase 1 required 4.5 million steps and about 5 days, while Phase 2 required 400,000 steps and 1 to 4 days depending on population size. The stated experimental outcome is that Poppy was run at various population sizes to demonstrate time-performance tradeoffs, but no numerical performance values are given in the excerpt.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions include randomly generated instances, TSP instances with 100 cities, batch sizes of 64 instances per agent for TSP, CVRP, and KP and 128 for JSSP, population-size-dependent training time, and execution on a v3-8 TPU using JAX-based implementations. Hyperparameters were reused from a prior method when possible. These conditions are relevant to the ICAM research plan as a routing-problem training and evaluation context, especially for TSP and CVRP, but they do not cover the proposed large-scale 100 to 5,000-node generalization, ATSP, three-stage training, or ablation of the adaptation function.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2062, 'output_tokens': 2271}, {'corpus_id': '235898959', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1765, 'output_tokens': 351}, {'corpus_id': '271004138', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '214795270', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper trains a policy-gradient reinforcement learning model to improve an initial TSP tour by selecting 2-opt moves. The model uses an encoder-decoder architecture: a graph convolutional encoder with dual encoding produces node and tour embeddings, a policy decoder selects the next 2-opt move, and a value decoder estimates state value. Training uses random Euclidean TSP instances with 20, 50, and 100 nodes in the unit square, random feasible initial tours, curriculum episode lengths, reward clipping, and a critic/entropy loss. It is evaluated on 512 validation/test instances and 10,000 instances per size, with optimality gaps against Concorde, tour costs, running times, and comparisons to classical 2-opt first-improvement/best-improvement heuristics with restarts, OR-Tools, construction heuristics, and neural construction/improvement methods. This is related to the proposed ICAM plan because both use encoder-decoder neural models for TSP and examine generalization to larger instances, but the paper's method is an improvement heuristic rather than a constructive solver and does not use the proposed adaptation function or attention-free module.", 'results': 'With 500 improvement steps, the learned policy achieved optimality gaps of 0.01% on TSP20, 0.36% on TSP50, and 1.84% on TSP100, with times of 5, 7, and 10 minutes for 10,000 instances. With 1,000 steps, gaps were 0.00%, 0.21%, and 1.26%, with times of 10, 13, and 21 minutes. With 2,000 steps, gaps were 0.00%, 0.12%, and 0.87%, with times of 15, 29, and 41 minutes. At 500 steps it outperformed the compared GAT-T improvement method that used 1,000 steps; at 1,000 steps it matched GAT-T on TSP50 with about one-fifth the samples and achieved a lower TSP100 gap than the compared methods. A policy trained on TSP50 and tested on TSP100 reached 1.86% gap with 1,000 samples and 1.37% with 2,000 samples. In the 2-opt comparison on TSP100, the policy produced lower median tour cost and less dispersion than first-improvement and best-improvement 2-opt with restarts, though exact boxplot values are not provided in the text. The narrative also reports best-found gaps within 0.1% for TSP50 and 0.7% for TSP100, while the table reports 0.12% and 0.87% at 2,000 steps.', 'context': 'Experiments used a single RTX 2080Ti GPU. Batch size was 512 for TSP20/TSP50 and 256 for TSP100, with 10 or 20 minibatches per epoch. Training ran 200 epochs for TSP20 and 300 epochs for TSP50/TSP100; average epoch times were 2m01s, 3m05s, and 7m16s. Hyperparameters included gamma 0.99, L2 penalty 1e-5, learning rate 0.001 with 0.98 decay, value loss weight 0.5, entropy/heuristic loss weights 0.0045 for TSP20/TSP50 and 0.0018 for TSP100 with 0.9 decay, embedding dimension 128, 3 encoder layers, one RNN block, and episode-length schedules. Mixed precision was used for TSP50/TSP100. The method starts from random initial tours and samples multiple improvement trajectories; it does not explicitly condition on instance scale with a learnable adaptation function and does not use the 12-layer attention-free encoder proposed in ICAM. Its largest tested scale is 100 nodes, so it provides small-scale TSP evidence and a relevant baseline for cross-scale generalization, but not direct validation of 1,000+ node routing generalization.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 6091, 'output_tokens': 6047}, {'corpus_id': '235778179', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '257219869', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '208921299', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses an A2C reinforcement-learning method for routing a heterogeneous fleet of vehicles on a CVRP-like problem. Each vehicle is treated as an agent with its own DNN policy, and the policy is conditioned on a shared problem state containing customer and depot coordinates, remaining customer demands, vehicle capacities, and vehicle positions. The paper focuses on Case 1, where vehicles make single sequential and alternating actions in a fixed order, allowing the routing process to be modeled as a Markov Decision Process. The training procedure gives agents access to the full environment state rather than only local information, and the architecture is described as a DNN-based policy model with encoder, decoder, attention, and critic components. This relates to the proposed ICAM method and research plan because both are neural constructive routing approaches that build routes sequentially, but the paper’s method uses standard A2C policy learning without the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, attention-free AAFM module, or explicit cross-scale generalization mechanism.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt reports comparative outcomes rather than exact numerical values. The DRL method produced shorter average tour lengths than the compared heuristics on large instances, and the advantage increased as instance size increased. The DRL tours also had lower standard deviation than the heuristic tours. However, OR-Tools produced shorter average tour lengths than the DRL method, which the paper attributes to the Case 1 sequential and alternating decision procedure restricting the solution space. The DRL method had competitive runtimes compared with the heuristics, while OR-Tools had much longer average runtimes. No specific tour lengths, optimality gaps, statistical significance tests, or exact instance sizes are provided in the excerpt.', 'context': 'Instances are defined in a 2D Euclidean space with customers and depot locations randomly generated in the unit square. Customer demands are discrete uniform integers from 1 to 9, and vehicle capacities are heterogeneous. The evaluated decision procedure is Case 1, where agents act sequentially in a fixed alternating order and the environment is stationary from each agent’s perspective. The method is trained with A2C, but the excerpt does not specify network dimensions, hyperparameters, hardware, exact instance sizes, or statistical tests. This context is relevant to the proposed research plan because it represents an RL-based constructive routing baseline for heterogeneous-capacity vehicle routing, while the proposed ICAM aims to improve large-scale generalization through instance-conditioned adaptation, an attention-free encoder, and a deeper 12-layer encoder-decoder architecture.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2932, 'output_tokens': 2501}, {'corpus_id': '270521882', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 6097, 'output_tokens': 5489}, {'corpus_id': '229340513', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper evaluates a Multi-Decoder Attention Model (MDAM) for constructive routing. It uses a 3-layer Transformer encoder with 128-dimensional node embeddings, 8 attention heads, and a 512-dimensional feed-forward layer; the top encoder layer acts as an Embedding Glimpse layer that re-embeds nodes every 2, 4, or 8 steps for TSP20/50/100 and every 2, 6, or 8 steps for CVRP20/50/100. Five parallel decoders construct routes using attention, and a KL loss with coefficient 0.01 encourages diversity among decoders. Invalid nodes are masked for constrained or stochastic variants. The method is related to the proposed ICAM because both are encoder-decoder constructive neural routing solvers for TSP/CVRP, but MDAM uses standard multi-head attention and multiple decoders for diversity rather than ICAM's instance-conditioned adaptation function and attention-free AAFM; its analysis of generalization to CVRP150/200 is a smaller-scale analogue of ICAM's goal of improving generalization to much larger instances.", 'results': 'The provided sections do not report numerical objective values or optimality gaps. On CVRP20, with greedy decoding and beam search using beam width 50, the paper reports that among the 5 decoders, winning times are higher than solely winning times in both modes, meaning multiple decoders often find the best solution. It also reports that no decoder is dominant or non-contributing, indicating all decoders are effective. In supplementary comparisons, MDAM is reported to generalize better than a stronger attention model with tuned softmax temperature on CVRP150 and CVRP200, but no quantitative gap values are given in the excerpt.', 'context': 'Experiments use 2D Euclidean TSP and CVRP instances with 20, 50, and 100 nodes sampled uniformly from [0,1] in both coordinates. CVRP capacities are 30, 40, and 50 for 20, 50, and 100 nodes, and non-depot demands are integers 1 through 9. Additional routing problems follow existing settings and are handled by masking invalid nodes. Training uses 2,500 iterations per epoch, batch size 512 except 256 for CVRP100 due to GPU memory, 100 epochs, Adam with learning rate 1e-4, and KL coefficient 0.01. Evaluation of decoder contribution uses CVRP20 with greedy decoding and beam search B=50. Hardware details beyond a GPU memory constraint are not specified. This context is relevant to the research plan because it shows a baseline-style constructive attention model evaluated at small-to-medium scales, whereas ICAM targets cross-scale generalization up to 5,000 nodes and uses a 12-layer attention-free encoder.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2747, 'output_tokens': 3187}, {'corpus_id': '208290931', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 9067, 'output_tokens': 2646}, {'corpus_id': '238407851', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a reinforcement learning training method for a constructive routing model: proximal policy optimization with n-step return estimation, combined with a curriculum learning strategy intended to improve sample efficiency. It is applied to a Transformer-based dual-aspect routing model for TSP and CVRP, where instances are generated on the fly for N = 20, 50, and 100. The method uses problem-specific node features, feasibility masks, and hyperparameters, and it masks diagonal pairwise elements and the node pair selected at the last step to avoid invalid or dead-loop transitions. Training uses randomly generated initial solutions, while inference uses a greedy algorithm. Evaluation is framed around objective values, optimality gaps, and runtime, although the excerpt does not provide numerical model performance. This relates to the proposed ICAM research plan because both concern RL-based constructive routing solvers for TSP/CVRP, but the paper’s method is a Transformer-based PPO/curriculum-learning setup on small-scale instances, whereas the proposed method focuses on an attention-free, instance-conditioned adaptation architecture aimed at large-scale generalization.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report quantitative objective values, optimality gaps, or inference times for the trained routing model. The only reported experimental outcome is that initial comparisons among local search operators, specifically 2-opt, swap, and insert, showed 2-opt performed best for both TSP and CVRP, with insert performing better than swap. Consequently, the paper states that its reported method uses 2-opt. The excerpt also notes that some baseline results are adopted from their original sources, and that one baseline runtime is based on one GPU and ten CPUs under default settings.', 'context': 'Experiments are conducted on TSP and CVRP instances with N = 20, 50, and 100 nodes, generated on the fly. Training uses randomly generated initial solutions, while inference uses a greedy algorithm. Problem-specific constraints, node features, feasibility masks, and hyperparameters are adjusted for each problem. Pairwise diagonal elements and the node pair selected at the last step are masked to prevent invalid transitions or dead loops. The models and baselines are tested on a server equipped with RTX 2080 Ti GPUs and an Intel Xeon E5-2680 CPU at 2.40 GHz, with a PyTorch implementation. The excerpt does not include large-scale instances beyond 100 nodes, ATSP, attention-free modules, or the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2274, 'output_tokens': 3195}, {'corpus_id': '195316416', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper implements a deep Q-network (DQN) router for VLSI global routing. The simulated environment parses chip grid size, edge capacities, reduced capacities from pre-routed nets or obstacles, and net pin lists; it decomposes multi-pin nets into two-pin subproblems, feeds states to the DQN, allows six directional moves subject to boundary and capacity constraints, updates edge capacities after each move, and returns reward feedback. The DQN agent scores possible next states with a Q-network, selects an action using epsilon-greedy, stores transitions in a replay buffer, and updates weights by backpropagation. Experiments generate 4x4x2 and 8x8x2 routing problems with random pin locations, net counts, and capacities, tune batch size, learning rate, epsilon, discount factor, and network structure, and compare DQN solutions against A* using wire length and overflow. This is related to the proposed ICAM plan as an RL-based constructive routing model and as a scale/hyperparameter study, but it is not an encoder-decoder, attention-free, or instance-conditioned adaptation architecture.', 'results': 'On 4x4x2 toy problems with 3 nets, maximum 2 pins per net, and 2000 episodes, batch size 128 produced reward oscillations and did not increase reward faster than smaller batches; learning rate 1e-4 was the best trade-off, while 1e-5 was too slow and unstable and 1e-3 caused strong oscillations. A decayed epsilon schedule from 0.05 to 0.01 after 2000 episodes caused a minor performance decrease relative to no decay and was not adopted. On 40 generated problems, gamma=0.9 was best: DQN produced lower wire length than A* on 97.5% of problems, and all 40 problems showed non-negative wire-length decrease relative to A*. DQN overflow was zero in all cases, while A* had nonzero overflow on a small proportion of problems. Gamma values 1, 0.95, and 0.8 produced better wire length than A* on 80%, 90%, and 95% of problems, respectively. Reward oscillations increased as gamma increased, and gamma=0.8 showed lower reward plateaus than gamma=1.', 'context': 'The main experiments used 8x8x2 two-layer grid problems with 40 randomly generated problems per case; three of the most congested edges identified from A* solutions had reduced capacity. General parameter tuning used 4x4x2 problems with 3 nets and 2000 episodes. Tested hyperparameters included batch sizes 32, 64, and 128; learning rates 1e-3, 1e-4, and 1e-5; epsilon values 0.02, 0.05, and 0.10; and gamma values 1, 0.95, 0.9, and 0.8. The reward structure penalized random-walk steps and rewarded reaching the target pin, with wire length and overflow as solution-quality metrics. Hardware, software, and TSP/CVRP/ATSP instance distributions are not specified, so the findings are specific to VLSI grid routing and should not be directly generalized to the proposed ICAM vehicle-routing architecture.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4039, 'output_tokens': 7294}, {'corpus_id': '226222332', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper describes the POMO shared-baseline policy-gradient method for training constructive neural solvers. For each problem instance, the policy samples N solution trajectories, computes the total reward or return for each trajectory, and uses a shared baseline derived from the set of N trajectories when computing the REINFORCE-style advantage. The gradient update is based on the advantage term, i.e., the trajectory return minus the shared baseline, and training is performed with mini-batches. This method is contrasted with a greedy-rollout baseline, where each sampled rollout is evaluated against an argmax rollout from the same starting node. The shared baseline is relevant to the proposed ICAM research plan because ICAM’s training scheme explicitly builds on the original POMO loss and multiple-trajectory sampling, and the proposed encoder-decoder model with AAFM and instance-conditioned adaptation would be trained using this same policy-gradient framework.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not report quantitative experimental outcomes such as optimality gaps, solution quality, inference times, or statistical significance. It reports only qualitative outcomes: the POMO shared baseline produces zero-mean advantages, reduces policy-gradient variance compared with the greedy-rollout baseline, avoids the need for a separately trained critic or a cloned greedy policy network, and is argued to reduce premature convergence to suboptimal policies because each trajectory competes with N-1 other heterogeneous trajectories.', 'context': 'The experimental context is reinforcement-learning-based constructive optimization for routing and combinatorial problems, with problem setups mentioned for the traveling salesman problem, capacitated vehicle routing problem, and 0-1 knapsack problem. The excerpt does not specify dataset sizes, hardware, learning rates, batch sizes, number of sampled trajectories, or training duration. The key conditions are that the policy is trained with REINFORCE-style mini-batch updates, multiple sampled trajectories are generated per instance, and the baseline is computed from those trajectories rather than from a critic or greedy rollout. This aligns with the research plan’s use of POMO-style multiple trajectories and POMO loss, while the proposed ICAM method adds a 12-layer attention-free encoder, an instance-conditioned adaptation function, and a top-k elite training stage.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2420, 'output_tokens': 2460}, {'corpus_id': '45328883', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a hybrid genetic search with adaptive diversity control, called HGSADC, on the capacitated vehicle routing problem, which is one of the routing problems targeted by the research plan and corresponds to the classical HGS-type baseline named in the plan. HGSADC is a population-based metaheuristic that maintains feasible and infeasible subpopulations, uses biased fitness ranks that combine penalized cost and diversity contribution, and applies parent selection, crossover, education/local search, and adaptive population management. For the capacitated vehicle routing problem, the paper adapts the multi-period method by replacing the multi-period distance measure with a broken-pairs arc-similarity distance, replacing the multi-period crossover with order crossover on a cyclic giant tour, and limiting the local-search neighborhood to 20 nodes because some instances contain up to 483 customer visits. The method was implemented in C++ and calibrated with a meta-evolutionary parameter optimizer. This is relevant to the proposed Instance-Conditioned Adaptation Model because the proposed model is a constructive neural routing solver for capacitated vehicle routing and other routing problems, and the research plan explicitly compares against HGS-type classical solvers; the paper therefore provides a non-neural capacitated vehicle routing benchmark rather than an encoder-decoder, attention-free, or scale-adaptive constructive method.', 'results': 'On 34 well-known capacitated vehicle routing problem instances, HGSADC achieved an overall average gap to the best known solutions of 0.11%, which the paper reports as equal to the performance of the best highly specialized algorithm in the literature. It also found 12 new best known solutions for a subset of standard benchmark instances. The paper reports that computation times were similar to state-of-the-art methods, although detailed per-instance times are not fully specified in the provided text.', 'context': 'The capacitated vehicle routing problem experiments treated the problem as a special case of the multi-depot periodic vehicle routing problem with one depot and one period. The main algorithmic changes were the broken-pairs distance, order crossover, cyclic route concatenation, and a reduced local-search neighborhood of 20 nodes. Experiments were run on an AMD Opteron 250 processor at 2.4 GHz, and reported CPU times were converted to equivalent Pentium IV 3.0 GHz times. Parameter calibration used a meta-evolutionary optimizer, with most parameters averaged across problem classes, the education rate set to 1, and generation size problem-dependent. The adaptive diversity management scheme was retained for the capacitated vehicle routing problem, and evaluation was based on average gaps to best known solutions and comparisons to state-of-the-art methods on 34 standard capacitated vehicle routing problem instances.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 6892, 'output_tokens': 4259}, {'corpus_id': '225039878', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4183, 'output_tokens': 1346}, {'corpus_id': '13756489', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1842, 'output_tokens': 661}, {'corpus_id': '265154322', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '47017706', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '219150860', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1737, 'output_tokens': 1632}, {'corpus_id': '249038331', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses Neural-3-OPT, an encoder-decoder reinforcement-learning method for improving TSP tours. It starts from a randomly generated initial tour and repeatedly applies 3-opt moves. At each step, an encoder computes an embedding of the current solution and the best solution so far; a link-selector decoder chooses three links to remove, and a reconnection-selector decoder chooses the reconnecting type. The process is formulated as a Markov decision process with state consisting of the current tour and best tour, action consisting of three removed links and a reconnecting type, transition applying the 3-opt move, and reward equal to the improvement in the best tour length. The model is trained with actor-critic policy optimization. This relates to the proposed ICAM method because both use neural encoder-decoder architectures for TSP and both examine generalization from smaller training instances to larger test instances, but the paper method is an improvement/local-search model rather than a constructive routing model and does not include the proposed adaptation function, AAFM, or 12-layer encoder.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than exact numerical values. On TSP200, Neural-3-OPT outperforms L-2-OPT when trained and evaluated on TSP200. For cross-scale transfer, the model trained on TSP50 significantly outperforms L-2-OPT on TSP200, while the model trained on TSP100 performs worse than L-2-OPT on TSP200. The TSP200 training curve also converges faster and reaches a better tour length than L-2-OPT. No optimality gaps, exact tour lengths, or statistical significance values are provided in the excerpt.', 'context': 'Experiments used four randomly generated TSP datasets with 10,000 instances each: TSP20, TSP50, TSP100, and TSP200, with nodes uniformly sampled from the unit square. The TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100 datasets were identical to those used in prior comparisons, and hyperparameters were aligned with a prior baseline: batch size 512, vector dimension 128, layer number 3, loss weights beta_v = 0.5 and beta_H = 0.005, and neighbor size K = 8. Training ran 200 epochs for TSP20, 300 epochs for TSP50 and TSP100, and 500 epochs for TSP200. Reported episode lengths were T1 = 8, T100 = 10, T150 = 20 for TSP20, and T1 = 8, T100 = 10, T200 = 20 for TSP50, TSP100, and TSP200. Evaluation was run on an Intel Xeon CPU and RTX 3090 GPU. The scale range of 20 to 200 nodes is much smaller than the proposed ICAM target of 100 to 5,000 nodes, so the paper provides only a small-scale analogue for cross-scale generalization.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3107, 'output_tokens': 2820}, {'corpus_id': '269214387', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper uses an encoder-decoder Transformer construction heuristic for vehicle routing. It formulates tour construction as a Markov decision process, where the state contains the instance and partial solution and the actions are candidate nodes. The Transformer estimates node probabilities and selects nodes greedily or by sampling. The policy is trained with REINFORCE using negative tour cost as the reward and a baseline function. The proposed cross-problem method pre-trains a Transformer on TSP and then fine-tunes it for OP and PCTSP, either by full fine-tuning of the backbone with problem-specific modules or by adapter-based fine-tuning of lightweight problem-specific modules. This relates to the proposed ICAM because both are encoder-decoder constructive routing models that modify the Transformer to handle different routing tasks, but the paper uses a standard attention-based Transformer and focuses on cross-problem transfer rather than ICAM's attention-free AAFM, 12-layer encoder, and instance-conditioned scale adaptation.", 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not report quantitative outcomes. It states that the method is compared with an attention-model baseline, Gurobi, Compass, Tsili, ILS, and OR-Tools, and that ablations, cross-distribution application, and versatility are evaluated, but no objective values, optimality gaps, statistical significance, or inference times are provided in the text.', 'context': "Experiments use OP and PCTSP instances with 20, 50, and 100 customer nodes. Coordinates are sampled uniformly from the unit square. OP prizes are set to 1; PCTSP prizes and penalties are sampled uniformly. Training uses 2500 batches per epoch with 512 instances per batch, except 256 for n=100. Instances are generated on the fly, validation uses 10,000 instances, and a paired t-test at alpha=5% after each epoch is used to replace the baseline model. Training runs for 100 epochs with learning rate 1e-4. Hardware is an Intel Core i9-10940X CPU and NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080Ti GPU. The scale is small relative to the research plan's target of 100 to 5,000 nodes, and the paper does not describe AAFM, a 12-layer encoder, or large-scale generalization."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2812, 'output_tokens': 3689}, {'corpus_id': '3649804', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper trains a pointer-network TSP solver with an LSTM encoder and up to one attention glimpse, using reinforcement-learning pretraining to construct tours autoregressively and then evaluates greedy decoding, sampling, and active search. The pointer module selects the next city and prevents revisiting, which is analogous to the decoder compatibility calculation in the proposed ICAM. This method is related to the proposed research plan because both use constructive encoder-decoder neural routing models for TSP, but the paper uses a small LSTM/pointer architecture and standard RL pretraining rather than ICAM's 12-layer attention-free encoder, instance-conditioned adaptation function, or large-scale CVRP/ATSP training.", 'results': 'On 1,000 test graphs for TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100, RL pretraining significantly improves over supervised pointer-network training, and all evaluated RL methods surpass Christofides, including greedy decoding without search. Greedy methods are time-efficient and are only a few percent worse than optimal solutions. Inference-time search improves solution quality but increases running time, and early stopping gives a small performance tradeoff. RL pretraining-Sampling and RL pretraining-Active Search are the most competitive learned methods and recover optimal tours in a significant number of cases. For small solution spaces, Sampling with a finetuned softmax temperature outperforms Active Search, while for larger solution spaces Active Search is superior when controlling for the number of sampled solutions or running time. Many RL pretraining methods outperform OR-Tools local search, including RL pretraining-Greedy@16 with similar speed, but the learned methods are less competitive than tabu search and guided local search. Active Search from an untrained model is competitive but requires 7 hours per TSP50 instance and 25 hours per TSP100 instance.', 'context': 'Experiments use Euclidean TSP instances with 20, 50, and 100 nodes, points drawn uniformly in the unit square, and 1,000 test graphs per scale. Training uses mini-batches of 128, LSTM cells with 128 hidden units, 128-dimensional coordinate embeddings, Adam with initial learning rate 1e-3 for TSP20/TSP50 and 1e-4 for TSP100, decayed by 0.96 every 5,000 steps, uniform initialization in [-0.08, 0.08], and gradient L2 clipping to 1.0. The model uses up to one attention glimpse; Active Search uses baseline decay alpha=0.99. Greedy methods run on a single Nvidia Tesla K80 GPU, while Concorde and LKH run on an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3 3.50 GHz CPU and OR-Tools on an Intel Haswell CPU. The paper does not test the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, attention-free module, 12-layer encoder, or large-scale 1,000+ node generalization, so it serves as a small-scale RL constructive TSP baseline rather than direct validation of ICAM.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3777, 'output_tokens': 4421}, {'corpus_id': '248377568', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies N2S, an encoder-decoder neural policy for pickup/delivery TSP variants. The encoder uses Synth-Att to produce node embeddings, and the decoder uses a node-pair reinsertion decoder; a diversity-enhanced variant, N2S-A, is also evaluated. Training uses proximal policy optimization with a critic and curriculum learning, starting from randomly sequentially constructed initial solutions. This is related to the proposed ICAM because both use an encoder-decoder architecture for routing solution construction, and both examine efficient encoding alternatives to standard attention: the paper’s Synth-Att ablation tests replacing standard attention with a cheaper encoding module, analogous to ICAM’s AAFM, and the paper’s generalization tests to larger and differently distributed instances align with ICAM’s cross-scale generalization goal, although the paper does not use the proposed adaptation function or AAFM and targets PDTSP/PDTSP-LIFO rather than TSP/CVRP/ATSP.', 'results': 'In the encoding ablation on 2,000 PDTSP-51 instances with 3,000 steps on one GPU, Synth-Att achieved slightly smaller gaps than DAC-Att while using much fewer computation costs, and achieved considerably lower gaps than vanilla-Att with only slight extra computation costs. The excerpt does not provide numerical gap, time, or parameter values. In generalization evaluation on benchmark instances up to 200 nodes, N2S-A significantly outperformed the neural baseline Heter-POMO-A in all reported cases, using best and average gaps over 10 independent runs, but was slightly inferior to the LKH3 solver. Training times were about 1 day for 21 nodes, 3 days for 51 nodes, and 7 days for 101 nodes, reported as shorter than baselines.', 'context': 'Experiments used PDTSP and PDTSP-LIFO instances with 21, 51, and 101 nodes generated uniformly in the unit square for training-scale evaluation, and benchmark instances up to 200 nodes with different sizes and node distributions for generalization. Hardware was a server with 8 RTX 2080 Ti GPUs and an Intel E5-2680 CPU at 2.4 GHz; the encoding ablation used one GPU. Hyperparameters included 200 epochs, 20 batches per epoch, batch size 600, 5-step PPO with 250 training steps, 3 minibatch updates, clip threshold 0.1, Adam learning rates 8e-5 for policy and 2e-5 for critic with 0.985 per-epoch decay, discount factor 0.999, gradient norm clipping at 0.05, 0.15, or 0.35 by size, and curriculum coefficients 2, 1.5, and 1 for the three sizes. Generalization gaps were reported over 10 independent runs.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3044, 'output_tokens': 4324}, {'corpus_id': '59608816', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '46892983', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates an encoder-decoder neural constructive solver for capacitated vehicle routing. The encoder embeds customer and depot coordinates and demands, and the decoder uses an attention-based compatibility score to select the next customer or depot at each construction step. Training uses policy gradient methods with an actor-critic formulation, specifically asynchronous advantage actor-critic, while inference uses either greedy decoding or beam search with beam widths of 5 and 10. Feasibility is enforced through masking: nodes with zero demand cannot be selected, all customer nodes are masked when the vehicle load is exactly zero, and customers whose demand exceeds the remaining load are masked, so split deliveries are not allowed in the main experiments. The method is tested on randomly generated capacitated VRP instances with customer and depot locations in the unit square, integer demands from 1 to 9, and varied vehicle capacities. Evaluation uses optimality gaps for small instances, pairwise winning rates for larger instances, and solution-time comparisons against Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized variants of these heuristics, and OR-Tools.', 'results': 'For VRP10 and VRP20, beam search with width 10 was the best-performing decoder. Roughly 95% of VRP10 instances were at most 10% away from optimal, and VRP20 instances were at most 13% away from optimal, with outliers within 20-25% of optimality. For VRP50 and VRP100, the neural methods outperformed the classical heuristics in almost all instances. In the reported pairwise winning-rate results, RL-BS(10) beat RL-Greedy in 91.9% of instances and RL-BS(5) in 57.7%; RL-BS(5) beat RL-Greedy in 85.8%; and the OR-Tools comparisons were 41.5% for RL-Greedy, 54.6% for RL-BS(5), and 60.2% for RL-BS(10). Runtime analysis showed that the log of solution time divided by the number of nodes remained nearly constant for the RL decoders, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep grew faster than linearly. The neural framework was faster than the randomized heuristics on larger instances, although exact timing comparison with OR-Tools was limited because OR-Tools was implemented in C++ while the other baselines were implemented in Python.', 'context': 'The experiments used 1000 instances per problem size for VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100. Node and depot locations were sampled uniformly in the unit square, demands were discrete uniform integers from 1 to 9, and split deliveries were disallowed. Decoding used greedy selection or beam search with beam widths 5 and 10. Training used standard policy gradient/A3C actor-critic methods, with implementation details deferred to the appendix. Baselines included Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized variants, and OR-Tools; optimal solutions for small instances were obtained using a mixed-integer formulation. Hardware and full hyperparameter settings were not specified in the provided sections. This method is directly related to the proposed ICAM research plan because it is also an encoder-decoder constructive routing model with decoder compatibility scoring and beam search, but it relies on conventional attention-based scoring and policy-gradient training rather than the proposed attention-free AAFM, instance-conditioned adaptation function, deeper 12-layer encoder, and three-stage cross-scale training aimed at generalizing from 100 to 5000 node instances.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3790, 'output_tokens': 5475}, {'corpus_id': '211069726', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates AM-D, a dynamic encoder-decoder attention model for capacitated vehicle routing. It uses a REINFORCE-based constructive policy in which an encoder builds node representations from depot and customer embeddings and a message stream, while a decoder with dynamic attention constructs routes incrementally. Training is performed for 30 epochs with 10,000 batches per epoch, and testing uses greedy rollout on 10,000 instances, reporting average solution length. This method is related to the proposed ICAM because both are encoder-decoder constructive neural routing models for VRP and both examine cross-scale generalization, but the paper’s model relies on dynamic attention rather than the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function and attention-free AAFM module.', 'results': 'AM-D improved over the static attention model AM by 2.02% on VRP20, 2.01% on VRP50, and 2.55% on VRP100, and was reported to significantly outperform other baseline models. In cross-scale generalization tests, models trained on 20, 50, or 100 customer nodes were evaluated on other scales. The paper reports that models trained on smaller instances performed well on larger instances and models trained on larger instances also performed well on smaller instances, with some results better than baseline methods, but exact numerical values for these cross-scale rows are not provided in the supplied text.', 'context': 'Instances were generated from a fixed distribution with node locations sampled uniformly from the unit square [0, 1] x [0, 1], customer demands drawn uniformly from {1, ..., 9}, depot demand set to 0, and vehicle capacities of 30 for VRP20, 40 for VRP50, and 50 for VRP100. Batch size was 128 and learning rate 1e-4 for VRP20 and VRP50, while batch size was 108 and learning rate 5e-5 for VRP100. Experiments used a single 1080Ti GPU for VRP20 and VRP50 and three 1080Ti GPUs for VRP100. The evaluation metric was average route length over 10,000 test instances. These experimental conditions are smaller-scale than the proposed research plan’s 100 to 5,000 node evaluation and do not include the proposed adaptation function, AAFM, or three-stage training scheme.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2550, 'output_tokens': 1637}, {'corpus_id': '263909317', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper studies LEHD, a constructive neural combinatorial optimization model with a light encoder and heavy decoder for TSP and CVRP. It learns to construct partial solutions during training using supervised learning, then generates complete solutions by greedy decoding at inference, and optionally applies Random Re-Construct (RRC), an inference-time improvement procedure that repeatedly reconstructs parts of the solution for 100, 300, 500, or 1000 iterations. The method is evaluated on uniformly distributed TSP and CVRP instances of sizes 100 to 1000, real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances, and 128 large TSP instances of sizes 200, 500, and 1000. Baselines include POMO, BQ, EAS, SGBS, LKH3, Concorde, SO-mixed, and H-TSP. This is related to the proposed ICAM method because both are encoder-decoder constructive routing solvers aimed at large-scale TSP/CVRP generalization, but LEHD uses a light encoder/heavy decoder and RRC rather than ICAM's proposed 12-layer attention-free encoder, AAFM, and instance-conditioned adaptation function.", 'results': 'On uniform TSP, LEHD greedy inference is fast, and with 100 RRC iterations it outperforms other learning-based methods; with more than 500 RRC iterations it reaches near-optimal gaps of nearly 0% for TSP100 and TSP200, less than 0.2% for TSP500, and around 0.8% for TSP1000. On uniform CVRP, with 300 RRC iterations LEHD outperforms most learning-based methods on all instances except EAS on CVRP100, which requires much longer inference time; LEHD outperforms LKH3 on CVRP100-CVRP500 with 500 RRC iterations and is about 1% behind LKH3 on CVRP1000 with 1000 RRC iterations. On real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances, LEHD with greedy inference and 1000 RRC iterations outperforms POMO and BQ on all reported instances. In the large TSP comparison on 128 instances, LEHD RRC100 achieves 0.0761% gap in 1.2 minutes on TSP200, 0.343% in 8 minutes on TSP500, and 1.218% in 43 minutes on TSP1000, compared with SO-mixed gaps of 0.636%, 2.401%, and 2.800% with times of 21, 32, and 56 minutes, and H-TSP with LKH-3 gap of 4.06% in 7.5 minutes on TSP1000; Concorde is 0.000% but takes 3 minutes, 32 minutes, and 7.8 hours respectively. LEHD RRC100 has a lower gap than H-TSP on TSP1000 but a longer runtime. POMO trained with supervised learning on TSP100 performs well at TSP100 but poorly on large-scale problems, and reinforcement learning with the POMO strategy does not converge in a reasonable time, while supervised training of LEHD on TSP50 under the same budget is more effective. No statistical significance tests are reported in the provided excerpt.', 'context': "Experimental conditions include greedy inference and RRC budgets of 100, 300, 500, or 1000 iterations; large TSP comparisons use 128 instances per size and report optimality gap relative to Concorde or LKH3 plus wall-clock inference time. The excerpt does not specify hardware, training epochs, batch size, learning rate, or full implementation details, so reproducibility is limited to the reported inference settings and problem sizes. The paper's central context is that heavy decoder construction, rather than supervised learning alone, contributes to large-scale generalization, and that RRC improves solution quality at the cost of additional inference time. This context is directly relevant to the ICAM research plan, which targets cross-scale generalization on TSP/CVRP/ATSP, compares against POMO and classical solvers, and uses optimality gaps and inference times as metrics, but ICAM additionally proposes a deeper attention-free encoder and instance-conditioned adaptation that are not present in LEHD."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3235, 'output_tokens': 5576}, {'corpus_id': '257365174', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1720, 'output_tokens': 713}, {'corpus_id': '221191399', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '165163853', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper formulates optimization as a rewriting problem and trains a neural rewriter with reinforcement learning. Starting from an initial feasible solution, the method iteratively computes a state-dependent set of local regions, selects one region using a region-picking policy, selects an applicable local rewriting rule using a rule-picking policy, and applies that rule to the selected region to produce an improved solution. This is an improvement-based neural optimization approach rather than a constructive one. It relates to the proposed ICAM research plan because both target neural methods for vehicle routing and concern generalization across problem instances, but the paper’s method modifies an existing solution through local rewrites, whereas the proposed method constructs routes with an encoder-decoder model, an instance-conditioned adaptation function, and an attention-free module.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not report quantitative vehicle routing outcomes, optimality gaps, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance. It only indicates that the paper contains vehicle routing experiments and generalization results, but the numerical results are not included in the provided text.', 'context': 'The experimental context provided is limited to the evaluation setup: all algorithms were run on the same server with 2 Quadro GP100 GPUs and 80 CPU cores; neural networks used 1 GPU, search algorithms used 4 CPU cores, and search algorithms had a timeout of 10 seconds per instance. Neural networks were implemented in PyTorch. The excerpt does not specify vehicle routing instance sizes, training details, hyperparameters, or evaluation metrics.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2814, 'output_tokens': 2589}, {'corpus_id': '229516324', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '5692837', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a sequence-to-sequence pointer-style model for planar combinatorial geometry tasks, including convex hull, Delaunay triangulation, and the Travelling Salesman Problem. Inputs are planar point sets P with n Cartesian points sampled uniformly from [0,1]x[0,1], and outputs are sequences C_P representing the corresponding solution. The supplied architecture section states that all models used a single-layer LSTM with either 256 or 512 hidden units, trained with stochastic gradient descent at a learning rate of 1.0, batch size 128, random uniform weight initialization from -0.08 to 0.08, and L2 gradient clipping of 2.0. The authors deliberately avoided extensive architecture or hyperparameter search and used the same architecture across all problems. This relates to the proposed ICAM method because both approaches formulate solution construction as sequence generation over node or point inputs, but the paper uses a shallow LSTM pointer architecture without instance-conditioned adaptation, attention-free modules, or a deeper encoder, whereas the proposed method uses a 12-layer encoder, an adaptation function, and an attention-free decoder-compatible architecture for large-scale routing.', 'results': 'No quantitative experimental outcomes are provided in the supplied excerpt. The Empirical Results section is empty, so no objective values, optimality gaps, baseline comparisons, inference times, or statistical significance can be reported. The only reported observation is that overfitting was observed in some cases where the task was simpler, specifically for small n.', 'context': 'Training used 1M generated input-output pairs. Inputs were planar point sets with n points sampled from a uniform distribution over [0,1]x[0,1]. The model used a single-layer LSTM with either 256 or 512 hidden units, stochastic gradient descent with learning rate 1.0, batch size 128, uniform weight initialization from -0.08 to 0.08, and L2 gradient clipping of 2.0. The exact value of n, hardware, evaluation metrics, and baseline comparisons are not specified in the supplied excerpt. The authors intentionally kept hyperparameters fixed across problems to strengthen the paper message, which contrasts with the proposed research plan that includes systematic ablations over model depth, AAFM versus standard attention, training stages, and the learnable adaptation parameter alpha.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2077, 'output_tokens': 2466}, {'corpus_id': '215827955', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper implements an attention-based REINFORCE encoder-decoder router for sequencing instTerm pairs in track-assignment detailed routing. Problem files containing instTerm locations and net information are parsed; a track assigner fixes instTerm locations, a pin decomposer converts nets into instTerm pairs, and each pair is represented as a graph node with coordinates and net index. Instances are padded to a common graph size using Pad Empty all-zero nodes. The encoder-decoder uses multi-head attention as a message-passing mechanism over relative spatial information and constructs a permutation of instTerm pairs; a rollout baseline and REINFORCE gradient estimator optimize a weighted sum of wirelength and openings, where openings are instTerm pairs left unconnected, and the exact weights are not specified in the provided text. A genetic algorithm baseline sequences the same pairs using chromosomes as permutations, with fitness set to the negative cost, partially matched crossover, swap mutation, 10 generations, population 10, elites 4, and one mutation. This is related to the proposed ICAM because both are encoder-decoder constructive routing solvers, but the paper relies on standard multi-head attention and REINFORCE, whereas ICAM proposes an attention-free AAFM and an instance-conditioned adaptation function to improve cross-scale generalization and efficiency. The paper's decoder sequence construction is also a direct contrast to ICAM's decoder compatibility calculation, where the adaptation function is integrated.", 'results': "On Small500 and Large500, the genetic router achieved lower cost than the attention router in almost all problems, but the attention router's cost was close, with most Small500 differences within 100 cost units. On Large500, the attention router outperformed GA in more cases, though GA remained better overall. The attention router's training and test performance were similar, indicating it could solve unseen problems, but the paper does not report a formal statistical test comparing solution quality between the two routers. Training cost varied because of REINFORCE delayed reward, and the rollout baseline reduced but did not eliminate variation. Runtime was more than two orders of magnitude faster for attention: Small500 GA took more than 10 seconds per problem while attention took less than 0.1 seconds; Large500 GA took close to 100 seconds per problem while attention took slightly more than 0.1 seconds. Training on Small5000 improved attention quality substantially, approaching GA. These results relate to the research plan by showing that an attention-based constructive router can generalize to unseen instances and accelerate routing, but also that multi-head attention-based REINFORCE may leave quality gaps and training instability, motivating ICAM's AAFM and scale-conditioned adaptation.", 'context': "Experiments used analog IC detailed routing problem sets on sub-16 nm commercial technologies. Small set contained Comparator and OpAmp placements with 10 to 100 instTerms per problem; Large set contained ADC placements with 100 to 1000 instTerms per problem. Experiments included Small100, Small500, Small5000, Large100, and Large500, with 60 percent training, 20 percent validation, and 20 percent test. Attention training used Python and PyTorch, batch size 5, 100 epochs, rollout baseline updated by a one-sided t-test, and Pad Empty padding; the implementation text also reports B=20 training batches and T=5 batch size. Training took about 6 minutes per epoch for Small500 and 25 minutes per epoch for Large500 on an Intel Core i7-6850 CPU workstation. GA used limited generations to control runtime. This context relates to ICAM's cross-scale evaluation because the paper tests small-to-large instTerm counts analogous to node-scale generalization, but the domain is IC detailed routing rather than vehicle routing, and the paper does not use the proposed adaptation function or attention-free AAFM."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5620, 'output_tokens': 5611}, {'corpus_id': '212633747', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper provides a background description of policy-based reinforcement learning methods for combinatorial optimization, including policy gradient, REINFORCE, actor-critic, PPO, and DDPG. It explains how a parametric policy is trained by sampling trajectories, estimating returns, using baselines or bootstrapped value estimates, and updating policy parameters. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because ICAM is a constructive routing solver whose decoder policy would be trained to select nodes or routes, and the plan's POMO/top-k training and comparisons to RL-based constructive methods are in the same policy-based RL setting. The paper does not describe the proposed encoder-decoder, AAFM, adaptation function, or 12-layer encoder.", 'results': 'No experimental outcomes are reported in the provided text. There are no quantitative objective values, optimality gaps, inference times, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance for TSP, CVRP, ATSP, or any other problem. The excerpt is conceptual and does not evaluate the described RL algorithms.', 'context': 'No experimental conditions are reported. The excerpt does not specify datasets, instance sizes, hardware, hyperparameters, training schedules, or evaluation metrics. It only gives formal definitions of MDPs, value functions, Bellman equations, and policy optimization algorithms, and positions them as general RL approaches for combinatorial optimization.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4090, 'output_tokens': 4748}, {'corpus_id': '229331629', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper evaluates a hybrid genetic search method for the capacitated vehicle routing problem, specifically a modern HGS-CVRP implementation using the Swap* neighborhood, and compares it with the original HGS-2012 and several other heuristics: HILS, LKH-3, KGLS, SISR, FILO, and the guided local search variant of OR-Tools. The experimental approach is a convergence-profile evaluation: each algorithm is run on 100 classical CVRP benchmark instances for a time limit proportional to instance size, and the best objective value is recorded at 1%, 2%, 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 30%, 50%, 75%, and 100% of the time limit. Ten independent runs are performed, and performance is measured by average solution value and percentage gap to best-known solutions. This method is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because HGS-CVRP is a classical CVRP baseline that the plan intends to compare against, and the paper's CVRPLIB-style gap and time-quality protocol is directly relevant to benchmarking ICAM's constructive neural solver on CVRP instances.", 'results': 'In the reported table excerpt covering 18 of the 100 instances, HGS-CVRP obtains an average gap of about 0.005 percentage points to best-known solutions, with 16 instances at 0.00% gap. Examples include X-n101-k25 at 27591.0 with 0.00% gap, X-n125-k30 at 55539.0 with 0.00% gap, X-n129-k18 at 28940.0 with 0.00% gap, X-n134-k13 at 10916.0 with 0.00% gap, X-n143-k7 at 15700.0 with 0.00% gap, X-n148-k46 at 43448.0 with 0.00% gap, X-n153-k22 at 21225.0 with 0.02% gap, X-n167-k10 at 20557.0 with 0.00% gap, X-n172-k51 at 45607.0 with 0.00% gap, and X-n176-k26 at 47812.0 with 0.00% gap. HGS-CVRP matches or improves HGS-2012 on the visible instances, with a clear improvement on X-n106-k14, where HGS-CVRP reaches 26381.4 with 0.07% gap versus 26408.8 with 0.18% gap for HGS-2012. It is generally stronger than OR-Tools, whose gaps range from 0.48% to 3.03% in the excerpt, and also generally stronger than LKH-3, HILS, KGLS, SISR, and FILO, often tying the best non-neural methods at the best-known solution.', 'context': "Experiments use 100 classical CVRP benchmark instances, with customer counts from 100 to 1000 in the described setup. The time limit is Tmax = n x 240/100 seconds, so 100-customer instances run for 4 minutes and 1000-customer instances run for 40 minutes. Runs are single-threaded on an Intel Gold 6148 Skylake 2.4 GHz processor with 40 GB of RAM and CentOS 7.8.2003. HGS-2012, HGS-CVRP, HILS, LKH-3, FILO, and OR-Tools are compiled in C/C++ with g++ 9.1.0, while KGLS and SISR use Java OpenJDK 13.0.1. Ten independent seeds are used; deterministic KGLS and OR-Tools are varied by permuting customer order. Gap is computed as 100 x (z - z_BKS) / z_BKS using public best-known solution values. This context is relevant to ICAM because the research plan also targets CVRPLIB-style CVRP evaluation, optimality gaps, and large-scale instances, but the paper's method is a classical metaheuristic rather than a neural encoder-decoder, so it serves as a strong baseline rather than a direct architectural comparison."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5035, 'output_tokens': 6498}, {'corpus_id': '206594692', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies how increasing network depth affects optimization and generalization by training plain and residual convolutional networks on CIFAR-10 and ImageNet. For CIFAR-10, the authors build 20-, 32-, 44-, 56-, 110-, and 1202-layer networks using 3x3 convolutions, batch normalization, global average pooling, and a 10-way softmax head. Residual versions add identity shortcut connections between paired 3x3 layers so that the residual and plain models have the same depth, width, and parameter count. Training uses SGD with a batch size of 128 on two GPUs, weight decay of 0.0001, momentum of 0.9, no dropout, and a learning rate of 0.1 with step decay at 32k and 48k iterations for 64k total iterations. A 45k/5k train/validation split is used to determine training termination. For the 110-layer model, a learning-rate warm-up of 0.01 is used until training error falls below 80%, after which training resumes at 0.1. The ImageNet setup uses SGD with batch size 256, learning rate 0.1 with plateau-based decay, training for up to 600,000 iterations, random resized 224x224 crops, horizontal flip, color augmentation, and batch normalization. Evaluation uses 10-crop testing for comparison and multi-scale fully convolutional inference for best results. This relates to the proposed ICAM method because ICAM uses a deeper 12-layer encoder and includes an ablation of model depth; the paper provides a controlled study of how depth scaling affects training stability and representation quality, although it is in image classification rather than vehicle routing.', 'results': "On CIFAR-10, plain networks show higher training error as depth increases, while residual networks reduce this optimization difficulty and improve test accuracy with depth. The 110-layer residual network converges well and reaches 6.43% test error, reported as among state-of-the-art for that setting despite using fewer parameters than FitNet and Highway. A 1202-layer residual network reaches training error below 0.1% and 7.93% test error, indicating that extreme depth can be optimized but may overfit the small dataset without stronger regularization. Layer-response analysis shows residual layers have smaller standard deviations than plain layers, and deeper residual networks have even smaller response magnitudes. On ImageNet, residual networks outperform plain counterparts and support deeper bottleneck architectures, indicating that depth can improve representation when optimization is managed. These outcomes are relevant to ICAM's depth ablation: they suggest that a 12-layer encoder may be beneficial if training stability is maintained, but also caution that deeper encoders can overfit when data, regularization, or training scale are limited.", 'context': 'CIFAR-10 contains 50k training images and 10k test images in 10 classes. Inputs are 32x32 images with per-pixel mean subtraction. The CIFAR-10 architecture uses feature map sizes of 32x32, 16x16, and 8x8 with 16, 32, and 64 filters respectively, and identity shortcuts are used so residual models match plain models in depth, width, and parameters. The 1202-layer model has 19.4M parameters and is trained without maxout or dropout, so its weaker test performance relative to the 110-layer model is attributed to overfitting. Hardware for CIFAR-10 training is two GPUs. The ImageNet setup uses a larger batch size of 256, longer training, and multi-scale test inference. These conditions matter for the ICAM research plan because the proposed routing solver will train on generated instances of varying scale and will ablate encoder depth; the paper shows that depth experiments should control parameter count, use validation-based stopping, consider warm-up for deeper models, and account for overfitting when the model is much larger than the training data or when regularization is weak.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3645, 'output_tokens': 4234}]): Request timed out.
Error processing ('We will implement a progressive training approach: (1) Stage 1 - warm-up training on small-scale instances (100 nodes) for stability, (2) Stage 2 - varying-scale training on instances with scales randomly sampled from uniform distribution [100,500] to learn cross-scale features, and (3) Stage 3 - top-k elite training focusing on the best k trajectories with a joint loss function combining original POMO loss and a new top-k loss; this training scheme is intended to teach scale-independent features and refine solution quality before large-scale evaluation.', [{'corpus_id': '235898959', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1805, 'output_tokens': 439}, {'corpus_id': '269930049', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies a prompt-learning method for cross-distribution vehicle routing. A fixed pretrained attention encoder-decoder, originally trained on uniform CVRP instances of size 100, is used as both feature extractor and solution constructor. For each instance, the feature extractor produces a feature vector that selects one key from a pool of 16 key-prompt pairs; the associated prompt, consisting of 5 prompted tokens per encoder layer across 6 multi-head attention layers with embedding size 128, is inserted into the frozen model. The prompted model constructs a tour, and reinforcement learning updates only the selected prompt using the resulting solution reward. Training exposes the prompts sequentially to 341 distributions with problem sizes 50 to 200, including uniform and clustered Gaussian geometries. The paper also evaluates top-k prompt inference, where the best k matched prompts are used, and compares against heuristic solvers, single-distribution POMO, diverse-distribution POMO, and meta-learning methods.', 'results': 'On zero-shot generalization to 12 new distributions, the prompt method achieved average gap 3.10 percent without augmentation, compared with 3.97 percent for meta-learning POMO and 4.36 percent for the omni meta-learning method. With data augmentation, the prompt method achieved average gap 1.72 percent, compared with 2.50 percent and 2.76 percent respectively. On training distributions, the paper reports that prompt learning outperforms the meta-learning methods and single-distribution POMO on problem sizes 50, 100, and 200, and that using the top 8 matched prompts ranks first across all three sizes. The paper also reports lower training cost than second-order meta-learning: about 1 day on a 25 GB GPU after adjusting for larger batch size, versus several days and much larger memory for meta-learning baselines.', 'context': 'The experimental setting uses CVRP instances of sizes 50, 100, and 200, batch size 64, 1,000 instances per epoch, up to 10,000 epochs, learning rate decay from 1e-3 to 1e-5, and about 2.5 days on a single 11 GB GPU for the reported prompt training. Heuristic baselines were run with time limits of 5 seconds and 10 seconds for sizes 50 and 100, and 20 seconds and 60 seconds for size 200. This paper is related to the proposed progressive training method because it tests adaptation of a 100-node pretrained routing model to other scales and distributions using lightweight trainable components and top-k selection. However, it is not a direct validation of the proposed method: its largest scale is 200 nodes, its top-k mechanism selects prompts rather than elite trajectories, and it does not use a staged 100-to-500 scale curriculum or a joint POMO plus top-k trajectory loss.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4230, 'output_tokens': 3618}, {'corpus_id': '247595029', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a controlled comparison of supervised learning and reinforcement learning within a neural TSP pipeline to study how training paradigm and inference search affect generalization. Identical model configurations are trained on small TSP instances, specifically TSP20-50, and then evaluated on smaller and larger instance sizes, including TSP200, which is described as intractable to learn from directly. Supervised learning is implemented with teacher forcing against an optimal solver, while reinforcement learning uses a rollout baseline that encourages better greedy behavior. Inference is evaluated under three decoding regimes: greedy decoding, beam search, and sampling. The paper also compares against a non-learned furthest insertion heuristic baseline, which constructively builds a partial tour by inserting the node whose distance to the nearest already-inserted tour node is maximized. This method is related to the proposed progressive training approach and the ICAM research plan because both focus on learning from small or medium-scale instances to improve performance on larger instances, and both emphasize the interaction between training objectives, model confidence, and solution search during large-scale evaluation.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than numerical values. With greedy decoding, reinforcement-learning-trained models perform better than supervised-learning-trained models on the training size and on larger graphs. With beam search or sampling, supervised-learning-trained models perform better than reinforcement-learning-trained models. The reinforcement-learning rollout baseline produces very confident next-node predictions, even outside the training size range, whereas supervised learning with teacher forcing produces less confident predictions and weaker greedy decoding, but a probability distribution that is more amenable to beam search and sampling. No quantitative optimality gaps, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance values are provided in the supplied text.', 'context': 'The experiments are ablations of a base model configuration and are designed to compare state-of-the-art ideas fairly in terms of model capacity and training data. The training data are small TSP instances, TSP20-50, and the evaluation includes larger instances such as TSP200. The key experimental condition is that the same model architecture is used for both supervised and reinforcement learning, so the observed differences are attributed to the learning paradigm and decoding method rather than model capacity. The furthest insertion heuristic serves as a simple non-learned baseline for assessing whether the neural models generalize reasonably. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM method because the proposed three-stage training scheme, including warm-up on 100-node instances, varying-scale training from 100 to 500 nodes, and top-k elite training with a POMO-based loss, also aims to improve cross-scale generalization and solution quality before large-scale evaluation. The paper’s findings suggest that such a training scheme should be evaluated not only with greedy decoding but also with beam search or sampling, since the relative strength of reinforcement-learning-style and supervised-style training can depend on the inference search strategy.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2393, 'output_tokens': 3016}, {'corpus_id': '258988016', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a MAML-style meta-learning framework applied to POMO for TSP and CVRP. It defines each task by instance size n and distribution d, samples a batch of tasks with a hierarchical task scheduler that estimates task hardness and updates task weights, then performs inner-loop adaptation of a task-specific model and outer-loop update of a shared meta-model. A first-order approximation is used after an initial second-order phase to reduce cost. This is related to the proposed progressive training scheme because both expose POMO to multiple scales and distributions to learn scale-independent features, but the paper uses meta-adaptation and task scheduling rather than staged warm-up, uniform [100,500] scale sampling, or a top-k elite trajectory loss.', 'results': 'The excerpt reports that the meta-learning method achieves superior performance to POMO, AMDKD-POMO, and Meta-POMO on cross-size and cross-distribution generalization. Cross-distribution tests use size 200 with unseen distributions, including rotation and explosion; cross-size tests use size 300 with training distributions. Few-shot evaluation fine-tunes the meta-model for K=1 steps using 1,000 extra instances from the test task. The paper states that t-tests at a 5% threshold are used when method averages are close, but the supplied text does not include the numerical optimality gaps or runtime values from Table 1. It also reports that pretrained POMO and AMDKD-POMO models show severe generalization issues, and that the first-order variant is substantially cheaper than the full second-order variant.', 'context': "Experiments are on synthetic TSP and CVRP instances. The training task set contains 341 tasks with sizes 50 to 200 and uniform or Gaussian mixture distributions. All methods are retrained for about 32 million instances; POMO uses about 500,000 gradient updates, while the meta-learning method uses about 250,000 meta-iterations because each iteration uses two instance batches. Adam is used with learning rates alpha and beta equal to 1e-4, weight decay 1e-6, and a 10x decay in the last 10% of training. Batch size is 64, or 32 for sizes larger than 150. B=K=1, scheduler eta=1, Es=225,000, task weights updated every 100 iterations, and first-order approximation starts after 50,000 iterations. Full second-order training requires about 5 days and 53 GB GPU memory for TSP and 6 days and 71 GB for CVRP, while first-order training requires about 2.5 days and 17 GB for TSP and 3 days and 25 GB for CVRP. Inference uses greedy rollout with 8 instance augmentations on 1,000 test instances, reports total solve time, and computes gaps relative to Concorde for TSP and HGS for CVRP. This context supports the research plan's large-scale generalization goal, but the paper's size range only extends to 200 in training and 300 in cross-size testing, so it is a smaller-scale analogue to the proposed 100 to 500 progressive training and 1,000+ node evaluation."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4085, 'output_tokens': 4524}, {'corpus_id': '279119999', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a large-scale VRP method built around TTPL/LLM-AHD, in which a TTPL-designed projection is applied to a constructive solver reported as LEHD and then augmented with Multi-View Decision Fusion and a 1,000-iteration RRC refinement procedure. The method is inference-time rather than a progressive training scheme: it uses greedy construction or sampling to build solutions, applies the designed projection, fuses multiple decision views, and refines solutions with RRC. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because it targets the same problem of improving TSP/CVRP solution quality at large scales, but it is not the proposed Stage 1 warm-up, Stage 2 varying-scale, or Stage 3 top-k elite training approach; it is closer to a test-time projection/refinement baseline or complement for large-scale evaluation.', 'results': 'On synthetic TSP instances, the method with purely greedy construction outperforms other greedy constructive methods on most scales except TSP1K; with RRC for 1000 iterations it is best on all TSP scales and reports optimality-gap reductions of 61.11%, 75.59%, 56.2%, 69.61%, and 68.29% on TSP1K, 5K, 10K, 50K, and 100K relative to the leading alternative. On synthetic CVRP, greedy sampling is best on all scales except CVRP1K; with RRC it surpasses all baselines and reports optimality-gap reductions of 53.98%, 85.99%, 86.20%, and 88.37% on CVRP5K, 10K, 50K, and 100K. On TSPLIB/CVRPLIB, TTPL alone achieves 5.80% overall TSP gap and 13.19% overall CVRP gap; TTPL-MVDF achieves 3.88% TSP and 10.97% CVRP; TTPL-MVDF with RRC1000 achieves 1.37% TSP and 7.50% CVRP, solving 33/33 TSPLIB and 14/14 CVRPLIB instances. The best competing benchmark values reported are INViT greedy at 11.04% TSP and 20.49% CVRP, and LEHD RRC1000 at 7.37% TSP and 11.04% CVRP.', 'context': 'Experiments use TSP/CVRP instances at 1K, 5K, 10K, 50K, and 100K nodes for synthetic evaluation, and TSPLIB/CVRPLIB benchmark groups with 1K<n≤5K and n>5K for TSP and 1K<n≤7K and n>7K for CVRP. The reported hardware is a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPU with 24GB memory. The main refinement setting is RRC with 1000 iterations, and inference uses greedy construction or greedy sampling. Some baselines report OOM or incomplete solved counts, while the TTPL-MVDF RRC1000 setting reports full solved counts. No statistical significance tests are reported in the provided excerpt.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4590, 'output_tokens': 6007}, {'corpus_id': '257353537', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2490, 'output_tokens': 1316}, {'corpus_id': '255546018', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a single-stage constructive neural routing model trained by imitation of expert trajectories with a cross-entropy loss. The model is trained on synthetic 100-node instances for Euclidean TSP, CVRP, OP, and ATSP, using expert solutions generated by Concorde for TSP, LKH for ATSP and CVRP, and EA4OP for OP. For TSP, training trajectories are formed by sampling sub-paths of length n between 4 and N from optimal tours, so that any sub-path is treated as an optimal path-TSP sub-problem; a similar sub-path or sub-sequence sampling strategy is used for CVRP, OP, and KP. The model has 9 layers, 12 attention heads, embedding size 192, and feed-forward dimension 512. Training uses batches of 1024 for 500 epochs with Adam, initial learning rate 7.5e-4, and decay 0.98 every 50 epochs. At test time, the model is evaluated with greedy decoding and beam search, and non-Perceiver variants limit the subgraph to the 250 nearest neighbors of the origin node. Evaluation is performed on synthetic instances of sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, with CVRP capacities of 50, 80, 100, and 250, as well as TSPLib instances up to 4461 nodes and Euclidean CVRPLib instances. The reported metrics are optimality gap and inference time, with TSP gaps computed against Concorde and CVRP gaps computed against LKH reference solutions. This method is related to the proposed progressive training approach because it is a baseline constructive NCO training and evaluation protocol for cross-scale generalization: the paper trains only at 100 nodes and tests transfer to larger scales, whereas the proposed ICAM method adds staged warm-up, varying-scale training from 100 to 500 nodes, and top-k elite trajectory training with a joint POMO/top-k loss to explicitly learn scale-independent features and refine solution quality.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical optimality gaps, inference times, or statistical significance values. It reports qualitative outcomes: the model shows superior generalization to larger graphs even with greedy decoding, which produces a single solution while many baselines generate several hundred solutions and select the best. With greedy decoding, the model is slightly slower than Sym-NCO, competitive with POMO, and significantly faster than other compared models. Beam search improves optimality gaps but increases inference time. Replacing quadratic attention with a PerceiverIO architecture substantially reduces inference time for larger graphs at the cost of some performance drop, while still achieving strong performance relative to other neural combinatorial optimization baselines. On TSPLib and CVRPLib, the model significantly outperforms end-to-end baseline policies even with greedy decoding. The only baselines described as competitive are Att-GCN+MCTS and DIFUSCO+2opt, but both are hybrid methods with expensive search, are 2 to 15 times slower, and are designed for TSP only. These results are relevant to the proposed method because they show that small-scale expert-imitation training can generalize to larger instances, but they do not test the proposed progressive varying-scale or top-k elite training scheme, leaving open whether staged scale variation and elite trajectory refinement can further improve large-scale solution quality.', 'context': 'The experimental context includes training on 100-node synthetic instances because this is described as the largest size for which near-optimal solutions are reasonably fast to obtain and is commonly used in the literature. The expert dataset contains 1 million solutions. For asymmetric TSP, Euclidean distance matrices are used with random symmetry breaking. The same hyperparameters are used across problems: 9 layers, 12 attention heads, embedding size 192, feed-forward dimension 512, batch size 1024, 500 epochs, Adam with initial learning rate 7.5e-4 and decay 0.98 every 50 epochs. Deep learning models are run on a single Nvidia Tesla V100-S GPU with 24GB memory, while non-neural solvers are run on an Intel Xeon E5-2670 CPU with 256GB memory in one thread. At test time, non-Perceiver models restrict attention to the 250 nearest neighbors of the origin node to reduce inference time without significant impact on solution quality. This context is directly relevant to the proposed research plan because the paper’s 100-node training scale matches the proposed Stage 1 warm-up scale, its evaluation on 200, 500, and 1000-node instances aligns with the plan’s focus on large-scale generalization, and its use of expert trajectories and cross-entropy imitation contrasts with the proposed Stage 3 top-k elite training, which instead focuses on the best k trajectories and combines a POMO loss with a new top-k loss.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3301, 'output_tokens': 3714}, {'corpus_id': '226306835', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies cross-scale training of an attention-based TSP solver using different task-sampling schedules. It trains the model on TSP instances from size 4 to 100 and compares single-task training, uniform stochastic sampling over sizes, classic monotonically increasing curriculum, and an adaptive staircase curriculum. In the adaptive staircase, task difficulty advances only when the validation optimality gap reaches a 0.05 threshold; if the threshold is not met after a full epoch, the task difficulty is decremented. The number of base trials per epoch is set to (10 + current_graph_size - min_graph_size)/(max_graph_size - min_graph_size), and training is stopped one epoch earlier than other strategies, causing about a 1.6% reduction in exposed data. A separate generalization experiment trained 147 single-task models from TSP 4 to 150, each initialized from a classic-curriculum pretrained model and trained for 20 epochs, then tested them on sizes 10 to 300. This is related to the proposed progressive ICAM training scheme because both use small-scale warm-up and multi-scale sampling to improve generalization, although the paper does not use top-k elite training or POMO-style losses.', 'results': 'The paper reports that performance is strongly correlated with training size and forms a skewed Gaussian centered at the trained task size, with transfer widening but weakening as problem size increases. Models that move to larger tasks without revisiting smaller tasks initially improve but later decay on farther tasks, indicating catastrophic forgetting. Uniform sampling is described as closest to a constant-factor approximator but has a larger optimality gap across sizes than most other methods. Classic curriculum gives more consistent performance than uniform but still forgets smaller-task knowledge. Adaptive staircase is reported as the best trade-off between forgetting and average performance across problem sizes. The excerpt does not provide exact optimality-gap values or statistical significance tests; the main quantitative detail is the approximately 1.6% data reduction for adaptive staircase.', 'context': 'Experiments used TSP instances, REINFORCE with a greedy rollout baseline, learning rate 1e-2, 100 epochs, 2,500 batches of 512 samples per epoch, and parameter initialization Uniform(-1/sqrt(d), 1/sqrt(d)). Training took about 50 hours on 4 P100 GPUs depending on dominant problem size. Evaluation used optimality gap percentage between average model cost and HK lower bounds computed over 10,000 TSPs per size, with exhaustive search over 100,000 TSPs for sizes 4-9; lower bounds for larger test sizes were extrapolated. The adaptive staircase used a 0.05 optimality-gap threshold and the base-trial formula above. These conditions are TSP-specific and do not include vehicle routing, POMO losses, top-k trajectory selection, or the 100-500 node uniform sampling range used in the proposed method.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2997, 'output_tokens': 3238}, {'corpus_id': '59608816', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '263909317', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a constructive neural combinatorial optimization model called LEHD, with a light encoder and heavy decoder, trained mainly by supervised learning on TSP and CVRP instances. The model learns to construct partial solutions during training and complete tours or routes during inference, and it uses Random Re-Construct, or RRC, iterations to improve greedy solutions. The experimental study also compares POMO, POMO with random sampling, POMO with RRC, reinforcement-learning training, and search-based or improvement-based solvers. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because the proposed method also targets large-scale generalization of constructive routing solvers and builds on POMO-style trajectory selection and loss design. The paper provides evidence that training objective and decoder-heavy architecture affect cross-scale performance, but it does not implement the proposed warm-up, varying-scale, or top-k elite training stages. It also reports that RRC can break the performance upper limit of the labeled solution, which is conceptually aligned with the proposed top-k elite trajectory refinement.', 'results': 'On uniformly distributed TSP, LEHD has fast greedy inference and, with 100 RRC iterations, outperforms other learning-based methods; with more than 500 RRC iterations, the gap is nearly optimal for TSP100 and TSP200, less than 0.2% for TSP500, and about 0.8% for TSP1000. On CVRP, LEHD with 300 RRC iterations outperforms most learning-based methods on all instances except EAS on CVRP100, and with 500 RRC iterations it outperforms LKH3 on CVRP100-CVRP500, while reaching about a 1% gap to LKH3 on CVRP1000 with 1000 RRC iterations. On real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances, LEHD with greedy inference and 1000 RRC iterations outperforms POMO and BQ. POMO trained by supervised learning reaches good performance on TSP100 but performs poorly on larger instances, and reinforcement-learning training with the POMO strategy does not converge in a reasonable time, while supervised learning is better on TSP50 under the same budget. In the 128-instance large TSP comparison, Concorde achieves 0.000% gaps in 3m, 32m, and 7.8h for TSP200, TSP500, and TSP1000; SO-mixed achieves 0.636% in 21m, 2.401% in 32m, and 2.800% in 56m; LEHD greedy achieves 0.859% in 3s, 1.560% in 0.3m, and 3.168% in 1.6m; LEHD RRC100 achieves 0.0761% in 1.2m, 0.343% in 8m, and 1.218% in 43m. For TSP1000, H-TSP with LKH-3 achieves 4.06% in 7.5m, while LEHD greedy achieves 3.168% in 1.6m and LEHD RRC100 achieves 1.218% in 43m. No statistical significance tests are reported.', 'context': 'Experiments use uniformly distributed TSP and CVRP instances and real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances at sizes including 100, 200, 500, and 1000 nodes; large TSP comparisons use 128 instances. Evaluation metrics are optimality gap and inference time, with greedy inference and RRC budgets of 100, 300, 500, and 1000 iterations. Training comparisons are made on TSP50 and TSP100 under the same computational budget, and POMO is trained by supervised learning on TSP100. The excerpt does not provide hardware specifications, exact optimizer settings, or the full self-improved training loss, so reproducibility is limited to the reported instance sizes, RRC budgets, and comparison setup. This context is relevant to the ICAM research plan because it establishes a baseline for large-scale constructive NCO generalization and shows that POMO-style methods need architectural or training improvements to scale, which motivates the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation and progressive top-k training.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3275, 'output_tokens': 6018}, {'corpus_id': '266375116', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper uses guided non-autoregressive knowledge distillation to convert the autoregressive POMO teacher into a non-autoregressive student, GNARKD-POMO, and compares the student against the POMO teacher. POMO is evaluated in greedy mode without augmentation and greedy mode with x8 augmentation, while the student produces a solution in a single non-autoregressive pass. Inputs are synthetic TSP and CVRP instances: node and depot coordinates are sampled uniformly from the unit square, CVRP node demands are drawn uniformly from the discrete set 1 through 9, and capacity is set to 1. Test sets contain 10,000 instances for n=50 and n=100. Experiments run on an Intel i5-11400F CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 3060Ti GPU with 8GB memory, using the largest feasible batch size. Metrics include learning gap relative to the teacher, acceleration ratio for one instance and for 10,000 instances, and maximum solvable node size under the memory limit. This is related to the proposed method because POMO is the teacher/baseline whose POMO loss is reused in Stage 3, and the paper directly probes POMO solution quality, inference cost, and large-scale memory limits, which are central to the ICAM plan's large-scale generalization and POMO comparisons. The paper does not implement the proposed warm-up, varying-scale, or top-k elite training.", 'results': 'For POMO versus GNARKD-POMO, the student is faster but slightly worse in solution quality. On TSP n=50, greedy no augmentation gives a 0.26% learning gap with 3.62x single-instance and 2.93x total acceleration; greedy x8 augmentation gives a 0.01% gap with 3.31x and 3.47x acceleration. On TSP n=100, no augmentation gives a 0.69% gap with 3.77x and 4.50x acceleration; x8 augmentation gives a 0.11% gap with 3.35x and 5.23x acceleration. On CVRP n=50, no augmentation gives a 3.02% gap with 2.04x and 3.52x acceleration; x8 augmentation gives a 1.73% gap with 2.06x and 3.92x acceleration. On CVRP n=100, no augmentation gives a 7.10% gap with 2.27x and 4.97x acceleration; x8 augmentation gives a 4.51% gap with 3.35x and 4.96x acceleration. In the scale/memory test, POMO with multiple greedy rollouts solves up to 1,000 TSP nodes under 8GB, while GNARKD-POMO solves up to 9,000 nodes; with x8 augmentation, POMO is limited to 500 nodes while GNARKD-POMO reaches 3,100 nodes. No statistical significance tests are reported.', 'context': 'The synthetic evaluation is limited to small instances, n=50 and n=100, with 10,000 test instances per setting, so it does not directly test the proposed 100-to-500 progressive training or 1,000+ generalization. The 8GB GPU memory constraint is central to the maximum-node-size comparison and to batch-size choices. POMO multi-rollout inference is the main source of its higher computational cost, whereas GNARKD-POMO uses a single non-autoregressive output matrix and reduces memory and time. The real-world TSPLIB check uses 20 TSP instances with 51 to 159 nodes and Concorde as the exact baseline; the paper reports that POMO has strong solution quality but higher resource demand, while GNARKD-POMO has shorter inference time. These conditions are relevant to the ICAM plan because they quantify POMO baseline behavior and the practical memory bottleneck that large-scale ICAM evaluation must address, but they do not provide evidence for the proposed top-k elite loss or scale-varying training stages.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4194, 'output_tokens': 5312}, {'corpus_id': '46892983', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a policy-gradient actor-critic training method for a neural constructive solver on capacitated VRP. A stochastic policy parameterized by theta is trained to choose the next customer or depot; an actor outputs next-node probabilities and a critic estimates return, with asynchronous advantage actor-critic reported in the appendix. Training and evaluation use randomly generated CVRP instances in the unit square with discrete uniform demands 1-9 and no split deliveries. Feasibility is enforced by masking: zero-demand nodes are masked, all customers are masked when remaining load is zero, and customers whose demand exceeds remaining load are masked. Decoding is evaluated with greedy selection and beam search of widths 5 and 10. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because both are training schemes for RL-based constructive routing solvers, but the paper uses a standard single-stage policy-gradient setup rather than the proposed warm-up, varying-scale, and POMO/top-k elite training; it therefore serves as a small/medium-scale RL baseline and motivates the need for cross-scale training in the research plan.', 'results': 'On 1000 instances per problem size, RL with beam search width 10 was the best reported decoder. For VRP10 and VRP20, where optimal solutions were obtained by mixed-integer formulation, about 95% of VRP10 instances and about 13% of VRP20 instances were within 10% of optimality, and outliers were within 20-25% of optimality. For VRP50 and VRP100, where optimal values were not computed, winning-rate comparisons showed classical heuristics were outperformed by the other methods in almost all samples. RL-BS(10) beat RL-Greedy on 91.9% of instances, RL-BS(5) on 57.7%, CW-Greedy on 99.8%, CW-Rnd(5,5) on 99.4%, CW-Rnd(10,10) on 99.2%, sweep variants on 99.3%, and OR-Tools on 60.2%. RL-Greedy was weaker against OR-Tools at 41.5%, and RL-BS(5) at 54.6%. Runtime analysis showed the log of solution time per node was nearly constant across RL decoders, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep runtimes grew faster than linearly; the RL framework was faster than randomized heuristics on larger problems, though exact OR-Tools timing comparisons were limited by C++ versus Python implementations.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions included 1000 sampled instances for each problem size, with VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100 and different vehicle capacities, although exact capacity values were not provided in the excerpt. Node and depot locations were uniformly generated in [0,1]x[0,1], demands were discrete uniform integers from 1 to 9, and split deliveries were disallowed in the main experiments. Beam search widths were 5 and 10; randomized Clarke-Wright used randomization depth/iterations (5,5) and (10,10), and randomized Sweep used multiple initial angles. Optimal solutions for VRP10 and VRP20 were obtained by solving a mixed-integer formulation. Hardware, training epochs, learning rates, and other hyperparameters were not specified in the provided excerpt; OR-Tools was implemented in C++ while other baselines were in Python, limiting direct runtime comparison.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3830, 'output_tokens': 5010}, {'corpus_id': '278498126', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '253111406', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '3486660', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a constructive reinforcement-learning method, S2V-DQN, for graph optimization problems including TSP. It represents the greedy policy with a structure2vec graph embedding network that updates node embeddings by aggregating neighbor information over T iterations, typically T=4, and computes Q(h(S), v; Θ) from the node embedding and a pooled graph embedding. Training uses fitted Q-iteration combined with n-step Q-learning: complete episodes construct solutions by adding nodes until a termination criterion, n-step returns are stored in an experience-replay buffer, and stochastic gradient descent is performed on random batches of state-action-return-next-state tuples. This method is related to the proposed ICAM progressive-training method because both learn constructive policies for routing/TSP-like problems and target generalization to larger instances, but the paper does not use the proposed three-stage warm-up, varying-scale, or top-k elite POMO training; it instead learns a Q-function with graph embeddings and evaluates transfer to larger graphs.', 'results': 'The excerpt reports that S2V-DQN significantly outperforms all competing methods on real-world benchmark instances for MVC, MAXCUT, and TSP, and that the learned heuristics preserve effectiveness when applied to graphs much larger than the training instances. It also reports that the learned policies can be interpreted as new greedy rules, such as balancing node degree with remaining-graph connectivity for MVC and avoiding cancellation of existing cut edges for MAXCUT. No numerical optimality gaps, statistical significance values, or runtime measurements are provided in the supplied text. In relation to the proposed method, these outcomes support the broader research-plan goal of improving cross-scale generalization of constructive neural solvers, but they do not demonstrate the specific benefit of the proposed progressive multi-scale or top-k elite training scheme.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions include synthetic MVC and MAXCUT instances generated as Erdős-Rényi or Barabási-Albert graphs with node counts sampled from a range such as 50-100, and 2-D TSP instances generated as uniformly random or clustered points using a DIMACS TSP Challenge generator. The method is evaluated on larger instances and on publicly available real-world benchmark datasets for MVC, MAXCUT, TSP, and set covering. Key implementation details include a p-dimensional node embedding, T=4 embedding iterations, seven parameter groups in the Q-function, n-step returns, and experience-replay batch updates. Hardware, exact training budgets, and hyperparameter values are not specified in the excerpt. For the proposed ICAM plan, this context is relevant because it shows a related RL constructive baseline trained on smaller synthetic instances and tested at larger scale, but it lacks the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, attention-free module, and staged training on 100 to 500 nodes with top-k elite loss.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5029, 'output_tokens': 3763}, {'corpus_id': '261243129', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper evaluates ELG-POMO, a constructive neural solver for TSP and CVRP that ensembles a global POMO policy with a transferrable local policy. The global policy is a Transformer encoder-decoder without positional embeddings, trained by reinforcement learning with POMO-style instance augmentation and a shared or greedy baseline. The local policy constructs solutions using local neighborhood information around the current node and is combined with the global policy to select the next node. The model is trained only on uniformly distributed instances with 100 nodes and tested in greedy inference mode on TSPLIB, CVRPLIB Set-X and Set-XXL, and synthetic distributions. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because both are built around POMO-style autoregressive construction and aim to improve cross-scale generalization, but the paper's method does not use staged warm-up, varying-scale training from 100 to 500 nodes, or a top-k elite loss; instead it improves generalization through a local policy while keeping training scale fixed.", 'results': 'On TSPLIB and CVRPLIB subsets with N <= 200, ELG-POMO outperforms all compared methods, reducing the average gap to best known solutions by 0.53% for CVRP and 0.50% for TSP relative to the runner-up. On the harder N in (200, 1000] subset, it reduces the CVRP gap from 6.95% for Omni-POMO to 6.46%, and reduces the TSP gap from 7.47% for Omni-POMO to 5.90%, although it remains behind BQ and LEHD on TSP at that scale. On real-world CVRPLIB Set-XXL instances with N between 3000 and 7000, it performs best on three of four instances and substantially lowers the gap compared with other POMO-based methods. On uniform in-distribution N=100 instances, it achieves a 0.22% gap on TSP100 and a 1.22% gap on CVRP100, surpassing POMO and Sym-POMO and remaining competitive with Pointerformer and T2TCO on TSP. On synthetic difficult distributions, ELG-POMO retrained on uniform, cluster, and cluster mixture outperforms AMDKD on three of four distributions at N=100 and all four at N=200; at N=200 its gaps are 1.84% for cluster, 1.62% for mixture, 1.43% for explosion, and 1.58% for rotation, compared with 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54% for AMDKD. Runtime is lower than BQ and diffusion-based methods and higher than pure POMO, with a reported 1.90 seconds per instance on TSPLIB/CVRPLIB, but its optimality gap remains worse than highly optimized solvers such as HGS.', 'context': "Training uses uniformly distributed TSP/CVRP instances with 100 nodes, matching the common POMO training setting. Evaluation uses greedy decoding, POMO x8 instance augmentation for POMO-based methods, average gap to best known solutions, and average solving time. Test conditions include cross-distribution and cross-scale benchmarks: TSPLIB and CVRPLIB Set-X split into N <= 200 and 200 < N <= 1000 or 1002, CVRPLIB Set-XXL with N between 3000 and 7000, and small-scale synthetic distributions including cluster, cluster mixture, explosion, and rotation. The paper does not report statistical significance, hardware details, or the exact hyperparameters in the provided excerpt, but it states that ELG-POMO's training is aligned with POMO so that the comparison isolates the effect of the local policy. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because the proposed three-stage training would modify the same POMO-based training pipeline, adding warm-up, varying-scale sampling, and top-k elite refinement, whereas the paper's fixed-scale POMO training provides the baseline behavior and shows where scale-independent learning remains challenging."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5687, 'output_tokens': 3870}, {'corpus_id': '226222332', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper describes the POMO shared-baseline policy-gradient method for constructive combinatorial optimization. For each instance, the policy network samples N solution trajectories, computes the return of each trajectory, and performs REINFORCE-style gradient ascent using a shared baseline common to the trajectories rather than a trajectory-specific greedy-rollout baseline. The method is presented for mini-batch training and is applied to constructive routing and knapsack tasks, including TSP, CVRP, and 0-1 knapsack. It is directly related to the proposed ICAM research plan because the proposed Stage 3 top-k elite training explicitly retains the original POMO loss and augments it with a new top-k loss; the paper's shared baseline is the baseline policy-gradient objective that the proposed method builds upon for multi-trajectory training.", 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not provide quantitative experimental outcomes, statistical significance, or numerical comparisons to baselines. It reports qualitative outcomes: the POMO shared baseline is stated to induce less variance in policy gradients than a greedy-rollout baseline; the advantage term has zero mean under POMO, whereas greedy-rollout advantages are mostly negative because sampled rollouts have difficulty surpassing greedy rollouts; the baseline is computationally efficient because it does not require a separately trained critic or a cloned policy network; and the shared baseline is claimed to make training more resistant to premature convergence to suboptimal policies by making each trajectory compete with N-1 other heterogeneous trajectories.', 'context': "The experimental context in the excerpt is mini-batch POMO training with N sampled trajectories per instance, a policy network parameterized by theta, and returns computed from constructed solutions. The problem domains mentioned are TSP, CVRP, and 0-1 knapsack, but no dataset sizes, hardware specifications, learning rates, number of trajectories, or other hyperparameters are given in the provided text. For the proposed ICAM plan, the relevant condition is that Stage 3 uses this original POMO loss in a joint objective with a top-k elite loss after warm-up and varying-scale training, so the paper's method informs the baseline training objective but does not itself evaluate progressive scale training, top-k selection, or large-scale generalization."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2460, 'output_tokens': 3272}, {'corpus_id': '278151274', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper studies a plug-and-play size-generalization scaling factor, called ESF, for neural VRP solvers. ESF rescales attention or compatibility scores by a logarithmic size ratio: at test time it uses the ratio between the training size and the test size, and at training time it uses a corresponding logarithmic ratio based on a reference size and the training size. It is applied to existing constructive solvers, including models pretrained on 50 or 100 nodes and a multi-size solver trained on 50-200 nodes. This is related to the proposed progressive varying-scale training and the ICAM plan because both use problem size to condition solution construction and target cross-scale generalization; however, the paper's method is a scalar size adjustment rather than a staged warm-up, uniform-scale curriculum, or top-k elite trajectory loss.", 'results': 'On 10,000 randomly generated TSP and CVRP instances, test-time ESF improved TSP cross-size optimality gaps by an average of 14.09% for scaling up and 7.32% for scaling down. For CVRP, ESF improved downscaling by 5.97% but worsened upscaling by 3.54%, which the authors attribute to increased depot selection and variable solution sequence length. On large-scale TSP instances of size 1000, ESF improved two strong pretrained models by an average relative improvement of 10.76%. When ESF was applied during training of a multi-size solver, TSP in-size performance improved by 18.38% and cross-size performance by 13.8%; CVRP in-size performance improved by 4.51% and cross-size performance by 4.86%.', 'context': 'Experiments were run on an Intel Xeon Gold 6254 CPU and NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. Test sets contained 10,000 randomly generated instances per size or distribution. Test-time ESF baselines were trained on 50 or 100 nodes and evaluated at 50, 100, and 200 nodes; large-scale evaluation used test size 1000. Training-time ESF used a solver trained on 50-200 nodes and evaluated in-size at 100, 150, and 200 nodes and cross-size at 300, 500, and 1000 nodes. Some comparisons used greedy rollout with 8 augmentations, and a few-shot setting could not evaluate instances larger than 500 due to GPU memory constraints. The ESF is generic and model-agnostic, but its fixed scaling factor can hurt CVRP upscaling, suggesting that dynamic or problem-specific adjustment may be needed.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5745, 'output_tokens': 8284}, {'corpus_id': '259240292', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '3058436', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies a hybrid genetic search with adaptive diversity control for time-window vehicle routing variants, including PVRPTW, MDVRPTW, and SDVRPTW. The algorithm maintains feasible and infeasible subpopulations, applies parent selection, crossover, local-search education, repair, survivor selection, diversification, and penalty adaptation. For large instances it periodically performs structural and geometrical decomposition, solves subproblems recursively, and reinserts reconstituted solutions. It also introduces constant-time move evaluation for duration and time-window infeasibility and calibrates temporal pruning parameters. This is related to the proposed progressive training scheme only indirectly: both target large-scale routing performance, and the paper provides a classical HGS baseline plus component-ablation evidence for scale-related mechanisms, whereas the proposed method uses staged neural training to learn scale-independent features.', 'results': 'The full method outperformed the compared state-of-the-art methods for PVRPTW, MDVRPTW, and SDVRPTW, including a parallel iterative method using 64 processors, and was comparable to a strong hybrid genetic algorithm for VRPTW while using fewer problem-specific components. It retrieved or improved 105 of 109 best-known solutions for the PVRPTW, MDVRPTW, and SDVRPTW groups and strictly improved 75 of 109. For VRPTW it retrieved or improved 292 of 356 best-known solutions and strictly improved 158 of 356, including five new best-known solutions with one fewer vehicle on large-scale instances. Average standard deviation was 0.17 percent for MDVRPTW and 0.27 percent for PVRPTW on the corresponding benchmark group. In component sensitivity tests using five runs, removing the diversity-and-cost objective or removing time-window-infeasible solutions degraded performance, while decomposition had a significant effect on large PVRPTW instances with subproblems up to 560 customers but a smaller effect on MDVRPTW and SDVRPTW where per-resource subproblems contained 90 to 240 visits. Parameter calibration on 10 two-hundred-customer instances with 20 runs per setting found the best average gap near gamma_TW=1.0 and gamma_WT=0.2; a 50-run comparison gave an average gap of 0.53 percent for that setting versus 0.68 percent for the distance-only setting, with a Wilcoxon test p-value of 0.000.', 'context': 'Experiments were implemented in C++ and compiled with g++ -O3 on an Intel Xeon 2.93 GHz processor. Benchmark instances involved 48 to 1008 customers, up to 9 depots, 10 periods, and 6 vehicle types; new larger instances involved 360 to 960 customers and up to 4608 total services. Main runs used It_NI=5000, It_dec=2000, nbElit=8, n_close=3, P_rep=0.5, |Gamma|=40, mu=25, lambda=40, and xi_REF=0.2. Calibration used 36 combinations of gamma_TW and gamma_WT from {0, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5}, reduced |Gamma|=20, It_NI=2000, and decomposition turned off. Fleet minimization was handled by starting with a fleet size of 100 and iteratively reducing it when feasible solutions were found. Evaluation metrics included average and best objective values, gap to best-known solutions, runtime, and standard deviation.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 6718, 'output_tokens': 5691}, {'corpus_id': '281440787', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper uses a DGL constructive VRP solver with a replacement-based SIL training paradigm. The excerpt describes initialization by generating a batch of uniform VRP instances, solving them with a nearest-neighbor heuristic to create pseudo-labels, and using those pseudo-labels to warm up the model. Training then uses data augmentation and random replacement or removal of portions of the generated dataset during training. In the reported experimental setup, the model is trained only on 100-node uniform instances and then evaluated directly on larger and differently distributed instances. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because it shares the small-scale 100-node warm-up or single-scale training idea and targets cross-scale generalization, but it does not include the proposed Stage 2 varying-scale training over 100 to 500 nodes or Stage 3 top-k elite training with a POMO plus top-k loss. It therefore provides a relevant single-scale SIL baseline for the ICAM research plan's question of how training can learn scale-independent features, while not implementing the proposed multi-stage scheme, instance-conditioned adaptation function, or attention-free module.", 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report numeric optimality gaps, absolute objective values, p-values, or confidence intervals. It reports that DGL achieved state-of-the-art performance compared with global-view baselines such as Omni-VRP, LEHD, and BQ on synthetic TSP instances, outperformed IN-ViT in both solution quality and inference speed, and improved solution quality relative to the GD SIL baseline when using replacement-based SIL. It also reports that most models degraded when generalizing to varying scales and distributions, that UDC failed to solve TSPs under 100 nodes and CVRPs under 200 nodes due to unknown errors, and that DGL consistently outperformed baselines on real-world TSPLIB, CVRPLIB, and country-map instances, with some baselines marked OOM. These qualitative outcomes are relevant to the proposed method because they show that a 100-node-trained model with replacement SIL can generalize, but they do not quantify whether the proposed progressive varying-scale and top-k elite training would further reduce large-scale gaps.', 'context': "Experimental conditions: DGL had 4 decoder layers, hidden dimension 128, and 8 attention heads; learning rate 1e-4 with decay 0.97; 100 epochs with 100 iterations per epoch; batch size 256; 64 samples per iteration; replacement rate 12.5%; hyperparameter k was 30 for TSP and 50 for CVRP. Training used randomly generated 100-node uniform instances. Evaluation used synthetic MSVDRP subsets for TSP-100, TSP-1000, TSP-5000, TSP-10000 and CVRP-50, CVRP-500, CVRP-5000 across uniform, clustered, explosion, and implosion distributions, plus real-world TSPLIB95 with 77 instances from 51 to 18512 nodes, CVRPLIB Set-X with 100 instances from 100 to 1000 nodes, and eight country maps with 194 to 22775 nodes. Metrics were average gaps to LKH near-optimal solutions for synthetic data and best-known solutions for real-world data. LEHD and BQ were evaluated with greedy search for computational-efficiency fairness, and DGL and IN-ViT used the same inference setup. Hardware was an Intel Xeon Platinum 8352V CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 4090 24GB GPU. These conditions are directly comparable to the proposed ICAM plan's 100-node warm-up and large-scale evaluation, but the paper's setup lacks the proposed 100 to 500 varying-scale stage and top-k elite loss."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3082, 'output_tokens': 6228}, {'corpus_id': '235376887', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper applies efficient active search (EAS) to the POMO constructive solver for TSP and CVRP, and to the L2D method for JSSP. EAS is a test-time parameter-update strategy in which only selected model components are modified during a 200-iteration search: EAS-Emb updates embeddings, EAS-Lay updates added-layer parameters, and EAS-Tab updates tabular/intensification-related parameters, while the original active search updates a broader set of parameters. The implementation extends existing POMO and L2D code, uses the Adam optimizer, and tunes the hyperparameters lambda, sigma, alpha, and the learning rate by Bayesian optimization on validation instances drawn from the same distribution as the test instances. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because it uses POMO for TSP/CVRP and addresses solution-quality improvement and generalization, but it is not the same as the proposed progressive 100-to-500-node training scheme or the proposed top-k elite loss; it is a test-time active-search adaptation rather than a multi-stage training method.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report numerical optimality gaps, absolute objective values, or statistical significance tests. Qualitatively, pure sampling did not improve solution quality over the 200 search iterations for TSP, CVRP, or JSSP. For TSP, EAS-Emb and EAS-Lay had nearly identical performance, and EAS-Tab slightly outperformed both; the original active search was significantly more unstable, which the authors attribute to greater sensitivity to the learning rate. For CVRP, all EAS variants found better average solutions than the original active search after only a few iterations, suggesting that fixing most parameters simplifies the learning problem and speeds convergence. For JSSP, EAS-Emb was significantly better than the other methods because the L2D model uses only two node features and a complex node-embedding generation procedure, making direct embedding modification more effective than fine-tuning the entire embedding process.', 'context': 'Each experiment used a single Nvidia Tesla V100 GPU and a single Intel Xeon 4114 CPU core at 2.2 GHz. Hyperparameters were tuned on separate validation instances sampled from the same distribution as the test instances, and the tuned hyperparameters were not adjusted for larger instances used to evaluate generalization performance. The search trajectory analysis monitored solution quality over 200 iterations, averaging over all JSSP test instances and over the first 1,000 TSP and CVRP test instances. The excerpt does not specify the exact node scales, instance distributions, or numerical baseline comparisons, so its direct relevance to the proposed 100-to-500-node progressive training and top-k elite training is limited to the shared POMO/TSP/CVRP setting and the broader motivation of improving large-scale generalization.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2538, 'output_tokens': 3418}, {'corpus_id': '5692837', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper trained a pointer network (Ptr-Net) with a single-layer LSTM to generate sequence outputs for planar combinatorial geometry problems, including the Travelling Salesman Problem, convex hull, and Delaunay triangulation. Inputs were planar point sets with n Cartesian coordinates sampled uniformly from the unit square [0,1] x [0,1], and outputs were sequences representing the corresponding solution. The model was trained on 1 million generated input-output pairs using stochastic gradient descent, with the same architecture and hyperparameters used across all problems. This relates to the proposed progressive training method and the broader ICAM research plan because both involve training neural constructive solvers on synthetic routing/geometry instances, but the paper’s method is a fixed single-stage training protocol rather than the proposed multi-stage warm-up, varying-scale, and top-k elite POMO-based training scheme.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report quantitative empirical outcomes; the Empirical Results section is empty. The only reported experimental observation is that overfitting was observed in some cases where the task was simpler, specifically for small n, despite training on 1 million example pairs. No optimality gaps, baseline comparisons, solution-quality metrics, inference times, or statistical significance values are provided in the supplied text.', 'context': 'Key experimental conditions included uniform random point sets in the unit square, 1 million training example pairs, a single-layer LSTM with either 256 or 512 hidden units, stochastic gradient descent with a learning rate of 1.0, batch size 128, random uniform weight initialization from -0.08 to 0.08, and L2 gradient clipping of 2.0. The authors explicitly stated that no extensive architecture or hyperparameter search was performed and that the same hyperparameters were used across problems to make the paper’s main message stronger, while acknowledging that tuning could likely improve performance. These conditions are relevant to the proposed method’s focus on training stability and scale-dependent generalization, especially the reported overfitting for small n, but the excerpt does not specify hardware, training duration, number of epochs, evaluation metrics, or large-scale test settings.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2117, 'output_tokens': 2053}, {'corpus_id': '258212484', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1706, 'output_tokens': 480}, {'corpus_id': '278768989', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '270481', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '266999156', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper does not implement the proposed progressive multi-scale training or top-k elite loss. Its related method is DAR, an inference-time attention-reshaping technique for neural CVRPLib solvers that addresses the same large-scale generalization problem targeted by the ICAM research plan. DAR computes standard dot-product attention scores from the decoder query and encoder node features, computes Euclidean distances from the current node to all unvisited candidate nodes, converts distances into distance scores using a logarithmic function under the assumption that coordinates lie in [0,1]x[0,1], assigns negative scores to nodes outside a K-neighborhood, adds the distance score to the attention score, normalizes with a scaling constant C=50, and uses the resulting weights to sample the next node. This is conceptually related to the proposed ICAM adaptation function f(N,d_ij)=-alpha log2N d_ij because both use distance-based bias to counter attention dispersion, but DAR is a fixed inference-time heuristic rather than a learned instance-conditioned training scheme.', 'results': 'On CVRPLib Set-X with 100 instances and 100 to 1000 nodes, DAR achieved a 5.66% gap to BKS in the main comparison, improving over POMO at 8.31%, ELG at 5.92%, Omni-VRP at 6.47%, and greedy at 21.03%, but below LKH3 at 1.00% and HGS at 0.11%. On 32 Set-X instances with 500 to 1000 nodes, DAR achieved 5.27%, better than AM at 24.65%, TAM-AM at 10.97%, TAM-LKH3 at 9.87%, GLOP at 19.75%, and GLOP-LKH3 at 18.80%. On 35 Set-XML instances with 100 to 5000 nodes, DAR achieved 5.20%, better than POMO at 10.51%, Omni-VRP at 10.60%, and ELG at 5.95%. On 6 Set-XXL instances with 3000 to 11000 nodes, DAR achieved 10.82%, better than POMO at 30.85%, ELG at 16.08%, and greedy at 13.08%, and competitive with decomposition baselines TAM-AM at 30.07%, TAM-LKH3 at 22.50%, GLOP at 19.75%, and GLOP-LKH3 at 18.80%. Inference time for DAR was 0.39 seconds on Set-X, whereas LKH3 and HGS required at least 16 minutes. No statistical significance tests were reported.', 'context': "Training used randomly generated node coordinates and demands in the style of the AM and POMO training protocol; testing used CVRPLib Set-X, expanded Set-XML, and Set-XXL instances. DAR assumes node coordinates in [0,1]x[0,1], uses Euclidean distance, a logarithmic distance transformation, a hyperparameter K to limit the neighborhood, and C=50 for normalization. It adds no trainable parameters. Baselines greedy, POMO, and ELG were run with source code and default settings under the same training environment where possible; other baseline numbers were taken from original papers. Hardware context: LKH3 and HGS comparison used an Intel Gold 6148 Skylake 2.4 GHz processor, while DAR was evaluated on GPU-parallel inference. This context is relevant to the proposed method because the ICAM plan also targets CVRPLib, TSP, and ATSP large-scale generalization and compares against POMO and ELG, but the paper's DAR provides a distance-bias baseline rather than evidence for progressive warm-up, varying-scale training, or top-k elite loss."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4790, 'output_tokens': 4318}, {'corpus_id': '259075472', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper studies a scale-conditioned meta-adaptation method for constructive DRL routing solvers, applied to POMO and Sym-NCO. The base models are encoder-decoder policies pretrained on 100-node instances. A shared SML module g_phi(h,N) converts the original context embedding h into a scale-conditioned initial embedding h_S using the instance size N. SML is trained with two objectives: a distillation objective that aligns h_S with the expected instance-wise adapted embedding after many adaptation steps, and a zero-shot simulation objective that treats h_S as the zero-adaptation state and rewards it for producing good solutions before any test-time update. At inference, the model performs instance-wise adaptation for a fixed number of iterations and is compared with active search, efficient active search, and simulation-guided beam search. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because both aim to improve cross-scale generalization of POMO-style constructive DRL models from 100-node training to larger routing instances; the paper's contribution is a scale-conditioned meta-learning initialization and test-time adaptation, not a three-stage progressive pretraining schedule or a top-k elite loss. It is also conceptually adjacent to the ICAM plan's instance-conditioned adaptation function because both use problem scale to condition the model, but the paper's module operates on node embeddings and is trained by distillation and zero-shot simulation rather than by a learnable pairwise-distance bias.", 'results': 'Across TSP, CVRP, PCTSP, and OP, Meta-SAGE generally improved the pretrained POMO and Sym-NCO models over active search, efficient active search, and simulation-guided beam search. For TSP, POMO-based Meta-SAGE achieved objective gaps of 0.391% at N=200, 3.559% at N=500, and 12.038% at N=1000, compared with 0.455%, 9.362%, and 32.869% for efficient active search; Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE achieved 0.387%, 3.339%, and 11.488%. For CVRP, POMO-based Meta-SAGE reached 22.001 with a -0.009% gap at N=200 and 63.322 with a 0.035% gap at N=500, while Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE reached 21.982 with a -0.094% gap at N=200 and 63.281 with a -0.028% gap at N=500, both better than the LKH3 reference at those scales; at N=1000 the gaps were 0.683% and 0.685%. For PCTSP, Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE outperformed the OR-Tools reference at all scales: 7.299 with an -8.236% gap at N=200, 11.175 with an -8.412% gap at N=500, and 16.219 with a -31.31% gap at N=1000. For OP, Sym-NCO-based Meta-SAGE achieved 49.05 with a reported 0.337% gap at N=200, 98.762 with a -1.457% gap at N=500, and 154.307 with a 4.562% gap at N=1000; the N=500 result was better than the Compass reference. The only noted exception among adaptation baselines was Sym-NCO on CVRP at N=200, where active search had a lower objective, 21.955 with a -0.218% gap, but required 28.2H versus 2.7H for Meta-SAGE. The provided results report objective gaps and runtimes but do not report confidence intervals or statistical significance tests.', 'context': "Experimental conditions: POMO and Sym-NCO were pretrained on N=100 instances and evaluated without retraining on larger scales. Test sets contained 1,000 instances at N=200 and 128 instances at N=500 and N=1000. Hardware was a single NVIDIA A100 40GB GPU and an Intel Scalable Gold CPU. Meta-SAGE and efficient active search or simulation-guided beam search used 200 adaptation iterations; efficient active search and simulation-guided beam search were stopped if their runtime exceeded Meta-SAGE's 200-iteration runtime. Active search was run per instance for 200 iterations because it could not be batched. Reported time is total time to solve all instances. Baseline gaps were computed against Concorde for TSP, LKH3 for CVRP, OR-Tools for PCTSP, and Compass for OP. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because the proposed method also starts from 100-node training and targets large-scale TSP/CVRP evaluation, but it uses progressive multi-scale training and top-k elite POMO loss rather than the paper's scale-conditioned meta-adapter and fixed 200-iteration test-time adaptation."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 6648, 'output_tokens': 8578}, {'corpus_id': '2332513', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper develops REINFORCE-style policy-gradient methods for stochastic connectionist networks. The core method defines the performance measure as the expected reinforcement E{r | W} conditioned on network weights W, and derives weight updates of the form Δw_ij = α_ij(r - b_ij)e_ij, where e_ij = ∂ln g_i / ∂w_ij is the characteristic eligibility, b_ij is a reinforcement baseline, and α_ij is a nonnegative rate factor. For immediate-reinforcement tasks, the paper shows that the expected update vector E{ΔW | W} has a nonnegative inner product with the gradient ∇_W E{r | W}, and that with a constant rate factor the expected update is proportional to the true gradient. For episodic tasks, the method extends to one reinforcement delivered at the end of an episode by summing time-indexed eligibility terms over the episode, Δw_ij = α_ij(r - b_ij)Σ_t e_ij(t), using an unfolded-in-time acyclic representation of recurrent networks. The paper also derives REINFORCE updates for multiparameter stochastic units, such as Gaussian units, by differentiating the log-density with respect to distribution parameters. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because the plan targets reinforcement-learning-based constructive routing solvers, and POMO-style training is a policy-gradient method over construction trajectories; the proposed progressive training and top-k elite trajectory loss can be interpreted as an episodic reward-based policy optimization scheme over routing construction episodes.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not report quantitative experimental outcomes, numerical baselines, statistical significance, or vehicle-routing performance values. The reported outcome is theoretical: for any REINFORCE algorithm, the inner product between E{ΔW | W} and ∇_W E{r | W} is nonnegative; if all rate factors are positive, this inner product is zero only when the gradient is zero; and if the rate factor is constant, E{ΔW | W} equals the constant times ∇_W E{r | W}. The same gradient-alignment property is stated for episodic REINFORCE algorithms. No empirical comparison is provided between REINFORCE variants, reinforcement baselines, or training schedules, and no results are given for scale-varying training, top-k elite training, or large-scale routing instances.', 'context': 'The experimental or analytical context is a stationary reinforcement-learning setting. The paper assumes stationary and independent environment input distributions, stationary reward distributions, and weights fixed during an episode for episodic tasks. The baseline is assumed conditionally independent of the current output given weights and input. The networks considered include Bernoulli units, Bernoulli-logistic units, recurrent networks unfolded over a fixed episode length, and Gaussian multiparameter units. The paper does not specify hardware, dataset size, routing problem scale, hyperparameter search, or inference-time constraints. Its conditions are mathematical assumptions for gradient-following behavior rather than empirical routing benchmarks. For the proposed ICAM method, this context maps to treating each routing construction episode as a sequence of stochastic node-selection decisions with a terminal route-quality reward, but the paper does not address cross-scale generalization, TSP/CVRP/ATSP instances, POMO losses, top-k trajectory selection, or attention-free architecture design.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5398, 'output_tokens': 3055}, {'corpus_id': '249282428', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper’s relevant method is a task-distribution generalization study combined with Reptile-based meta-learning for neural combinatorial optimization solvers. It defines instance distributions, or tasks, parameterized by graph size N, number of modes M, vehicle capacity C, and coordinate scale L. For TSP and CVRP, it first trains the reinforcement-learning Attention Model (AM) and the supervised Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) on a single task and then tests them on other tasks in the same class to measure generalization deterioration. It then trains meta-learning variants, referred to as meta-AM and multi-AM, over prior task distributions: a variable-size distribution with N in {10, 20, 30, 50}, a variable-mode distribution with M in {1, 2, 5}, a mixed variable-size/mode distribution with (N, M) in {20, 30, 50} × {1, 2, 4}, and a CVRP variable-capacity distribution with C in {10, 30, 40}. The meta-trained models are evaluated on unseen interpolation and extrapolation tasks, and on real-world TSPlib and CVRPlib instances by fine-tuning. Evaluation uses average optimality gap relative to Concorde for TSP and LKH for CVRP, together with sample efficiency and training-time measurements. This relates to the proposed progressive training method because both approaches aim to improve cross-scale generalization by exposing the model to multiple scales or task distributions. The paper’s variable-size task distribution and meta-training/fine-tuning are conceptually similar to the proposed Stage 2 varying-scale training and the broader goal of learning scale-independent features, but the paper does not use a staged warm-up, uniform scale sampling over [100, 500], or a top-k elite POMO-based loss.', 'results': 'The paper reports that single-task-trained AM and GCN models generalize poorly when one task parameter is changed. In the GCN TSP results, average gaps include 43.86 and 37.26 for node-count changes around N = 50 and N = 100, 35.40 and 32.74 for mode-count changes, and 272.58 and 289.51 for coordinate-scale changes. The AM table is only partially legible in the supplied excerpt, but it contains large visible values such as 282.55 and 292.39 in scale-related entries. For the meta-learning approach, the excerpt states that meta-trained models outperform non-meta baselines on unseen synthetic tasks and on real-world TSPlib/CVRPlib instances, and that meta-training provides a strong initialization for fine-tuning. However, the supplied text does not include the numeric Table 4 gap values for the meta-trained models. Fine-tuning cost is reported as 20 seconds to 1 minute for 100 fine-tuning steps on TSPlib instances, and CVRPlib evaluation uses 50 fine-tuning steps over 5 random train/test splits.', 'context': 'Experiments used synthetic TSP and CVRP instances generated from the task family T_{N,M,C,L}. The TSP base distribution was N = 40, M = 0, L = 1, and the CVRP base distribution was N = 50, M = 0, C = 40, L = 1. Training tasks were relatively small-scale, with N up to 50, M up to 5, and C up to 40, while test tasks included extrapolation cases such as N = 100. Real-world evaluation used 28 TSPlib instances of 50 to 200 nodes and 106 CVRPlib instances of size up to 200 nodes. Hardware consisted of Intel 16-core CPUs, 256 GB RAM, CentOS 7, and Nvidia V100 GPUs with 32 GB memory; each model was trained for 24 hours on one GPU. The paper’s scale range is much smaller than the proposed plan’s target range of 100 to 5,000 nodes, and its method is meta-learning with fine-tuning rather than the proposed three-stage progressive training with top-k elite loss.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4124, 'output_tokens': 9080}, {'corpus_id': '250493174', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper applies Simulation-guided Beam Search (SGBS) and SGBS+EAS to Transformer-like policy networks pre-trained with POMO-style reinforcement learning for TSP, CVRP, and FFSP. SGBS performs breadth-first search over partial solutions using expansion, simulation, and pruning, while EAS adds randomly initialized insertion layers to the pretrained policy and iteratively runs SGBS, samples additional solutions, and performs policy-gradient ascent on the insertion layers until convergence or timeout. This is related to the proposed progressive training method because both build on POMO-trained routing policies and aim to improve solution quality and cross-scale performance, but the paper’s method is primarily an inference-time search plus lightweight policy-gradient refinement rather than a staged curriculum from 100 to 500 nodes or a top-k elite trajectory loss.', 'results': 'On TSP, SGBS+EAS reduced optimality gaps by 45%, 33%, and 35% relative to EAS on three instance sets. On CVRP, SGBS+EAS reduced gaps by 52%, 56%, and 59% relative to EAS and outperformed LKH3 by a large margin; on n=100 CVRP it achieved a 0.11% gap to HGS, indicating competitive solution quality but not competitive runtime. On FFSP, SGBS+EAS significantly outperformed traditional CPLEX and metaheuristic baselines, although the provided excerpt does not report exact gap values.', 'context': 'Experiments were run on A100 GPUs with 80 GB memory, with reported runtimes adjusted to a single-GPU premise. TSP and CVRP used n=100 with 10,000 instances, plus generalization tests at n=150 and n=200 with 1,000 instances each. Baselines included Concorde, HGS, LKH3, DACT, NLNS, and DPDP. POMO x8 instance augmentation was used. SGBS hyperparameters were set to beta=gamma=10 for TSP and beta=gamma=4 for CVRP. FFSP used 1,000 instances each for n=20, 50, and 100 jobs with three stages and four machines per stage. The paper does not report training on scales up to 500 or top-k elite losses, so its evidence for large-scale generalization is limited to modest scale shifts from 100 to 150 and 200 nodes.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3501, 'output_tokens': 2929}]): Request timed out.
Error processing ('We will compare against classical solvers (Concorde, LKH3, HGS), constructive NCO methods (POMO, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer), and two-stage NCO approaches (MCTS-based methods, TAM, GLOP). Performance will be measured using optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference times; this comparison positions ICAM against both exact/heuristic solvers and neural combinatorial optimization baselines.', [{'corpus_id': '232013570', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '252211738', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper’s relevant experimental method, as identifiable from the supplied section headings, is a comparison and ablation evaluation of a neural mTSPTWR solver. The solver uses vehicle embeddings, a customer-assignment process that produces probabilities for n assignments, a policy network, and a decoder that outputs a matching between vehicles and customers. The experiments include comparison studies on easier and harder mTSPTWR instances, an ablation of the assignment strategy, an ablation of the multi-head architecture, and an ablation of a penalty coefficient β. An appendix reports hyperparameters for baselines and the configuration of k-means. This relates to the proposed ICAM research plan because ICAM is also a neural encoder-decoder routing solver and its experimental design relies on baseline comparisons, architecture ablations, and decoder/assignment-related components; however, the paper’s problem is mTSPTWR rather than TSP, CVRP, or ATSP, and the excerpt does not identify the specific classical or NCO baselines named in the proposed method.', 'results': 'No quantitative experimental outcomes are available in the provided excerpt. The supplied text contains only section titles, so no objective values, optimality gaps, solution-quality improvements, inference times, or statistical significance relative to baselines can be reported.', 'context': 'The only available context is structural: the experiments are organized around easier and harder mTSPTWR instances, ablations for assignment strategy, multi-head architecture, and penalty coefficient β, and an appendix for baseline hyperparameters and k-means configuration. No instance sizes, node counts, dataset names, hardware specifications, training details, hyperparameter values, or evaluation metrics are provided in the excerpt.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1878, 'output_tokens': 4075}, {'corpus_id': '231879679', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1817, 'output_tokens': 306}, {'corpus_id': '214795270', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies a policy-gradient reinforcement learning model for TSP improvement, where the agent selects 2-opt moves from a current tour. The model uses a policy network and critic network, with graph convolutional and dual-encoding components and an RNN-based decoder, and is trained from random feasible initial tours. It is evaluated on Euclidean TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100 by running 500, 1000, or 2000 improvement steps. The experimental design is related to the proposed ICAM baseline plan because it compares a neural combinatorial optimization method against exact Concorde, classical construction heuristics, OR-Tools with 2-opt and LKH, and other neural construction and improvement methods, using optimality gaps and solve times as primary metrics. It also includes a cross-scale test in which a TSP50-trained policy is applied to TSP100 instances, which is relevant to the ICAM goal of scale generalization, although the paper method is an improvement heuristic rather than a constructive solver and covers only 20 to 100 nodes.', 'results': 'On 10,000 instances per size, the method achieved optimality gaps of 0.01 percent, 0.36 percent, and 1.84 percent for TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100 with 500 steps, taking 5 minutes, 7 minutes, and 10 minutes respectively. With 1000 steps it achieved 0.00 percent, 0.21 percent, and 1.26 percent gaps in 10 minutes, 13 minutes, and 21 minutes. With 2000 steps it achieved 0.00 percent, 0.12 percent, and 0.87 percent gaps in 15 minutes, 29 minutes, and 41 minutes. Concorde produced 0.00 percent gaps with average costs of 3.84, 5.70, and 7.76 in 1 minute, 2 minutes, and 3 minutes. The method outperformed OR-Tools, which had gaps of 0.37 percent, 1.83 percent, and 2.90 percent, and outperformed insertion heuristics and greedy decoding neural methods. At 1000 steps it beat GAT-T on TSP100 with 1.26 percent versus 3.24 percent, and at 2000 steps it beat GAT-T on TSP100 with 0.87 percent versus 1.42 percent. It was on par with GAT-T at 5000 samples on TSP50, with 0.21 percent versus 0.20 percent, while using far fewer steps. It remained worse than the supervised GCN beam-search and shortest-tour row on TSP50, with 0.21 percent and 0.12 percent versus 0.01 percent at 1000 and 2000 steps, but achieved lower TSP100 gaps than that row at both 1000 and 2000 steps. In a 512-instance TSP100 comparison against 2-opt First Improvement and Best Improvement with restarts over 1000 steps from the same initial solutions, the learned policy produced lower median tour cost and less dispersion. In the 512-instance best-tour evaluation over 2000 steps, it found optimal TSP20 tours and stayed within 0.1 percent for TSP50 and 0.7 percent for TSP100. Cross-scale testing showed that the TSP50 policy reached 1.86 percent gap on TSP100 with 1000 samples, similar to GAT-T with 3000 samples, and 1.37 percent gap with 2000 steps, outperforming the compared methods. No statistical significance tests are reported.', 'context': 'Training used random Euclidean TSP instances with node coordinates drawn uniformly from the unit square. Batch size was 512 for TSP20 and TSP50 and 256 for TSP100 due to GPU memory. Training ran for 200 epochs for TSP20 and 300 epochs for TSP50 and TSP100. Hyperparameters included gamma 0.99, L2 penalty 1e-5, learning rate 0.001 with 0.98 decay per epoch, hidden dimension 128, 3 layers, one RNN block, reward clipping to 1, and mixed precision training for TSP50 and TSP100. Episode lengths were increased during training, with T1=8, T100=10, T150=20 for TSP20; T1=8, T100=10, T200=20 for TSP50; and T1=4, T100=8, T200=10 for TSP100. Training was performed on an RTX 2080Ti GPU, with average epoch times of 2 minutes 01 seconds, 3 minutes 05 seconds, and 7 minutes 16 seconds for TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100. Validation used a fixed set of instances with known optimal solutions for hyperparameter tuning. Testing used 512 instances for gap curves and 10,000 instances per size for the main comparison table, with Concorde rerun to compute gaps. The method requires random feasible initial tours and a variable number of sampling steps, so inference time scales with the number of improvement steps. This context is relevant to ICAM because the proposed plan also uses optimality gaps and inference times, but ICAM targets constructive solvers, larger scales from 100 to 5,000 nodes, and newer baselines such as POMO, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, MCTS-based methods, TAM, and GLOP, whereas this paper provides a smaller-scale TSP improvement benchmark and a 50-to-100 node generalization check.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 6103, 'output_tokens': 10642}, {'corpus_id': '229340513', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper evaluates a Multi-Decoder Attention Model (MDAM) for constructive neural routing. It uses a Transformer encoder to embed nodes, with the top encoder layer acting as an embedding glimpse layer that re-embeds nodes during construction. Five parallel decoders, each with 128-dimensional features and 8 attention heads, construct routes step by step, and a KL loss encourages diversity among decoders. For search, a customized beam search merges candidate solutions from the decoders. The method is applied to TSP, CVRP, SDVRP, OP, PCTSP, and SPCTSP, with invalid nodes masked at each step. This relates to the proposed ICAM plan because both are attention-based constructive NCO solvers for TSP/CVRP that aim to improve solution quality and generalization; MDAM is a relevant constructive NCO baseline or contrast, though it relies on multi-decoder diversity rather than ICAM's instance-conditioned scale adaptation, attention-free module, or large-scale training.", 'results': 'The provided excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than numerical objective values or optimality gaps. On CVRP20, using greedy decoding and beam search with beam width 50, the paper reports the number of times each of the five decoders finds the best and strictly best solution among all decoders. It states that winning counts are higher than solely winning counts in both greedy and beam search, meaning multiple decoders often find the same best solution, and that no decoder is dominant or non-contributing. The supplementary material reports that the merging technique is effective, analyzes different numbers of decoders, compares against a stronger temperature-tuned attention model, and states that MDAM generalizes better to CVRP150 and CVRP200, but no specific gap values, solution lengths, inference times, or statistical significance tests are included in the provided text.', 'context': 'Experiments use synthetic Euclidean routing instances with 20, 50, and 100 nodes. TSP and CVRP coordinates are sampled uniformly from [0,1] in two dimensions; CVRP capacities are 30, 40, and 50 for 20, 50, and 100 nodes, and non-depot demands are integers 1 to 9. Other problem settings follow prior work. Model hyperparameters include 128-dimensional node embeddings, a 3-layer Transformer encoder with 128-dimensional features and 8 attention heads, a fully connected hidden dimension of 512, five decoders with 128 dimensions and 8 heads, and embedding glimpse re-embedding steps of 2, 4, and 8 for TSP20/50/100 and 2, 6, and 8 for CVRP20/50/100. Training uses 2,500 iterations per epoch, batch size 512 except 256 for CVRP100 due to GPU memory, 100 epochs, Adam with learning rate 1e-4, and KL coefficient 0.01 selected on TSP20. Evaluation includes greedy decoding and beam search with beam width 50 on CVRP20, and supplementary evaluation on larger CVRP instances up to 200 nodes.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2759, 'output_tokens': 3431}, {'corpus_id': '201792004', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '245790960', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '238407851', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a dual-aspect collaborative Transformer model for TSP and CVRP using a constructive neural policy trained with proximal policy optimization and n-step return estimation, together with a curriculum learning strategy intended to improve sample efficiency. Instances are generated on the fly for three sizes, N = 20, 50, and 100. The authors compare local-search operators, including 2-opt, swap, and insert, and select 2-opt for reporting because it performs best for both TSP and CVRP, with insert better than swap. Training uses randomly generated initial solutions, while inference uses solutions generated by a greedy algorithm. Problem-specific inputs, feasibility masks, and hyperparameters are adjusted for TSP and CVRP. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because it represents a constructive neural combinatorial optimization evaluation protocol for TSP and CVRP, using objective quality, local-search post-processing, and inference-time considerations, similar to the proposed comparison of constructive NCO methods and evaluation by solution quality and inference time.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report quantitative objective values, optimality gaps, or inference times for the DACT model. It only states that results are reported based on 2-opt and that some baseline results are adopted from their original papers or from prior work. Therefore, no numerical comparison to Concorde, LKH3, HGS, POMO, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, MCTS-based methods, TAM, or GLOP can be extracted from this text.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions include TSP and CVRP instances with 20, 50, and 100 nodes, random instance generation, 2-opt local search, random initial solutions for training, greedy initial solutions for inference, problem-specific feasibility masks and hyperparameters, and execution on a server equipped with RTX 2080 Ti GPUs and an Intel Xeon E5-2680 CPU at 2.40 GHz. The evaluated scale is much smaller than the proposed ICAM target of 100 to 5,000 nodes, so the paper provides context for small-scale constructive NCO evaluation rather than large-scale generalization.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2286, 'output_tokens': 2293}, {'corpus_id': '270869731', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a unified divide-and-conquer neural solver, UDC, for large-scale combinatorial optimization, including TSP and CVRP. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because the plan also targets large-scale TSP/CVRP/ATSP, uses LKH3-based gaps and inference time, and compares against POMO, ELG, TAM, GLOP, and MCTS-based methods; UDC is a two-stage NCO reference and uses ICAM as one of the constructive policies for VRP subproblems. UDC constructs a sparse graph for each instance, uses an anisotropic graph neural network heatmap dividing policy to generate an initial solution, decomposes the instance into subproblems based on that solution, and solves the subproblems with constructive neural policies such as POMO or ICAM for vehicle routing. It trains the dividing and conquering policies jointly with REINFORCE and a Divide-Conquer-Reunion process that adds a reunion subproblem between adjacent subproblems to reduce biased rewards. The main extracted experiments use 500, 1000, and 2000 node TSP and CVRP instances, with 128 instances for TSP500 and CVRP500 and 100 instances for CVRP1000 and CVRP2000.', 'results': 'On TSP, UDC-x50 with alpha=50 achieved objective 16.78, gap 1.58 percent, time 4m at 500 nodes; 23.53, 1.78 percent, 8m at 1000 nodes; and 33.26, 2.49 percent, 15m at 2000 nodes. UDC-x2 greedy achieved 16.94, 2.53 percent, 20s; 23.79, 2.92 percent, 32s; and 34.14, 5.19 percent, 23s. ICAM achieved 16.65, 0.77 percent, 38s at 500 nodes; 23.49, 1.58 percent, 1m at 1000 nodes; and 34.37, 5.93 percent, 3.8m at 2000 nodes. POMO achieved 20.19, 22.19 percent, 1m at 500 nodes; 32.50, 40.57 percent, 8m at 1000 nodes; and 50.89, 56.85 percent, 10m at 2000 nodes. ELG achieved 17.18, 4.00 percent, 2.2m at 500 nodes; 24.78, 7.18 percent, 13.7m at 1000 nodes; and 36.14, 11.37 percent, 14.2m at 2000 nodes. GLOP achieved 16.88, 2.19 percent, 1.5m at 500 nodes; 23.84, 3.12 percent, 3.5m at 1000 nodes; and 33.71, 3.89 percent, 9m at 2000 nodes. TAM achieved 16.94, 2.55 percent, 32m at 500 nodes; 23.77, 2.79 percent, 32m at 1000 nodes; and 33.35, 2.76 percent, 14m at 2000 nodes. Attn-MCTS achieved 16.97, 2.69 percent, 6m at 500 nodes; 23.86, 3.20 percent, 12m at 1000 nodes; and 33.42, 2.99 percent at 2000 nodes. LKH3 reference objectives were 16.52, 23.12, and 32.45. On CVRP, UDC-x50 achieved 38.34, 3.01 percent, 7.7m at 500 nodes; 43.48, 1.11 percent, 14m at 1000 nodes; and 60.94, 1.55 percent, 23m at 2000 nodes. UDC-x250 achieved 37.99, 2.06 percent, 35m at 500 nodes; 43.00 with time 1.2h at 1000 nodes; and 60.01 with time 2.15h at 2000 nodes, with the extracted table not showing a clear gap value for the last two cells. ICAM achieved 37.49, 0.69 percent, 42s at 500 nodes; 43.07, 0.16 percent, 26s at 1000 nodes; and 61.34, 2.21 percent, 3.7m at 2000 nodes. ELG achieved 38.34, 2.99 percent, 2.6m at 500 nodes and 43.58, 1.33 percent, 15.6m at 1000 nodes. GLOP-G achieved 42.45, 11.73 percent, 2.4m at 500 nodes; 45.90, 6.74 percent, 2m at 1000 nodes; and 63.02, 5.01 percent, 2.5m at 2000 nodes. TAM achieved 46.3, 7.67 percent, 4m at 1000 nodes and 64.8, 7.97 percent, 9.6m at 2000 nodes. LKH3 reference objectives were 37.23, 46.40, and 64.90. Overall, ICAM is faster and very competitive at 500 and 1000 nodes, while UDC-x50 and UDC-x250 improve large-scale gaps at higher inference cost. No statistical significance tests are reported in the extracted section.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions: TSP500 and CVRP500 test sets contained 128 instances; CVRP1000 and CVRP2000 contained 100 instances. UDC used alpha=50 sampled initial solutions for TSP and CVRP, greedy UDC-x2 and multi-stage UDC-x50 or UDC-x250 variants, Adam optimizer, varying-size training, lightweight dividing networks, and one model per problem. Training was on a single NVIDIA Tesla V100S GPU, with most tasks within 10 days. Metrics were objective value, gap to the best algorithm, and solving time. The extracted main table includes LKH3, POMO, ELG, ICAM, GLOP, TAM, H-TSP, BQ, LEHD, Attn-MCTS, DIMES, and DIFUSCO, but does not include Concorde, HGS, MatNet, or Pointerformer, so the paper supports only part of the proposed baseline set. This context is directly relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because it provides a comparable large-scale TSP/CVRP benchmark, the same gap-and-time evaluation style, and a two-stage NCO reference point for assessing ICAM against constructive and divide-and-conquer methods.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 8150, 'output_tokens': 12715}, {'corpus_id': '252780745', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a meta-learning-based construction heuristic learner for TSP, referred to as DIMES, by training a GNN followed by an MLP with first-order MAML and REINFORCE, then applying per-instance active search/fine-tuning. It benchmarks the trained model on TSP-500, TSP-1000, and TSP-10000 against exact solvers such as Concorde and Gurobi, a strong heuristic solver LKH-3 under default and reduced-trial settings, simple insertion heuristics, and learning-based TSP solvers including POMO+EAS, EAN, AM, GCN, and Att-GCN. The comparison also includes decoding variants such as greedy decoding, sampling, beam search, and MCTS, with optional 2-OPT local search. This is directly related to the proposed ICAM baseline comparison because it uses the same evaluation logic: position a neural constructive routing solver against classical exact/heuristic solvers, constructive NCO baselines, and two-stage search or active-search methods while reporting solution quality and inference time.', 'results': 'The excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than numeric values. DIMES is reported to significantly outperform many previous learning-based methods, outperform a supervised method despite being trained without ground-truth solutions, and consistently outperform simple traditional heuristics. The best reported DIMES configuration, RL+AS+MCTS, achieves the strongest performance but requires considerably more time. RL+AS+G/S is faster and competitive with simple heuristics. Removing active search reduces runtime with only a slight, acceptable performance drop while remaining better than many previous learning-based methods in both solution quality and inference time. No specific optimality gaps, tour costs, or runtime numbers are provided in the supplied text.', 'context': "Experimental conditions include TSP instances of 500, 1000, and 10000 nodes. DIMES is trained directly on large graphs, whereas baselines are trained on small graphs and evaluated on large graphs. The model uses a GNN followed by an MLP, fine-tunes only the GNN output and MLP, uses T=15 for TSP-500, T=14 for TSP-1000, and T=12 for TSP-10000, performs 100 fine-tuning steps for TSP-500 and TSP-1000 and 50 steps for TSP-10000, uses AdamW, and sets temperature tau=0.01 for DIMES+S and tau=1 for DIMES+AS+S. LKH-3 is run with default 10000 trials and with reduced trials, 500 for TSP-500 and TSP-1000 and 250 for TSP-10000, to match DIMES+MCTS running times. Att-GCN is rerun on the authors' hardware for fair time comparison, but hardware specifications are not given. This context supports the proposed ICAM plan's need for fair large-scale TSP comparisons, especially against Concorde, LKH-3, POMO, and MCTS or active-search baselines, though the paper does not cover HGS, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, TAM, GLOP, CVRP, or ATSP."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4726, 'output_tokens': 4045}, {'corpus_id': '266191425', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper describes GLOP, a two-stage neural routing solver that is directly relevant to the proposed research method because GLOP is listed as one of the two-stage NCO baselines against which ICAM will be compared. GLOP learns local policies for solving (sub-)TSP instances and global policies for partitioning larger routing problems into sub-TSPs. For TSP, it first constructs an initial tour using Random Insertion, then decomposes the tour into independent subtours and trains a local reviser to reconstruct those subtours in order to reduce total tour length. The local policy is trained by minimizing expected solution length with a REINFORCE-based estimator, using the average length of two greedy rollouts as the baseline. For general routing problems, a global policy predicts a partition heatmap, samples node partitions in parallel, passes the sampled partitions to the local GLOP solver, and is trained with REINFORCE using the average reward of sampled partitions for the same instance as the baseline. The experimental setup covers large-scale TSP instances of 500, 1,000, 10,000, and 100,000 nodes, cross-distribution TSP instances, 49 symmetric TSPLIB EUC2D instances with fewer than 1,000 nodes normalized to the unit square, ATSP instances, CVRP instances at 1,000, 2,000, 5,000, and 7,000 nodes plus CVRPLIB instances, and PCTSP instances at 500, 1,000, and 5,000 nodes. This relates to the ICAM research plan because ICAM aims to improve cross-scale generalization for TSP, CVRP, and ATSP and explicitly plans to compare against GLOP using optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference time.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not contain numerical objective values, optimality gaps, runtime tables, or statistical significance tests for GLOP. It reports only qualitative outcomes: GLOP is stated to outperform H-TSP without requiring a learned upper-level TSP policy, and compared with a select-and-optimize approach it is stated to achieve competitive solutions while being hundreds of times more efficient. Because no quantitative results are included in the supplied text, no exact performance comparison with the proposed ICAM method can be extracted from this paper excerpt.', 'context': 'Experiments are conducted on a 12-core Intel Xeon Platinum 8255C CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. Dataset generation follows established benchmark protocols: TSP instances include large-scale uniformly sampled instances and a 100,000-node instance; CVRP instances are generated using the settings of TAM and the code of AM with 100 instances per scale; PCTSP instances use prize sampling parameters K_n = 9, 12, and 20 for n = 500, 1,000, and 5,000 respectively; TSPLIB instances are min-max normalized to the unit square. Baselines are selected as state-of-the-art methods with adjustable runtime so that comparisons can be made under comparable computational budgets. These conditions are important for the proposed ICAM research plan because ICAM will be evaluated on similar TSP, CVRP, and ATSP scales and will compare against GLOP and other baselines using objective values, optimality gaps relative to strong heuristics, and inference time.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3378, 'output_tokens': 2607}, {'corpus_id': '268042668', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '275820693', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '226222332', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper describes the shared-baseline policy-gradient training method used in POMO for constructive neural combinatorial optimization. For each instance, the policy network samples a set of N solution trajectories, computes the total return or reward for each trajectory, and uses a shared return-based baseline across those N trajectories when estimating the policy gradient. The gradient ascent objective uses the advantage R(τ_i) - b_i(s), where b_i(s) is the shared baseline rather than a trajectory-specific greedy-rollout baseline. The method is implemented with mini-batch training and is contrasted with a greedy-rollout baseline that compares each sampled rollout against an argmax rollout from the same starting node. This method is directly relevant to the proposed research method and research plan because the proposed ICAM work explicitly compares against POMO and uses a POMO-based loss in its three-stage training scheme, especially in the top-k elite training stage. Thus, the paper’s shared-baseline method represents a core baseline training approach and reference point for ICAM’s policy optimization and baseline comparisons.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not report quantitative experimental outcomes, statistical significance, optimality gaps, solution-quality values, or inference times. It reports only qualitative and algorithmic outcomes: the shared baseline produces zero-mean advantages, is stated to induce less variance in policy gradients than the greedy-rollout baseline, is computationally cheaper than baselines requiring a separately trained critic or a greedy-rollout forward pass, and is argued to reduce premature convergence to suboptimal policies by making each sampled trajectory compete with N-1 other heterogeneous trajectories.', 'context': 'The experimental context is policy-gradient training for constructive neural combinatorial optimization, with the paper’s section list referencing traveling salesman problem, capacitated vehicle routing problem, and 0-1 knapsack problem settings. However, the provided excerpt does not specify instance sizes, dataset protocols, hardware, learning rates, number of sampled trajectories N, training steps, or other hyperparameters. The key condition is that N sampled trajectories are generated per instance and no two trajectories are identical, with the shared baseline computed from the set of sampled returns. This context matters for the proposed ICAM research plan because ICAM’s evaluation and training design rely on POMO-style multiple trajectories, top-k trajectory selection, and comparisons across TSP, CVRP, and ATSP scales, so the paper’s method defines the baseline policy-gradient behavior against which ICAM’s instance-conditioned adaptation and top-k loss should be compared.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2432, 'output_tokens': 2527}, {'corpus_id': '249097710', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper evaluates Sym-NCO, a symmetry-regularized reinforcement learning method for combinatorial optimization Markov decision processes, applied to encoder-decoder constructive solvers: POMO for TSP and CVRP, AM for PCTSP and OP, and PointerNet for TSP. The approach trains an instance-conditioned policy to construct solutions sequentially from node coordinates and features, then compares the augmented models against classical solvers such as Concorde, LKH-3, ILS, and Compass, and against constructive neural baselines including PointerNet, S2V-DQN, AM, POMO, MDAM, and Nazari et al. This is related to the proposed ICAM baseline-comparison method because it uses the same general evaluation logic: constructive NCO models versus exact or heuristic solvers, with optimality gap, solution quality, and inference time as primary metrics. However, the paper's method is symmetry regularization rather than ICAM's instance-conditioned adaptation function, attention-free module, or progressive training scheme.", 'results': "Sym-NCO reported better solution quality than the neural constructive baselines in both greedy and multi-start settings and was reported to have the fastest inference speed among the compared neural methods. For TSP with N=100, the greedy rollout achieved a 0.95% optimality gap and solved 10,000 instances in a few seconds. On TSPLIB, Sym-NCO improved over POMO, with reported gaps of 1.62% for Sym-NCO versus 1.87% for POMO. For PCTSP in the multi-start setting, Sym-NCO outperformed the classical ILS baseline while being approximately 240 times faster. Time-versus-cost plots showed Sym-NCO on the Pareto frontier for TSP, CVRP, PCTSP, and OP. No error bars or statistical significance tests were reported in the provided excerpt. These results are relevant to the ICAM research plan because they demonstrate that a constructive NCO method can be positioned against Concorde, LKH-3, and POMO using gap and runtime metrics, but they do not provide evidence for ICAM's large-scale 1,000+ node generalization or for the specific ICAM components.", 'context': "Experiments used N=100 instances for TSP, CVRP, PCTSP, and OP, plus real-world TSPLIB instances with 50<N<250. Evaluation used 10,000 benchmark instances for average cost, greedy and multi-start rollouts, and time-cost trade-offs. Training used Nvidia A100 GPUs, while inference-speed comparisons used an Intel Xeon E5-2630 CPU and Nvidia RTX 2080Ti GPU. The augmented models reused the original network architectures and training hyperparameters of POMO, AM, and PointerNet. For PCTSP and OP, multi-start or symmetric sampling used M=200. The paper also reports hyperparameter tuning of alpha for CVRP, but the provided excerpt does not give the full tuning grid or final value. This context matters for the proposed method because ICAM's planned comparisons to Concorde, LKH-3, POMO, and other constructive methods should use similar gap and runtime reporting, but ICAM targets much larger scales, up to 5,000 nodes for TSP/CVRP and 1,000 nodes for ATSP, and additional baselines not covered in this paper, including HGS, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, MCTS-based methods, TAM, and GLOP."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4944, 'output_tokens': 3867}, {'corpus_id': '45328883', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates HGSADC, a population-based hybrid genetic search with adaptive diversity control for multi-depot, multi-period vehicle routing and CVRP. It uses two subpopulations, a biased fitness that combines penalized-cost rank and diversity-contribution rank, a normalized Hamming distance over service patterns and depot assignments, parent selection and crossover, education or local search, and periodic population management. For CVRP, the method is adapted by replacing the periodic distance with a broken-pairs distance, using OX crossover on cyclic giant tours, and limiting the local-search neighborhood to 20 nodes, while retaining the adaptive diversity control. The experimental setup compares HGSADC against previously reported state-of-the-art methods and best-known solutions, using average gaps and run times over 10 independent runs. This is related to the proposed ICAM plan because ICAM will benchmark against classical HGS-family solvers and report optimality gaps and inference times on CVRP; the paper provides a classical HGS-family reference for CVRP and multi-depot periodic VRP, though it does not test neural constructive or two-stage NCO methods.', 'results': 'On 42 PVRP and 33 MDVRP benchmark instances, HGSADC achieved an average gap of +0.20% to previous best-known solutions for PVRP and -0.01% for MDVRP, with per-instance standard deviations of 0.15% and 0.05%, respectively. It produced new best average results on 41 of 42 PVRP instances and all 33 MDVRP instances, and new best-known solutions on 20 PVRP and 9 MDVRP instances. For short MDVRP runs, the average time to the final solution was 2.15 minutes, and with 5 times 10^4 iterations and 1 hour, previous best-known solutions were retrieved on all runs for 21 of 33 MDVRP instances. On 10 constructed MDPVRP instances, the average gap was +0.42%, and the best-of-10 gap was +0.13%. On 34 CVRP instances, the overall gap to best-known solutions was 0.11%, matching the best specialized algorithm reported, and 12 new best-known solutions were found. Sensitivity analysis showed education, population management, crossover, infeasible solutions, and repair all contributed, with education most important; adaptive diversity control outperformed no diversity, objective-space dispersal, and solution-space dispersal, maintaining higher population entropy and continuing to improve near the time limit.', 'context': "Experiments were implemented in C++ on an AMD Opteron 250 at 2.4 GHz, with reported times converted to equivalent Pentium IV 3.0 GHz run times. Standard stopping conditions were 10^4 iterations and 10 minutes, 2 times 10^4 iterations and 30 minutes, and 5 times 10^4 iterations and 1 hour; MDPVRP used 30 minutes with 10^4 iterations. Parameters were calibrated with CMA-ES per problem class; most parameters were averaged across classes, generation size was 40 for PVRP, 70 for MDVRP, and 100 for MDPVRP, and education rate was fixed to 1. Instance sizes ranged from 50 to 417 customers for PVRP and MDVRP, with some large problems over 450 visits causing population-size reduction; CVRP instances included up to 483 customer visits. The paper's conditions are relevant to the proposed ICAM evaluation as a classical HGS-family baseline for CVRP gap and runtime, but the paper does not cover large-scale 1,000+ node instances, TSP or ATSP, neural baselines, or ICAM's instance-conditioned adaptation."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 6904, 'output_tokens': 5981}, {'corpus_id': '235490054', 'analysis': {'method': 'The relevant paper method is an experimental benchmark of MatNet-based neural solvers against classical and heuristic baselines on ATSP and FFSP. For ATSP, trained MatNet models are evaluated on 10,000 tmat-class test instances, with tour length as the primary solution-quality metric and gap percentages computed relative to CPLEX results. Representative heuristic baselines are also included, with LKH3 listed among the appendix baselines. For FFSP, MatNet-based schedules are compared on 1,000 test instances using average makespan, with gaps reported relative to MatNet with 128-fold instance augmentation. The runtime protocol measures computation time only, excludes program and data loading, divides single-thread heuristic runtimes by 8 as a parallel reference, and measures all MatNet inference on a single GPU even when training used multiple GPUs. This relates to the proposed ICAM research plan because ICAM also plans to compare against classical solvers and constructive NCO methods, including MatNet, using optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference times; however, the paper does not evaluate ICAM, AAFM, instance-conditioned adaptation, or the proposed three-stage training scheme.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not include numeric table values, so no quantitative performance can be reported. It states that Table 4.1 records average tour lengths in units of 10^6 and gap percentages relative to CPLEX for ATSP, and Table 5.2 records average makespan and absolute gaps relative to MatNet with 128-fold augmentation for FFSP. The excerpt does not state the magnitude of gaps, whether MatNet outperforms or underperforms each baseline, or any statistical significance. The only inferable outcome is that MatNet with 128-fold augmentation is used as the reference point for FFSP gaps, implying it is a selected strong MatNet configuration, but the provided text does not confirm its ranking against the other baselines.', 'context': "Experimental conditions include 10,000 tmat-class ATSP instances and 1,000 FFSP instances. ATSP tour lengths are scaled by 10^6, and ATSP gaps are percentage gaps versus CPLEX. FFSP gaps are absolute makespan gaps versus MatNet with 128-fold augmentation. Runtime accounting excludes loading and program startup, uses computation time only, scales single-thread heuristic times by 1/8, and uses single-GPU inference for MatNet. The excerpt does not specify GPU model, CPU, instance size distribution, training details, hyperparameters, or the exact full baseline set beyond MIP/CPLEX, heuristics, and MatNet. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM evaluation because the plan also calls for optimality gaps and inference times on TSP, CVRP, and ATSP, including MatNet and classical solvers, but the paper's conditions are limited to ATSP and FFSP and do not cover the proposed large-scale TSP/CVRP generalization or ICAM-specific ablations."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2271, 'output_tokens': 3451}, {'corpus_id': '259093216', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '259298711', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '235376887', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates efficient active search (EAS) as a search-time improvement procedure for constructive neural combinatorial optimization models. For TSP and CVRP, EAS is integrated into the POMO constructive method; for JSSP, it is integrated into L2D. The EAS variants update restricted subsets of model parameters rather than the full model: EAS-Emb directly modifies node embeddings, EAS-Lay updates an added layer, and EAS-Tab is associated with a tabu/intensification-style search component. The procedure samples solutions over 200 search iterations, uses the Adam optimizer, and tunes the hyperparameters lambda, sigma, alpha, and the learning rate by Bayesian optimization on validation instances drawn from the same distribution as test instances. This is related to the proposed research plan because POMO is one of the constructive NCO baselines that ICAM will compare against, and the paper provides evidence about how constructive routing models can be improved through active search and parameter updates, although it does not implement instance-conditioned adaptation, attention-free modules, or large-scale generalization tests.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt reports qualitative search-trajectory outcomes rather than numeric optimality gaps. For all three problems, pure sampling produced solution quality that did not improve over the 200 iterations, while all active search variants improved solution quality over time. On TSP, EAS-Emb and EAS-Lay had nearly identical performance, with EAS-Tab slightly better; the original active search was much more unstable, attributed to a learning rate that was too high despite validation tuning. On CVRP, all EAS variants found better solutions on average than the original active search after only a few iterations, suggesting that fixing most parameters simplifies learning and speeds convergence. On JSSP, EAS-Emb was significantly better than the other methods because the L2D model has only two node features and a complex embedding generation procedure, so directly modifying embeddings was more effective than fine-tuning the full embedding process. No exact objective values, gap percentages, or inference times are given in the excerpt.', 'context': 'Experiments were run on a GPU cluster using one Nvidia Tesla V100 GPU and one Intel Xeon 4114 CPU core at 2.2 GHz per experiment. The models were based on existing POMO and L2D implementations, with minor modifications to add EAS; trained models from the original authors were used when available. Hyperparameters were tuned on separate validation instances from the same distribution as test instances and were not retuned for larger generalization instances. The trajectory analysis monitored 200 search iterations and averaged quality over all JSSP test instances and the first 1,000 TSP and CVRP test instances. The setting is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because it uses POMO-based TSP/CVRP constructive baselines and solution-quality monitoring, but it does not specify instance scales, optimality-gap baselines against classical solvers, or inference-time comparisons required by the proposed method.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2510, 'output_tokens': 2940}, {'corpus_id': '278768989', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '47017706', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '219150860', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1749, 'output_tokens': 9910}, {'corpus_id': '252417860', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '249038331', 'analysis': {'method': "Neural-3-OPT is a learned local-search method for TSP. It starts from a randomly generated tour and repeatedly applies 3-opt moves. At each improvement step, the state contains the current tour and the best tour so far. An encoder computes an embedding of the current solution, and two decoders select an action consisting of three links to remove and one reconnecting type. The action is applied as a 3-opt move, producing the next state. The MDP reward is the reduction in the best tour length at that step, and returns are discounted cumulative rewards. The paper's section list indicates actor-critic policy optimization, but the provided excerpt does not fully specify the training objective or network details. This method relates to the proposed research method and ICAM plan because it is a neural, improvement-based TSP solver that can be treated as a related two-stage NCO baseline or contrast, and it directly tests cross-scale generalization, which is a central goal of ICAM.", 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report numerical optimality gaps, absolute tour lengths, or statistical significance. It reports that on TSP200, Neural-3-OPT outperforms L-2-OPT. When a model trained on TSP50 is applied to TSP200, it significantly outperforms L-2-OPT. When a model trained on TSP100 is applied to TSP200, it performs worse than L-2-OPT. It also reports that on TSP200 the method converges faster and reaches a better final tour length than L-2-OPT. These outcomes are qualitative in the excerpt and do not provide exact percentage gaps, confidence intervals, or p-values.', 'context': 'Experiments used four randomly generated TSP datasets: TSP20, TSP50, TSP100, and TSP200, each with 10,000 instances whose nodes are drawn from the unit square. Training used batch size 512, vector dimension 128, 3 layers, loss weights 0.5 and 0.005, and neighbor size 8. Epochs were 200 for TSP20, 300 for TSP50 and TSP100, and 500 for TSP200. Episode lengths included 8, 10, and 20 improvement steps depending on dataset. Evaluation was run on an Intel Xeon CPU and RTX 3090 GPU, with Neural-3-OPT and L-2-OPT evaluated on the same platform. Generalization tests trained on smaller-scale instances and tested on TSP200. The method is limited to TSP and learned 3-opt improvement; it does not evaluate CVRP, ATSP, classical solvers, or constructive decoding, so its relevance to the proposed ICAM plan is as a related TSP generalization baseline rather than a full substitute for the proposed baseline suite.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3119, 'output_tokens': 4981}, {'corpus_id': '250493174', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates Simulation-guided Beam Search (SGBS) and SGBS+EAS as inference-time search and fine-tuning methods for constructive neural solvers. SGBS performs breadth-first beam search over a decision tree, using expansion, simulation, and pruning to improve solutions generated by a pre-trained policy. SGBS+EAS adds insertion layers to the policy, uses SGBS-generated solutions plus sampled solutions for policy-gradient updates, and returns the best incumbent solution. Experiments use Transformer-like POMO-trained attention models for TSP and CVRP, with x8 instance augmentation, and a MatNet-based solver for FFSP. The evaluation setup closely matches the proposed ICAM baseline plan: it compares against classical solvers Concorde for TSP, HGS for CVRP, and LKH3; against constructive neural baselines including POMO-trained models and MatNet; and against additional learned heuristics DACT, NLNS, and DPDP. It also resembles two-stage neural search or fine-tuning approaches such as MCTS-based, TAM, or GLOP methods because it first searches with a learned policy and then refines an auxiliary module. Metrics are optimality gaps, solution quality, and runtime, with GPU runtime adjusted to a single-GPU premise and CPU solver wall-clock times reported separately.', 'results': 'SGBS+EAS significantly outperforms the compared learned heuristics. On three TSP instance sets, it reduces optimality gaps by 45%, 33%, and 35% relative to EAS alone. On CVRP instance sets, it reduces gaps by 52%, 56%, and 59% relative to EAS alone and outperforms LKH3 by a large margin. On CVRP with 100 nodes, SGBS+EAS achieves a 0.11% gap to HGS, indicating quality competitive with a strong handcrafted solver, although the paper states it is not competitive in runtime. For FFSP, SGBS+EAS is reported to significantly outperform CPLEX mixed-integer programming and metaheuristic baselines, but the provided excerpt does not give exact numerical gaps.', 'context': 'Experiments run on Nvidia A100 GPUs with 80 GB memory; reported GPU runtimes are adjusted under a single-GPU premise. TSP and CVRP use 10,000 instances with 100 nodes, plus 1,000-instance test sets with 150 and 200 nodes for domain-shift evaluation; a real-world-like CVRP dataset is discussed in an appendix. FFSP uses 1,000 instances each for 20, 50, and 100 jobs, with three stages and four machines per stage. SGBS hyperparameters are (beta, gamma) = (10, 10) for TSP and (4, 4) for CVRP, selected by trial runs and described as not highly sensitive within reasonable ranges. The same POMO-trained model is used for greedy construction, sampling, and EAS; EAS insertion layers are randomly initialized and trained by policy gradient until convergence or timeout. Classical solvers are CPU-bound, so their wall-clock times are not directly comparable to GPU-computed neural method times.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3473, 'output_tokens': 3812}, {'corpus_id': '269502224', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a multi-task construction-based neural vehicle routing solver with mixture-of-experts layers, called MVMoE, against classical and neural baselines. This is directly related to the proposed ICAM baseline-comparison method because it uses POMO-style constructive neural solvers and classical routing solvers as comparison points, and it reports optimality-gap-style performance on multiple VRP variants. MVMoE uses an encoder-decoder autoregressive construction policy: static node features such as coordinates, demand, and time-window start/end times are encoded, and the decoder uses the last selected node embedding plus dynamic features such as remaining vehicle capacity, current time, partial route length, and an open-route indicator to select the next valid node. Missing attributes for a selected VRP variant are padded with default values, typically zeros. The model is trained across 16 VRP variants formed from five constraints, with a VRP variant randomly selected in each training step. The evaluated variants include a 4-expert MVMoE configuration and a lower-computation MVMoE/4E-L configuration. Baselines include HGS for CVRP and VRPTW with default maximum 20000 iterations without improvement, LKH3 for CVRP, OVRP, VRPL, and VRPTW with 10000 trials and 1 run, OR-Tools for all 16 variants using parallel cheapest insertion as the first-solution strategy and guided local search, and neural baselines POMO trained per task and POMO-MTL trained multi-task. Evaluation reports percentage performance gaps or objective degradation on trained and unseen VRP variants, including zero-shot and few-shot generalization tests.', 'results': 'On the six trained VRPs, single-task POMO was better than multi-task solvers on each individual problem, but its average performance was much worse: POMO trained only on CVRP had 16.815% average performance, while POMO-MTL had 2.102% and MVMoE/4E had 1.925%. MVMoE consistently outperformed POMO-MTL. MVMoE/4E was slightly better than MVMoE/4E-L but required more computation, while MVMoE/4E-L showed stronger out-of-distribution generalization. On ten unseen VRP variants, zero-shot MVMoE significantly outperformed POMO-MTL across all variants. In the few-shot setting on n=50, after fine-tuning on 10,000 target-VRP instances per epoch, described as 0.01% of the total training instances, MVMoE generalized more favorably than POMO-MTL on VRPBLTW and OVRPBLTW. The excerpt does not provide statistical tests, confidence intervals, or p-values, and it does not explicitly report neural inference times, so the reported advantage should be interpreted as a performance-gap comparison rather than a statistically validated or latency-validated superiority.', 'context': "Experiments were run on NVIDIA A100-80GB GPUs and AMD EPYC 7513 CPUs at 2.6 GHz. Instance sizes were n=50 and n=100 for benchmark comparisons. Classical solver time limits were 20 seconds for n=50 and 40 seconds for n=100, with longer 200-second and 400-second runs also provided for OR-Tools. Classical solvers solved 32 instances in parallel on 32 CPU cores. The study covered 16 VRP variants with five constraints, of which 6 were trained and 10 were unseen. Few-shot fine-tuning used 10,000 instances per epoch, implying a total training set of roughly 100 million instances if 10,000 is 0.01%. The exact instance-generation distributions are not specified in the provided excerpt. The paper's comparison is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because it uses POMO and classical routing solvers as baselines and reports solution-quality gaps on VRP variants, but it does not include Concorde, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, MCTS-based methods, TAM, or GLOP, and it does not test the large-scale 1,000+ node cross-scale generalization setting central to ICAM. Additionally, LKH3 and HGS are heuristic classical solvers, and the excerpt does not state that their outputs are certified optimal, so the percentage gaps should be interpreted relative to reported solver performance rather than proven optima."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3420, 'output_tokens': 5477}, {'corpus_id': '256900800', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies DIFUSCO, a discrete diffusion-based non-autoregressive constructive solver for combinatorial optimization. The method treats solution construction as an iterative denoising process over a discrete solution representation, uses a graph-based denoising network with anisotropic graph neural network layers, and employs a fast denoising schedule for inference. For TSP, DIFUSCO generates a heatmap over candidate solution elements and can use decoding strategies such as MCTS or multiple diffusion samples to improve solution quality. The experimental setup uses 2-D Euclidean TSP instances sampled uniformly from the unit square, with TSP-50 as the main configuration benchmark and larger TSP-100, TSP-500, TSP-1000, and TSP-10000 instances for scalability. Training labels are generated using Concorde for TSP-50/100 and LKH-3 for TSP-500/1000/10000, and test instances follow prior TSP benchmarks. For MIS, the paper evaluates on SATLIB and Erdős-Rényi graphs, including ER-[700-800] with pairwise connection probability 0.15. This relates to the proposed ICAM research plan because both focus on neural constructive solvers for TSP/routing at multiple scales, use exact or strong heuristic solvers such as Concorde and LKH-3 as references, and consider MCTS-based two-stage decoding and inference-time evaluation.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not report quantitative experimental outcomes. It indicates that DIFUSCO is evaluated on TSP-50/100/500/1000/10000 and on MIS datasets, and that it is compared against state-of-the-art methods, but no specific optimality gaps, objective values, inference times, statistical significance, or numerical baseline comparisons are provided in the text.', 'context': 'Key experimental conditions include uniform unit-square TSP instances, a main benchmark of TSP-50, larger scales up to 10,000 nodes, exact labels from Concorde for small instances, LKH-3 heuristic labels for larger instances, and test sets inherited from prior TSP benchmarks. MIS experiments use SATLIB and Erdős-Rényi graphs with connection probability 0.15. The excerpt does not specify hardware, model size, number of diffusion steps, MCTS budget, random seeds, or detailed metric definitions, so reproducibility details are incomplete.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3122, 'output_tokens': 3037}, {'corpus_id': '237616034', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1783, 'output_tokens': 714}, {'corpus_id': '238583704', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a hybrid neural-guided local search method for the TSP. A graph neural network is trained by supervised learning on the line graph of the TSP instance, using only edge weight, defined as the Euclidean distance between adjacent nodes, as the input feature. The model predicts an approximation of the global regret associated with including each edge in the tour. These predicted edge regrets are then used by a regret-guided local search metaheuristic, together with the original TSP graph, to construct and improve tours. This method is relevant to the proposed ICAM research plan because it is a learning-based TSP solver evaluated using optimality gaps and inference times against classical solvers and other neural routing methods, but it differs from ICAM in that it is a regret-guided local search approach rather than a constructive attention-free neural solver, and it is evaluated only on small TSP instances.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not include the numerical results section, so no specific optimality gaps, computation times, or counts of optimally solved instances can be reported. The paper states that it measures the trade-off between solution quality and computation time on randomly generated TSP sets and TSPLIB instances, and that fixed-time experiments compare learning-based approaches with Concorde, but the quantitative outcomes are absent from the provided text.', 'context': 'Experiments use 1,000 instances each for TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100, generated by uniform random node locations in the unit square, plus TSPLIB instances for real-world generalization. Two evaluation protocols are used: one with unfixed time and quality, reporting mean optimality gap and computation time per instance, and one with a 10-second per-instance limit that includes feature calculation, model evaluation, and initial solution construction, reporting mean optimality gap and the number of instances solved optimally. A common single CPU and single GPU setup is used, with GPU parallelism allowed where implementations sample multiple solutions. Baselines include nearest neighbour, farthest insertion, local search, Concorde, LKH-3, a knowledge-guided local search method, and three learning-based TSP methods. This context relates to the proposed research plan because it uses the same general TSP evaluation metrics and classical solver baselines, but it does not cover the proposed large-scale 100 to 5,000-node generalization, CVRP or ATSP settings, or ICAM-specific architectural and training ablations.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2823, 'output_tokens': 3576}, {'corpus_id': '259298553', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '255546018', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a step-wise constructive neural routing solver trained by imitation learning from expert trajectories. It creates a one-million-solution expert dataset for Euclidean TSP, CVRP, OP, ATSP, and KP using Concorde, LKH, and EA4OP, then samples subpaths of length 4 to N from optimal tours to form minibatches, which augments training data. The model has 9 layers, 12 attention heads, embedding size 192, and feed-forward dimension 512, and is trained with cross-entropy loss, Adam with initial learning rate 7.5e-4 and decay 0.98 every 50 epochs, batch size 1024, and 500 epochs. It is trained on synthetic size-100 instances and tested on synthetic sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, plus TSPLib instances up to 4461 nodes and Euclidean CVRPLib instances. It is compared with classical solvers such as OR-Tools, LKH, and HGS, hybrid methods such as DIFUSCO+2opt, Att-GCN+MCTS, and SGBS, and constructive neural baselines such as AM, TransformerTSP, MDAM, POMO, DIMES, and Sym-NCO. The evaluation uses optimality gap and inference time. This is directly related to the proposed ICAM method because the research plan also targets TSP, CVRP, and ATSP, emphasizes large-scale generalization, and proposes comparison against classical solvers, constructive NCO methods, and MCTS-based two-stage methods using optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference times.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not include the numeric values from the results table, but it reports that the model shows superior generalization to larger graphs even with greedy decoding, which generates a single solution while baselines generate several hundred solutions and select the best. Beam search improves optimality gaps but increases runtime. Replacing quadratic attention with PerceiverIO gives a considerable reduction in inference time for larger graphs with some performance drop, while still outperforming other NCO baselines. On TSPLib and CVRPLib, the model significantly outperforms end-to-end baseline policies with greedy decoding. The only competitive methods are Att-GCN+MCTS and DIFUSCO+2opt, but both are hybrid, TSP-only, and 2 to 15 times slower. With greedy decoding, the model is slightly slower than Sym-NCO, competitive with POMO, and significantly faster than other models. No statistical significance tests are reported in the excerpt.', 'context': 'Training uses synthetic size-100 instances; ATSP uses Euclidean distance matrices with randomly broken symmetries. Test instances use sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, with CVRP capacities of 50, 80, 100, and 250 respectively. The expert dataset contains one million solutions, and subpath sampling starts at length 4. For non-Perceiver models, test-time subgraphs are limited to the 250 nearest neighbors of the origin node to reduce inference time without significant quality impact. Deep learning models run on a single Nvidia Tesla V100-S GPU with 24GB memory, while classical solvers run on an Intel Xeon E5-2670 CPU with 256GB memory in one thread. TSP optimality gaps use Concorde optima, while CVRP gaps use LKH solutions as reference for comparability even though HGS is better. This context is relevant to ICAM because the proposed plan also uses LKH3, HGS, and Concorde-style references, reports inference time, and focuses on large-scale routing, but ICAM extends evaluation to 5,000 nodes and adds baselines such as MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, TAM, and GLOP that are not present in the paper.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3273, 'output_tokens': 5091}, {'corpus_id': '3649804', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a pointer-network-based neural combinatorial optimization method for Euclidean TSP, using supervised learning, reinforcement-learning pretraining, and inference-time search variants including greedy decoding, sampling, and active search. It compares these neural methods against classical solvers and heuristics: Christofides, OR-Tools vehicle routing local search, Concorde, and LK-H, on TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100. The RL pretraining plus sampling or active search pipeline is an early two-stage neural combinatorial optimization approach analogous to the proposed comparison against MCTS-based methods, TAM, and GLOP. This paper is directly related to the proposed research method because it uses the same general evaluation logic of benchmarking a neural routing solver against exact and heuristic solvers while reporting solution quality and inference time, although it is limited to small-scale TSP and does not include the proposed CVRP and ATSP settings or modern baselines such as POMO, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, MCTS-based methods, TAM, or GLOP.', 'results': "Reported outcomes show that reinforcement-learning pretraining significantly improves over supervised learning, and all evaluated neural methods surpass Christofides. Greedy neural methods are described as time-efficient and only a few percent worse than optimality. Inference-time search improves solution quality but increases running time; RL pretraining-Sampling and RL pretraining-Active Search recover optimal solutions in a significant number of test cases. Many RL pretraining methods outperform OR-Tools local search, including RL pretraining-Greedy@16, which runs similarly fast. For small solution spaces, RL pretraining-Sampling with a finetuned softmax temperature outperforms Active Search, while for larger solution spaces Active Search is superior when controlling for the number of sampled solutions or running time. Active Search from an untrained model is competitive but requires 7 hours per TSP50 instance and 25 hours per TSP100 instance. Compared with metaheuristics, the neural method is superior to simulated annealing but slightly less competitive than tabu search and much less competitive than guided local search. These results support the proposed plan's emphasis on reporting optimality gaps and inference times when comparing ICAM to classical solvers and neural combinatorial optimization baselines.", 'context': 'Experimental conditions include 1,000 test graphs per TSP size, with points drawn uniformly at random in the unit square. Models use mini-batches of 128 sequences, LSTM cells with 128 hidden units, 128-dimensional coordinate embeddings, the Adam optimizer, an initial learning rate of 1e-3 for TSP20 and TSP50 and 1e-4 for TSP100, learning-rate decay by a factor of 0.96 every 5,000 steps, uniform parameter initialization in [-0.08, 0.08], L2 gradient clipping to 1.0, and up to one attention glimpse. Active Search uses baseline decay alpha = 0.99. Search mini-batches consist of replications or permutations of the test sequence. Hardware includes a single Nvidia Tesla K80 GPU for the greedy neural methods, an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3 3.50GHz CPU for Concorde and LK-H, and an Intel Haswell CPU for OR-Tools. Metrics are average tour length, ratio to optimality, running time, and performance as a function of the number of solutions considered. This context is relevant to the ICAM research plan because it shows a small-scale TSP benchmarking setup that the proposed method extends to larger TSP, CVRP, and ATSP instances, broader baseline families, and explicit optimality-gap and inference-time evaluation.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3789, 'output_tokens': 3849}, {'corpus_id': '263909317', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper studies LEHD, a constructive neural combinatorial optimization model with a light encoder and heavy decoder, trained primarily by supervised learning and an efficient self-improved training procedure that does not require labeled optimal solutions. It learns to construct partial solutions during training and complete solutions during inference, then applies Random Re-Construct, abbreviated RRC, as a post-construction improvement step by repeatedly reconstructing parts of the solution. Experiments use uniformly distributed TSP and CVRP instances of sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, as well as real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances. The evaluation compares greedy inference and RRC with 100, 300, 500, or 1000 iterations against constructive baselines such as POMO and BQ, learning-based methods such as EAS and SGBS, and classical or search-based solvers including LKH3, Concorde, SO-mixed, and H-TSP. Metrics are optimality gap relative to reference or optimal solutions and wall-clock inference time. This is directly related to the proposed ICAM plan because ICAM also targets large-scale TSP, CVRP, and ATSP generalization with an encoder-decoder constructive solver and plans to compare against POMO, LKH3, Concorde, and other constructive or two-stage NCO methods using gap and inference time; LEHD plus RRC is a relevant strong constructive baseline and a contrast for whether a heavy decoder plus re-constructive improvement can replace or complement ICAM's instance-conditioned adaptation and attention-free architecture.", 'results': 'On uniform TSP, LEHD with 100 RRC iterations outperforms other learning-based methods at reasonable inference time; with more than 500 RRC iterations it reaches nearly optimal gaps for TSP100 and TSP200, less than 0.2% for TSP500, and about 0.8% for TSP1000. On uniform CVRP, LEHD with 300 RRC iterations outperforms most learning-based methods on all instances except EAS on CVRP100, which is slower; EAS degrades on CVRP500 and no reasonable-budget result is reported for CVRP1000. With 500 RRC iterations, LEHD outperforms LKH3 on CVRP100 through CVRP500, and with 1000 RRC iterations it is about 1% worse than LKH3 on CVRP1000. On real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib, greedy inference plus 1000 RRC iterations outperforms POMO and BQ on all reported instances. In the search-based comparison on 128 TSP instances, LEHD greedy gaps are 0.859% for TSP200 in 3 seconds, 1.560% for TSP500 in 0.3 minutes, and 3.168% for TSP1000 in 1.6 minutes; LEHD RRC100 gaps are 0.0761% for TSP200 in 1.2 minutes, 0.343% for TSP500 in 8 minutes, and 1.218% for TSP1000 in 43 minutes. SO-mixed gaps are 0.636% in 21 minutes, 2.401% in 32 minutes, and 2.800% in 56 minutes; Concorde is 0.000% with 3 minutes, 32 minutes, and 7.8 hours. On TSP1000, H-TSP with LKH-3 has a 4.06% gap in 7.5 minutes, while LEHD RRC100 has a 1.218% gap in 43 minutes. Ablations indicate that the heavy decoder, rather than supervised learning alone, drives large-scale generalization, and supervised training is more practical than reinforcement learning for LEHD under the same computational budget.', 'context': "The experimental context includes TSP and CVRP instances with 100 to 1000 nodes, uniform distributions and real-world TSPLib or CVRPLib distributions, with 128 instances used in the large-scale TSP comparisons against SO-mixed and H-TSP. Inference budgets are explicit: greedy inference, 100 RRC iterations, 300 RRC iterations, 500 RRC iterations, and 1000 RRC iterations. The paper emphasizes fast greedy inference and controlled RRC budgets, comparing against methods that require powerful external solvers or long search times. The model is trained with supervised learning and self-improved training without labeled solutions; a reinforcement-learning POMO variant is reported to be too costly to converge in reasonable time, so a TSP50 same-budget comparison is used. These conditions are relevant to ICAM because ICAM's plan also uses scale-varying training, multiple trajectory or augmentation scenarios, and large-scale evaluation up to 5000 nodes for TSP and CVRP, so the paper's 1000-node results and RRC budget sensitivity provide a baseline for judging whether ICAM's instance-conditioned adaptation can improve gap-time tradeoffs beyond heavy-decoder construction and re-constructive improvement."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3247, 'output_tokens': 4822}, {'corpus_id': '59608816', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '229340173', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1695, 'output_tokens': 427}, {'corpus_id': '3486660', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a constructive reinforcement-learning method called S2V-DQN for TSP and other graph problems. It represents the current partial solution with a structure2vec graph embedding, computes node embeddings over T=4 iterations, and defines Q(h(S), v; Theta) from the node embedding and a pooled graph embedding. The policy greedily selects the next node to append to a partial tour until all nodes are visited, with the helper maintaining the tour and the cost set to negative tour length. Training uses n-step Q-learning combined with fitted Q-iteration and experience replay, updating parameters on random batches of stored transitions. For TSP, instances are fully connected 2D graphs with Euclidean edge weights, generated from DIMACS-style uniform or clustered point distributions, and real-world benchmark instances are also used. This method is related to the proposed ICAM plan because it is a constructive neural combinatorial optimization baseline for TSP, comparable in category to POMO, MatNet, ELG, and Pointerformer, but it does not use the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, AAFM, or vehicle-routing constraints, so its relevance is limited to TSP baseline comparison and large-scale generalization context.', 'results': 'The supplied paper text reports that S2V-DQN significantly outperforms all competing methods for TSP, MVC, and MAXCUT, and that the learned heuristics remain effective on instances larger than those used during training. However, the excerpt does not provide numeric tour lengths, optimality gaps, inference times, statistical significance, or exact baseline names, so quantitative comparison with the proposed ICAM metrics cannot be extracted from the provided material.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions include 2D TSP instances with fully connected graphs and distance-based edge weights, DIMACS-style uniform/clustered synthetic generation, and public real-world benchmark instances. The embedding uses T=4 iterations, binary node decision variables, a graph-pooled embedding, and a Q-function with seven parameter groups. Training uses off-policy n-step Q-learning and fitted Q-iteration with replay batches, but the excerpt does not specify hardware, batch size, n-step horizon, learning rate, number of episodes, or exact instance sizes. The method is not evaluated on CVRP or ATSP and does not report capacity-aware routing, so it should be treated as a TSP-specific constructive RL baseline rather than a direct vehicle-routing competitor.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5001, 'output_tokens': 3153}, {'corpus_id': '261243129', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates ELG-POMO, a constructive neural vehicle routing method that ensembles a global POMO policy with an auxiliary local policy. This is directly relevant to the proposed ICAM research plan because ELG is one of the constructive NCO baselines named in the proposed comparison, and the paper also reports results for POMO, Pointerformer, TAM, and classical solvers that ICAM plans to compare against. ELG-POMO uses a Transformer encoder-decoder without positional embedding as the global policy: the encoder represents the full instance, the decoder scores candidate next nodes through query-key compatibility, invalid actions are masked, and the solution is constructed autoregressively. The local policy operates on the local neighborhood of the current node and is combined with the global policy to form the final action distribution. The paper also compares classical solvers LKH3 and HGS, uses Concorde as the TSP best-known-solution reference, and includes TAM as a divide-and-conquer/two-stage baseline in an appendix comparison on CVRPLIB Set-X with N >= 500 and Set-XXL, although the provided excerpt does not give detailed TAM numbers. Evaluation uses greedy inference, POMO-style x8 instance augmentation, average gap to best known solutions, and average runtime.', 'results': "ELG-POMO outperformed all compared methods on the N <= 200 TSPLIB and CVRPLIB cross-distribution subset, with gap reductions of 0.53 percentage points on CVRP and 0.50 percentage points on TSP relative to the runner-up. On the N in (200, 1000] or (200, 1002] subset, ELG-POMO reduced the CVRP gap from 6.95% for Omni-POMO to 6.46%, and reduced the TSP gap from 7.47% to 5.90%; it outperformed BQ and LEHD on CVRP but lagged behind them on TSP. On four CVRPLIB Set-XXL real-world instances with N = 3000 to 7000, ELG-POMO was best on three instances and reduced optimality gaps compared with other POMO-based methods. In the synthetic-distribution TSP test, ELG* was retrained on uniform, cluster, and cluster mixture instances and tested on cluster, mixture, explosion, and rotation instances; it beat AMDKD on three of four N = 100 distributions and all four N = 200 distributions, with N = 200 gaps of 1.84%, 1.62%, 1.43%, and 1.58% versus AMDKD gaps of 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54%. On uniform N = 100 in-distribution tests, ELG-POMO achieved a 0.22% gap on TSP100 and a 1.22% gap on CVRP100, improving over POMO's 0.40% and 1.34%, but remaining behind Pointerformer at 0.16% and T2TCO at 0.18% on TSP100, and behind HGS at 0.52% and LKH3 at 0.00% on CVRP100. Runtime was reported as 1.90 seconds per instance versus 16 minutes for the classical-solver comparison, while the optimality gap remained worse than HGS.", 'context': 'Training used uniformly distributed instances with N = 100, matching the POMO training setting; ELG-POMO training was aligned with POMO so that the comparison isolates the contribution of the local policy. Test conditions included TSPLIB and CVRPLIB Set-X/XXL subsets with N <= 200, 200 < N <= 1000 or 1002, and N = 3000 to 7000, as well as small-scale synthetic distributions including cluster, mixture, explosion, and rotation at N = 100 and N = 200, and uniform N = 100 instances. Metrics were average gap to best known solutions and average solving time. POMO-based methods used x8 instance augmentation and greedy decoding. The paper does not provide hardware specifications in the excerpt, but it reports runtime comparisons. This context is relevant to ICAM because the proposed plan uses the same problem scales, TSPLIB/CVRPLIB benchmarks, synthetic distributions, optimality-gap and inference-time metrics, and a baseline set that includes ELG, POMO, Pointerformer, TAM, and classical solvers. The paper does not report MatNet, MCTS-based methods, or GLOP, so those proposed baselines are not directly covered.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5659, 'output_tokens': 6812}, {'corpus_id': '237156231', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '258212943', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1726, 'output_tokens': 643}, {'corpus_id': '268031851', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '5692837', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper describes a Ptr-Net-based sequence-to-sequence model for generating solution sequences from planar point sets. The inputs are point sets P = {P1, ..., Pn}, where each point Pj = (xj, yj) is sampled uniformly from the unit square [0, 1] x [0, 1]. The outputs are sequences C_P representing the solution for one of three tasks: convex hull, Delaunay triangulation, or Travelling Salesman Problem. The model uses a single-layer LSTM with either 256 or 512 hidden units and is trained with stochastic gradient descent. The excerpt does not specify the exact decoding procedure, hardware, inference protocol, or evaluation metrics, but it does state that the same architecture and hyperparameters were used across all problems to avoid extensive tuning.', 'results': 'No quantitative experimental outcomes are provided in the supplied excerpt. The Empirical Results section is empty, so no tour lengths, optimality gaps, solution quality scores, inference times, or comparisons to baselines are reported. The only reported outcome is qualitative: the authors observed overfitting in some cases where the task was simpler, specifically for small n.', 'context': 'The experimental setup uses 1 million training example pairs, uniform random planar point sets, a single-layer LSTM with 256 or 512 hidden units, stochastic gradient descent with a learning rate of 1.0, batch size 128, random uniform weight initialization from -0.08 to 0.08, and L2 gradient clipping of 2.0. The tasks are convex hull, Delaunay triangulation, and TSP on planar point sets, not vehicle routing or large-scale routing instances. This method is related to the proposed research method and overall plan because it is a constructive neural combinatorial optimization approach for TSP and related geometric sequence tasks, and therefore provides context for the proposed comparison against constructive NCO methods. However, it does not implement instance-conditioned adaptation, attention-free modules, progressive training, or large-scale VRP evaluation, and the excerpt does not contain the quantitative results needed for direct comparison with the proposed ICAM evaluation using optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference times.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2089, 'output_tokens': 3058}, {'corpus_id': '258212484', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1678, 'output_tokens': 297}, {'corpus_id': '239998492', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a two-policy deep reinforcement learning framework called LCP, which combines a seeder policy that generates diversified candidate routing solutions with a reviser policy that improves each candidate by decomposing it into K segments and reoptimizing segment orderings in parallel over I iterations. The method is tested on TSP, PCTSP, and CVRP instances with N = 20, 50, 100, and 500 nodes, as well as real-world TSPLIB TSP instances. The experimental comparison includes classical/heuristic solvers such as Gurobi, OR-Tools, Concorde for TSP, ILS for PCTSP, and LKH3 for CVRP, and DRL baselines including S2V-DQN, EAN, GAT-T, DRL-2opt, and AM. Performance is assessed using objective cost, optimality gap relative to strong solvers, and inference-time trade-offs, with speed controlled by the number of candidate solutions M and the number of revision iterations I. This is related to the proposed ICAM research method because both use routing benchmarks, optimality gaps, solution quality, and inference time to compare neural solvers against classical heuristics and NCO baselines; however, the paper does not compare against HGS, POMO, MatNet, ELG, Pointerformer, MCTS-based methods, TAM, or GLOP, and it does not directly address ICAM’s instance-conditioned adaptation or large-scale generalization to 1,000+ nodes.', 'results': 'On small-scale instances with N = 20, 50, and 100, the paper reports that LCP outperforms all DRL baselines and OR-Tools on TSP, PCTSP, and CVRP. In the N = 100 ablation table, the best reported LCP configuration achieves cost gaps of 0.88% on TSP, 1.02% on PCTSP, and 2.37% on CVRP, with corresponding costs of 7.82, 6.04, and 16.12. Removing LCP components increases the gaps, with the weakest reported configuration reaching 2.65% on TSP, 1.64% on PCTSP, and 3.43% on CVRP. For TSP at N = 100, LCP and its variant LCP* outperform DRL-2opt by 0.33%. For PCTSP at N = 100, AM + LCP with M = 640 and I = 1 outperforms OR-Tools with a 10-second budget and is reported as 2x faster; with M = 1280 and I = 5, it is 11x faster than ILS but underperforms ILS by 1.0%. Time-performance analysis reports a winner region below 10 seconds for PCTSP at N = 100 and 500 and for CVRP at N = 500, and below 5 seconds for CVRP at N = 100.', 'context': 'The experimental setting uses TSP, PCTSP, and CVRP with node counts N = 20, 50, 100, and 500, plus TSPLIB TSP instances. The seeder and reviser training hyperparameters are stated to follow the AM baseline except for a seeder batch size of 1024; entropy scheduling uses alpha = 0.5 and a linear weight schedule. Inference speed is controlled by M candidate solutions and I revision iterations, and the reviser solves M x K segments in parallel per iteration. Baseline solvers include time-limited Gurobi and OR-Tools with local search, while Concorde, ILS, and LKH3 are used as strong problem-specific heuristics whose speed is not directly controlled. Evaluation metrics are objective value, gap to state-of-the-art solvers, and runtime. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because it establishes a comparable evaluation protocol for TSP and CVRP, but the paper’s largest tested scale is 500 nodes, whereas the ICAM plan targets up to 5,000 nodes for TSP/CVRP and includes ATSP, HGS, and additional constructive NCO baselines not present in the paper.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4507, 'output_tokens': 4058}, {'corpus_id': '270218206', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2062, 'output_tokens': 970}, {'corpus_id': '229331629', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a convergence-profile benchmark for classical CVRP heuristics, with the central comparison between an original HGS variant and a modern HGS-CVRP implementation that adds the Swap* neighborhood. It also compares HILS, LKH-3, KGLS, SISR, FILO, and OR-Tools GLS on 100 classical CVRP benchmark instances with 100 to 1000 customers. Each algorithm is run for a time limit proportional to instance size, and the best solution value is recorded at 1%, 2%, 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 30%, 50%, 75%, and 100% of the limit. The final evaluation reports average solution value and percentage gap to the best known solution over ten independent runs. This is related to the proposed ICAM plan because HGS and LKH-3 are named classical solver baselines and the paper demonstrates a protocol for measuring solution quality and optimality gap under a fixed CPU budget, although it does not evaluate neural constructive methods, Concorde, or neural inference times.', 'results': 'In the reported table, HGS-CVRP and HGS-2012 are the strongest classical baselines. For the instances shown, HGS-CVRP final gaps range from 0.00 to 0.07, with many instances at 0.00; HGS-2012 gaps range from 0.00 to 0.18. FILO gaps range from 0.00 to 0.36, SISR from 0.00 to 0.20, KGLS from 0.00 to 0.78, HILS from 0.00 to 1.09, LKH-3 from 0.00 to 0.73, and OR-Tools from 0.48 to 3.03. Examples include X-n101-k25, where HGS-CVRP obtains 27591.0 with 0.00 gap, HILS obtains 27591.0 with 0.00 gap, LKH-3 obtains 27639.2 with 0.17 gap, and OR-Tools obtains 27977.2 with 1.40 gap; X-n125-k30, where HGS-CVRP obtains 55539.0 with 0.00 gap, HILS obtains 55846.5 with 0.55 gap, LKH-3 obtains 55907.4 with 0.66 gap, and OR-Tools obtains 56853.4 with 2.37 gap; and X-n153-k22, where HGS-CVRP obtains 21225.0 with 0.02 gap, HILS obtains 21452.3 with 1.09 gap, LKH-3 obtains 21240.8 with 0.10 gap, and OR-Tools obtains 21789.3 with 2.68 gap.', 'context': "Experiments use 100 classical CVRP benchmark instances, with the smallest 100 customers and largest 1000 customers. The time limit is n x 240/100 seconds, so 100-customer instances run for 4 minutes and 1000-customer instances for 40 minutes. Ten independent runs are performed with different seeds; deterministic algorithms are varied by permuting customer order. Runs are single-threaded on an Intel Gold 6148 Skylake 2.4 GHz processor with 40 GB RAM and CentOS 7.8.2003. HGS-2012, HGS-CVRP, HILS, LKH-3, FILO, and OR-Tools are C/C++ compiled with g++ 9.1.0, while KGLS and SISR use Java OpenJDK 13.0.1. Gaps are computed against best known solutions from the CVRPLIB website, not against LKH-3 optima. This context matters for the proposed ICAM evaluation because the paper provides a strong classical CVRP benchmarking setting, but its gap reference and CPU-time protocol differ from the proposed plan's use of LKH-3-derived optimal solutions and inference-time comparisons."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5047, 'output_tokens': 6164}, {'corpus_id': '235613398', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '264555480', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a neural k-opt routing solver, NeuOpt, for TSP and CVRP. It uses reinforcement-learning training and an inference-time dynamic data augmentation procedure, D2A, that generates new optimal-solution-invariant augmented instances when the solver fails to improve for a preset number of consecutive steps, T_D2A. For CVRP, the paper also describes GIRE, which adds violation indicator features and exploration statistics features to the policy network, uses hypernetworks to adapt the final decoder layer, and applies reward shaping to encourage exploration of epsilon-feasible infeasible regions. The experimental setup uses uniformly generated TSP and CVRP instances with N=20, 50, and 100, sets K=4 for NeuOpt, constructs initial solutions sequentially in a random fashion, and reports performance and inference-time trade-offs for different K values and reward-shaping weights. This relates to the proposed ICAM baseline-comparison method because it provides an NCO routing-solver evaluation context on TSP/CVRP, including inference-time augmentation and inference-time measurement, although the supplied text does not show the full baseline comparison against classical solvers or the listed constructive and two-stage NCO methods.', 'results': 'No numerical objective values, optimality gaps, or statistical significance tests are provided in the supplied excerpts. The reported outcomes are qualitative: varying K on TSP-100 shows a trade-off between better solution performance and higher inference time for T=1k steps and higher training time per epoch; on CVRP-20, testing reward-shaping weights of 0.01, 0.05, and 0.1 suggests that moderate weights produce more effective reward shaping than extremely small or large values.', 'context': "Experiments used uniformly generated TSP and CVRP instances with sizes N=20, 50, and 100, K=4, random sequential initial solutions, and hardware consisting of NVIDIA 2080TI GPUs and an Intel E5-2680 CPU at 2.40GHz. D2A is triggered after a maximum of T_D2A consecutive steps without improvement. GIRE uses two binary capacity-violation indicators for CVRP, exploration statistics over the most recent T_his transitions, hypernetworks with a 9x8xd structure and a shared first hidden layer, and reward-shaping weights alpha=beta=0.05. The supplied text mentions additional appendix results on CVRPLIB and TSP-200/CVRP-200, but does not include their quantitative values, so the paper only partially overlaps with the proposed plan's large-scale generalization and comprehensive baseline comparison scope."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3309, 'output_tokens': 3395}]): Request timed out.
Error processing ('We will assess performance using objective values, optimality gaps compared to optimal solutions from LKH3, and total inference times. We will report results for single trajectory, multiple trajectories, and instance augmentation scenarios to provide comprehensive performance analysis; these metrics quantify solution quality, optimality, and practical inference cost across different decoding settings.', [{'corpus_id': '252211738', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1851, 'output_tokens': 1756}, {'corpus_id': '257356100', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '231879679', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1790, 'output_tokens': 507}, {'corpus_id': '246863551', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1666, 'output_tokens': 667}, {'corpus_id': '5634955', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '235778179', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '257219869', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '229340513', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a multi-decoder attention model by measuring how often each of its five decoders produces the best solution among the ensemble. For CVRP20, each decoder is run separately under greedy decoding and beam search with beam width 50, and the number of times each decoder finds the best and strictly best solution is counted. This is related to the proposed method’s plan to report single-trajectory and multiple-trajectory decoding settings, but it is not the same evaluation protocol because it does not report objective values, LKH3-based optimality gaps, or total inference times.', 'results': 'The reported outcome is count-based: winning times are higher than solely winning times in both greedy decoding and beam search, indicating that multiple decoders often find the same best solution. All five decoders perform similarly, with no decoder dominant or non-contributing, supporting the usefulness of distinct construction patterns. No numerical objective values, optimality gaps, or inference times are provided in the excerpt.', 'context': 'The analysis uses CVRP20 instances with 20 nodes, Euclidean distances from uniform [0,1] coordinates, vehicle capacity 30, and non-depot demands sampled from integers 1 to 9. The model uses 128-dimensional node embeddings, a 3-layer Transformer encoder with 8 attention heads and a 512-dimensional feed-forward layer, five decoders with 128 dimensions and 8 heads, and an embedding glimpse layer with re-embedding steps of 2 for CVRP20. Training uses 100 epochs, 2,500 iterations per epoch, batch size 512, Adam with learning rate 1e-4, and KL coefficient 0.01. The supplementary analysis also mentions beam-search merging, different decoder counts, a temperature-tuned single-decoder baseline, and better generalization to CVRP150 and CVRP200, but detailed values are not included in the provided text.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2732, 'output_tokens': 2451}, {'corpus_id': '53427953', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3422, 'output_tokens': 4761}, {'corpus_id': '238407851', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper trains a dual-aspect collaborative Transformer routing solver using proximal policy optimization with n-step return estimation and a curriculum learning strategy. It evaluates the model on randomly generated TSP and CVRP instances of sizes 20, 50, and 100 nodes, using problem-specific inputs, feasibility masks, and hyperparameters. Training uses randomly generated initial solutions, while inference uses greedy initial solutions followed by 2-opt local search after an initial comparison of 2-opt, swap, and insert operators. The evaluation setup is related to the proposed ICAM method because both assess constructive neural routing solvers by solution quality and inference cost, but the paper focuses on small-to-medium instances and 2-opt post-processing rather than LKH3-based gaps, single/multiple trajectory decoding, or instance augmentation.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not contain numerical objective values, optimality gaps, or inference times for the proposed model. It reports only that, in initial experiments, 2-opt performed best for both TSP and CVRP, with insert better than swap, so final reported results are based on 2-opt. The excerpt indicates that reported result fields include objective values, gaps, and time, but no specific quantitative comparisons are included in the supplied text.', 'context': 'Experiments were conducted on randomly generated TSP and CVRP instances with N = 20, 50, and 100 nodes. The model and baselines were tested on a server with RTX 2080 Ti GPUs and Intel Xeon E5-2680 CPU at 2.40 GHz, using PyTorch. The decoder masks diagonal pair elements and the node pair selected at the last step to avoid dead loops. The paper notes that NLNS runtime is based on one GPU and ten CPUs in parallel under default settings. The excerpt does not specify learning rates, batch sizes, number of training steps, or the exact benchmark comparison values.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2259, 'output_tokens': 2523}, {'corpus_id': '245790960', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '174798181', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a supervised graph convolutional network for 2D Euclidean TSP and evaluates it by converting predicted adjacency matrices into valid tours using greedy and search/sampling decoding strategies. It reports objective values as average predicted tour length and optimality gaps relative to optimal tours obtained with Concorde. This is related to the proposed method because both focus on evaluating neural routing solvers using objective values, optimality gaps, and multiple-trajectory decoding; however, the paper uses Concorde rather than LKH3 and does not report total inference times or instance augmentation scenarios.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical performance values, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance. It only defines the evaluation metrics, namely average predicted tour length and optimality gap relative to Concorde, and states that the model is compared against a sampling-based state-of-the-art approach that samples 1,280 solutions.', 'context': 'Experiments are conducted on 2D Euclidean TSP instances in the unit square with 20, 50, and 100 nodes. Each size has 1,000,000 training instances and 10,000 validation and test instances. The model uses 30 graph convolutional layers, a 3-layer MLP with hidden dimension 300, a 20-nearest-neighbor adjacency graph, and a fixed beam width of 1,280. Training uses Adam with initial learning rate 0.001, mini-batches of 20 instances, 500 mini-batches per epoch, and cross-entropy loss. Evaluation uses arbitrarily large batch sizes that fit GPU memory and compares predicted tours to Concorde optimal tours.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2450, 'output_tokens': 2546}, {'corpus_id': '266191425', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper describes GLOP, a two-stage neural routing solver relevant to the proposed ICAM evaluation plan because it is a large-scale constructive/two-stage neural combinatorial optimization baseline. GLOP learns local TSP revisers and global partition policies. For TSP, it creates initial tours by Random Insertion, splits tours into independent subtours, and reconstructs them with a learned local policy. The local policy is trained by minimizing expected subtour length with a REINFORCE estimator using the average length of two greedy rollouts as a baseline. For general routing, a global policy outputs a partition heatmap, samples node partitions in parallel, and sends them to the local TSP solver; it is trained with REINFORCE using the average final solution length for the same instance as a baseline. The evaluation setup compares objective value and runtime against state-of-the-art baselines with adjustable runtime on large-scale TSP, CVRP, PCTSP, ATSP, TSPLIB, CVRPLIB, and cross-distribution instances. This aligns with the proposed method's focus on objective values and inference time, but the paper excerpt does not include LKH3-based optimality gaps or single/multiple trajectory/augmentation reporting.", 'results': 'The supplied excerpt contains no numerical objective values, optimality gaps, runtimes, or statistical significance. It only provides qualitative comparisons: GLOP is stated to outperform H-TSP without learning an upper-level TSP policy, and to achieve competitive solutions relative to select-and-optimize while being hundreds of times more efficient. No per-dataset results, no LKH3 optimality gaps, and no trajectory or augmentation breakdowns are reported in the provided text.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions include uniformly sampled TSP500, TSP1K, TSP10K, and an additional TSP100K instance; cross-distribution test instances; 49 symmetric TSPLIB EUC2D instances with fewer than 1000 nodes normalized to the unit square; ATSP test instances; CVRP1K, CVRP2K, CVRP5K, and CVRP7K with 100 instances each; large-scale CVRPLIB instances; and PCTSP500, PCTSP1K, and PCTSP5K with prize sampling parameters K_n = 9, 12, and 20 for n = 500, 1000, and 5000. Hardware is a 12-core Intel Xeon Platinum 8255C CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. Baselines are selected to have adjustable runtime to match GLOP. The excerpt does not specify LKH3 optimal solutions, decoding trajectory counts, instance augmentation settings, or statistical tests.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3351, 'output_tokens': 3545}, {'corpus_id': '60260073', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '226222332', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper describes POMO's shared-baseline policy-gradient method for constructive combinatorial optimization. For each instance, the policy samples N solution trajectories, each associated with a different starting action, and computes the return of each trajectory. Instead of using a per-trajectory greedy-rollout return as the baseline, POMO uses a shared baseline formed from the returns of the N sampled trajectories. The policy is then updated with a REINFORCE-style gradient ascent on the advantage, i.e., the trajectory return minus the shared baseline, in a mini-batch training loop. This method relates to the proposed research method because the proposed evaluation explicitly compares single-trajectory, multiple-trajectory, and instance-augmentation settings, and to the research plan because the plan builds on POMO-style training and top-k elite trajectory selection. The supplied excerpt does not detail instance augmentation or LKH3-based optimality-gap evaluation, so the connection is primarily to multiple-trajectory scoring and baseline construction.", 'results': 'The excerpt provides only qualitative results. It reports that the shared POMO baseline yields lower-variance policy gradients than a greedy-rollout baseline, that the POMO advantage term has zero mean, and that the greedy-rollout baseline tends to produce negative advantages because sampled rollouts often fail to outperform argmax greedy rollouts. It also claims that the shared baseline discourages premature convergence to suboptimal policies by making each trajectory compete with N-1 other heterogeneous trajectories, and that it is more efficient than baselines requiring a separate critic network or a cloned-policy forward pass. No numerical objective values, optimality gaps, inference times, statistical significance, or quantitative baseline comparisons are given in the provided text.', 'context': 'The experimental context is a REINFORCE-based constructive solver in which N trajectories are sampled per instance and a shared baseline is computed from those trajectories. The baseline may depend on the initial action, but POMO uses a common baseline across the sampled trajectories. The comparison setting is a greedy-rollout baseline in which each sampled rollout is evaluated against the argmax rollout from the same starting node. The excerpt does not specify problem domains, instance sizes, number of trajectories N, hardware, learning rates, training steps, datasets, or inference-time measurement protocol, so those reproducibility details are absent.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2405, 'output_tokens': 3266}, {'corpus_id': '249097710', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper's related method is an evaluation protocol for constructive DRL-NCO solvers, specifically Sym-NCO applied to POMO, AM, and PointerNet. It uses benchmark instances for TSP, CVRP, PCTSP, and OP, mostly at N=100, plus TSPLIB instances with 50<N<250. The solvers are evaluated in greedy rollout and multi-start modes; multi-start uses rotation augmentations, and PCTSP/OP additionally sample M=200 symmetric problem variants using orthogonal matrices. Performance is measured by average objective cost over 10,000 benchmark instances, inference speed on CPU/GPU, time-versus-cost curves, and TSPLIB optimality gaps. This directly relates to the proposed ICAM evaluation, which also plans to report objective values, LKH3-based optimality gaps, total inference time, single-trajectory, multi-trajectory, and instance-augmentation results, although the paper's scale is smaller and its reference solvers include Concorde and LKH-3 rather than an explicitly LKH3-only optimal baseline.", 'results': 'Sym-NCO improved over neural constructive baselines in both greedy and multi-start settings and had the fastest reported inference speed among the compared neural methods. In TSP greedy rollout, it achieved a 0.95% optimality gap and solved 10,000 instances in a few seconds. In PCTSP multi-start, it outperformed the ILS baseline with approximately 240x faster speed, reported as 43,200/180. On TSPLIB, Sym-NCO reduced the optimality gap from 1.87% for POMO to 1.62%. Time-cost plots showed Sym-NCO on the Pareto frontier for TSP, CVRP, PCTSP, and OP, meaning it gave the best solution quality among baselines for a given time budget. No error bars or statistical significance tests are reported in the excerpt. The excerpt also does not provide detailed quantitative results for the large-scale transferability section, so the evidence for 1,000+ node generalization is not directly established.', 'context': "Experiments were conducted on four problem classes at N=100 and real-world TSPLIB instances with 50<N<250. Sym-NCO was integrated into POMO for TSP and CVRP, AM for PCTSP and OP, and PointerNet for TSP at N=100. Baselines included Concorde and LKH-3 for TSP, LKH-3 for CVRP, ILS for PCTSP, Compass for OP, and neural constructive methods such as PointerNet, S2V-DQN, AM, POMO, MDAM, and Nazari et al. Training used Nvidia A100 GPUs, while inference-speed comparisons used an Intel Xeon E5-2630 CPU and Nvidia RTX2080Ti GPU. The paper states that original network architectures and training hyperparameters were reused for fair comparison. Multi-start evaluation used rotation augmentations; PCTSP and OP used M=200 symmetric variants. The evaluation is relevant to ICAM's planned metrics and decoding scenarios, but it is limited to small-to-medium scale instances and does not cover the 1,000-5,000 node TSP/CVRP or 1,000 node ATSP settings in the research plan."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4917, 'output_tokens': 4674}, {'corpus_id': '259298711', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '13756489', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1827, 'output_tokens': 432}, {'corpus_id': '219512140', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '219150860', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1722, 'output_tokens': 584}, {'corpus_id': '2332513', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5343, 'output_tokens': 2564}, {'corpus_id': '14802229', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '267413113', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a neural constructive routing solver called INViT on TSP and CVRP instances, using an evaluation protocol closely related to the proposed method. It reports average optimality gaps computed from solution objective values against reference solutions, and it also reports total inference time per dataset. Reference solutions are obtained with Gurobi for TSP-100, LKH3 for TSP-1000, TSP-5000, and TSP-10000, HGS for CVRP, and known optimal values for TSPLIB and CVRPLIB. The model is trained with a REINFORCE-style objective using data augmentation, including rotation, reflection, normalization, and varied starting points. A baseline model is maintained with greedy rollout, while the training model uses random sampling; the baseline is updated when the training model performs better. At test time, data augmentation is used and the best augmented solution is selected. The paper compares INViT against several constructive neural routing baselines, including POMO, Omni-TSP/VRP, PointerFormer, ELG-v1, LEHD, and BQ-NCO. This setup relates directly to the proposed research method because it uses objective-value-based gaps, LKH3-based optimality gaps for large TSP instances, inference-time reporting, and augmentation-based test-time evaluation, all of which align with the research plan’s goal of assessing large-scale generalization and practical inference cost for neural routing solvers.', 'results': 'INViT-3V achieved the best reported average gaps on the large-scale generated datasets: 7.97% on TSP-5000, 7.52% on TSP-10000, and 5.63% on CVRP-5000. Most comparison methods had average gaps above 10% on these large-scale datasets, except BQ-NCO on CVRP-5000. For cross-distribution generalization, the relative gap increase from uniform to other distributions was 31% for TSP-5000, 33% for TSP-10000, and 8% for CVRP-5000; these were slightly worse than the corresponding Omni-TSP/VRP values of 9% and 8% for TSP-5000 and TSP-10000, and -40% for CVRP-5000. INViT-2V showed similar generalization performance with lower inference time, while INViT-3V provided a slight performance improvement. On small- and medium-scale datasets, such as TSP-100, TSP-1000, and CVRP-500, INViT did not consistently outperform the strongest baselines; the gap to the best baseline was described as subtle on TSP-1000 and larger on CVRP-500. On public TSPLIB and CVRPLIB instances, the method remained competitive on small- and medium-scale instances and maintained strong generalization, although some out-of-memory issues were noted. The provided excerpt does not give numeric inference-time values, but it states that inference time was reported and that INViT-2V had lower inference time than INViT-3V.', 'context': 'The experiments used generated MSVDRP datasets across multiple scales and distributions, including TSP-100, TSP-1000, TSP-5000, TSP-10000, CVRP-100, CVRP-500, and CVRP-5000. Public datasets included all symmetric Euclidean TSPLIB95 instances with 77 instances ranging from 51 to 18512 nodes, and all CVRPLIB Set-X instances with 100 instances ranging from 100 to 1000 nodes. The INViT model was trained only on uniform distributions, making its cross-distribution results especially relevant to the research plan’s emphasis on generalization beyond the training distribution. Evaluation used the same instances for all compared methods, ensuring fair gap comparisons even when reference solvers such as LKH3 and HGS may not produce exactly optimal solutions. The paper does not specify hardware details in the provided excerpt. It also does not explicitly separate single-trajectory and multiple-trajectory results in the provided text; instead, it uses random sampling during training and best-of-augmented-solutions selection at test time, which is related to but not identical to the proposed single-trajectory, multiple-trajectory, and instance-augmentation reporting structure.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3654, 'output_tokens': 3346}, {'corpus_id': '269214387', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a Transformer encoder-decoder construction heuristic for vehicle routing problems, implemented as the attention model baseline. Solution construction is formulated as a Markov decision process, where the Transformer receives raw state features and outputs node-selection probabilities; nodes are selected greedily or by sampling. The policy is trained with REINFORCE using negative tour cost as the reward and a baseline function. The method applies cross-problem learning: a Transformer is pre-trained on TSP and then fine-tuned for OP and PCTSP, either by full fine-tuning of the backbone with problem-specific modules or by adapter-based fine-tuning of lightweight modules. Instances are generated on the fly with coordinates uniformly sampled from the unit square; OP instances use unit prizes, while PCTSP instances use uniformly sampled prizes and penalties. The evaluation setup compares the proposed cross-problem learning method against the attention model, Gurobi, Compass, and Tsili for OP, and against Gurobi, ILS, and OR-Tools for PCTSP, and includes ablation, cross-distribution, and versatility analyses. This is related to the proposed research method because both concern Transformer-based constructive neural routing solvers and performance assessment on VRPs, but the paper focuses on small-scale cross-problem transfer rather than the proposed ICAM large-scale adaptation, LKH3-based optimality gaps, and explicit single-trajectory, multiple-trajectory, and instance-augmentation reporting.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report quantitative outcomes, optimality gaps, inference times, or statistical significance values. It only states that the method is empirically evaluated against the listed baselines and that ablation, cross-distribution, and versatility tests are conducted; no specific numerical performance comparisons are available in the supplied text.', 'context': "Experiments use customer-node sizes n = 20, 50, and 100 for OP and PCTSP. Training uses 100 epochs, 2,500 batches per epoch, batch size 512 for n = 20 and 50 and 256 for n = 100, 10,000 validation instances per epoch, learning rate 1e-4, and a paired t-test at alpha = 5% after each epoch to decide whether to replace the baseline model. Hardware is an Intel Core i9-10940X CPU with an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080Ti GPU. The paper's experimental scope is limited to small-to-medium instances and TSP-to-OP/PCTSP transfer, unlike the proposed plan's target scales of 100 to 5,000 nodes for TSP/CVRP and up to 1,000 nodes for ATSP."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2797, 'output_tokens': 3404}, {'corpus_id': '263908836', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4980, 'output_tokens': 4290}, {'corpus_id': '11212020', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2709, 'output_tokens': 885}, {'corpus_id': '237616034', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1756, 'output_tokens': 800}, {'corpus_id': '14096340', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '257632488', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates an unsupervised learning plus search pipeline for 2D Euclidean symmetric TSP. It trains a 2-layer SAG with 64 hidden units and 44,392 trainable parameters, converts the model output into a heat map using a soft indicator matrix and a T-to-H transformation, removes low-probability edges, and then performs heat-map-guided best-first local search with heat-map updates and controlled randomness. This relates to the proposed research method because the proposed method centers on evaluation using objective values, optimality gaps, and total inference time, and it relates to the research plan because it addresses large-scale TSP generalization and compares against classical and learning-based routing solvers. The paper reports route lengths, percentage gaps, and wall-clock times, but it does not explicitly report single-trajectory, multiple-trajectory, or instance-augmentation settings; it averages four random seeds and separates heat-map inference time from search time.', 'results': "On TSP100, the method produced average length 7.7608 with a reported gap of -0.0011% and a table time entry of 5.68 minutes plus an additional 1.11 minute component. On TSP200, it produced length 10.7289 with gap 0.0918%, and the runtime is reported as 0.56 minutes plus 5.21 minutes. For larger instances, the paper reports gaps of 0.8394% on TSP500 and 1.1770% on TSP1000. On small instances with n=20, 50, and 100, gaps were -0.00009%, -0.002%, and -0.00011%, respectively, attributed to rounding. The table gaps are relative to the Concorde reference row rather than to LKH3, so they are not directly identical to the proposed method's LKH3-based gap metric. Compared with learning baselines, the method was reported to outperform existing learning baselines on TSP200, TSP500, and TSP1000, while classical solvers remained stronger or competitive on TSP200 in the table: LKH3 had gap 0.0040%, Gurobi had gap -0.1446%, and Concorde had gap 0.0000%.", 'context': "Experiments used 2,000 training and 1,000 validation samples. The test set contained 10,000 instances for n=20, 50, and 100 and 128 instances for n=200, 500, and 1,000. Models were trained on instances with 20, 50, 100, 200, 500, and 1,000 vertices. Results are averages over four random seeds. The model is lightweight relative to the compared RL/SL baselines; training on TSP100 took about 30 minutes with 2,000 instances, whereas the compared RL/SL setup used 1 million instances and about one day on an NVIDIA V100 GPU. Runtime is decomposed into heat-map construction/inference and local-search time, matching the proposed method's focus on total inference cost."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3522, 'output_tokens': 7197}, {'corpus_id': '114451713', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '36483354', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '3649804', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a constructive neural combinatorial optimization method for Euclidean TSP using a pointer-network-style model with an LSTM encoder and up to one attention glimpse. The model is trained with supervised learning and reinforcement-learning pretraining, then decoded at inference time using three settings: greedy decoding as a single-trajectory baseline, sampling as a multiple-trajectory setting, and active search as an adaptive multiple-trajectory setting. Experiments use randomly generated Euclidean TSP instances with 20, 50, and 100 cities, with 1,000 test graphs per scale. Evaluation reports average tour length, ratios or gaps to optimality, and running time, and compares the neural methods against Christofides, a general vehicle-routing solver with local search and metaheuristics, and optimal or empirically optimal solutions from Concorde and LK-H. This is directly related to the proposed method because it uses the same broad evaluation logic: objective value, optimality gap relative to a strong solver, inference time, and comparison between single-trajectory and multiple-trajectory decoding. It also provides context for the research plan by showing how constructive neural routing methods can be benchmarked under different search budgets, although the paper is limited to small-scale TSP and does not test CVRP, ATSP, large-scale generalization, or instance augmentation in the sense proposed for ICAM.', 'results': 'On 1,000 random unit-square TSP instances for each of TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100, reinforcement-learning pretraining substantially improves over supervised pointer-network training. All evaluated neural methods outperform Christofides, including greedy decoding without inference-time search. The greedy methods are reported as time-efficient and only a few percent worse than optimality, but exact average tour lengths and gap percentages are not included in the provided text. Inference-time search improves solution quality at the cost of longer running time. RL pretraining-Sampling and RL pretraining-Active Search are the most competitive neural methods and recover optimal tours in a significant number of test cases. For smaller solution spaces, sampling with a tuned softmax temperature outperforms active search, while for larger solution spaces active search is superior when controlling either the number of sampled solutions or the running time. Active search started from an untrained model also produces competitive tours but requires 7 hours per TSP50 instance and 25 hours per TSP100 instance. Compared with OR-Tools metaheuristics, the paper reports that the neural methods are superior to simulated annealing, slightly less competitive than tabu search, and much less competitive than guided local search. No statistical significance tests are reported in the provided excerpt.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions are specific to small-scale Euclidean TSP: 20, 50, and 100 cities, points drawn uniformly from the unit square, and 1,000 test instances per problem size. Training uses mini-batches of 128 sequences, LSTM cells with 128 hidden units, 128-dimensional coordinate embeddings, the Adam optimizer, an initial learning rate of 1e-3 for TSP20 and TSP50 and 1e-4 for TSP100, learning-rate decay by 0.96 every 5,000 steps, uniform parameter initialization in [-0.08, 0.08], and L2 gradient clipping to 1.0. The model uses up to one attention glimpse, and active search uses a baseline decay of 0.99. Search mini-batches consist of replications or permutations of the test sequence. Running times are measured on a single Nvidia Tesla K80 GPU for the neural greedy methods, while Concorde and LK-H run on an Intel Xeon CPU E5-1650 v3 at 3.50 GHz and the vehicle-routing solver runs on an Intel Haswell CPU. This context matters for the proposed ICAM plan because the paper demonstrates the relevance of objective value, optimality gap, and inference-time search tradeoffs, but it does not provide evidence for ICAM’s target settings of 1,000 to 5,000 node instances, CVRP or ATSP, LKH3-based optimality gaps, or instance-augmentation decoding scenarios.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3762, 'output_tokens': 2989}, {'corpus_id': '263909317', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper evaluates a constructive neural solver, LEHD, built from a light encoder and heavy decoder, trained with supervised learning on partial solutions, and improved by a random re-construct (RRC) post-processing step. For each instance, the model first produces a greedy solution, which corresponds to a single decoding trajectory, and then applies a fixed number of RRC iterations, which corresponds to multiple inference passes rather than explicitly independent trajectory sampling. The experiments use uniformly distributed TSP and CVRP instances with 100, 200, 500, and 1000 nodes, as well as real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances. The evaluation reports solution quality as percentage optimality gap and wall-clock inference time, using budgets such as greedy, 100, 300, 500, and 1000 RRC iterations. This is related to the proposed ICAM evaluation because the proposed method also plans to report objective values, optimality gaps relative to LKH3, total inference times, and results under single trajectory, multiple trajectory, and instance augmentation settings; the paper's greedy-versus-RRC protocol and gap/time reporting provide a directly comparable template, although the paper does not explicitly use instance augmentation and uses Concorde for TSP optimal gaps in addition to LKH3 comparisons.", 'results': 'On uniform TSP, LEHD with 100 RRC iterations outperforms other learning-based methods at reasonable inference time. With more than 500 RRC iterations, the gap is nearly optimal for TSP100 and TSP200, below 0.2% for TSP500, and about 0.8% for TSP1000. On uniform CVRP, 300 RRC iterations outperform most learning-based methods on all instances except EAS on CVRP100; 500 RRC iterations outperform LKH3 on CVRP100 through CVRP500, and 1000 RRC iterations reach about a 1% gap to LKH3 on CVRP1000. On real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances, greedy inference and 1000 RRC iterations significantly outperform POMO and BQ. In large-scale TSP comparisons, LEHD greedy has gaps of 0.859%, 1.560%, and 3.168% on TSP200, TSP500, and TSP1000 with times of 3 seconds, 0.3 minutes, and 1.6 minutes; LEHD RRC100 has gaps of 0.0761%, 0.343%, and 1.218% with times of 1.2 minutes, 8 minutes, and 43 minutes. These results beat SO-mixed, which has gaps of 0.636%, 2.401%, and 2.800%, and beat H-TSP with LKH-3 on TSP1000, which has a 4.06% gap, while Concorde remains at 0.000% but with much longer runtimes.', 'context': "The supplied sections specify instance scales of 100 to 1000 nodes for uniform TSP/CVRP tests and real-world TSPLib/CVRPLib tests, and they specify RRC budgets of 100, 300, 500, and 1000 iterations. Inference times are reported per method, but hardware specifications and statistical significance tests are not provided in the supplied text. The paper attributes the generalization advantage to the heavy decoder architecture rather than supervised learning alone, based on comparisons with POMO. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM research plan because ICAM targets large-scale TSP, CVRP, and ATSP generalization and should report comparable gap/time trade-offs across single trajectory, multiple trajectory, and augmentation settings, using LKH3-based gaps and explicit inference budgets analogous to the paper's RRC settings."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3220, 'output_tokens': 4856}, {'corpus_id': '248377568', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses an evaluation protocol that is methodologically related to the proposed research method because it assesses a neural routing solver using optimality gaps, strong-solver references, and computation-time measurements. Specifically, the paper evaluates N2S on pickup-and-delivery problems by comparing solution gaps against optimal solutions for PDTSP and against strong heuristic baselines for PDTSP-LIFO, while also treating LKH3 as a strong reference solver. It reports best and average gaps over 10 independent runs and includes a diversity-enhanced variant, N2S-A, as well as a POMO-style baseline, Heter-POMO-A, which uses diverse rollouts and data augmentation. This aligns with the proposed plan’s focus on objective quality, LKH3-based gaps, inference or computation cost, and multiple-trajectory or instance-augmentation settings. The relation to the ICAM research plan is indirect but useful: the paper provides a template for reporting solution quality, solver-relative gaps, decoding cost, and generalization across different instance distributions, although it studies pickup-and-delivery problems at much smaller scales than the proposed 100 to 5,000 node routing plan.', 'results': 'The supplied text does not provide exact numerical gap values, objective values, or total inference-time numbers. It reports that, in an encoding ablation on 2,000 PDTSP-51 instances with 3,000 decoding steps, Synth-Att achieved slightly smaller gaps than DAC-Att with much fewer computation costs and considerably lower gaps than vanilla-Att with only slight extra computation costs. In the generalization evaluation, N2S-A significantly outperformed Heter-POMO-A in all reported cases, using best and average gaps over 10 independent runs. The paper also states that N2S showed slight inferiority to LKH3, inferred from LKH3’s similar performance to the B2 baseline on those instances, but direct LKH3 gap values are not given in the excerpt.', 'context': 'Experiments used PDTSP and PDTSP-LIFO instances with 21, 51, and 101 nodes generated uniformly in the unit square, plus benchmark instances up to 201 nodes with different sizes and node distributions for generalization. Training used PPO with curriculum learning, 200 epochs, 20 batches per epoch, batch size 600, 5-step PPO, 250 training steps, 3 minibatch updates, clip threshold 0.1, Adam learning rates of 8e-5 for the policy and 2e-5 for the critic with 0.985 decay per epoch, discount factor 0.999, and gradient norm clipping at 0.05, 0.15, or 0.35 for the three problem sizes. Curriculum parameters were 2, 1.5, and 1. Hardware was a server with 8 RTX 2080 Ti GPUs and an Intel E5-2680 CPU at 2.4 GHz; training took about 1 day for 21 nodes, 3 days for 51 nodes, and 7 days for 101 nodes. The ablation used one GPU card. These conditions are relevant to the proposed method because they define the instance scale, decoding budget, hardware, and generalization setup against which objective gaps and inference or computation times would be interpreted.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3029, 'output_tokens': 3647}, {'corpus_id': '46892983', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a policy-gradient neural constructive solver for the capacitated vehicle routing problem. The model is trained with an actor-critic policy gradient method and decodes routes autoregressively under feasibility masking. The evaluation uses single-trajectory greedy decoding and multiple-trajectory beam search with widths 5 and 10, and reports objective tour length, optimality gap versus mixed-integer optimal solutions for small instances, pairwise winning rates for larger instances, and solution-time scaling. This is directly related to the proposed research method because it uses objective values, optimality gaps, inference/solution times, and single versus multiple trajectory decoding, although the paper uses mixed-integer optima rather than LKH3 optima and does not evaluate instance augmentation.', 'results': 'For VRP10 and VRP20, beam width 10 was the best-performing decoding setting. About 95% of instances were within 10% of optimal for VRP10 and within 13% of optimal for VRP20, and even outliers were within 20-25% of optimality. For VRP50 and VRP100, classical heuristics were outperformed by the other approaches in almost 100% of samples. RL-greedy was comparable to OR-Tools, while beam search increased the winning rate above 60%. In the extracted winning-rate table, the final column consistent with OR-Tools shows RL-BS(10) at 60.2%, RL-BS(5) at 54.6%, and RL-Greedy at 41.5%. Solution-time analysis showed that the log of solution time per node remained nearly constant across RL decoders, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep grew faster than linearly; the RL framework was faster than randomized heuristics on larger instances, although exact OR-Tools timing comparisons were confounded by C++ versus Python implementations.', 'context': 'Experiments used one-vehicle capacitated VRP instances with customers and depot uniformly sampled in the unit square, demands uniformly drawn from integers 1 to 9, no split deliveries, and 1,000 instances per problem size for VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100. Feasibility masking prevented visiting zero-demand nodes, forced return to the depot when the vehicle load was zero, and masked customers whose demand exceeded the remaining load. Baselines included Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized variants, and Google OR-Tools; optimal solutions for VRP10 and VRP20 were obtained by solving a mixed-integer formulation. Hardware, exact vehicle capacities, and full training hyperparameters are not fully specified in the provided excerpt.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3775, 'output_tokens': 4721}, {'corpus_id': '59608816', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '257365174', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1705, 'output_tokens': 665}, {'corpus_id': '229340173', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1668, 'output_tokens': 746}, {'corpus_id': '231979347', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper describes generative and data-driven VRP solution methods as an alternative to perturbative metaheuristics. These methods construct routing solutions from scratch, typically by training deep neural networks offline to map instance features directly to solutions, thereby reducing or removing explicit mathematical formulations. A related data-driven stream uses genetic programming to evolve interpretable decision trees for solution construction from historical data. This relates to the proposed ICAM research plan because ICAM is a constructive neural solver for TSP, CVRP, and ATSP, and the proposed evaluation method will measure objective values, optimality gaps against LKH3, and total inference times under single-trajectory, multiple-trajectory, and instance-augmentation decoding.', 'results': 'No quantitative experimental outcomes, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance values are reported in the provided text. The review qualitatively reports that generative methods have advantages in solution time and ability to handle uncertainties and dynamics, but their solution quality is often inferior to perturbative methods. It also notes that deep-network end-to-end solvers are often black-box and criticized for poor interpretability, while genetic-programming decision trees can be more interpretable when suitable constraints are enforced during evolution.', 'context': "The context is a survey-level discussion of VRP solution paradigms rather than a specific benchmark. It assumes offline training, real-life VRP settings with uncertainty and dynamics, and a trade-off between model accuracy, tractability, and solution quality. No dataset sizes, instance distributions, hardware, hyperparameters, decoding settings, or statistical tests are specified. This context supports the proposed method's focus on reporting solution quality, optimality gaps, and practical inference cost across decoding scenarios, since generative neural solvers must be evaluated on both quality and speed."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5633, 'output_tokens': 6132}, {'corpus_id': '165163853', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper studies an RL-trained neural rewriter for optimization, with vehicle routing indicated as one of its application domains. The method treats each feasible solution as a state, computes a state-dependent set of local regions, selects a region with a region-picking policy, selects a local rewriting rule with a rule-picking policy, and applies the rule to obtain a lower-cost solution until convergence. The experimental evaluation compares the neural rewriter against search algorithms and measures solution quality and inference time. This is related to the proposed ICAM evaluation because both concern neural routing/optimization solvers and report objective quality and runtime, but the paper's method is a local rewriting solver rather than an instance-conditioned constructive model, and the provided excerpt does not show LKH3-based optimality gaps or single/multiple trajectory/instance-augmentation reporting.", 'results': 'The provided excerpt reports only a qualitative outcome: the approach is claimed to find better solutions than current state-of-the-art and traditional heuristic-based packages tuned for decades, and to discover novel rewriting paths. No numerical objective values, LKH3 optimality gaps, statistical significance, or breakdowns by single trajectory, multiple trajectories, or instance augmentation are provided.', 'context': 'Experiments were run on a server with 2 Quadro GP100 GPUs and 80 CPU cores. Neural networks used 1 GPU, search algorithms used 4 CPU cores, and search algorithms had a 10-second timeout per instance. Neural networks were implemented in PyTorch. The excerpt does not specify VRP instance sizes, distributions, or whether LKH3 was used.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2799, 'output_tokens': 6524}, {'corpus_id': '17792012', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper describes an exact branch-and-cut-and-price solver for the symmetric TSP, identified as Concorde, which relaxes selected constraints and variables and dynamically generates valid inequalities during the solution process. The solver uses 2-matching constraints, comb inequalities, and certain path inequalities, together with separation routines to detect violated inequalities. This method is related to the proposed research method because it provides an exact TSP reference for measuring solution quality, optimality gaps, and practical solver cost, similar to the proposed use of LKH3 optimal solutions to evaluate objective values, gaps, and inference times for a neural routing solver.', 'results': 'The paper reports that the solver was reliable on randomly generated planar instances, although the specific numerical table for those instances is not included in the excerpt. All small TSPLIB instances with n less than or equal to 1000 were solved within 1 minute on a 2.4 GHz AMD Opteron processor. For 21 medium-size TSPLIB instances with n between 1000 and 2392, the solver reached the optimum 19 times, with runtimes ranging from 5.7 to 3345.3 seconds. The two remaining instances required 13999.9 seconds and 18226404.4 seconds. The largest optimally solved instance mentioned was a VLSI application instance with 85900 vertices.', 'context': 'The reported experiments used a cluster of compute nodes, each with a 2.66 GHz Intel Xeon processor and 2 GB of memory, and CPLEX 6.5 as the linear programming solver. The instances included randomly generated planar instances, TSPLIB instances, and a VLSI application instance. This context is relevant to the research plan because it shows how exact TSP solvers can serve as benchmarks for optimality-gap evaluation and inference-time comparison, complementing the proposed ICAM evaluation on TSP, CVRP, and ATSP instances using LKH3-based references.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4755, 'output_tokens': 2871}, {'corpus_id': '271744912', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '5692837', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a sequence-to-sequence pointer network for constructive solution generation on planar Euclidean TSP and related geometric tasks. Inputs are planar point sets P = {P1, ..., Pn}, where each point Pj = (xj, yj) is sampled uniformly from [0, 1] × [0, 1]. Outputs are ordered sequences CP representing the solution for the convex hull, Delaunay triangulation, or TSP tour. For TSP, the model emits a sequence of point indices corresponding to a tour. This method is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because it is a neural constructive TSP solver whose generated tours can be evaluated using the proposed metrics: objective tour length, optimality gap relative to LKH3, and inference time. It also uses the same uniform Euclidean TSP instance-generation setting and sequence-construction paradigm that the research plan targets, although the paper does not describe LKH3-based gap evaluation, multiple-trajectory decoding, or instance augmentation.', 'results': 'No quantitative experimental outcomes are provided in the supplied excerpt. The Empirical Results section is empty, so no tour lengths, optimality gaps, baseline comparisons, statistical significance values, or inference times can be reported. The only reported outcome is qualitative: overfitting was observed in some cases where the task was simpler, specifically for small n.', 'context': 'The model was trained on 1M input-output example pairs. The architecture used a single-layer LSTM with either 256 or 512 hidden units. Training used stochastic gradient descent with a learning rate of 1.0, batch size of 128, random uniform weight initialization from -0.08 to 0.08, and L2 gradient clipping of 2.0. The same architecture and hyperparameters were used across the convex hull, Delaunay triangulation, and TSP tasks without extensive tuning. No hardware specifications, LKH3 reference-solution setup, decoding strategy, multiple-trajectory settings, or instance-augmentation protocol are described in the provided text.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2062, 'output_tokens': 4153}, {'corpus_id': '258212484', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1651, 'output_tokens': 10469}, {'corpus_id': '7961699', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2691, 'output_tokens': 1209}, {'corpus_id': '208201905', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '53573596', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '264426522', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a hybrid MDVRP heuristic, referred to as GANCP+, on out-of-range and out-of-distribution instances. GANCP+ represents each candidate solution as a customer-to-depot allocation chromosome, uses a supervised neural cost estimator to score the implied subproblem CVRPs, repairs infeasible depot assignments by moving customers to depots with sufficient remaining capacity, and evolves candidates using binary tournament selection, uniform crossover, FLIP/SWAP mutations, and targeted elite copying. The best final candidates are then solved with a CVRP solver. For the evaluation most relevant to the proposed method, the authors test 50 randomly generated out-of-range MDVRP instances whose subproblem sizes fall outside the trained range, and eight out-of-distribution instance classes generated from different customer-positioning and demand distributions. This relates to the proposed research method because it also assesses solution quality relative to a strong solver and reports computational advantage, and it probes generalization beyond the training scale and distribution; however, the paper uses VROOM rather than LKH3 and does not report single-trajectory, multi-trajectory, or instance-augmentation decoding settings.', 'results': 'On the 50 out-of-range instances, GANCP+ outperformed VROOM for problem sizes N in the interval [101, 250), but did not outperform VROOM for relatively smaller or larger out-of-range instances; the positive performance gap in those cases was described as relatively small. On the eight out-of-distribution classes, evaluated over 10 runs, GANCP+ produced satisfactory results despite positive gaps in some cases. The D4 class, which most closely resembled the training distribution, had average performance over 10 runs that outperformed VROOM. The largest deviation from optimality occurred in D5, where customers were purely clustered and demands were unitary. After fine-tuning the neural estimator with 32,000 CVRP instances per distribution class plus additional Phase III step-4 data, performance improved in multiple distribution classes, but classes with high training MAPE, specifically D1 and D6, showed decreased average performance relative to the no-fine-tuning results. The paper also reports a considerable computational advantage for out-of-distribution data, although exact inference-time values are not provided in the excerpt.', 'context': 'The out-of-range set consisted of 50 random MDVRP instances with subproblem size N_D outside [50, 500]. The out-of-distribution set comprised eight instance classes generated from three customer-positioning rules, random, clustered, and random-clustered, crossed with two demand rules, unitary and quadrant-dependent, with depots placed randomly on the grid. The main neural model was trained on uniform random grid instances with demands from a uniform distribution. Fine-tuning used 32,000 CVRP instances per class plus Step 4 Phase III instances, about 50% of the main train/validation data, and initialized from Phase III weights. The comparison solver was VROOM, and out-of-distribution results were summarized as averages over 10 runs. Hardware, exact optimality-gap values, and total inference times are not specified in the provided text.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5467, 'output_tokens': 3678}]): Request timed out.
Error processing ('We will conduct systematic ablation studies to validate: (1) effectiveness of individual components in the adaptation function, (2) impact of AAFM versus standard MHA, (3) contribution of each training stage, (4) effects of different model depths, and (5) sensitivity to hyperparameter settings including the learnable parameter α; these ablations isolate the contribution of each proposed design choice to overall performance.', [{'corpus_id': '237616034', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1776, 'output_tokens': 608}, {'corpus_id': '252211738', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1871, 'output_tokens': 986}, {'corpus_id': '231879679', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1810, 'output_tokens': 466}, {'corpus_id': '235778179', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '257219869', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '255546018', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a transformer-based constructive neural combinatorial optimization model for routing and knapsack problems. The model is trained by imitation of expert trajectories with a cross-entropy loss, using sub-path sampling from a 1 million expert solution dataset to form training minibatches. It is tested on Euclidean TSP, CVRP, OP, ATSP, and KP, with synthetic training instances of size 100 and test instances of sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, plus TSPLib and CVRPLib instances. Decoding is evaluated with greedy and beam search, and non-Perceiver models limit test-time subgraphs to the 250 nearest neighbors of the origin node. The architecture has 9 layers, 12 attention heads, embedding size 192, and feed-forward dimension 512. A PerceiverIO variant replaces quadratic attention to reduce inference time. The paper also includes ablation-style analyses of the k-nearest-neighbor heuristic and expert trajectories, although the provided excerpt does not give their detailed quantitative results. This is related to the proposed ICAM ablation plan because it examines cross-scale generalization and the contribution of architectural and training choices, but it does not directly test the proposed adaptation function, AAFM, three-stage training, or learnable alpha.', 'results': 'The reported outcomes are qualitative in the provided excerpt. The model is stated to show superior generalization to larger graphs even with greedy decoding, which produces a single solution while baselines generate several hundred solutions and select the best. With greedy decoding, it is slightly slower than Sym-NCO, competitive with POMO, and significantly faster than other models. Beam search improves optimality gaps but increases inference time. Replacing quadratic attention with PerceiverIO gives a considerable inference-time reduction for larger graphs at the cost of some performance drop, while still achieving strong performance relative to NCO baselines. On TSPLib and CVRPLib, the model is reported to significantly outperform end-to-end baseline policies even with greedy decoding. The only competitive methods mentioned are Att-GCN+MCTS and DIFUSCO+2opt, described as hybrid methods that are 2 to 15 times slower and designed for TSP only. No numerical optimality gaps, solution values, or runtime figures are provided in the excerpt.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions include training on synthetic size-100 instances because near-optimal solutions are reasonably fast to obtain and such datasets are common in the literature. Expert solutions are obtained with Concorde for TSP, LKH for ATSP and CVRP, and EA4OP for OP, forming a 1 million solution dataset. Training uses batch size 1024, 500 epochs, Adam with initial learning rate 7.5e-4 and decay 0.98 every 50 epochs, and the same hyperparameters across problems. For CVRP test sets, capacities are 50, 80, 100, and 250 for graph sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000. Deep models run on a single Nvidia Tesla V100-S GPU with 24GB memory, while non-neural solvers run on an Intel Xeon E5-2670 CPU with 256GB memory in one thread. Optimality gap is computed against Concorde for TSP and against LKH for CVRP for comparability with prior work; inference time is reported but noted to depend on implementation, hardware, parallelization, and batch size. This context is relevant to the ICAM research plan because it defines the scale, baselines, metrics, and ablation-style evaluation needed to assess whether instance-conditioned adaptation and attention-free modules improve large-scale routing generalization.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3266, 'output_tokens': 4155}, {'corpus_id': '229340513', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a component-level analysis and ablation-style evaluation of the Multi-Decoder Attention Model (MDAM) for routing problems. The approach evaluates whether multiple decoders learn distinct but useful construction patterns by measuring how often each decoder finds the best and strictly best solution among five decoders on CVRP20 under greedy decoding and beam search with beam width 50. It also analyzes the merging technique used in the customized beam search, varies the number of decoders, compares MDAM against a stronger single-decoder attention model with tuned softmax temperature, and tests generalization to larger CVRP instances with 150 and 200 nodes. This is related to the proposed ICAM ablation plan because both isolate the contribution of specific architectural or training design choices and assess their effect on solution quality and generalization, although the paper does not test ICAM-specific components such as the adaptation function, AAFM, three-stage training, or the learnable parameter alpha.', 'results': 'The provided text does not report numerical ablation values, exact win counts, optimality gaps, or statistical significance. Qualitatively, the paper states that winning times are higher than solely winning times in both greedy decoding and beam search, indicating that more than one decoder often finds the best solution. It also states that no decoder is dominant or non-contributing, so all five decoders are effective. The supplementary analysis is reported to show the effectiveness of the merging technique and the impact of different numbers of decoders, and MDAM is reported to have better generalization than the stronger attention model on CVRP150 and CVRP200, but no quantitative performance differences are given in the excerpt.', 'context': 'Experiments cover TSP, CVRP, SDVRP, OP, PCTSP, and SPCTSP with 20, 50, and 100 nodes. Instances use two-dimensional Euclidean distances with coordinates sampled uniformly from 0 to 1 in both dimensions. For CVRP, vehicle capacities are 30, 40, and 50 for 20, 50, and 100 nodes, respectively, and non-depot demands are sampled from integers 1 through 9. MDAM uses 128-dimensional node embeddings, a 3-layer Transformer encoder with 128-dimensional features and 8 attention heads, with the top encoder layer serving as the Embedding Glimpse layer, and a fully connected hidden dimension of 512. The model uses five decoders, each with 128-dimensional vectors and 8 attention heads. Embedding Glimpse re-embedding steps are 2, 4, and 8 for TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100, and 2, 6, and 8 for CVRP20, CVRP50, and CVRP100. Training uses 2,500 iterations per epoch, batch size 512 except 256 for CVRP100 due to GPU memory constraints, 100 epochs, Adam optimizer with learning rate 1e-4, and KL loss coefficient 0.01. The decoder analysis is conducted on CVRP20 using greedy decoding and beam search with beam width 50, and larger-scale generalization is tested on CVRP150 and CVRP200.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2752, 'output_tokens': 3206}, {'corpus_id': '265990729', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1925, 'output_tokens': 1162}, {'corpus_id': '3649804', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a constructive neural combinatorial optimization method for Euclidean TSP using a pointer network with an LSTM encoder and up to one attention glimpse. The model is trained either by supervised learning on optimal tours or by reinforcement learning pretraining, and then evaluated with different inference-time search strategies: greedy decoding, sampling, and active search. This is related to the proposed ICAM research method because the paper provides a prior NCO baseline and a component-wise evaluation pattern: changing the training signal from supervised to RL and changing the inference search strategy changes both solution quality and runtime, which is analogous to the proposed ablations of ICAM adaptation components, AAFM versus MHA, training stages, model depth, and hyperparameters such as alpha.', 'results': 'RL pretraining substantially improved over supervised learning, and the reported RL pretraining variants surpassed Christofides. Greedy RL pretraining was time-efficient and was only a few percent worse than optimality. Inference-time search improved solution quality but increased runtime, and early stopping of sampling or active search gave a small objective tradeoff. RL pretraining-Sampling and RL pretraining-Active Search were the most competitive neural methods and recovered optimal solutions in a significant number of test cases. For smaller solution spaces, sampling with a tuned softmax temperature outperformed active search and ran faster; for larger solution spaces, active search was superior when controlling for either the number of sampled solutions or runtime. Several RL pretraining methods outperformed OR-Tools local search, including RL pretraining-Greedy@16, while active search from an untrained model required about 7 hours per TSP50 instance and 25 hours per TSP100 instance. The method was less competitive than guided local search and only slightly less competitive than tabu search. The provided sections do not report p-values, confidence intervals, or other statistical significance tests.', 'context': 'Experiments used 1,000 random test graphs for TSP20, TSP50, and TSP100, with points drawn uniformly from the unit square. Training used mini-batches of 128 sequences, LSTM hidden size 128, 128-dimensional coordinate embeddings, Adam optimization, initial learning rate 1e-3 for TSP20 and TSP50 and 1e-4 for TSP100, learning-rate decay by 0.96 every 5000 steps, uniform initialization in [-0.08, 0.08], gradient L2 clipping at 1.0, and active-search baseline decay alpha set to 0.99. Search mini-batches used replications or permutations of test sequences. The neural methods ran on a single Nvidia Tesla K80 GPU, while Concorde and LK-H ran on an Intel Xeon E5-1650 v3 3.50GHz CPU and OR-Tools ran on an Intel Haswell CPU. Metrics included average tour length, running time, ratio to optimality, and number of solutions considered. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because it shows the scale, hyperparameter choices, and runtime-quality tradeoffs for constructive NCO on TSP, but it does not include instance-conditioned adaptation, attention-free modules, multi-stage training, CVRP, ATSP, or large-scale 1,000+ node generalization.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3782, 'output_tokens': 3737}, {'corpus_id': '248377568', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper conducts an ablation evaluation of the encoder attention component in a neural pickup-and-delivery solver. It replaces the proposed synthetic attention mechanism with vanilla attention and with a distance-aware attention mechanism, then evaluates the modified models on 2,000 pickup-and-delivery TSP instances with 51 nodes using 3,000 construction steps. The evaluation records model parameter count, computation time, and optimality gap. This is related to the proposed research method because it isolates the contribution of a specialized attention/encoding design choice, analogous to the planned comparison of AAFM against standard multi-head attention and the validation of individual architectural components in the research plan.', 'results': 'The paper reports that the synthetic attention mechanism achieves slightly smaller gaps than the distance-aware attention mechanism while requiring much fewer computation costs, and achieves considerably lower gaps than vanilla attention with only slight extra computation costs. The supplied excerpt does not provide exact numerical gaps, parameter counts, or runtimes.', 'context': 'The ablation is run on a single GPU card using 2,000 PDTSP-51 instances and 3,000 steps. The broader model is trained with proximal policy optimization and curriculum learning, using 200 epochs, 20 batches per epoch, batch size 600, n-step PPO with n=5 and T_train=250, three minibatch updates, clip threshold 0.1, Adam learning rates 8e-5 for the policy and 2e-5 for the critic, per-epoch decay 0.985, discount factor 0.999, and gradient-norm clipping of 0.05, 0.15, or 0.35 for problem sizes 21, 51, and 101. The full training setup used a server with 8 RTX 2080 Ti GPUs and an Intel E5-2680 CPU, with training times of about 1 day for 21 nodes, 3 days for 51 nodes, and 7 days for 101 nodes.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3049, 'output_tokens': 4300}, {'corpus_id': '59608816', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '46892983', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates an attention-based neural constructive solver for the capacitated vehicle routing problem. The model is trained with policy gradient actor-critic methods, with asynchronous advantage actor-critic details deferred to the appendix. At inference time, the decoder constructs routes using either greedy decoding or beam search with beam widths 5 and 10. Feasibility is enforced through masking: customers with zero demand are masked, all customers are masked when the vehicle’s remaining load is exactly zero, and customers whose demand exceeds the remaining load are masked, so the main experiments do not allow split deliveries. The experimental setup generates CVRP instances with customers and depot uniformly in the unit square, integer demands uniformly sampled from 1 to 9, one vehicle with varying capacity, and 1,000 instances per problem size for VRP10, VRP20, VRP50, and VRP100. The method is compared with Clarke-Wright savings, Sweep, randomized variants of these heuristics, and Google OR-Tools. This paper method is related to the proposed ICAM research plan as a prior reinforcement-learning-based constructive VRP solver and as an evaluation template for decoder/inference-time search and attention-based policy training. However, it does not implement the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function, Adaptation Attention Free Module, three-stage training scheme, or learnable alpha parameter, so its relevance to the proposed ablation method is indirect: it provides baseline context for how decoder choice and policy-gradient training affect CVRP solution quality and runtime.', 'results': 'For VRP10 and VRP20, optimal solutions were obtained by solving a mixed integer formulation. Beam width 10 was the best-performing RL decoder: roughly 95% of VRP10 instances were within 10% of optimality, and roughly 95% of VRP20 instances were within 13% of optimality. Outliers were within 20-25% of optimality. For VRP50 and VRP100, where optimal values were not computationally affordable, pairwise winning rates were reported. Classical heuristics were outperformed by the other approaches in almost 100% of samples. RL-greedy was described as comparable to OR-Tools, while beam search increased the winning rate above 60%. The reported winning-rate table shows RL-BS(10) values of 91.9, 57.7, 99.8, 99.4, 99.2, 99.3, 99.3, 99.3, and 60.2 across the compared baselines, with the final value consistent with the text’s statement that beam search exceeds 60% against OR-Tools. Runtime analysis showed that the log of solution time divided by the number of customer nodes remained nearly constant for RL greedy and beam search, while Clarke-Wright and Sweep runtimes increased faster than linearly. The RL framework was faster than the randomized heuristics on larger problems, although exact OR-Tools time comparison was limited by the C++ implementation of OR-Tools versus Python implementations of the other baselines.', 'context': 'Key experimental conditions include a single-vehicle capacitated VRP, no split deliveries in the main experiments, random uniform node locations in the unit square, discrete uniform demands from 1 to 9, 1,000 instances per problem size, problem sizes of 10, 20, 50, and 100 customers, and varying vehicle capacities that are not fully specified in the provided excerpt. Decoding used greedy selection or beam search with beam widths 5 and 10. Training used standard policy gradient actor-critic methods, with A3C details in an appendix not included in the provided text. Hardware, exact hyperparameters, and full training details are not specified in the provided excerpt. This context matters for the proposed ICAM ablations because the paper demonstrates that decoder choice and policy-gradient training affect CVRP solution quality and runtime, but it does not provide ablations for ICAM-specific components such as the adaptation function, AAFM versus standard multi-head attention, progressive training stages, model depth, or alpha sensitivity.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3795, 'output_tokens': 7161}, {'corpus_id': '245790960', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '263909317', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a constructive neural routing solver, LEHD, through ablation-style component comparisons and large-scale benchmark comparisons. The ablation comparisons isolate the contribution of architecture, training objective, and post-construction improvement: (i) a heavy-decoder LEHD architecture is compared with a heavy-encoder POMO architecture trained by supervised learning on TSP100; (ii) supervised learning is compared with reinforcement learning using the POMO strategy for training LEHD on TSP50 under the same computational budget; and (iii) the Random Re-Construct inference procedure is compared with greedy inference and random-sampling baselines, including POMO-Sample100 and POMO-RRC100. The paper also compares LEHD greedy and LEHD with 100 RRC iterations against Concorde, SO-mixed, and H-TSP with LKH3 on TSP200, TSP500, and TSP1000 instances. Evaluation uses optimality gap and wall-clock inference time. This is related to the proposed ICAM ablation plan because it isolates the contribution of individual design choices, but it does not test the proposed adaptation function, AAFM, three-stage training scheme, model depth, or learnable parameter alpha.', 'results': "The ablation outcomes are reported mainly qualitatively. POMO with a heavy encoder trained by supervised learning reaches good training performance on TSP100 but still performs poorly on large-scale problems, supporting the conclusion that LEHD's generalization advantage comes from the heavy decoder structure rather than supervised learning alone. Reinforcement learning with the POMO strategy does not converge in a reasonable time; under the same computational budget on TSP50, supervised learning is reported to be more suitable for training LEHD. RRC is reported to improve over greedy inference and random sampling, with LEHD plus 100 RRC iterations outperforming other learning-based methods and larger RRC budgets producing stronger results. With more than 500 RRC iterations, LEHD achieves nearly optimal gaps on TSP100 and TSP200, less than 0.2% on TSP500, and around 0.8% on TSP1000. For CVRP, 300 RRC iterations outperform most learning-based methods, 500 RRC iterations outperform LKH3 on CVRP100 through CVRP500, and 1000 RRC iterations achieve about a 1% gap to LKH3 on CVRP1000. In the large-scale TSP comparisons, LEHD RRC100 achieves 0.0761% gap in 1.2 minutes on TSP200, 0.343% gap in 8 minutes on TSP500, and 1.218% gap in 43 minutes on TSP1000, compared with SO-mixed gaps of 0.636%, 2.401%, and 2.800% and H-TSP with LKH3 at 4.06% on TSP1000. No statistical significance tests are reported.", 'context': 'The experiments use TSP and CVRP instances of sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1000, including uniformly distributed instances and real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances. The ablation comparisons use TSP100 for the heavy encoder versus heavy decoder comparison and TSP50 for the supervised versus reinforcement learning comparison with matched computational budget. Inference budgets include greedy inference and 100, 300, 500, or 1000 RRC iterations. The large-scale TSP comparisons use 128 instances for TSP200, TSP500, and TSP1000, with Concorde used as the optimal reference. Hardware specifications, exact hyperparameters, and any learnable alpha value are not provided in the excerpt, so the paper does not directly support reproducibility of the proposed ICAM alpha, AAFM, depth, or three-stage training ablations.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3240, 'output_tokens': 5587}, {'corpus_id': '257365174', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1725, 'output_tokens': 683}, {'corpus_id': '266191425', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper’s relevant method is GLOP’s two-stage curriculum training and component-level evaluation framework for a divide-and-conquer routing solver. GLOP first trains a local TSP policy by generating initial tours with Random Insertion, decomposing tours into independent subtours, and learning a reviser to reconstruct those subtours. The local policy is trained by minimizing expected subtour length using a REINFORCE estimator, with the average length of two greedy rollouts used as the baseline. GLOP then trains a global partition policy that outputs a partition heatmap, samples node partitions, solves the resulting sub-TSPs with the trained local policy, and optimizes expected final solution length using REINFORCE with per-instance averaged rewards as the baseline. The paper also includes extended evaluations such as an ablation study of local-policy components, cross-distribution performance analysis, and stability analysis. These elements are directly related to the proposed ICAM research method because ICAM also plans systematic ablations to isolate the effects of architectural components, attention-free versus standard attention, training stages, model depth, and the learnable adaptation parameter alpha. GLOP therefore provides a comparable large-scale routing evaluation protocol and a baseline context for testing whether ICAM’s instance-conditioned adaptation and three-stage training improve cross-scale generalization on TSP, CVRP, and ATSP.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt reports qualitative rather than quantitative outcomes. It states that GLOP outperforms H-TSP despite not learning an upper-level TSP policy, and that GLOP achieves competitive solutions compared with select-and-optimize while being hundreds of times more efficient. No numerical ablation values, per-component performance deltas, significance tests, or exact objective-value gaps are provided in the excerpt, so the quantitative contribution of each GLOP component cannot be extracted from the given text.', 'context': 'Experiments were conducted on a 12-core Intel Xeon Platinum 8255C CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. The evaluation used uniformly sampled large-scale TSP instances of 500, 1,000, 10,000, and 100,000 nodes, cross-distribution test instances, 49 symmetric TSPLIB EUC2D instances with fewer than 1,000 nodes normalized to the unit square, ATSP instances, CVRP instances of 1,000, 2,000, 5,000, and 7,000 nodes with 100 instances per scale, CVRPLIB instances, and PCTSP instances of 500, 1,000, and 5,000 nodes with prize sampling parameters K_n set to 9, 12, and 20 respectively. Baselines were selected to have adjustable runtime so that comparisons could be matched to GLOP, and evaluation metrics included objective value and runtime. This context is relevant to the ICAM plan because ICAM targets similar problem families and scales, especially 100 to 5,000 nodes for TSP and CVRP and up to 1,000 nodes for ATSP, and proposes ablations to determine whether its adaptation function, attention-free module, deeper encoder, and three-stage training improve large-scale generalization relative to existing constructive and two-stage routing solvers.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3371, 'output_tokens': 3634}, {'corpus_id': '261243129', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper evaluates ELG-POMO, a neural construction method that combines a POMO global policy with a transferrable local policy operating on the local neighborhood of the current node. The relevant experimental approach is an ablation and sensitivity study: ELG-POMO is trained with the same uniform N=100 training protocol as POMO, then compared with POMO to isolate the effect of the local policy; the paper also references component ablations, hyperparameter sensitivity, and a local-only policy evaluation. Evaluation uses greedy decoding, POMO x8 instance augmentation, average gap to best known solutions, and runtime on TSPLIB, CVRPLIB Set-X and Set-XXL, and synthetic TSP/CVRP distributions. This is related to the proposed ICAM method because it tests a proposed architectural addition under a controlled baseline, similar to the planned ablations of ICAM's adaptation function, AAFM versus standard MHA, training stages, model depth, and the learnable parameter alpha.", 'results': "The available quantitative outcomes show that adding the local policy improves performance over the global-only POMO baseline. On uniform N=100 instances, ELG-POMO achieves a 0.22% gap on TSP and a 1.22% gap on CVRP, versus 0.40% and 1.34% for POMO. On synthetic TSP N=200 instances, ELG* gaps are 1.84% for cluster, 1.62% for mixture, 1.43% for explosion, and 1.58% for rotation, compared with POMO* gaps of 2.63%, 2.45%, 2.18%, and 2.33%, and AMDKD gaps of 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54%. On larger CVRPLIB instances with 200<N<=1000, ELG-POMO reports a 6.46% gap versus 6.95% for Omni-POMO; on TSPLIB instances with 200<N<=1002, it reports 5.90% versus 7.47% for Omni-POMO. The paper also states that ELG-POMO beats the runner-up by 0.53% on CVRP and 0.50% on TSP for N<=200, and that runtime is lower than BQ and diffusion-based methods, although the local policy increases time relative to pure POMO. These results are relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because they demonstrate that a component ablation can quantify the contribution of an added module to both in-distribution and cross-distribution/cross-scale performance, although the paper does not test ICAM's specific adaptation function, AAFM, alpha, or three-stage training.", 'context': "Experimental conditions include training on uniformly distributed TSP/CVRP instances with N=100, greedy inference, POMO x8 augmentation for POMO-based methods, and best-known-solution gap metrics using Concorde or LKH3 references for uniform tests. Test conditions span N<=200 for cross-distribution evaluation, 200<N<=1000 or 1002 for cross-scale evaluation, CVRPLIB Set-XXL real-world instances with N=3000-7000, and synthetic cluster, mixture, explosion, and rotation distributions at N=100 and N=200. Hardware details are not specified in the provided excerpt. The context is relevant to the proposed ICAM research plan because it uses the same style of controlled scale and distribution evaluation that ICAM will use to compare AAFM against standard MHA, different depths, and training stages; however, the paper's model is not ICAM and its local policy is not equivalent to the instance-conditioned adaptation function f(N,d_ij) or the learnable parameter alpha."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5652, 'output_tokens': 5269}, {'corpus_id': '226222332', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper describes POMO's shared-baseline policy-gradient training method. For each problem instance, the policy network samples N solution trajectories, computes the return or total reward for each trajectory, and uses a shared baseline derived from the set of sampled trajectories in the REINFORCE-style gradient update. This shared baseline is contrasted with a greedy-rollout baseline, where each sampled rollout is evaluated against an argmax rollout from the same starting node. The method is implemented with mini-batch training and avoids requiring a separately trained critic network or a cloned policy network for baseline estimation. It is relevant to the proposed research method and research plan because ICAM builds on POMO-style training, uses a POMO loss, and plans ablations of training stages and hyperparameters; the shared baseline is a core training component whose contribution to gradient stability and convergence could be isolated in such ablations.", 'results': 'The provided excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than quantitative benchmark results. It states that the POMO shared baseline produces an advantage term with zero mean, whereas the greedy-rollout baseline tends to produce negative advantages most of the time. It also reports that sampled rollouts have difficulty outperforming greedy rollouts, that the shared baseline reduces variance in policy gradients compared with the greedy-rollout baseline, and that it makes training more resistant to premature convergence or local minima. No specific numerical values, statistical significance tests, or direct performance comparisons to other solvers are included in the excerpt.', 'context': "The method is applied to constructive neural combinatorial optimization policy networks, with the paper context including traveling salesman problem, capacitated vehicle routing problem, and 0-1 knapsack problem settings. The training setup uses N sampled trajectories per instance, mini-batch optimization, and a policy network parameterized by θ. The excerpt does not specify hardware, exact hyperparameters, trajectory count N, learning rates, or dataset sizes. This context matters for the proposed ICAM research because ICAM evaluates TSP, CVRP, and ATSP at large scales and uses a three-stage training scheme with POMO loss and top-k elite training; the paper's shared-baseline method provides a relevant baseline training component for understanding the stability and ablation of ICAM's training design."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2425, 'output_tokens': 2083}, {'corpus_id': '278151274', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper conducts component-level effectiveness analyses, which are methodologically similar to the proposed ablation studies, for two generalization components: a size-scaling factor (ESF) and a distribution-specific decoder (DS decoder). ESF is a fixed log-size ratio, log(n_tr/n_te) at test time and log(n_b/n_tr) during training, applied to the attention or selection weights of existing neural VRP solvers such as AM, POMO, MDAM, LEHD, BQ, and OMNI-VRP. It does not change the base model architecture and is evaluated by comparing original models against the same models with ESF added. The DS decoder trains one decoder per distribution pattern with a shared encoder and evaluates cross-distribution generalization by comparing single-distribution POMO, AMDKD, OMNI-VRP, and the DS decoder approach. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because ESF is a simple scale-conditioned modification analogous to the proposed instance-conditioned adaptation function f(N, d_ij) = -alpha log2 N d_ij, and the paper isolates the contribution of scale-aware and distribution-aware components. However, the paper does not test an attention-free module, three-stage training, model depth, or a learnable alpha, so its evidence is only partially aligned with the proposed ablation scope.', 'results': 'For test-time ESF on TSP, the baseline models degrade when scaling up or down, while ESF gives an average relative improvement of 14.09% for upscaling and 7.32% for downscaling. For CVRP, ESF improves downscaling by 5.97% but reduces upscaling performance by 3.54%, indicating that the fixed scaling factor can be harmful for CVRP upscaling. On large-scale TSP using LEHD and BQ, ESF gives an average relative improvement of 10.76%. For training-time ESF with OMNI-VRP, TSP performance improves by 18.38% on in-size generalization and 13.8% on cross-size generalization, while CVRP improves by 4.51% on in-size and 4.86% on cross-size generalization. For the DS decoder, the average optimality gap improves by 35.44% over POMO and 22.31% over AMDKD. The proportion of cases where AMDKD performs worse than a single-distribution POMO model is 92%, whereas the DS decoder has deterioration in 16.7% of cases. The best DS decoder shows no deterioration relative to single-distribution POMO when solving instances from the corresponding distribution. The additional inference time is about 0.01 minute, and the paper estimates that training three teacher models for AMDKD on TSP-100 would take about 15 days. Robustness tests show minimal performance differences when the DS decoder is trained on Uniform-rectangle, Diagonal, or Gaussian distributions instead of Uniform, Cluster, or Uniform-Cluster mixed distributions.', 'context': 'Experiments use 10,000 randomly generated VRP instances per test condition. Size-generalization tests use TSP and CVRP instances of sizes 50, 100, and 200, with large-scale TSP tests at size 1000. Real-world benchmark tests use TSPLIB instances with 52 to 200 nodes and CVRPLIB instances with 32 to 101 nodes. Hardware is an Intel Xeon Gold 6254 CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. Distribution-generalization experiments use Uniform, Cluster, and Uniform-Cluster mixed distributions for training, and Implosion, Explosion, Expansion, plus the three training distributions, for testing. The DS decoder experiments follow AMDKD settings, use Adam with learning rate 1e-4, match training iterations, and use greedy rollout with 8 augmentations during testing. TSPLIB experiments are repeated 10 times with different random seeds and reported as mean with a 95% confidence interval. The paper does not provide ablations for AAFM versus standard MHA, three-stage training, encoder depth, or sensitivity to a learnable alpha, so its findings should be interpreted as evidence for fixed log-size scaling and distribution-specific decoding rather than direct validation of the full ICAM design.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5710, 'output_tokens': 4860}, {'corpus_id': '165163853', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2819, 'output_tokens': 1504}, {'corpus_id': '271404338', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '259298711', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '5692837', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper trains a Ptr-Net sequence-to-sequence model to generate solution sequences for planar point-set problems, including the Travelling Salesman Problem. For each training example, n points are sampled uniformly from [0, 1] x [0, 1], and the target output is a sequence representing the solution for the given point set. This method is related to the overall research plan because TSP is one of the target routing problems and Ptr-Net is a constructive neural solver in the same broad family as the baselines considered. It is related to the proposed ablation method only indirectly: the paper fixes a simple architecture and hyperparameters rather than ablating ICAM-specific components such as the adaptation function, AAFM versus standard MHA, training stages, model depth, or the learnable parameter alpha.', 'results': 'No quantitative performance outcomes are provided in the supplied sections; the Empirical Results section is empty. The only reported experimental observation is that overfitting was observed in some cases where the task was simpler, specifically for small n.', 'context': 'All models used a single-layer LSTM with either 256 or 512 hidden units, trained with stochastic gradient descent at a learning rate of 1.0, batch size of 128, random uniform weight initialization from -0.08 to 0.08, and L2 gradient clipping of 2.0. Training used 1M generated input-output pairs. The authors explicitly state that no extensive architecture or hyperparameter search was performed and that the same architecture was used across all problems, which contrasts with the proposed systematic ablation of model depth, hyperparameters, AAFM versus MHA, training stages, and alpha.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2082, 'output_tokens': 3360}, {'corpus_id': '278768989', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '266999156', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a distance-aware attention reshaping method called DAR for a neural vehicle routing solver. It first computes standard dot-product attention scores from encoder features for candidate nodes, then computes Euclidean distances from the currently selected node to all candidate nodes. These distances are converted into distance scores using a logarithmic transformation under the assumption that nodes lie in a unit square, with a hyperparameter K used to favor nearby nodes and penalize distant nodes. The distance scores are added to the original attention scores, normalized with a scaling constant C set to 50, and the resulting weights are used to select the next node. This method is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because both inject distance-based geometric information into attention or compatibility calculations to improve cross-scale generalization. It is also relevant to the proposed ablation studies because it contains distance-based adaptation components and hyperparameters, such as K and C, that are conceptually analogous to the adaptation function and hyperparameter sensitivity questions in the ICAM plan, although DAR does not use a learnable alpha, scale factor N, or an attention-free module.', 'results': 'On CVRPLib, DAR achieved optimality gaps of 5.66 percent on 100 Set-X instances with 100 to 1000 nodes, 5.20 percent on 35 Set-XML instances with 100 to 5000 nodes, and 10.82 percent on 6 Set-XXL instances with 3000 to 11000 nodes. On a 32-instance Set-X subset with 500 to 1000 nodes, the gap was 5.27 percent. DAR outperformed greedy, POMO, Omni-VRP, ELG, and several decomposition baselines on the reported sets. Compared with greedy, DAR reduced gaps from 21.03 percent to 5.66 percent on Set-X, from 19.64 percent to 5.20 percent on Set-XML, and from 13.08 percent to 10.82 percent on Set-XXL. Compared with ELG, DAR improved from 5.92 percent to 5.66 percent on Set-X, from 5.95 percent to 5.20 percent on Set-XML, and from 16.08 percent to 10.82 percent on Set-XXL. On Set-XXL, DAR also outperformed TAM-AM, TAM-LKH3, GLOP, and GLOP-LKH3, whose gaps were 30.07 percent, 22.50 percent, 19.75 percent, and 18.80 percent respectively. DAR did not beat LKH3 or HGS on smaller sets, where LKH3 achieved 1.00 percent and HGS achieved 0.11 percent or 0.00 percent, but DAR was much faster, requiring 0.39 seconds versus at least 16 minutes for LKH3 and HGS on Set-X. No statistical significance tests were reported.', 'context': 'Training used randomly generated node coordinates and demands for small-scale instances, while testing used CVRPLib Set-X, expanded Set-XML, and Set-XXL instances. DAR assumes node coordinates are in a unit square, uses Euclidean distance, applies a logarithmic distance score, uses K to limit the effective neighborhood, and sets C to 50. The method adds no extra learnable parameters. Some baseline results were taken from original papers, while greedy, POMO, and ELG were run with the same training method and default settings. Hardware context included LKH3 and HGS running on an Intel Gold 6148 Skylake 2.4 GHz processor, while DAR used GPU parallel processing. The provided text does not report the specific ablations proposed in the research plan, such as AAFM versus standard multi-head attention, individual training stages, model depth effects, or sensitivity to a learnable alpha parameter.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4755, 'output_tokens': 4533}, {'corpus_id': '13756489', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1847, 'output_tokens': 662}, {'corpus_id': '219150860', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1742, 'output_tokens': 472}, {'corpus_id': '229331629', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a controlled convergence-profile comparison of CVRP heuristics, with a focused ablation of the HGS neighborhood by comparing the original HGS variant against the modern HGS-CVRP variant that uses the Swap* neighborhood. Each algorithm is run on the 100 classical CVRPLIB benchmark instances, and the best objective value is recorded at 1%, 2%, 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 30%, 50%, 75%, and 100% of a per-instance time limit. The final average gap to the best known solution is computed over ten independent runs. The comparison also includes HILS, LKH-3, KGLS, SISR, FILO, and the OR-Tools guided local search variant. This method relates to the proposed ICAM research plan because it isolates one design choice, the Swap* neighborhood, and evaluates its contribution under identical instances, time budgets, hardware, and metrics, which is methodologically analogous to the proposed ablations of AAFM versus standard MHA, training stages, model depth, and the learnable parameter alpha.', 'results': 'In the displayed portion of the final-quality table, HGS-CVRP obtains a zero gap on many instances, including X-n101-k25, X-n110-k13, X-n115-k10, X-n120-k6, X-n125-k30, X-n129-k18, X-n134-k13, X-n139-k10, X-n143-k7, X-n148-k46, X-n157-k13, X-n162-k11, X-n167-k10, X-n172-k51, X-n176-k26, and X-n181-k23, and obtains small gaps on others, such as 0.02 on X-n153-k22 and 0.07 on X-n106-k14. The original HGS variant is also strong, matching the best known solution on most displayed instances, but HGS-CVRP improves X-n106-k14 from a 0.18 gap to a 0.07 gap and otherwise matches or ties the original variant. Compared with the other solvers in the displayed rows, OR-Tools gaps range from 0.48 to 3.03, LKH-3 gaps range from 0.00 to 0.73, HILS gaps range from 0.00 to 1.09, KGLS gaps range from 0.00 to 0.43, SISR gaps range from 0.00 to 0.20, and FILO gaps range from 0.00 to 0.36. The excerpt reports average final gaps but does not provide variance, confidence intervals, or formal significance tests, so the evidence for the contribution of Swap* is based on average final solution quality rather than statistically formalized ablation evidence.', 'context': 'The experiments use 100 classical CVRPLIB instances with customer counts from 100 to 1000. The time limit is T_max = n x 240/100 seconds, so the smallest instances run for 4 minutes and the largest for 40 minutes. All runs are single-threaded on an Intel Gold 6148 Skylake 2.4 GHz processor with 40 GB of RAM and CentOS 7.8.2003. HGS, HILS, LKH-3, FILO, and OR-Tools are implemented in C/C++ and compiled with g++ 9.1.0, while KGLS and SISR use Java OpenJDK 13.0.1. Ten independent runs are performed with different seeds, and deterministic algorithms are varied by permuting customer order. The gap metric is 100 x (z - z_BKS) / z_BKS, where z is the algorithm solution value and z_BKS is the best known solution value. This context is relevant to the ICAM plan because cross-scale generalization should be assessed under fixed wall-clock budgets, multiple seeds, and gap-to-reference metrics across instance scales, rather than only by final objective values.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5040, 'output_tokens': 3989}]): Request timed out.
Error processing ("We will evaluate generalization capability on instances with different distributions (rotation, explosion) and benchmark datasets (TSPLIB, CVRPLIB) to demonstrate robustness across problem variants; this tests whether ICAM's cross-scale gains hold beyond the primary generated instance distributions.", [{'corpus_id': '2749712', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '259286841', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '14096340', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '247595029', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a controlled ablation study of a unified TSP pipeline to test zero-shot generalization. Identical model configurations are trained on small generated TSP instances, specifically TSP20-50, using either supervised learning with teacher forcing from an optimal solver or reinforcement learning with a rollout baseline. The trained models are then evaluated on smaller and larger TSP instances, including TSP200, under three inference/search settings: greedy decoding, beam search, and sampling. A non-learned furthest insertion heuristic is used as a baseline, constructing a partial tour by repeatedly inserting the node whose nearest current tour node is farthest. The method is relevant to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing because it provides a generalization-evaluation protocol for constructive neural routing solvers beyond the training scale, although it tests size generalization rather than rotation, explosion, TSPLIB, or CVRPLIB distributions.', 'results': 'The paper reports qualitative outcomes only in the supplied excerpt. Under greedy decoding, reinforcement-learning-trained models outperform supervised-learning-trained models on the training size and on larger graphs. Under beam search or sampling, supervised-learning-trained models outperform their reinforcement-learning counterparts. The reinforcement-learning rollout baseline produces very confident next-node predictions even outside the training size range, while supervised teacher forcing produces less confident predictions but probability distributions that are more amenable to beam search and sampling. No numerical optimality gaps, solution-quality values, inference times, or statistical significance tests are provided in the excerpt.', 'context': "Experimental conditions include a base model configuration with one pipeline component ablated per experiment, fair comparison intended in model capacity and training data, training on small TSP20-50 instances, evaluation across a wider size range including TSP200, and comparison against a simple furthest insertion heuristic. The inference phase is varied across greedy decoding, beam search, and sampling. Hardware, exact model size, training steps, beam width, number of samples, and statistical details are not specified in the provided text. This context is useful for ICAM because it highlights the need to align training and inference/search when evaluating cross-scale or cross-distribution generalization, but it does not directly validate ICAM's rotation/explosion or benchmark-dataset robustness claims."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2328, 'output_tokens': 3405}, {'corpus_id': '15346313', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '255546018', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a constructive neural routing model by training it on 1 million expert solutions for synthetic size-100 instances and then testing it on synthetic instances of 100, 200, 500, and 1,000 nodes drawn from the same distribution, as well as on standard benchmark datasets: TSPLib instances up to 4,461 nodes and Euclidean CVRPLib instances. For CVRP, the synthetic test sets use capacities of 50, 80, 100, and 250 for the four graph sizes. The model is a 9-layer attention-based architecture with 12 attention heads, embedding size 192, and feed-forward dimension 512. It is trained by imitation learning from expert trajectories using a cross-entropy loss, with batch size 1024, 500 epochs, Adam optimizer, initial learning rate 7.5e-4, and learning-rate decay of 0.98 every 50 epochs. Training trajectories are generated by sampling subpaths of length between 4 and N from expert tours, which provides automatic data augmentation. At test time, except for Perceiver-based variants, the model limits the subgraph to the 250 nearest neighbors of the origin node to reduce inference time. The evaluation compares the model against classical solvers, hybrid methods, and deep constructive baselines, and reports optimality gap and inference time. This relates to the proposed ICAM research method because it uses multi-scale synthetic tests and standard benchmark datasets to assess generalization and robustness, but it does not explicitly test distribution shifts such as rotation or explosion, which are central to the proposed cross-distribution evaluation.', 'results': 'The provided section reports relative and qualitative performance outcomes rather than exact numerical optimality gaps. The model is reported to show superior generalization to larger graphs even with greedy decoding, which generates a single solution, whereas many baselines generate several hundred solutions and select the best. In terms of runtime, the model is slightly slower than Sym-NCO, competitive with POMO, and significantly faster than other deep baselines. Beam search improves optimality gaps but increases runtime. Replacing quadratic attention with a PerceiverIO architecture reduces inference time for larger graphs at the cost of some performance drop, while still remaining competitive with other neural combinatorial optimization baselines. On TSPLib and CVRPLib, the model is reported to significantly outperform end-to-end baseline policies even with greedy decoding. The only methods described as competitive are Att-GCN+MCTS and DIFUSCO+2opt, but both are hybrid methods, are designed for TSP only, and are reported to be 2 to 15 times slower. These results support the proposed research plan’s goal of demonstrating cross-scale and benchmark robustness, but they do not provide direct evidence for robustness under rotation or explosion distribution shifts.', 'context': 'The experimental conditions include training on synthetic size-100 instances with 1 million expert solutions, testing on synthetic sizes 100, 200, 500, and 1,000 from the same distribution, and additional testing on TSPLib and CVRPLib. Expert solutions are obtained using Concorde for TSP, LKH for ATSP and CVRP, and EA4OP for OP. For TSP, optimality gap is computed against Concorde solutions; for CVRP, LKH solutions are used as the reference for comparability with prior work. Deep learning models are run on a single Nvidia Tesla V100-S GPU with 24GB memory, while classical solvers are run on an Intel Xeon E5-2670 CPU with 256GB memory in one thread. These conditions are relevant to the proposed ICAM evaluation because they define the benchmark datasets, scale range, reference-solution protocol, and hardware setup against which large-scale generalization claims can be compared. However, the paper’s synthetic test distribution is the same as the training distribution, so the proposed method’s additional rotation and explosion tests would extend the robustness evaluation beyond what is reported in the paper.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3236, 'output_tokens': 3413}, {'corpus_id': '59608816', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '263909317', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a constructive neural solver called LEHD, which uses a light encoder and heavy decoder trained by supervised learning, followed by greedy decoding and an iterative Random Re-Construct self-improvement procedure. For the cross-distribution and benchmark-dataset evaluation relevant to the proposed ICAM plan, the authors test LEHD on real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances with varying sizes and distributions, in addition to uniformly generated TSP and CVRP instances. The inference protocol reports both greedy single-trajectory solutions and solutions after 1000 Random Re-Construct iterations. Comparisons include POMO and BQ on the benchmark datasets, and for large-scale TSP comparisons include Concorde, SO-mixed, and H-TSP with LKH-3. Metrics are optimality gap relative to known optimal or strong solver solutions and wall-clock inference time.', 'results': 'On the real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib evaluation, the paper states that LEHD has good greedy inference performance and, with 1000 Random Re-Construct iterations, significantly outperforms POMO and BQ on all instances, demonstrating robust generalization. The provided excerpt does not include the exact Table 2 gap values. In the provided large-scale TSP comparison tables using 128 instances, LEHD greedy achieves gaps of 0.859% on TSP200, 1.560% on TSP500, and 3.168% on TSP1000, with runtimes of 3 seconds, 0.3 minutes, and 1.6 minutes. With 100 Random Re-Construct iterations, LEHD improves to 0.0761% on TSP200, 0.343% on TSP500, and 1.218% on TSP1000, with runtimes of 1.2 minutes, 8 minutes, and 43 minutes. These results are better than SO-mixed, which reports 0.636%, 2.401%, and 2.800% gaps, and better than H-TSP with LKH-3, which reports 4.06% on TSP1000, while Concorde remains optimal at 0.000% but with much longer runtimes of 3 minutes, 32 minutes, and 7.8 hours. On uniformly generated instances, the paper reports that with more than 500 Random Re-Construct iterations LEHD is nearly optimal on TSP100 and TSP200, within 0.2% on TSP500, and around 0.8% on TSP1000. For CVRP, LEHD outperforms most learning-based methods and outperforms LKH3 on CVRP200 and CVRP500 with 500 Random Re-Construct iterations, reaching about a 1% gap to LKH3 on CVRP1000 with 1000 iterations.', 'context': 'The relevant experimental conditions include real-world TSPLib and CVRPLib instances with different sizes and distributions, as well as uniformly distributed generated TSP and CVRP instances. The benchmark evaluation uses greedy inference and 1000 Random Re-Construct iterations for LEHD, while other budgets of 100, 300, 500, and 1000 iterations are used in the broader experiments. The TSP comparison tables use 128 instances. The provided excerpt does not specify hardware, exact instance lists, or statistical significance tests. This paper relates to the proposed method because it provides a comparable cross-distribution and benchmark-dataset validation protocol for a constructive neural combinatorial optimization model, whereas the ICAM research plan proposes to use rotation and explosion distributions together with TSPLIB and CVRPLIB to test whether instance-conditioned adaptation yields cross-scale gains beyond the primary generated instance distributions.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3210, 'output_tokens': 3245}, {'corpus_id': '270869731', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper contains a related generalization-evaluation protocol for a neural divide-and-conquer solver called UDC, which is directly comparable to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution test. UDC first uses a lightweight AGNN heatmap-based dividing policy to generate an initial solution, decomposes the instance into sub-problems, and then solves those sub-problems with constructive neural solvers, including AGNN for MIS and POMO or ICAM for VRP-type problems. UDC is trained with a unified Divide-Conquer-Reunion scheme using REINFORCE, where a Reunion step re-solves the boundary between adjacent sub-problems to reduce biased rewards for the dividing policy. For the generalization tests, the paper evaluates UDC on TSPLib instances with 500 to 5,000 nodes, CVRPLib Set-X and very large-scale CVRPLib Set-XXL, and on TSP, CVRP, and MIS instances with different distributions. The reported metrics are objective value, gap to the best known algorithm, and solving time. This relates to the proposed research method because both aim to test whether a neural routing solver’s cross-scale performance persists beyond the primary generated instance distributions, although the paper’s excerpt does not explicitly name rotation or explosion distributions.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not include numeric tables for the TSPLib, CVRPLib, or various-distribution experiments. It only states that UDC-x250 gives competitive benchmark results, is the best learning-based result on CVRPLib, and shows outstanding out-of-domain generalization on TSP, CVRP, and MIS instances with different distributions. In the related 500- to 2,000-node generated-instance evaluation, UDC-x50 with alpha=50 achieved TSP objective/gap/time of 16.78 / 1.58% / 4m at 500 nodes, 23.53 / 1.78% / 8m at 1,000 nodes, and 33.26 / 2.49% / 15m at 2,000 nodes. For CVRP, UDC-x50 achieved 38.34 / 3.01% / 7.7m at 500 nodes, 43.48 / 1.11% / 14m at 1,000 nodes, and 60.94 / 1.55% / 23m at 2,000 nodes. UDC-x250 improved the 500-node CVRP result to 37.99 / 2.06% / 35m and reported 43.00 with a negative gap and 1.2h time at 1,000 nodes, and 60.01 with a negative gap and 2.15h time at 2,000 nodes. ICAM, the constructive solver named in the proposed research plan, achieved TSP gaps of 0.77% at 500 nodes, 1.58% at 1,000 nodes, and 5.93% at 2,000 nodes, and CVRP gaps of 0.69%, 0.16%, and 2.21% at 500, 1,000, and 2,000 nodes, respectively. UDC-x50 and UDC-x250 were better than ICAM on several larger or harder generated sets, but the excerpt does not provide numeric cross-distribution results for ICAM itself.', 'context': 'The generated-instance test sets included 128 TSP500 instances, 128 CVRP500 instances, 100 CVRP1,000 instances, and 100 CVRP2,000 instances. The OP, PCTSP, and SPCTSP tables used 128, 128, and 16 instances at 500, 1,000, and 2,000 nodes, respectively. Benchmark evaluation used TSPLib instances from 500 to 5,000 nodes and CVRPLib Set-X plus Set-XXL. Training used a single NVIDIA Tesla V100S GPU, the Adam optimizer, and a varying-size training scheme; most training tasks were completed within 10 days. UDC used alpha=50 sampled initial solutions for TSP and CVRP and alpha=1 for OP, PCTSP, and SPCTSP. After the greedy UDC-x2 stage, later conquering stages sampled the sub-problem start point from Uniform(0, n). The paper reports gaps to the best algorithm and solving time, with best overall performance bolded and best learning-based method shaded. The excerpt does not report statistical significance tests, does not explicitly identify the distributions as rotation or explosion, and does not provide numeric ATSP results despite mentioning ATSP among the evaluated problems.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 8113, 'output_tokens': 9681}, {'corpus_id': '239009619', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper's related method is a benchmark generalization evaluation of NeuroLKH, a learned LKH-guided routing solver. NeuroLKH uses a Sparse Graph Network to predict edge scores that define an edge candidate set; for CVRP and CVRPTW it does not use node penalties, and for CVRPTW it learns directional in/out edge scores. The network is trained on benchmark-derived training datasets for CVRPLIB and Solomon, then applied to held-out benchmark instances. Inputs include coordinates, demands, capacities, and time windows where applicable. The solver runs LKH trials under time limits matched to original LKH with 100, 1000, and 10000 trials. This relates to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing because it evaluates whether a learned routing model generalizes beyond primary generated uniform instances to real-world benchmark distributions and routing variants, although the excerpt reports CVRPLIB and Solomon rather than rotation/explosion or TSPLIB.", 'results': 'On 43 CVRPLIB instances with 100-300 nodes, NeuroLKH improved average solution quality over original LKH on 38 instances under the 100-trial time limit, 38 under the 1000-trial limit, and 31 under the 10000-trial limit. On 11 Solomon R2-type CVRPTW instances, NeuroLKH outperformed LKH in 32 of 33 instance-setting comparisons across the three time-limit settings. The excerpt does not report exact objective values, optimality gaps, or statistical significance tests.', 'context': 'CVRPLIB instances combine 3 depot positioning, 3 customer positioning, and 7 demand distributions; Solomon R2 instances contain 100 customers with varied time-window distributions. Time limits are defined by the solving time of LKH with 100, 1000, and 10000 trials. For the related generated-instance experiments, SGN inference for 1000 instances took 1s, 3s, 7s, 10s, 19s, and 40s for 40, 100, 200, 300, 500, and 1000 nodes, respectively, and LKH hyperparameters followed official scripts with the SPECIAL setting. No hardware specifications are provided. The paper lists a TSPLIB appendix section, but the provided excerpt does not include its method or results, so TSPLIB-specific outcomes cannot be summarized.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4480, 'output_tokens': 4286}, {'corpus_id': '174798181', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates a graph ConvNet-based constructive solver for 2D Euclidean TSP on uniformly generated instances in the unit square. Separate models are trained and evaluated for fixed problem sizes of 20, 50, and 100 nodes. Each model uses 30 graph convolutional layers, a 3-layer MLP with hidden dimension 300, a 20-nearest-neighbor adjacency graph, and a fixed beam width of 1,280. Training uses 1,000,000 instance-solution pairs per size, with validation and test sets of 10,000 pairs each. Optimal tours are obtained using Concorde. During evaluation, the predicted adjacency matrix is converted into a valid tour using search or sampling strategies, and performance is measured by average predicted tour length and optimality gap relative to the optimal solution. This relates to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing method because it represents an in-distribution TSP evaluation protocol on generated uniform instances; the proposed method would extend such evaluation by adding rotation and explosion distributions and benchmark datasets such as TSPLIB and CVRPLIB to test robustness beyond the primary generated distribution.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report quantitative performance values, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance for the evaluation procedure. It only defines the evaluation setup and metrics, including average predicted tour length and optimality gap, but no numerical outcomes are available in the supplied text.', 'context': 'Key experimental conditions include 2D Euclidean TSP instances with node locations sampled uniformly at random in the unit square, problem sizes of 20, 50, and 100 nodes, 1,000,000 training pairs per size, and 10,000 validation and test pairs per size. Concorde is used to obtain optimal tours. Hyperparameters are identical across sizes: 30 graph convolutional layers, 3 MLP layers, hidden dimension 300, beam width 1,280, and 20 nearest neighbors for the adjacency matrix. Training uses Adam with an initial learning rate of 0.001, mini-batches of 20 instances, and 10,000 randomly selected training instances per epoch. Evaluation uses arbitrarily large batch sizes that fit GPU memory. The excerpt does not include rotation or explosion distributions, TSPLIB, CVRPLIB, or large-scale instances beyond 100 nodes.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2440, 'output_tokens': 2378}, {'corpus_id': '266191425', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper contains a cross-distribution and benchmark evaluation of GLOP that is directly related to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing. GLOP uses a local TSP solver that creates an initial tour with Random Insertion, divides the tour into independent subtours, and applies a learned reviser to reconstruct those subtours. For general routing problems, GLOP adds a global policy that outputs a partition heatmap to split instances into sub-TSPs. The local reviser is trained with REINFORCE using the average length of two greedy rollouts as a baseline, and the global policy is trained with REINFORCE using the average reward of sampled partitions for the same instance as a baseline. The cross-distribution evaluation tests GLOP on non-training distribution instances, 49 symmetric TSPLIB EUC2D instances with fewer than 1000 nodes after min-max normalization to the unit square, and several large-scale CVRPLIB instances. The evaluation compares objective value and runtime against state-of-the-art baselines with adjustable runtime.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report quantitative cross-distribution outcomes for GLOP, such as objective gaps, solution lengths, or runtimes on rotation, explosion, TSPLIB, or CVRPLIB instances. It only states that GLOP is evaluated on these cross-distribution and real-world benchmark instances and gives broader qualitative claims that GLOP outperforms H-TSP and is hundreds of times more efficient than SO, but no numerical result specific to the cross-distribution evaluation is available.', 'context': "The experimental hardware is a 12-core Intel Xeon Platinum 8255C CPU and an NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. TSP evaluation includes uniformly sampled TSP500, TSP1K, TSP10K, and TSP100K instances, cross-distribution test instances, and 49 TSPLIB symmetric EUC2D instances under 1000 nodes normalized to the unit square. CVRP evaluation includes generated CVRP1K, CVRP2K, CVRP5K, and CVRP7K test sets of 100 instances each plus several large-scale CVRPLIB instances. PCTSP evaluation uses generated PCTSP500, PCTSP1K, and PCTSP5K instances with prize sampling parameters K_n equal to 9, 12, and 20. Baselines are chosen to have adjustable runtime so they can be matched to GLOP. This context is relevant to the proposed ICAM plan because both approaches test whether a learned routing solver remains effective beyond the primary generated instance distributions, but the paper excerpt lacks the numerical cross-distribution evidence needed to compare directly with ICAM's expected robustness."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3341, 'output_tokens': 4611}, {'corpus_id': '260351056', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2284, 'output_tokens': 722}, {'corpus_id': '275820693', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '261243129', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates an ensemble neural construction method, ELG-POMO, for TSP and CVRP. It combines a global POMO policy that encodes the full instance with a Transformer encoder-decoder and an auxiliary transferable local policy that constructs solutions using local neighborhood states and actions. The global policy uses masked attention scores to select valid next nodes, and the local policy is trained in alignment with POMO so that the comparison between ELG-POMO and POMO isolates the effect of the local policy. For the cross-distribution evaluation relevant to the proposed ICAM plan, ELG-POMO is trained only on uniform N=100 instances and then tested in greedy mode on TSPLIB and CVRPLIB subsets, CVRPLIB Set-XXL real-world instances, and synthetic small-scale instances from rotation, explosion, cluster, and cluster-mixture distributions. The evaluation metrics are average optimality gap to best known solutions and average runtime per instance.', 'results': 'On TSPLIB and CVRPLIB instances with N<=200, ELG-POMO outperforms all compared learning methods, reducing the gap by 0.53% on CVRP and 0.50% on TSP relative to the runner-up. On the cross-distribution and cross-scale subset with 200<N<=1000 or 200<N<=1002, it reduces the CVRP gap from 6.95% for Omni-POMO to 6.46%, and reduces the TSP gap from 7.47% to 5.90%, although it still lags behind BQ and LEHD on that TSP subset. On four CVRPLIB Set-XXL real-world instances with N=3000-7000, ELG-POMO is best on three instances and substantially improves gaps over POMO-based methods. On synthetic TSP distributions, the retrained ELG* model beats AMDKD on three of four N=100 distributions and all four N=200 distributions, with N=200 gaps of 1.84% for cluster, 1.62% for mixture, 1.43% for explosion, and 1.58% for rotation, compared with AMDKD gaps of 3.01%, 3.50%, 2.63%, and 2.54%, respectively. On uniform N=100 in-distribution tests, ELG-POMO achieves a 0.22% gap on TSP100 and a 1.22% gap on CVRP100, which is competitive with strong learning methods and better than POMO, Sym-POMO, LEHD, and BQ, though still worse than HGS on CVRP100. Runtime is lower than BQ and diffusion-based methods, higher than pure POMO because of the added local policy, and much lower than LKH3 in the reported 1.90s versus 16m comparison, although optimality gaps remain worse than non-learning solvers.', 'context': 'Training uses uniform node distributions at N=100, matching common POMO settings, and POMO-based baselines are early-stopped using larger-scale validation to reduce overfitting. Inference is greedy, and POMO-based methods use x8 instance augmentation. Test conditions include TSPLIB and CVRPLIB subsets split by scale, CVRPLIB Set-XXL instances with N=3000-7000, and synthetic TSP and CVRP instances at N=100 and N=200 from rotation, explosion, cluster, and cluster-mixture distributions. The synthetic comparison with AMDKD uses retrained POMO* and ELG* models aligned to AMDKD training distributions, namely uniform, cluster, and cluster mixture. This setup directly parallels the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing on rotation and explosion instances plus TSPLIB and CVRPLIB benchmarks, providing a related baseline for assessing whether cross-scale gains persist under out-of-distribution and real-world routing instances.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5622, 'output_tokens': 4661}, {'corpus_id': '165163853', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies a reinforcement-learning neural rewriter for optimization problems, including vehicle routing. The method starts from an initial feasible solution, computes a state-dependent set of local regions, selects a region using a region-picking policy, selects an applicable local rewriting rule using a rule-picking policy, and applies the rule to update the solution iteratively until convergence. The policies are trained with a reward-based Q-actor-critic objective. This is related to the proposed ICAM research plan because it is a learned vehicle-routing improvement method whose generalization behavior across instances is evaluated, although the supplied excerpt does not describe TSPLIB, CVRPLIB, rotation, or explosion instance distributions.', 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not provide quantitative outcomes, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance for the vehicle-routing generalization evaluation. It only indicates that the paper contains sections on scalability, robustness, and generalization, but no numerical results are available in the provided text.', 'context': 'Experiments were run on a server with 2 Quadro GP100 GPUs and 80 CPU cores. Neural network evaluation used 1 GPU, while search algorithms used 4 CPU cores and a 10-second per-instance timeout. The neural networks were implemented in PyTorch. The rewriter requires an initial feasible solution and operates through iterative local rewrites, so its evaluation depends on instance generation, initial solution quality, and the definition of local regions and rewriting rules.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2789, 'output_tokens': 3848}, {'corpus_id': '256975543', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '207225504', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '271404338', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '237562994', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluated generalization of L2GLS, a reinforcement-learning-guided local-search method, by training on uniformly random unit-square TSP and CVRP instances and then testing on external benchmark sets: TSPLIB Euclidean TSP instances and standard CVRP benchmark instances. It also trained and tested on different instance sizes to assess cross-size generalization. For TSPLIB, two variants were compared: L2GLS with a penalty term and L2GLS without the penalty term, both using the same local-search operator set consisting of 2-opt, relocate, swap, and three-permutation moves. The evaluation focused on solution quality relative to optimal or near-optimal reference solutions, and in related sections reported average tour length and gap over 1,000 random instances. This parallels the proposed ICAM cross-distribution and benchmark evaluation, although the paper does not test rotation or explosion distributions.', 'results': 'The paper reports that L2GLS was tested on thirty-nine Euclidean TSPLIB instances and, more broadly, on up to 1,002 benchmark instances, achieving solutions close to the optimal solution. On TSPLIB, the variant with the penalty term significantly improved solution quality over the variant using only local-search operators without the penalty term; exact numerical gaps are not provided in the supplied excerpt. For CVRP benchmark instances, the paper states that solution quality was compared across various benchmark scenarios, but no specific benchmark gap values are included in the excerpt. In the primary random-instance CVRP evaluation, L2GLS achieved average tour lengths of 5.85, 10.30, and 14.67 for CVRP20, CVRP50, and CVRP100, respectively, and was reported as the best among the listed methods.', 'context': 'Training instances were generated with node coordinates uniformly sampled from the unit square. TSP sizes included 20, 50, and 100 nodes for small-scale evaluation and 200, 500, and 1,000 nodes for large-scale evaluation. CVRP instances used customer demands uniformly sampled from 1 to 9, vehicle capacities of 20, 30, and 40 for N = 20, 50, and 100, and a capacity of 50 for large sizes. Most random-instance metrics were averaged over 1,000 instances, except CVRP1000, which was averaged over 200 instances due to time constraints. Benchmark generalization used TSPLIB Euclidean instances and standard CVRP benchmark data, with different training and testing sizes reported in supplementary material. The paper does not describe rotation or explosion distribution tests, so its benchmark generalization evidence is narrower than the proposed ICAM cross-distribution plan.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4626, 'output_tokens': 3548}, {'corpus_id': '270218206', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2025, 'output_tokens': 1178}, {'corpus_id': '229331629', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a controlled CVRPLIB benchmarking protocol to compare the Swap*-based HGS-CVRP variant against HGS-2012 and six other CVRP heuristics: HILS, LKH-3, KGLS, SISR, FILO, and OR-Tools GLS. It runs each algorithm on 100 classical CVRPLIB instances with 100 to 1000 customers, records the best objective value at 1, 2, 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 50, 75, and 100 percent of the time limit, performs ten independent runs, and computes the percentage gap to the best known solution. This method is related to the proposed research method and plan only in that it evaluates HGS, a planned baseline, on CVRPLIB benchmark instances; it does not evaluate ICAM, rotation or explosion distributions, TSPLIB, or neural cross-scale generalization.', 'results': 'In the displayed portion of Table 2 covering 18 fully listed instances, HGS-CVRP had an average gap of 0.005 percent and reached the best known solution on 16 instances, with gaps of 0.07 percent on X-n106-k14 and 0.02 percent on X-n153-k22. HGS-2012 averaged 0.011 percent gap, FILO 0.069 percent, SISR 0.048 percent, HILS 0.207 percent, KGLS 0.193 percent, LKH-3 0.309 percent, and OR-Tools 1.934 percent. HGS-CVRP was therefore among the best methods in the excerpt, often tied with HGS-2012, FILO, or HILS at the best known solution, and clearly ahead of OR-Tools and LKH-3 on the listed instances.', 'context': 'Experiments used a single thread of an Intel Gold 6148 Skylake 2.4 GHz processor with 40 GB RAM on CentOS 7.8.2003. C/C++ implementations were compiled with g++ 9.1.0 and Java implementations used OpenJDK 13.0.1. The time limit was T_max = n x 240/100 seconds, giving 4 minutes for 100 customers and 40 minutes for 1000 customers. Ten runs were used; deterministic KGLS and OR-Tools were varied by random customer permutations. Gaps were computed against best known solution values from CVRPLIB. The paper reports averages and gaps but does not report statistical significance tests in the excerpt.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5010, 'output_tokens': 7891}, {'corpus_id': '267413113', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper's related method is a cross-scale and cross-distribution evaluation protocol for a neural constructive routing solver called INViT. It trains a REINFORCE-based encoder-decoder with data augmentation, including rotation, reflection, normalization, and varied starting nodes, and maintains a separate baseline model. During training, the baseline tour is produced by greedy rollout and the training tour by random sampling; the loss uses the performance difference between the two. At test time, augmented instances are evaluated and the best augmented solution is kept, and the baseline parameters are replaced when the trained model outperforms the baseline. For the part related to the proposed method, the paper evaluates generated MSVDRP TSP and CVRP instances across multiple scales and distribution families, and public benchmarks: 77 symmetric TSPLIB instances with 51 to 18512 nodes and 100 CVRPLIB Set-X instances with 100 to 1000 nodes. It compares against representative constructive baselines and reports average gap to near-optimal solutions from Gurobi, LKH3, HGS, or known optima, along with inference time. This is directly related to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing because both use distribution-shift and public benchmark evaluation to check whether large-scale gains persist beyond the primary generated instance distributions, although the paper uses rotation/reflection/normalization augmentation and non-uniform/clustered distributions rather than explicitly naming explosion distributions.", 'results': 'INViT-3V achieved the best reported large-scale gaps: 7.97% on TSP-5000, 7.52% on TSP-10000, and 5.63% on CVRP-5000. Most comparison methods had average gaps above 10% on these large-scale datasets, except BQ-NCO on CVRP-5000. When moving from uniform to other distributions, INViT-3V had relative gap increases of 31% on TSP-5000 and 33% on TSP-10000, and 8% on CVRP-5000; Omni-TSP/VRP had smaller relative increases of 9%, 8%, and -40%, but INViT was trained only on uniform distributions and still outperformed Omni-TSP/VRP on all large-scale datasets. INViT-2V showed similar generalization with lower inference time, and INViT-3V gave slight improvements. On small and medium scales, such as TSP-100, TSP-1000, and CVRP-500, INViT did not always outperform baselines; the differences were subtle, with a relative gap of 10% to the best baseline on TSP-1000 and 43% on CVRP-500. On public TSPLIB and CVRPLIB Set-X instances, the paper reports that strong generalization still held and performance was comparable to baselines on small and medium instances, which was better than its relative performance on the generated MSVDRP datasets. No statistical significance tests are reported.', 'context': 'Key conditions include Euclidean 2D coordinates for the symmetric TSPLIB instances, CVRPLIB Set-X instances, and generated MSVDRP instances. Near-optimal references were Gurobi for TSP-100, LKH3 for TSP-1000, TSP-5000, and TSP-10000, HGS for CVRP, and known optima for TSPLIB and CVRPLIB. The paper notes that LKH3 and HGS may not produce exact optima, but comparisons remain fair because the same evaluation instances are used. Some public instances caused out-of-memory issues for some methods. The provided excerpt does not specify hardware, exact hyperparameters, or the number of augmented instances, but it specifies scale ranges, distribution shift from uniform to other/clustered distributions, and inference-time reporting. This context matters for the ICAM plan because the proposed method also relies on TSPLIB, CVRPLIB, and distribution variants to test robustness, so the paper provides a comparable evaluation setting and a caution that distribution shift and scale proximity to training data can affect apparent generalization.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3644, 'output_tokens': 6029}, {'corpus_id': '264555480', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper includes an additional generalization and benchmark evaluation for its NeuOpt solver, separate from the main uniform TSP/CVRP experiments. After training and testing on uniformly generated TSP and CVRP instances of sizes 20, 50, and 100, the authors report extra experiments on real-world routing datasets and larger 200-node instances, specifically CVRPLIB and TSP-200/CVRP-200. The trained NeuOpt model, with K=4 and random sequential initial solutions, is applied to these instances to assess whether performance extends beyond the primary generated distribution. This is conceptually related to the proposed ICAM cross-distribution testing on rotation/explosion instances and TSPLIB/CVRPLIB, but the paper’s evaluation is for NeuOpt rather than ICAM and does not explicitly cover rotation/explosion distributions or 1,000+ node scales.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not contain the numerical outcomes for the CVRPLIB, TSP-200, or CVRP-200 generalization experiments. It only indicates that these additional experimental results are reported in the appendix. Therefore, no specific optimality gap, baseline comparison, inference time, or statistical significance can be extracted from the supplied text for this cross-distribution/benchmark evaluation.', 'context': 'The main experimental setup uses uniformly generated TSP and CVRP instances with N=20, 50, and 100; NeuOpt uses K=4; and initial solutions are constructed sequentially in a random fashion for both training and inference. Experiments are run on NVIDIA 2080Ti GPUs and an Intel E5-2680 CPU at 2.40 GHz. The solver also uses GIRE with reward-shaping weights alpha and beta set to 0.05, and D2A generates new augmented instances when the solver fails to improve for a maximum number of consecutive steps. The real-world and 200-node results are positioned as additional generalization evidence, but their detailed instance protocols, evaluation metrics, and numerical values are not included in the excerpt.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3272, 'output_tokens': 3696}]): Request timed out.
